{"row_id": "q001", "objective": "Tamuzimod safety findings and adverse events in Moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["Tamuzimod"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q001-3", "query": "Tamuzimod UEGW 2024 safety", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Tamuzimod UEGW 2024 safety", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 950.4, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "VTX002 UEGW 2024", "url": "https://ir.ventyxbio.com/static-files/47cbb9ba-f8c5-43e5-9235-01ca63f91f25", "domain": "ir.ventyxbio.com", "snippet": "Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data\n...\n• Tamuzimod (formerly VTX002) is a novel oral selective S1PR1 modulator in development for the \ntreatment of UC\n• The efficacy and safety of tamuzimod as induction therapy was previously demonstrated in a phase 2, \nmulticentre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial (NCT05156125)1\n...\n• Maintenance treatment with both 30 mg and 60 mg tamuzimod was efficacious and   \nwell-tolerated for up to 52 weeks\n• High rates of both clinical and endoscopic remission observed during tamuzimod \ninduction and maintenance therapy potentially a result of rapid and sustained absolute lymphocyte count (ALC) reductions\n• 58.9% (tamuzimod 30 mg) and 71.5% (tamuzimod 60 mg) ALC decrease from baseline at Week 52\n• Efficacy and safety data from this LTE treatment period support the continued clinical", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-01-28"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "UEG Week 2024 - CONFERENCE REPORTPEER-REVIEWED", "url": "https://conferences.medicom-publishers.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/E-UEGW-MCR-2024.pdf", "domain": "conferences.medicom-publishers.com", "snippet": "bowel \n...\nThe cumulative 24-week and 52-week drug survival rates were similar between the 12-week and 8-week dosing groups\n(70.2% vs 76.1%; P=0.45; see Figure).\n...\n“These were, however, preliminary study results, and the completed follow-up—expected in May 2025—is needed for further confirmation.”\n...\n“Etrasimod was well tolerated,” highlighted Prof. D’Haens.\nThe most common treatment-emergent adverse events in the 2 mg and 3 mg arms were worsening of CD (25% and\n21.6%, respectively), headache (10.7% and 16.2%), arthralgia\n(10.7% and 13.5%), and COVID-19 (10.7% and 24.3%).\n“Severe \ninfections were seen in 3.6% and 5.4% of the participants in the \n2 and 3 mg arms,” added Prof. D’Haens.\nFinally, 2 participants\nin the 3 mg arm had a first-degree AV block, 1 participant in the 2 mg experienced bradycardia, and 1 participant in the 3 mg arm had hypertension.\n...\nTamuzimod delivers promising long-term data \nin UC \nTamuzimod was associated with improved clinical, endoscopic, and histologic outcomes compared with placebo in participants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) at week 52 of a phase 2 study.\nMoreover, the drug had a favourable safety profile.\nThe selective S1PR1 modulator tamuzimod displayed encouraging efficacy and safety data as induction therapy for patients with UC [1].\n...\nAt week 52, the clinical remission rates were 50% in both \ntamuzimod arms and 18.2% in the placebo arm (P=0.021; \nP=0.0078).\nEndoscopic remission was documented in 46.4%\nof participants in the 60 mg arm, 43.8% in the 30 mg arm, \n...\n“The drug was \nvery safe, and there were no major adverse events,” according to Prof. Danese.\n“This is probably due to the high selectivity of tamuzimod,” he argued.\n“Maintenance treatment with either 30 mg or 60 mg of\ntamuzimod was efficacious and well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks, supporting the continued development of tamuzimod as a treatment for patients with UC,” concluded\nProf. Danese.\n1. Sands BE, et al.\n...\n2. Danese S, et al.\n...\n“The severe adverse event rates were low, with 10% (n=3) \nof the participants in the 100 mg arm and 3% (n=1) of the \nparticipants in the 250 mg arm,” expressed Dr Ma.\nNo acute infusion reactions or peri-infusion reactions were reported.\nThe infection rate was 37% in the 100 mg arm and 46% in the 250 mg arm.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-28"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase ...", "url": "https://ir.ventyxbio.com/news-releases/news-release-details/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-phase-2-trial/", "domain": "ir.ventyxbio.com", "snippet": "### Late-breaking abstract highlights high rates of clinical and endoscopic remission among LTE completers at Week 52\nSAN DIEGO, Oct. 15, 2024 (GLOBE NEWSWIRE) -- Ventyx Biosciences, Inc.\n(Nasdaq: VTYX) (“Ventyx”), a clinical-stage biopharmaceutical company focused on advancing novel oral therapies that address a broad range of inflammatory diseases with significant unmet medical need, today announced that a late-breaking abstract including new long-term extension (LTE) data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis was presented on October 15, 2024 during the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria.\n“We are excited to present the new long-term extension data from the tamuzimod Phase 2 trial in patients with ulcerative colitis at UEG Week,” said Raju Mohan, PhD, Chief Executive Officer.\n“These 52-week data continue to reinforce the potential best-in-class profile of our S1P1R modulator tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis, with a potential best-in-disease safety profile amongst all the oral options for UC therapy.\nWe believe the high rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission position tamuzimod as the backbone of future combination therapies for UC.”\n...\n**Title: **“Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data”", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-03-23"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "tamuzimod (VTX002) / Eli Lilly", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=c9a09d69-e0fc-4b37-ab3a-5af0056bd49c", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## VTX002 Versus Placebo for the Treatment of Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=213 | Terminated | Sponsor: Oppilan Pharma Ltd | The Sponsor decided to terminate clinical conduct in the Open-Label Extension Period. The decision to terminate the study was not due to safety concerns.\n...\n## Tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis: a multicentre, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial. (PubMed, Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol) - P2 | \"Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis. These results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.\"\n...\nOctober 15, 2024\n## Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase 2 Trial of VTX002 (Tamuzimod) in Ulcerative Colitis at UEG Week 2024 (GlobeNewswire) - \"Ventyx Biosciences, Inc...announced that a late-breaking abstract including new long-term extension (LTE) data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis was presented on October 15, 2024 during the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences' Tamuzimod Shows Promising Long-Term ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/news/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-from-the-phase-2-trial-of-vtx002", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "## Key Insights\n- Ventyx BiosciencesView company profile presented 52-week data from its Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease at UEG Week 2024, showcasing sustained efficacy.\n- The data reinforce tamuzimodView drug details's potential as a best-in-class S1P1R (search)Search target modulator with a favorable safety profile among oral ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease therapies.\n- High rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission suggest tamuzimodView drug details could serve as a foundational therapy in future combination treatments for ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nVentyx BiosciencesView company profile announced new 52-week data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nThe results, presented at the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria, highlight the sustained efficacy and favorable safety profile of the oral S1P1R (search)Search target modulator.\nThe long-term extension data demonstrated continued clinical and endoscopic remission rates, suggesting a durable treatment effect.\n...\nHe also noted the potential for tamuzimod to be a \"backbone of future combination therapies for UC\" due to its safety profile compared to other oral options.\n...\nThe Phase 2 trial evaluated the efficacy and safety of tamuzimodView drug details in patients with ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease over a 52-week period.\nKey highlights from the presented data include:\n- Sustained clinical remission rates\n- Continued endoscopic remission\n- A safety profile that positions tamuzimod as potentially best-in-disease among oral UC therapies", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences' Tamuzimod Shows Promising Long-Term ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/news/6f5a2d16ea2a572e/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-from-the-phase-2-trial-of-vtx002", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "## Key Insights\n- Ventyx BiosciencesView company profile presented 52-week data from its Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease at UEG Week 2024, showcasing sustained efficacy.\n- The data reinforce tamuzimodView drug details's potential as a best-in-class S1P1R (search)Search target modulator with a favorable safety profile among oral ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease therapies.\n- High rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission suggest tamuzimodView drug details could serve as a foundational therapy in future combination treatments for ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nVentyx BiosciencesView company profile announced new 52-week data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nThe results, presented at the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria, highlight the sustained efficacy and favorable safety profile of the oral S1P1R (search)Search target modulator.\nThe long-term extension data demonstrated continued clinical and endoscopic remission rates, suggesting a durable treatment effect.\n...\nHe also noted the potential for tamuzimod to be a \"backbone of future combination therapies for UC\" due to its safety profile compared to other oral options.\n...\nThe Phase 2 trial evaluated the efficacy and safety of tamuzimodView drug details in patients with ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease over a 52-week period.\nKey highlights from the presented data include:\n- Sustained clinical remission rates\n- Continued endoscopic remission\n- A safety profile that positions tamuzimod as potentially best-in-disease among oral UC therapies", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Tamuzimod - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/6bbbf8f2c65146ceb8edfda558d17dc9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|NCT05156125 (UEGW2024) Manual|Phase 2|Ulcerative colitis, active moderate | Ulcerative colitis, active severe|85|Placebo|mftmnfbiwk(kjipcuuyyb) = rowbxvixfn glcevholsc (dcfepzicpo ) View more|Positive|17 Oct 2024|\n|NCT05156125 (UEGW2024) Manual|Phase 2|Ulcerative colitis, active moderate | Ulcerative colitis, active severe|85|TamuzimodTamuzimod 30 mg|mftmnfbiwk(kjipcuuyyb) = snnzblsxso glcevholsc (dcfepzicpo ) View more|Positive|17 Oct 2024|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-25"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39793589/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** Tamuzimod (VTX002) is a selective sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1 modulator in development for ulcerative colitis.\nWe aimed to assess the safety and efficacy of tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.\n...\n** This double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial was conducted at 122 centres across 15 countries in Asia, Europe, and North America.\nPatients aged 18-80 years with a modified Mayo score (MMS) of 4-9 and an inadequate response or a loss of response, or intolerance to one or more approved ulcerative colitis therapies were randomly assigned (1:1:1) to once-daily oral tamuzimod (60 mg or 30 mg) or placebo for 13 weeks.\n...\nAt week 13, clinical remission was reached by 19 (28%) of 68 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 17 (24%) of 71 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and eight (11%) of 70 patients receiving placebo (risk difference 16·5% [95% CI 3·2 to 29·4], p=0·018, for tamuzimod 60 mg vs placebo and 12·5% [-0·2 to 24·9], p=0·041, for tamuzimod 30 mg vs placebo).\nTreatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo.\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.\n...\n** Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-19"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative colitis, active severe - Drugs, Targets, Patents - Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/disease/884b63886644433694ee6557e3324413", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "We aimed to assess the safety and efficacy of tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.METHODSThis double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial was conducted at 122 centres across 15 countries in Asia, Europe, and North America.\n...\nTreatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo.\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.INTERPRETATIONInduction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.FUNDINGVentyx Biosciences.\n...\nThe overall safety profile was favourable and consistent with that of guselkumab in approved indications.\nIn the induction study, adverse events were reported by 49% of patients in both groups (208 of 421 guselkumab-treated patients and 138 of 280 placebo-treated patients), serious adverse events were reported by 3% (12 of 421) of guselkumab-treated patients and 7% (20 of 280) of placebo-treated patients, and adverse events leading to treatment discontinuation were reported by 2% (seven of 421) of guselkumab-treated patients and 4% (11 of 280) of placebo-treated patients.\nIn the maintenance study, adverse event rates were similar among groups, and the most frequently reported adverse events in all groups were ulcerative colitis, COVID-19, and arthralgia.\nNo active tuberculosis, anaphylaxis, serum sickness, or clinically important hepatic disorders were reported in either study.INTERPRETATIONGuselkumab was effective and safe as induction and maintenance therapy in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.FUNDINGJanssen Research and Development.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-21"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx reports positive Phase 2 ulcerative colitis trial data By Investing.com", "url": "https://au.investing.com/news/company-news/ventyx-reports-positive-phase-2-ulcerative-colitis-trial-data-93CH-3486002", "domain": "au.investing.com", "snippet": "SAN DIEGO - Ventyx Biosciences, Inc.\n(NASDAQ:VTYX), a biopharmaceutical company specializing in inflammatory diseases, announced today the presentation of new data from its Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod for ulcerative colitis (UC).\nThe data, showcased at the United European Gastroenterology Week in Vienna, Austria, highlighted the drug's efficacy and safety over a 52-week period.\nThe oral presentation, titled \"Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data,\" was delivered by Dr. Silvio Danese from the IRCCS Ospedale San Raffaele in Milan, Italy.\nThe session took place on Tuesday morning, during which the company shared the long-term extension (LTE) data from the trial.\nAccording to Ventyx CEO Raju Mohan, PhD, the LTE data reinforces tamuzimod's potential as a leading oral therapy for UC, citing its safety profile and high rates of clinical and endoscopic remission.\n...\nHowever, it also acknowledges the inherent risks and uncertainties in the development of pharmaceutical products, such as the possibility of adverse side effects, regulatory challenges, and the need for additional research.", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-06-24"}], "raw": {"id": "30505781-96fe-4906-9580-d1fd68d96d8c"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:41+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q001", "objective": "Tamuzimod safety findings and adverse events in Moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["Tamuzimod"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q001-4", "query": "Tamuzimod open-label extension adverse events", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Tamuzimod open-label extension adverse events", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1121.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "VTX002 UEGW 2024", "url": "https://ir.ventyxbio.com/static-files/47cbb9ba-f8c5-43e5-9235-01ca63f91f25", "domain": "ir.ventyxbio.com", "snippet": "Open-label extension – Tamuzimod 60 mg up to ~ 2\nyears\nPatients with loss of response\n...\nKey eligibility criteria:\n•\nModerately to severely active UC\n•\nNo/insufficient response, loss of response, and/or intolerance to conventional or advanced therapies (≤ 2 biologics with different mechanisms of action or 1 biologic + a Janus kinase inhibitor)\n...\nLong-term extension: clinical responders (≥ 2-point and ≥ 30% decrease from baseline in MMS, and ≥ 1-point decrease from baseline   \nin RB or an absolute RB ≤ 1) at Week 13 were eligible for maintenance therapy with previously assigned treatment for up to 39 weeks\n...\nAbbreviations: LTE, long-term extension; OLE, open-label extension.\n...\nAdverse event (n=1)\n...\nSafety through Week 52 \nPlacebo \n(N=25)\nTamuzimod 30 mg \n(N=37)\nTamuzimod 60 mg \n(N=33)\nAny adverse event (AE), n (%)\n13 (52)\n25 (68)\n22 (67)\nAE related to study drug, n (%)\n2 (8)\n4 (11)\n5 (15)\nAE leading to study drug discontinuationa, n (%)\n1 (4)\n1 (3)\n1 (3)\nAny serious adverse event (SAE), n (%)\n2 (8)\n3 (8)\n0\nGastrointestinal disorders, n (%)\n2 (8)\n2 (5)\n0\nHaemorrhagic diarrhoea, n (%)\n0\n1 (3)\n0\nRectal haemorrhage, n (%)\n0\n1 (3)\n0\nAnal fistula, n (%)\n1 (4)\n0\n0\nColitis ulcerative, n (%)\n1 (4)\n0\n0\nHepatobiliary disorders, n (%)\n0\n1 (3)\n0\nCholecystitis acute, n (%)\n0\n1 (3)\n0\nInfections and infestations, n (%)\n0\n1 (3)\n0\nPeritonitis, n (%)\n0\n1 (3)\n0\nSAE related to study drug, n (%)\n0\n0\n0\nDeath, n (%)\n0\n0\n0\na Oral thrush (Grade 2, placebo, related to study drug); joint pain (Grade 2, 30 mg, related to study drug); alanine aminotransferase increased (Grade 2, 60 mg, related to study drug).\n...\n• Maintenance treatment with both 30 mg and 60 mg tamuzimod was efficacious and   \nwell-tolerated for up to 52 weeks", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-01-28"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Corporate Presentation - datocms-assets.com", "url": "https://www.datocms-assets.com/92605/1772147952-ventyx-investor-deck-june-2025-final.pdf", "domain": "datocms-assets.com", "snippet": "Preliminary open-label extension data cut reported Q1 2024 (as of February 2024)\nInduction results reported Q4 2023\nOPEN-LABEL EXTENSION – VTX002 60 MG UP TO ~2 YEARS\nPatients with \nmoderate to \nsevere UC\n(N=213)\n...\nSubgroup analysis demonstrated differentiated clinical remission and endoscopic remission in patients with prior exposure to advanced therapies\nNo cases of atrioventricular block, \nbradycardia, serious or opportunistic \ninfections, or macular edema \n...\nFurther improvement in clinical and endoscopic remission rates at OLE week 39\n...\n▪At least 38% (NRI) of patients in 60mg 52wk treat-through \narm were in clinical remission at both week 13 and week 52", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-08"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Font.pdf", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1851194/000095017025057492/vtyx_-_ars_-_2024_form_1.pdf", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "adults, tamuzimod was well tolerated with no serious adverse events.\n...\nIn our Phase 1 trial, tamuzimod was well-tolerated with no serious adverse events or notable safety findings.\n...\nTamuzimod was well tolerated in both the 30 mg and 60 mg dose cohorts, with no treatment-related serious adverse events observed.\nThere were no serious or opportunistic infections, and no instances of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, or macular edema were observed.\n...\nIn October 2024, we announced positive results from the long-term extension of the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in participants with UC.\nMaintenance treatment with tamuzimod was well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks and was associated with high rates of clinical and endoscopic remission at Week 52 (Figure 23).\nSpecifically, at week 52, 50% of patients on the tamuzimod 60 mg dose or tamuzimod\n30 mg dose achieved clinical remission compared to 11% of patients on placebo (Figure 23).\nOn the secondary endpoint of endoscopic\n...\nAn “open-label” clinical trial is one where both the patient and investigator know whether the patient is receiving the investigational product candidate or either an existing approved drug or placebo.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-05-06"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "UEG Week 2024 - CONFERENCE REPORTPEER-REVIEWED", "url": "https://conferences.medicom-publishers.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/E-UEGW-MCR-2024.pdf", "domain": "conferences.medicom-publishers.com", "snippet": "events (n=168 vs n=315) and serious adverse events (n=15 \n...\nIn the 12-week dosing group, \n26 participants experienced a so-called drug event, defined \nas a change in dosing interval or re-introduction during the follow-up period.\nOf these, 22 intensified their ustekinumab therapy (mostly switching to an 8-week regimen), 3 switched therapies, and 1 discontinued therapy due to a malignancy.\n...\nThe most common treatment-emergent adverse events in the 2 mg and 3 mg arms were worsening of CD (25% and\n21.6%, respectively), headache (10.7% and 16.2%), arthralgia\n(10.7% and 13.5%), and COVID-19 (10.7% and 24.3%).\n“Severe \ninfections were seen in 3.6% and 5.4% of the participants in the \n2 and 3 mg arms,” added Prof. D’Haens.\nFinally, 2 participants\nin the 3 mg arm had a first-degree AV block, 1 participant in the 2 mg experienced bradycardia, and 1 participant in the 3 mg arm had hypertension.\n...\nTamuzimod delivers promising long-term data \nin UC \nTamuzimod was associated with improved clinical, endoscopic, and histologic outcomes compared with placebo in participants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) at week 52 of a phase 2 study.\nMoreover, the drug had a favourable safety profile.\nThe selective S1PR1 modulator tamuzimod displayed encouraging efficacy and safety data as induction therapy for patients with UC [1].\n...\nAlso, participants on placebo who lost response during the long-term extension were allowed to switch to the open-label extension phase and received tamuzimod\n60 mg.\n...\nAt week 52, the clinical remission rates were 50% in both \ntamuzimod arms and 18.2% in the placebo arm (P=0.021; \nP=0.0078).\n...\n“The drug was \nvery safe, and there were no major adverse events,” according to Prof. Danese.\n...\n“Maintenance treatment with either 30 mg or 60 mg of\ntamuzimod was efficacious and well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks, supporting the continued development of tamuzimod as a treatment for patients with UC,” concluded\nProf. Danese.\n...\n170 weeks.\n...\n“The severe adverse event rates were low, with 10% (n=3) \nof the participants in the 100 mg arm and 3% (n=1) of the \nparticipants in the 250 mg arm,” expressed Dr Ma.\nNo acute infusion reactions or peri-infusion reactions were reported.\nThe infection rate was 37% in the 100 mg arm and 46% in the 250 mg arm.\n“These were, however, predominantly upper respiratory infections.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-28"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "tamuzimod (VTX002) / Eli Lilly", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=c9a09d69-e0fc-4b37-ab3a-5af0056bd49c", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## VTX002 Versus Placebo for the Treatment of Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=213 | Terminated | Sponsor: Oppilan Pharma Ltd | The Sponsor decided to terminate clinical conduct in the Open-Label Extension Period. The decision to terminate the study was not due to safety concerns.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety Patterns over Time with Ozanimod During an Open-label Extension Trial in Patients with Relapsing Multiple Sclerosis (P10-6.012) | Neurology", "url": "https://www.neurology.org/doi/10.1212/WNL.0000000000204931", "domain": "neurology.org", "snippet": "The proportion of patients with RMS who experienced TEAEs in the OLE trial decreased over 6 years of ozanimod 0.92 mg treatment.", "date": "2024-04-09", "last_updated": "2025-04-04"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39793589/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo.\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.\n...\n** Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-19"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "The Current Sphingosine 1 Phosphate Receptor Modulators ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12111885/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "However, its non-discriminatory effect on all S1PRs is associated with a less favourable cardiac safety profile, with bradycardia occurring in 0.6% of patients in a large real-world study [17].\n...\nIn True North, adverse events occurred in 37–49% patients in the ozanimod and placebo groups [25].\nThere was a higher rate of adverse events in the ELEVATE studies, but the rates were comparable between the etrasimod and placebo groups (71% vs. 81%, respectively) [40].\n...\nS1PR1 is expressed by cardiac tissue, and following receptor activation, an intracellular shift in potassium occurs leading to reduced excitability and transient bradycardia.\nThis occurs in a dose-dependent manner [38].\nThese effects can be mitigated with gradual dose escalation for ozanimod, but this is not required for etrasimod [25,40,44].\nBradycardia: Symptomatic bradycardia occurred in five (0.6%) patients in the treatment arm of the True North study [25].\nOf these, only one was symptomatic and resolved following drug discontinuation without the need for additional intervention.\nNo patients in the True North study developed a second or third-degree atrioventricular (AV) block.\nAcross the ELEVATE UC trials, nine occurrences of bradycardia (<1%) were observed in the treatment arms [40]; two of these were symptomatic and led to study discontinuation.\nTwo cases of first-degree AV block and one case of Mobitz type 1 AV block occurred.\nAll three cases of AV block resolved following treatment discontinuation without the need for additional intervention.\nOverall, the risk of cardiac conduction disorders with the use of S1PR modulators is low in appropriately selected patients.\n...\nS1PR modulators can disrupt this delicate balance and cause macular oedema.\nIn the trials, macular oedema occurred in three patients receiving ozanimod and two patients receiving etrasimod [25,40].\nAll bar one discontinued treatment, leading to resolution of the macular oedema (one patient receiving etrasimod continued without treatment interruption).\n...\nHerpes Zoster: In True North, the rate of herpes zoster infections was 0.4% and 2.2% in the induction and maintenance phases, respectively, with no events occurring in the placebo group [25].\nFour patients in the ELEVATE studies had herpes zoster infections (two receiving etrasimod, two receiving placebo) [40].\nNone of these adverse events led to discontinuation of the study drug.\nLymphopenia: In keeping with their mechanism of action, a reduction in the absolute lymphocyte count occurred, with a 50% reduction from baseline values by Week 2 and Week 10 in patients receiving etrasimod and ozanimod, respectively [25,40].\nThe resulting lymphopenia is reversible, with lymphocyte counts returning to baseline within 2–8 weeks of treatment cessation.\n...\nThere has only been one reported case of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) in a patient with MS who was treated with ozanimod [49].\nNo cases of PML have been reported in the UC population to date.\n...\nOne case of basal cell carcinoma was diagnosed during the induction phase of True North in a patient receiving ozanimod [25].\nDuring the maintenance period, cancer was diagnosed in four (<0.9%) patients and included two cases of basal cell carcinoma, two cases of colorectal cancer and one of breast cancer.\n...\nTamuzimod was investigated in a phase 2, multi-centre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study for patients with moderate to severely active UC [53].\nIt met its primary endpoint of clinical remission with superiority over placebo (27.9% vs. 11.4%, *p* = 0.0184).\nThere were no new safety signals identified.\nLong-term and open-label extension phases of the study are ongoing.", "date": "2025-05-15", "last_updated": "2026-02-15"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative colitis, active severe - Drugs, Targets, Patents - Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/disease/884b63886644433694ee6557e3324413", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo.\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.INTERPRETATIONInduction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.FUNDINGVentyx Biosciences.\n...\nIn the induction study, adverse events were reported by 49% of patients in both groups (208 of 421 guselkumab-treated patients and 138 of 280 placebo-treated patients), serious adverse events were reported by 3% (12 of 421) of guselkumab-treated patients and 7% (20 of 280) of placebo-treated patients, and adverse events leading to treatment discontinuation were reported by 2% (seven of 421) of guselkumab-treated patients and 4% (11 of 280) of placebo-treated patients.\nIn the maintenance study, adverse event rates were similar among groups, and the most frequently reported adverse events in all groups were ulcerative colitis, COVID-19, and arthralgia.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-21"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] The Current Sphingosine 1 Phosphate Receptor Modulators in the ...", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/2bc3/73995f756a365a78f1d2678cdc97ddda6021.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "safety profile, with bradycardia occurring in 0.6% of patients in a large real-world study \n[17].\nOff-target effects on S1PR receptor subtypes also led to a greater incidence of other \nadverse events such as macular oedema (2%) and neoplasms (2%) [18,19].\n...\nWhilst the recent analysis of True North and its open-label extension (OLE) has positioned ozanimod as an effective and well-tolerated treatment for patients naïve to advanced therapies (AT), there remains a paucity of data on AT-exposed patients [27].\n...\nNo new safety signals were identified [32,33], but the risk of rarer adverse events with longer-term use remains to be elucidated.\n...\nS1PR modulators have shown a favourable safety profile in their\nphase 2 and 3 trials [38].\nThe adverse effects of S1PR modulators relate to their interactions with S1PR1 and S1PR5 in various tissues [38].\nIn True North, adverse events occurred in 37–49% patients in the ozanimod and placebo groups [25].\nThere was a higher rate of adverse events in the ELEVATE studies, but the rates were comparable between the etrasimod and placebo groups (71% vs. 81%, respectively) [40].\n...\nS1PR1 is expressed by cardiac tissue, and following receptor activation, an intracellular shift in potassium occurs leading to reduced excitability and transient bradycardia.\nThis occurs in a dose-dependent manner [38].\nThese effects can be mitigated with gradual dose escalation for ozanimod, but this is not required for etrasimod [25,40,44].\nBradycardia: Symptomatic bradycardia occurred in five (0.6%) patients in the treat- ment arm of the True North study [25].\nOf these, only one was symptomatic and resolved following drug discontinuation without the need for additional intervention.\nNo patients in the True North study developed a second or third-degree atrioventricular (AV) block.\nAcross the ELEVATE UC trials, nine occurrences of bradycardia (<1%) were observed in\n...\nS1PR modulators can disrupt this delicate balance and cause macular oedema.\nIn the trials, macular oedema occurred in three patients receiving ozanimod and two patients receiving etrasimod [25,40].\nAll bar one discontinued treatment,\n...\nHerpes Zoster: In True North, the rate of herpes zoster infections was 0.4% and 2.2%\n...\nTamuzimod was investigated in a phase 2, multi-centre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study for patients with moderate to severely active UC [53].\nIt met its primary endpoint of clinical remission with superiority over placebo (27.9% vs. 11.4%,\np = 0.0184).\nThere were no new safety signals identified.\nLong-term and open-label exten-", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-09"}], "raw": {"id": "d1020dd3-6f2f-4063-ad51-83f7b2fe8f00"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:42+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q001", "objective": "Tamuzimod safety findings and adverse events in Moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["Tamuzimod"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q001-2", "query": "Tamuzimod maintenance adverse events ulcerative colitis", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Tamuzimod maintenance adverse events ulcerative colitis", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2363.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Silvio Danese - ScienceDirect.com", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/author/7004453034/silvio-danese", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "Treatment-emergent adverse events were recorded at 238·5 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 233·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg.\nEvent rates of serious treatment-emergent adverse events were 11·7 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 12·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg.\nThe most common adverse events of special interest were hepatic disorder, lymphopenia, creatine phosphokinase elevation, serious infection, neutropenia, and herpes zoster.\nThree treatment-emergent adverse events leading to death were reported in the long-term extension-only safety population.This interim analysis supports the positive long-term risk–benefit profile for upadacitinib 15 mg and 30 mg among patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.AbbVie.\n...\nTreatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo.\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk−benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.Ventyx Biosciences.\n...\nIn the induction studies, the most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs; ≥5% of patients in any group) were abdominal pain; arthralgia; an exacerbation, flare, or worsening of Crohn's disease; headache; nasopharyngitis; nausea; and pyrexia.\nIn the maintenance study, the most frequently reported TEAEs (≥5% of patients in any filgotinib or associated placebo group) were those reported in the induction studies (except for headache) and abdominal distension, upper abdominal pain, anaemia, and flatulence.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-24"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Sphingosine-1-Phosphate (S1P) Receptor Modulators for the ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12650479/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "They also appear to have a favourable safety profile, although awareness of potential cardiovascular and ophthalmological side-effects is crucial for safe use.\n...\nBradycardia occurred more often with ozanimod in the induction cohort; however, there was no difference from placebo during the maintenance study [23].\n...\nTamuzimod is a selective S1PRM that acts on S1PR1 and has completed phase two trials in UC.\nThese trials demonstrated that tamuzimod at both 30 mg (*p* = 0.041) and 60 mg (*p* = 0.018) was associated with significantly higher rates of remission at 13 weeks compared to placebo [3].\nThere were no cardiovascular-related AEs reported and no cases of macular oedema.\nPhase 3 trials are awaited.", "date": "2025-10-29", "last_updated": "2026-08-21"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "UEG Week 2024 - CONFERENCE REPORTPEER-REVIEWED", "url": "https://conferences.medicom-publishers.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/E-UEGW-MCR-2024.pdf", "domain": "conferences.medicom-publishers.com", "snippet": "mirikizumab 300 mg, intravenous, every 4 weeks up to week \n...\nAfter 28 weeks of maintenance therapy, \n89.6% (as observed) of baseline corticosteroid users had \ndiscontinued this therapy.\n...\nIn the 12-week dosing group, \n26 participants experienced a so-called drug event, defined \nas a change in dosing interval or re-introduction during the follow-up period.\nOf these, 22 intensified their ustekinumab therapy (mostly switching to an 8-week regimen), 3 switched therapies, and 1 discontinued therapy due to a malignancy.\n...\nTamuzimod delivers promising long-term data \nin UC \nTamuzimod was associated with improved clinical, endoscopic, and histologic outcomes compared with placebo in participants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) at week 52 of a phase 2 study.\nMoreover, the drug had a favourable safety profile.\nThe selective S1PR1 modulator tamuzimod displayed encouraging efficacy and safety data as induction therapy for patients with UC [1].\n...\nAt week 52, the clinical remission rates were 50% in both \ntamuzimod arms and 18.2% in the placebo arm (P=0.021; \nP=0.0078).\nEndoscopic remission was documented in 46.4%\n...\n“The drug was \nvery safe, and there were no major adverse events,” according to Prof. Danese.\n“This is probably due to the high selectivity of tamuzimod,” he argued.\n“Maintenance treatment with either 30 mg or 60 mg of\ntamuzimod was efficacious and well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks, supporting the continued development of tamuzimod as a treatment for patients with UC,” concluded\nProf. Danese.\n1. Sands BE, et al.\n...\n2. Danese S, et al.\n...\n“The severe adverse event rates were low, with 10% (n=3) \nof the participants in the 100 mg arm and 3% (n=1) of the \nparticipants in the 250 mg arm,” expressed Dr Ma.\nNo acute infusion reactions or peri-infusion reactions were reported.\nThe infection rate was 37% in the 100 mg arm and 46% in the 250 mg arm.\n“These were, however, predominantly upper respiratory infections.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-28"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Mirikizumab as Induction and Maintenance Therapy for Ulcerative Colitis | NEJM", "url": "https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2207940", "domain": "nejm.org", "snippet": "Opportunistic infection (herpes zoster) occurred in 1 patient who received placebo in the induction trial.During the 52-week treatment period, among the 1217 patients who received mirikizumab, cancer was reported in 8.\nAdenocarcinoma of the colon was reported in 2 patients during the induction trial, and nonmelanoma skin cancer and gastric cancer in 1 patient each during the double-blind portion of the maintenance trial (Table 2).\n...\nThere were two deaths during the follow-up period for the induction trial: one from sudden cardiac arrest, and one from disseminated intravascular coagulation.\n...\nThere were no instances of a major adverse cardiovascular event (MACE) during the induction trial.\nOne instance of MACE (ischemic stroke) occurred in the placebo group during the maintenance trial.\nIn the mirikizumab group during the induction trial, both cancers were colon adenocarcinoma.\nDuring the maintenance trial, nonmelanoma skin cancer (basal-cell carcinoma) occurred in one patient in the placebo group and gastric cancer in one patient in the mirikizumab group.\n...\nOne patient in the mirikizumab group during the maintenance trial had a suicide attempt.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "AGA LIVING GUIDELINES: PHARMACOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12162142/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Large observational studies have suggested no difference in the risk of adverse clinical outcomes between patients who continue vs. stop 5-ASA after starting advanced therapies or immunomodulators^99–101^.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Sphingosine 1-Phosphate (S1P) Receptor Modulators as ... - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41968221/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** Eight trials involving 2663 participants were included.\nAnalysis comparing S1PR modulators with placebo included etrasimod, ozanimod, tamuzimod, and KRP203.\n...\nAdverse events were slightly increased with S1PR modulators (RR 1.13; p = 0.002), while serious adverse events (p = 0.73) and discontinuations (p = 0.67) did not differ significantly from placebo.\n...\n** S1PR modulators demonstrate robust efficacy during the induction phase across all key clinical and tissue-based outcomes, with more modest or variable effects during maintenance.\nTheir safety profile remains acceptable, supporting their role as a promising therapeutic option for UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Endpoint Outcomes", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ibdjournal/article/30/12/2245/7624797", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Serious adverse events were reported in 5.2% of patients; 2.8% discontinued treatment due to adverse events.\n...\nSevere treatment-emergent adverse events were reported in 4.5% of patients; 5.2% experienced serious adverse events, with 2.8% discontinuing treatment due to an adverse event.\nThe most common treatment-emergent adverse events were pyrexia, diarrhea, injection site pain, abdominal pain, and gastroenteritis.\nThere were no deaths through week 104.\n...\nCOVID-19 (12.1%) was observed as a most common adverse event (≥3%), which is not surprising, as this study was held during the COVID-19 epidemic.\n...\nNo new safety signals were identified, and the discontinuation rate due to adverse events was 2.8%.", "date": "2024-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-26"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Practice Guideline on Pharmacological Management of ...", "url": "https://www.mdcalc.com/guidelines/10594/aga/Pharmacological-Management-of-Ulcerative-Colitis", "domain": "mdcalc.com", "snippet": "In patients with UC who are in corticosteroid-free clinical remission for at least 6 months on combination therapy of TNF antagonists and an immunomodulator, the AGA makes no recommendation in favor of withdrawing immunomodulators or continuing combination therapy.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-08"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancements in therapeutic strategies and drug ...", "url": "https://www.explorationpub.com/Journals/ei/Article/1003232", "domain": "explorationpub.com", "snippet": "The overall risk of infection remains low, with non-serious upper respiratory symptoms being the most common [199].\n...\nImportantly, serious adverse event rates were similar across treatment and placebo groups [241].\n...\nTamuzimod (VTX002), an investigational oral S1PR1 modulator, has also shown promising clinical results for UC.\nIn a Phase 2 induction trial, 28% of patients receiving 60 mg of tamuzimod and 24% of those receiving 30 mg achieved clinical remission by Week 13, compared to just 11% on placebo (*P* = 0.018 and *P* = 0.041, respectively) [252].\nTamuzimod induction therapy was also well-tolerated.\nThe most common treatment-emergent adverse events were upper respiratory tract infection (9% for 60 mg, 1% for 30 mg vs. 1% for placebo), anemia (3% for 60 mg, 5% for 30 mg vs. 9% for placebo), and headache (6% for 60 mg, 7% for 30 mg vs. 3% for placebo).\nNotably, there were no reported adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular edema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths [252].\nThese positive results and the favorable risk-benefit profile collectively support the ongoing clinical development of tamuzimod for treating moderately-to-severely active UC.", "date": "2025-12-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Articles Tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis: a multicentre, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2468125324003868", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "Treatment-emergent adverse events were reported 3347%) 70 tamuzod  mg,34 (47%) of 73 tamuzod  mg, 24 (34%) of 70 on placebo, with most events being mild or moderate.\nThe most commonly treatment-ement adverse (in5% of any treatment group) included respiratory tract (six9% the tamimod60 mg, one [1%] the tamimod30 mg, and one [1%] in the placebo group), an (three3% four [], and [9]), andfour [%], [7], and two [3%]).\nThere were no recorded cases atrioricular block bradardia, macular ed, severe opportunistic, malign, or fatalities.\n...\nAlthough S1PR modulators showed a higher incidence of any adverse event compared to placebo, serious adverse events did not differ significantly between the groups.\n...\nTreatment with both doses of tamuzimod was well tolerated, and a dose titration over a week helped reduce-dose effects, as brycardia and conduction issues, commonly associated with this class of drugs.\nImportantly, no adverse events related to atrioventricular block, macular ed, severe opportunistic, malign, or fatalities were reported in this trial.\n...\nTamuzimod was found to be effective and well tolerated in this short-term randomized controlled trial involving patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.", "date": "2025-03-05", "last_updated": "2025-11-05"}], "raw": {"id": "aa6eb8f5-5561-4cb7-a5ca-0825702a6c33"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:43+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q001", "objective": "Tamuzimod safety findings and adverse events in Moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["Tamuzimod"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q001-1", "query": "Tamuzimod long-term extension safety ulcerative colitis", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Tamuzimod long-term extension safety ulcerative colitis", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 3021.5, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ...", "url": "https://ventyxbio.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/VTX002-Phase-2-LTE-Presentation-UEG-Week-2024.pdf", "domain": "ventyxbio.com", "snippet": "Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data\n...\ntreatment of UC\n• The efficacy and safety of tamuzimod as induction therapy was previously demonstrated in a phase 2, \nmulticentre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial (NCT05156125)1\n...\nKey eligibility criteria:\n•\nModerately to severely active UC\n•\nNo/insufficient response, loss of response, and/or intolerance to conventional or advanced therapies (≤ 2 biologics with different mechanisms of action or 1 biologic + a Janus kinase inhibitor)\nPrimary endpoint: clinical remission (MMS stool frequency (SF) subscore ≤ 1, rectal bleeding (RB) = 0, endoscopic subscore (ES) ≤ 1)   \nat Week 13 in patients with baseline MMS 5-9\nLong-term extension: clinical responders (≥ 2-point and ≥ 30% decrease from baseline in MMS, and ≥ 1-point decrease from baseline   \nin RB or an absolute RB ≤ 1) at Week 13 were eligible for maintenance therapy with previously assigned treatment for up to 39 weeks\n...\n• Maintenance treatment with both 30 mg and 60 mg tamuzimod was efficacious and   \nwell-tolerated for up to 52 weeks\n• High rates of both clinical and endoscopic remission observed during tamuzimod \ninduction and maintenance therapy potentially a result of rapid and sustained absolute lymphocyte count (ALC) reductions\n• 58.9% (tamuzimod 30 mg) and 71.5% (tamuzimod 60 mg) ALC decrease from baseline at Week 52\n• Efficacy and safety data from this LTE treatment period support the continued clinical\ndevelopment in UC and the use of tamuzimod 60 mg in phase 3", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-28"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Silvio Danese - ScienceDirect.com", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/author/7004453034/silvio-danese", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "In the long-term extension-only safety analysis, we assessed data from 467 patients, representing 1027·9 patient-years of continuous long-term extension exposure on a consistent upadacitinib dose.\nTreatment-emergent adverse events were recorded at 238·5 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 233·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg.\nEvent rates of serious treatment-emergent adverse events were 11·7 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 12·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg.\nThe most common adverse events of special interest were hepatic disorder, lymphopenia, creatine phosphokinase elevation, serious infection, neutropenia, and herpes zoster.\nThree treatment-emergent adverse events leading to death were reported in the long-term extension-only safety population.This interim analysis supports the positive long-term risk–benefit profile for upadacitinib 15 mg and 30 mg among patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.AbbVie.\n...\nWe aimed to assess the safety and efficacy of tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.This double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial was conducted at 122 centres across 15 countries in Asia, Europe, and North America.\n...\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk−benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.Ventyx Biosciences.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-24"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase ...", "url": "https://www.nasdaq.com/press-release/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-phase-2-trial-vtx002-tamuzimod", "domain": "nasdaq.com", "snippet": "### Late-breaking abstract highlights high rates of clinical and endoscopic remission among LTE completers at Week 52\nSAN DIEGO, Oct. 15, 2024 (GLOBE NEWSWIRE) -- Ventyx Biosciences, Inc.\n(Nasdaq: VTYX) (“Ventyx”), a clinical-stage biopharmaceutical company focused on advancing novel oral therapies that address a broad range of inflammatory diseases with significant unmet medical need, today announced that a late-breaking abstract including new long-term extension (LTE) data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis was presented on October 15, 2024 during the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria.\n“We are excited to present the new long-term extension data from the tamuzimod Phase 2 trial in patients with ulcerative colitis at UEG Week,” said Raju Mohan, PhD, Chief Executive Officer.\n“These 52-week data continue to reinforce the potential best-in-class profile of our S1P1R modulator tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis, with a potential best-in-disease safety profile amongst all the oral options for UC therapy.\nWe believe the high rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission position tamuzimod as the backbone of future combination therapies for UC.”\n...\n**Title: **“Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data”", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-03-28"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "UEG Week 2024 - CONFERENCE REPORTPEER-REVIEWED", "url": "https://conferences.medicom-publishers.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/E-UEGW-MCR-2024.pdf", "domain": "conferences.medicom-publishers.com", "snippet": "A subgroup analysis of the phase 3 LUCENT-2 study demonstrated that the corticosteroid-sparing effect of mirikizumab was clinically meaningful among\nparticipants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC).\n...\nbowel \n...\nTamuzimod delivers promising long-term data \nin UC \nTamuzimod was associated with improved clinical, endoscopic, and histologic outcomes compared with placebo in participants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) at week 52 of a phase 2 study.\nMoreover, the drug had a favourable safety profile.\nThe selective S1PR1 modulator tamuzimod displayed encouraging efficacy and safety data as induction therapy for patients with UC [1].\n...\nAt week 52, the clinical remission rates were 50% in both \ntamuzimod arms and 18.2% in the placebo arm (P=0.021; \nP=0.0078).\n...\n“The drug was \nvery safe, and there were no major adverse events,” according to Prof. Danese.\n“This is probably due to the high selectivity of tamuzimod,” he argued.\n“Maintenance treatment with either 30 mg or 60 mg of\ntamuzimod was efficacious and well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks, supporting the continued development of tamuzimod as a treatment for patients with UC,” concluded\nProf. Danese.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-28"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "The Current Sphingosine 1 Phosphate Receptor Modulators ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12111885/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "No new safety signals were identified [32,33], but the risk of rarer adverse events with longer-term use remains to be elucidated.\n...\nThe study is still ongoing, and an interim analysis demonstrated clinical response and remission rates comparable to the True North studies, with no new safety signals identified.\n...\nOverall, the risk of cardiac conduction disorders with the use of S1PR modulators is low in appropriately selected patients.\n...\nTamuzimod was investigated in a phase 2, multi-centre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study for patients with moderate to severely active UC [53].\nIt met its primary endpoint of clinical remission with superiority over placebo (27.9% vs. 11.4%, *p* = 0.0184).\nThere were no new safety signals identified.\nLong-term and open-label extension phases of the study are ongoing.\nLong term extensions and phase 4 studies of ozanimod and etrasimod will provide additional data regarding long term efficacy and safety in ulcerative colitis.", "date": "2025-05-15", "last_updated": "2026-02-15"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "tamuzimod (VTX002) / Eli Lilly", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=c9a09d69-e0fc-4b37-ab3a-5af0056bd49c", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## VTX002 Versus Placebo for the Treatment of Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=213 | Terminated | Sponsor: Oppilan Pharma Ltd | The Sponsor decided to terminate clinical conduct in the Open-Label Extension Period. The decision to terminate the study was not due to safety concerns.\n...\n## Sphingosine 1-Phosphate (S1P) Receptor Modulators as an Induction and Maintenance Therapy for Moderate to Severe Ulcerative Colitis: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials. (PubMed, Dig Dis Sci) - \"S1PR modulators demonstrate robust efficacy during the induction phase across all key clinical and tissue-based outcomes, with more modest or variable effects during maintenance. Their safety profile remains acceptable, supporting their role as a promising therapeutic option for UC.\"\n...\n## Tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis: a multicentre, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial. (PubMed, Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol) - P2 | \"Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis. These results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.\"\n...\n## Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase 2 Trial of VTX002 (Tamuzimod) in Ulcerative Colitis at UEG Week 2024 (GlobeNewswire) - \"Ventyx Biosciences, Inc...announced that a late-breaking abstract including new long-term extension (LTE) data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis was presented on October 15, 2024 during the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "VTX002 Versus Placebo for the Treatment of Moderately to Severely ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/clinical-trial/b4d8c6e9b19a166d/nct05156125-clinical-efficacy-safety-vtx002-ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "# A Phase 2, Multicenter, Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled, Parallel-Group Study to Evaluate the Clinical Efficacy and Safety of VTX002 in Subjects With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\n...\nThis is a study to understand if taking VTX002 daily as a tablet orally is safe and effective in participants diagnosed with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis (UC).\nApproximately 189 participants will take VTX002 Dose A, VTX002 Dose B, or matching placebo, once daily.\nThe study consists of a 28-day Screening Period (to see if a participant qualifies for the study), a 13-week double-blind period (a participant receives either active Dose A, Dose B or Placebo), a Long-Term Extension (LTE) Treatment Period of up to 39 weeks, an Open-Label Extension (OLE) Treatment Period of up to 143 weeks, and a 2-week Follow-Up Period.\nThe maximal duration of treatment including the Induction Period, LTE and OLE will be 36 months.\n...\nThis is a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of VTX002 in subjects with moderately to severely active UC following daily oral administration of VTX002 as a tablet.\n...\nThe study consists of a 28-day Screening Period, a 13-week double-blind Induction Treatment Period (including 7 days of titration followed by 12 weeks of treatment at the assigned dose), a Long-Term Extension (LTE) Treatment Period of up to 39 weeks, an Open-Label Extension (OLE) Treatment Period of up to 143 weeks, and a 2-week Follow-Up Period.\nThe maximal duration of treatment including the Induction Period, LTE and OLE will be 36 months.\n...\n### Safety\n**Safety**\n|Group|Serious adverse events|Deaths|\n|--|--|--|\n|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Induction Period)|3 of 70|0 of 70|\n|VTX002 30 mg Once Daily (Induction Period)|2 of 73|0 of 73|\n|Placebo Once Daily (Induction Period)|0 of 70|0 of 70|\n|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|0 of 33|0 of 33|\n|VTX002 30 mg Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|2 of 37|0 of 37|\n|Placebo Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|2 of 25|0 of 25|\n|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Open-Label Extension Period)|8 of 190|0 of 190|\nMost frequent serious adverse events (participants)\n...\n|Event|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Induction Period)|VTX002 30 mg Once Daily (Induction Period)|Placebo Once Daily (Induction Period)|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|VTX002 30 mg Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|Placebo Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Open-Label Extension Period)|\n...\n|Colitis ulcerative|1 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|1 of 25|3 of 190|\n|Anaemia|1 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Cholecystitis acute|0 of 70|1 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Pulmonary oedema|1 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Rectal haemorrhage|0 of 70|1 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Anal fistula|0 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|1 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Diarrhoea haemorrhagic|0 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|1 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Peritonitis|0 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|1 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Gastroduodenal haemorrhage|0 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|1 of 190|\n|Angina unstable|0 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|1 of 190|", "date": "2024-11-13", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences' Tamuzimod Shows Promising Long-Term ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/news/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-from-the-phase-2-trial-of-vtx002", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "## Key Insights\n- Ventyx BiosciencesView company profile presented 52-week data from its Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease at UEG Week 2024, showcasing sustained efficacy.\n- The data reinforce tamuzimodView drug details's potential as a best-in-class S1P1R (search)Search target modulator with a favorable safety profile among oral ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease therapies.\n- High rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission suggest tamuzimodView drug details could serve as a foundational therapy in future combination treatments for ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nVentyx BiosciencesView company profile announced new 52-week data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nThe results, presented at the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria, highlight the sustained efficacy and favorable safety profile of the oral S1P1R (search)Search target modulator.\nThe long-term extension data demonstrated continued clinical and endoscopic remission rates, suggesting a durable treatment effect.\n...\nHe also noted the potential for tamuzimod to be a \"backbone of future combination therapies for UC\" due to its safety profile compared to other oral options.\n...\nThe Phase 2 trial evaluated the efficacy and safety of tamuzimodView drug details in patients with ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease over a 52-week period.\nKey highlights from the presented data include:\n- Sustained clinical remission rates\n- Continued endoscopic remission\n- A safety profile that positions tamuzimod as potentially best-in-disease among oral UC therapies", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase 2 Trial of VTX002 (Tamuzimod) in Ulcerative Colitis at UEG Week 2024", "url": "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/ventyx-biosciences-presents-52-week-120000621.html", "domain": "finance.yahoo.com", "snippet": "**New Abstract Reveals High Rates of Clinical and Endoscopic Remission Among LTE Participants at Week 52**\n...\n\"We are thrilled to share the new long-term extension findings from the tamuzimod Phase 2 study involving ulcerative colitis patients at UEG Week,\" remarkedu Moh, PhD Chief Executive.\n\"The data from this 52-week period further underscore the promising best-in-class characteristics of our S1P1R modulator, tamuzimod, in treating ulcerative colitis, potentially offering the best safety profile among available oral therapies for this condition.", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Study to Evaluate Safety and Efficacy of ...", "url": "https://www.dovepress.com/a-randomized-controlled-pilot-study-evaluating-the-safety-and-efficacy-peer-reviewed-fulltext-article-DDDT", "domain": "dovepress.com", "snippet": "Despite nifuroxazide’s traditional approval for short-term use in treating acute diarrhea, new evidence of its immunomodulatory effects led to an extension of its administration in this trial to six months.\n...\n1. Lack of Long-Term Follow-Up: The study was conducted over a 6-month period, which limits our ability to assess the long-term efficacy of nifuroxazide in sustaining remission or preventing disease relapse.\n...\nWhile no significant safety concerns were observed, the lack of prior published clinical studies using nifuroxazide over such a prolonged period necessitates cautious interpretation of the results.\n...\nIn conclusion, the study suggests that the combination of mesalamine and nifuroxazide enhanced the treatment outcomes for patients with UC, compared to mesalamine alone.\n...\nWhile side effect profiles were comparable between the two treatments, the combination therapy appeared to be well-tolerated.", "date": "2025-06-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}], "raw": {"id": "abfc42e2-879f-4008-a4c6-699bcaa6e154"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:43+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q002", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q002-3", "query": "AZD5148 RePreve comparator anti-toxin antibody", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "AZD5148 RePreve comparator anti-toxin antibody", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1760.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12594360/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a humanized anti-TcdB mAb that neutralizes toxin activity by blocking the delivery of the enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into host cells.\n...\nAZD5148 potently neutralized TcdB1 and TcdB2 *in vitro*, with EC~50~ values 1,000- to 14,000-fold lower than those of bezlotoxumab.\nIn a mouse CDI model induced by TcdB1- or TcdB2-expressing strains, AZD5148 provided robust protection against weight loss and mortality at significantly lower doses than bezlotoxumab.\nFurthermore, the addition of the anti-TcdA mAb, PA50, provided no additional protective benefit.\n...\nBezlotoxumab (Zinplava), an FDA-approved mAb targeting TcdB, was effective in reducing recurrence but has recently been discontinued.\nWe previously identified AZD5148, a humanized mAb that blocks the delivery of the toxic TcdB glucosyltransferase enzyme into host cells by targeting a conserved region of the enzymatic domain.\n...\nWe previously described PA41 (now called AZD5148), a humanized mAb that binds a highly conserved TcdB GTD epitope and prevents the delivery of the GTD enzymatic cargo into the host cytoplasm [25,26].\n...\nPrevious studies using strain culture supernatants indicated that AZD5148 broadly neutralizes TcdB, demonstrating superior efficacy compared to bezlotoxumab, except against toxins from RT017 strains [25].\n...\nIn toxin neutralization assays conducted in Vero cells, which are highly sensitive to TcdB [27], AZD5148 neutralized TcdB1 and TcdB2 with EC~50~ values of 88 pM and 59 pM, respectively (Fig 1A), representing 965-fold and 14,237-fold greater potency than bezlotoxumab (Fig 1B).\nHowever, AZD5148 did not neutralize TcdB3, while bezlotoxumab retained activity with an EC~50~ of 65 pM (Fig 1C).\n...\nThese results suggest that the prophylactic administration of AZD5148 alone is effective in preventing severe CDI in mice.\n...\nUnlike bezlotoxumab, which binds the TcdB CROPS domain and prevents binding to the CSPG4 receptor [24,27], AZD5148 targets the TcdB GTD, blocking its translocation into host cells [25,26].\n...\nIn conclusion, our data show that AZD5148 provides robust protection in a mouse model of CDI caused by diverse *C. difficile* strains.\nBy blocking the delivery of toxic cargo into the cell, AZD5148 efficacy is independent of the receptor tropism differences observed across TcdB subtypes.", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2025-11-12"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/232/5/e741/8234296", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "*Clostridioides difficile* toxin B is a virulence factor for *C. difficile* infections and a clinically validated target for prevention of *C. difficile* infection recurrence.\nAZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nHerein, we showed that the AZD5148 epitope is highly conserved across all geographic regions and sequence types and has a higher average conservation frequency in its binding site (99.58%) when compared with bezlotoxumab (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes.\nOur comprehensive analysis of global sequences found the AZD5148 epitope to be highly conserved and thus unlikely to be affected by most genotypic variations in recently circulating *C. difficile* ribotypes and sequence types.\n...\nWe recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16].\n...\nAZD5148 targets the GTD region of TcdB (Figure 1*A*).", "date": "2025-11-14", "last_updated": "2026-03-24"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "P-1055. Anti-Toxin B Neutralizing Monoclonal Antibody ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ofid/article/12/Supplement_1/ofae631.1244/7988549", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Because toxin B is a clinically validated target for CDI, we have generated an anti-toxin B neutralizing monoclonal antibody (mAb) AZD5148.\nWe previously demonstrated that AZD5148 administered at 10, 1 and 0.5 mg/kg prevents severe disease development in a gnotobiotic piglet model, which recapitulates the clinical signs and gross pathology observed in human CDI, we compared the protective efficacy of AZD5148 with the clinically approved anti-toxin B mAb bezlotoxumab.\n...\nPiglets treated with c-IgG or bezlotoxumab exhibited signs of severe disease as measured by diarrhea color and texture.\n...\nNo significant difference was observed between the c-IgG and bezlotoxumab groups.\nIn contrast, AZD5148 reduced signs of disease severity as measured by diarrhea scores, gross pathology scoring of descending and spiral colons and damage to the gut epithelium along with inflammation.\nThese data suggest that AZD5148 may offer some therapeutic benefit in preventing CDI in humans.", "date": "2025-01-29", "last_updated": "2025-10-25"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=4a84a7e5-36eb-4570-816b-0a66845a7ec3", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\nTcdB binds at least three classes of receptors including CSPG4, Nectin-3, and PVRL3 [35,36,37].\n...\nTherefore, it is possible that TcdB could escape neutralization by Zinplava^®^ [14].\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-01"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection ... - PLOS", "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plospathogens/article/figures?id=10.1371/journal.ppat.1013651", "domain": "journals.plos.org", "snippet": "AZD5148 potently neutralizes TcdB1 and TcdB2 but not TcdB3 in vitro.\n(A-B) In vitro neutralization of 30 fM TcdB1, TcdB2, or TcdB3 by AZD5148 (A) or bezlotoxumab (B) in Vero cells.\n...\nAZD5148 protects against severe disease caused by the RT027 strain R20291.\n...\nAZD5148 provides superior protection compared to bezlotoxumab at equivalent low doses during R20291 infection.\n...\nAZD5148 protects against mortality during infection with the TcdB1-producing strain VPI 10463.\n...\nAZD5148 attenuates submucosal edema and inflammation during infection with the TcdB3-producing RT017 strain M68.", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2026-05-19"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "683. Understanding Clostridioides difficile toxinB gene conservation through surveillance of public data", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ofid/article/10/Supplement_2/ofad500.745/7446541?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "CDI is mediated by the action of toxin B (TcdB) in humans.\nMonoclonal antibodies (mAbs) that neutralize TcdB function have shown protection against CDI in preclinical models and more recently in the clinic.\nSeveral regions on the TcdB gene have been targeted by therapeutic mAbs including the glucosyltransferase (GTD) and the C-terminal combined repetitive oligopeptides (CROPS) domains.\nWe have developed AZD5148, an anti-TcdB mAb targeting a region within the GTD.\nTo understand its strain coverage, we examined sequence variations of GTD including the AZD5148 epitope in contemporary *C. difficile* strains utilizing a public data repository.\n...\nWe found GTD to be highly conserved in US isolates as well as globally.\nAdditionally, among contact residues for AZD5148, any variations observed were single, and were seen for positions 323 (Y-H), 329 (G-E), 349 (V-A), and 350 (L-I).\nThese findings have important implications for drug development including for AZD5148.\nSpecifically, our findings reveal that the GTD domain of the TcdB gene has lower variance globally over the last 7 years making it a promising target for a\n*C. difficile* TcdB neutralizing mAb.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "AstraZeneca advances science of infectious disease ...", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/media-centre/press-releases/2024/astrazeneca-advances-science-of-infectious-disease-protection-at-idweek-2024.html", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "Pre-clinical data will be shared showing that AZD5148, an anti-toxin B neutralizing monoclonal antibody now in Phase I trials, may provide protection against *Clostridioides difficile* *(C. diff) * infection, a condition that can cause life-threatening diarrhea and intestinal inflammation.^9^\n...\n|Anti-toxin B neutralizing monoclonal antibody AZD5148 provides protection in a *clostridioides difficile* gnotobiotic piglet model|Poster PresentationSession Title: New Drug Development Date: Friday, October 18, 2024 Time: 12:15 PM - 1:30 PM PT Location: Halls J & K|", "date": "2024-10-16", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "H1 and Q2 2026 Results Clinical Trials Appendix", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/h1q2/H1-and-Q2-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD5148\nanti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of \nC.diff recurrence\n...\nClostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\nEndpoints\nStatus\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\nAdults with qualifying C.\ndifficile infection episode\n230\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\nSecondary endpoint: safety, PK\n•\nFPCD: Q4 2025\n•\nData anticipated: >2027\nPhase I\nNCT06469151\nHealthy volunteers\n84\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose escalation\n•\nCohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2b: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.,  Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 3: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4a: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4b: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v., Chinese participants) or \n...\n•\n...\n•", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Immune aspects of Clostridioides difficile infection and vaccine ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12767691/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "This is supported by clinical evidence that: (1) hospital outbreaks have been caused by TcdA-negative and TcdB-positive *C. difficile* strains^7,8^; (2) higher antibody titers against TcdB (but not TcdA) correlate with a lower risk of recurrent infection^7,9^; and (3) neutralizing TcdB with bezlotoxumab reduces the risk of recurrent infections and adverse outcomes, while treatment with an anti-TcdA neutralizing mAb does not^10^.\nThis evidence makes TcdB the key therapeutic target.\n...\nHigh levels of anti-toxin neutralizing IgG antibodies correlate with decreased disease severity^9,11^; however, many people fail to develop a protective immune response after primary CDI, which is reflected in their high susceptibility to recurrence^9,12^.\n...\nAnother well-characterized neutralizing mAb, PA41 (now called AZD5148), binds to a single site on the GTD (Figure 2D), which allows TcdB uptake, but prevents the formation of the toxin pore and GTD translocation into the cytosol^89^.\n...\n- The neutralizing mAb Zinplava^™^ (bezlotoxumab) was discontinued in January 2025; however, a promising antibody candidate, AZD5148 (formerly PA41), is currently in early clinical trials (NCT06469151).", "date": "2025-08-12", "last_updated": "2026-06-09"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trials Appendix - Q1 2026 Results Update", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/eq1/Q1-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD5148\nanti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of C.diff recurrence\n...\nAZD5148 C. difficile mAb - Prevention of Recurrence (US)\n...\nClostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\n...\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\nAdults with qualifying C.\n...\n230\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\n...\nPhase I\n...\n•\n...\n•\n...\n•\n...\n•", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-09"}], "raw": {"id": "d60e30c3-5c4d-4b81-a726-d9e524da82e5"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:45+00:00"}
