readout: Q1 2026", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-09"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "H1 and Q2 2026 Results Clinical Trials Appendix", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/h1q2/H1-and-Q2-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD5148\nanti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of \nC.diff recurrence\n...\nClostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\nEndpoints\nStatus\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\nAdults with qualifying C.\ndifficile infection episode\n230\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\nSecondary endpoint: safety, PK\n•\nFPCD: Q4 2025\n•\nData anticipated: >2027\nPhase I\nNCT06469151\nHealthy volunteers\n...\n•\n...\n•\nCohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2b: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.,  Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 3: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4a: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4b: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v., Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 5: AZD5148 (dose 4, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: safety\n•\nSecondary endpoint: PK parameters\n•\nFPCD: Q2 2024\n•\nLPCD: Q4 2024\n•\nData readout: Q1 2026", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=af775e43-3e18-4b1b-b35f-7510a7360bfd", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\nTcdB binds at least three classes of receptors including CSPG4, Nectin-3, and PVRL3 [35,36,37].\n...\nTherefore, it is possible that TcdB could escape neutralization by Zinplava^®^ [14].\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].\n...\nFollowing this promising proof of concept, a phase II study has been initiated and is recruiting at the time of this writing [244].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-12"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad Review of the Field", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13337890/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\n...\nThe two most recently discovered receptors are known to bind TcdB outside of the CROPs domain.\nTherefore, it is possible that TcdB could escape neutralization by Zinplava^®^ [14].\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].\n...\nFurthermore, no adverse events were associated with TRL1068 treatment [243].\nFollowing this promising proof of concept, a phase II study has been initiated and is recruiting at the time of this writing [244].\n...\n- 40.AstraZeneca.\nA phase I double-blind, placebo-controlled study to evaluate the safety, tolerability and pharmacokinetics of AZD5148 in healthy adults.\n2025. https://www.clinicaltrials.gov.\nAccessed 10 Nov 2025.\n- 41.AstraZeneca.\nA phase IIb, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for prevention of recurrence of Clostridioides difficile infection in individuals 18 years of age and above.\n2025. https://www.clinicaltrials.gov.\nAccessed 10 Dec 2025.\n...\n- 62.Aridis Pharmaceuticals, Inc.\n...\n- 76.Soezi M, Piri-Gavgani S, Ghanei M, et al.", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-07-18"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "A single amino acid substitution determines susceptibility of ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13253237/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "2026 May 28;16:1849926.\n...\nIn this brief report, we uncover the molecular mechanism underlying a key limitation of bezlotoxumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting *Clostridioides difficile* toxin B (TcdB), specifically its reduced neutralizing activity against certain TcdB variants.\n...\nThis may also explain why AZD5148, a monoclonal antibody developed by AstraZeneca, exhibits reduced activity against TcdB3 *in vitro*, despite only a single amino acid substitution at the center of its epitope (Peritore-Galve et al., 2025).\n...\n[DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n...\n23. Peritore-Galve F.\nC., Kroh H.\nK., Shupe J.\nA., Ehni A.\nG., Cano Rodríguez R., Kordus S.\nL., et al.\n(2025).\nThe monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against TcdB-diverse Clostridioides difficile strains in mice.\nPloS Pathog.", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-08-14"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/our-therapy-areas/pipeline.html", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "Phase II\n### Phase II\n- **\n...\n** *metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis*\n#### AZD2389 - Phase II\n...\n- **Molecule size:** Small molecule\n...\n## Infectious Disease (as of 27 July 2026)\n...\n### Phase II\n...\n- **\nAZD5148\n** *reduction of C. diff recurrence*\n#### AZD5148 - Phase II\nClose\n- **Mechanism:** anti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb\n- **Area under investigation:** reduction of C. diff recurrence\n- **Molecule size:** Large molecule\n- **", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Clostridium Difficile Infections Pipeline 2026: FDA Updates,", "url": "https://www.openpr.com/news/4584063/clostridium-difficile-infections-pipeline-2026-fda-updates", "domain": "openpr.com", "snippet": "The report also covers a detailed description of the drug, including the mechanism of action of the drug, clinical studies, NDA approvals (if any), and product development activities comprising the technology, collaborations, mergers acquisition, funding, designations, and other product-related details.Some of the key takeaways from the Clostridium Difficile Infections Pipeline Report:• Companies across the globe are diligently working toward developing novel Clostridium Difficile Infections treatment therapies with a considerable amount of success over the years.\n...\n• In April 2026, AstraZeneca announced results from a Phase IIb randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study assessing the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for preventing recurrence of Clostridioides difficile Infection in individuals aged 18 years and older.• In April 2026, Vedanta Biosciences announced results from a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase III clinical trial evaluating VE303 for the prevention of recurrent Clostridioides difficile Infection.", "date": "2026-07-22", "last_updated": "2026-07-24"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12594360/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "# The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against TcdB-diverse Clostridioides difficile strains in mice\n...\nAZD5148 is a humanized anti-TcdB mAb that neutralizes toxin activity by blocking the delivery of the enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into host cells.\n...\nAZD5148 potently neutralized TcdB1 and TcdB2 *in vitro*, with EC~50~ values 1,000- to 14,000-fold lower than those of bezlotoxumab.\nIn a mouse CDI model induced by TcdB1- or TcdB2-expressing strains, AZD5148 provided robust protection against weight loss and mortality at significantly lower doses than bezlotoxumab.\nFurthermore, the addition of the anti-TcdA mAb, PA50, provided no additional protective benefit.\nAlthough AZD5148 did not neutralize TcdB3 *in vitro*, it significantly reduced intestinal edema and inflammatory cell infiltration in mice infected with a TcdB3-producing strain.\nThese findings demonstrate that AZD5148 offers broad-spectrum protection against *C. difficile* strains and retains *in vivo* efficacy even in the absence of *in vitro* neutralization.\nIts distinct mechanism of action and superior potency compared to bezlotoxumab support its continued development as a promising therapeutic candidate for the prevention of a first CDI episode and prevention of recurrence.\n...\nNotably, AZD5148 was effective at lower doses than bezlotoxumab and provided protection, even against a strain that produced a TcdB variant that it could not neutralize *in vitro*.\n...\nBezlotoxumab (Zinplava), a TcdB-neutralizing monoclonal antibody (mAb), was FDA-approved to prevent recurrent CDI in high-risk patients, but its production was discontinued in January 2025 [16,17].\n...\nWe previously described PA41 (now called AZD5148), a humanized mAb that binds a highly conserved TcdB GTD epitope and prevents the delivery of the GTD enzymatic cargo into the host cytoplasm [25,26].\n...\nThese results suggest that the prophylactic administration of AZD5148 alone is effective in preventing severe CDI in mice.\n...\nThese results suggest that AZD5148 provides superior protection compared to bezlotoxumab, particularly at low doses, and significantly reduces weight loss, survival, and inflammatory responses during infection with the TcdB2-producing R20291 strain.\n...\nIn conclusion, our data show that AZD5148 provides robust protection in a mouse model of CDI caused by diverse *C. difficile* strains.\n...\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated in a Phase I clinical trial for safety, tolerability, and pharmacokinetics in healthy adults (NCT06469151).", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2025-11-12"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Clinical Trials Appendix - FY 2025 Results Update - AstraZeneca", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2025/Q4-FY/Full-year-Q4-2025-clinical-trial-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD5148\nanti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of C. diff recurrence\n...\n139\nClostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\nEndpoints\nStatus\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\n≥ 18 years, with a qualifying C. \ndifficile infection episode at \nthe time of providing\ninformed consent\n230\n•\nRandomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo (1:1)\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\nSecondary endpoint: safety, PK\n•\nFPCD: Q4 2025\n•\nData anticipated: >2027\nPhase I\nNCT06469151\nHealthy volunteers\n84\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose escalation\n•\nCohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12614991/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nHerein, we showed that the AZD5148 epitope is highly conserved across all geographic regions and sequence types and has a higher average conservation frequency in its binding site (99.58%) when compared with bezlotoxumab (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes.\n...\nAn anti-TcdB monoclonal antibody (mAb), Zinplava (bezlotoxumab), was approved in 2016 and recently withdrawn from the market by the sponsor [5].\n...\nWe recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16].\n...\nAZD5148 targets the GTD region of TcdB (Figure 1*A*).\n...\nIn the present study, we report that AZD5148, an anti-TcdB mAb, shows broad neutralizing coverage in vitro against clinically relevant *C. difficile* ribotypes and has a highly conserved binding epitope in 9134 *C. difficile* genomes obtained globally between 2015 and 2023.\n...\nTaken together, this study has revealed that AZD5148 is expected to neutralize >97% of recently circulating *C. difficile* strains in 2 cell lines.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-08-09"}], "raw": {"id": "35498843-dbca-4f34-b4b0-b1da9516c915"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:45+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q002", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q002-4", "query": "AZD5148 TcdB epitope binding mechanism", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "AZD5148 TcdB epitope binding mechanism", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2225.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12614991/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nTcdB is a potent virulence factor and a clinically validated target [5–9], which initiates a multistep process starting with the combined repetitive oligopeptide sequences (CROPs) domain binding to a specific receptor, endocytosis, pore formation in acidified endosomes involving the delivery domain, and translocation of the glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) and autoprotease domain into the cytosol [10].\nIt was recently uncovered that the CROPs domain is often only conformationally required and its direct binding to a cell receptor is not necessary for intoxication or cell entry [11–13].\n...\nWe recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16].\n...\nAZD5148 targets the GTD region of TcdB (Figure 1*A*).\n...\nThe binding epitope of AZD5148 was defined by an x-ray–determined crystal complex, which can be visualized within the TcdB structure in Figure 1*A* (PDB ID 5VQM).\nThe epitope has an average conservation frequency of 96.57%, and the binding residues have an average conservation frequency of 99.73%.\n...\nContrastingly, the strategy of targeting the GTD, as employed by AZD5148, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and showed potent neutralization, except for RT-017, in vitro against both cell lines in our study.\nThe mechanism of blocking toxin translocation from within the endosome by targeting the GTD has proven to be an effective strategy in neutralizing TcdB activity.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-08-09"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12594360/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a humanized anti-TcdB mAb that neutralizes toxin activity by blocking the delivery of the enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into host cells.\n...\nWe previously identified AZD5148, a humanized mAb that blocks the delivery of the toxic TcdB glucosyltransferase enzyme into host cells by targeting a conserved region of the enzymatic domain.\nThis contrasts with bezlotoxumab, which binds to the TcdB combined repetitive oligopeptide sequence (CROPS) domain and functions by interfering with receptor binding.\n...\nThese toxins bind to host cell receptors and are internalized via endocytosis.\nEndosomal acidification triggers toxin conformational changes that facilitate pore formation and translocation of the glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into the host cytosol.\nThe GTD then irreversibly inactivates Rho- or Ras-family GTPases, disrupting the cytoskeleton, inducing proinflammatory cytokine production, and causing cell death [7].\n...\nBezlotoxumab blocks TcdB binding to the chondroitin sulfate proteoglycan 4 (CSPG4) receptor, but is less effective against different variants due to differences in receptor binding [23,24].\n...\nWe previously described PA41 (now called AZD5148), a humanized mAb that binds a highly conserved TcdB GTD epitope and prevents the delivery of the GTD enzymatic cargo into the host cytoplasm [25,26].\n...\nThese results demonstrate that the amino acid variation in the epitopes targeted by AZD5148 and bezlotoxumab plays a critical role in their ability to neutralize diverse TcdB subtypes.\n...\nUnlike bezlotoxumab, which binds the TcdB CROPS domain and prevents binding to the CSPG4 receptor [24,27], AZD5148 targets the TcdB GTD, blocking its translocation into host cells [25,26].\nThis mechanism of action downstream of surface receptor engagement could offer broader coverage across TcdB variants that differ in their receptor and substrate specificity.\n...\nThe effect of these differences is that, unlike most TcdB GTDs, which glucosylate Rho-family GTPases, the TcdB3 GTD is specific for Ras family GTPases [37,38].\n...\nThe second feature is that, despite the significant sequence differences that impact the GTD substrate specificity, the epitope where AZD5148 binds is highly conserved [21].\nThe crystal structure of the TcdB GTD:AZD5148 Fab complex revealed only one amino acid contact residue that differed between TcdB3 and other subtype sequences; TcdB1 and TcdB2 have a tyrosine at the center of the AZD5148 epitope that is replaced with a histidine in TcdB3 [21,26].\nIn conclusion, our data show that AZD5148 provides robust protection in a mouse model of CDI caused by diverse *C. difficile* strains.\nBy blocking the delivery of toxic cargo into the cell, AZD5148 efficacy is independent of the receptor tropism differences observed across TcdB subtypes.\nFurther, the AZD5148 epitope is, for the most part, strictly conserved across TcdB subtypes.", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2025-11-12"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=2487fb5c-a174-4489-a3aa-8931585c2cf7", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\nTcdB binds at least three classes of receptors including CSPG4, Nectin-3, and PVRL3 [35,36,37].\nThe two most recently discovered receptors are known to bind TcdB outside of the CROPs domain.\nTherefore, it is possible that TcdB could escape neutralization by Zinplava^®^ [14].\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-04"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Immune aspects of Clostridioides difficile infection and vaccine ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12767691/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Endosome acidification triggers structural changes in the toxins, resulting in pore formation and cytosolic delivery of an enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nTcdA and TcdB consist of four functional domains: the N-terminal glucosyltransferase domain (GTD), an autoprotease domain (APD), the delivery domain (DD) with roles in receptor binding, pore formation, and GTD translocation, and the CROPS (Figure 2A)^5^.\n...\nIn binding this site, bezlotoxumab blocks the TcdB conformation that can interact with chondroitin sulfate proteoglycan 4 (CSPG4), blocking cellular entry via this receptor (Figure 2B,C)^88^.\nAnother well-characterized neutralizing mAb, PA41 (now called AZD5148), binds to a single site on the GTD (Figure 2D), which allows TcdB uptake, but prevents the formation of the toxin pore and GTD translocation into the cytosol^89^.\nIncreased available *tcdB* sequences have revealed sequence variability across TcdB subtypes^90^.\nThis is concerning because there is substantial amino acid variation in the bezlotoxumab binding sites, and one key amino acid change in the AZD5148 binding site between TcdB subtypes^88–90^.\nSuch variations reduce the binding and neutralizing capabilities of mAbs *in vitro* ^89,90^, emphasizing the need to identify conserved neutralizing epitopes across TcdB subtypes.", "date": "2025-08-12", "last_updated": "2026-06-09"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "A single amino acid substitution determines susceptibility of ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13253237/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Bezlotoxumab neutralized TcdB1 and TcdB3 mediated cell death in human colonic epithelial cells, but not TcdB2 or TcdB4.\nSequence analysis revealed that TcdB1 and TcdB3 share a conserved residue within a key antibody-neutralizing region, containing glutamic acid at position 2033 (E2033), whereas TcdB2 and TcdB4 contain an alanine at this position (A2033).\nSubstitution of A2033 with E2033 in TcdB2 restored susceptibility to antibody-mediated neutralization.\n...\nAntibodies recognize epitopes on proteins that consist of specific amino acid sequences or residues.\nStudies have shown that bezlotoxumab has limited activity against the TcdB2 variant (Chen et al., 2021), raising the possibility that naturally occurring sequence variation in TcdB may limit antibody efficacy.\n...\nImmunoblot analysis demonstrated that bezlotoxumab was able to recognize TcdB1 and TcdB3, but not TcdB2 or TcdB4 (**Figures 2A, B**).\nConsistent with these interaction findings, bezlotoxumab inhibited TcdB1 and TcdB3-mediated cell death in human colonic epithelial cells, but not TcdB2 and TcdB4 induced cell death (**Figure 2C**).\nTogether, these findings suggest that sequence variation in TcdB determines susceptibility to antibody neutralization.\n...\nGlutamate at position 2033 (E2033) of the TcdB protein has been identified in computational studies (Hernandez et al., 2015; Karnchanapandh et al., 2024), as well as our computational analysis (**Supplementary Figure 1**), as a potentially important residue in mediating interaction at a key bezlotoxumab epitope site.\nThese analyses predict a favorable electrostatic interaction between TcdB residue E2033 and bezlotoxumab residue R59 (**Supplementary Figure 1**).\nTcdB1 and TcdB3 possess E2033, whereas TcdB2 and TcdB4 contain alanine at this position (A2033) (**Figures 3A, B**).\nThis supports the hypothesis that E2033 is critical for bezlotoxumab neutralization.\n...\nUsing immunoblot analysis, caspase activation, and cell death assays, we found that substitution of A2033 with E2033 in the TcdB2 variant restored susceptibility to antibody neutralization (**Figures 3C, D**).\nThese findings demonstrate that a single amino acid substitution at position 2033 is sufficient to restore susceptibility to bezlotoxumab neutralization.\n...\nOur results demonstrate that the A2033E substitution in TcdB2 restores susceptibility to bezlotoxumab and suggests that variation at this residue may contribute to neutralization among TcdB variants.\nWe found that TcdB sequence variation influences bezlotoxumab efficacy: the antibody neutralizes TcdB1 and TcdB3, which contain glutamate at position 2033, but not TcdB2 or TcdB4, and introducing E2033 into TcdB2 restores susceptibility, highlighting that a single amino acid can determine therapeutic effectiveness.\n...\nOur work provides mechanistic insight into how a single amino acid variation can drive antibody escape.\nThis may also explain why AZD5148, a monoclonal antibody developed by AstraZeneca, exhibits reduced activity against TcdB3 *in vitro*, despite only a single amino acid substitution at the center of its epitope (Peritore-Galve et al., 2025).", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-08-14"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "683. Understanding Clostridioides difficile toxinB gene conservation through surveillance of public data", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ofid/article/10/Supplement_2/ofad500.745/7446541?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "CDI is mediated by the action of toxin B (TcdB) in humans.\nMonoclonal antibodies (mAbs) that neutralize TcdB function have shown protection against CDI in preclinical models and more recently in the clinic.\nSeveral regions on the TcdB gene have been targeted by therapeutic mAbs including the glucosyltransferase (GTD) and the C-terminal combined repetitive oligopeptides (CROPS) domains.\nWe have developed AZD5148, an anti-TcdB mAb targeting a region within the GTD.\nTo understand its strain coverage, we examined sequence variations of GTD including the AZD5148 epitope in contemporary *C. difficile* strains utilizing a public data repository.\n...\nWe found GTD to be highly conserved in US isolates as well as globally.\nAdditionally, among contact residues for AZD5148, any variations observed were single, and were seen for positions 323 (Y-H), 329 (G-E), 349 (V-A), and 350 (L-I).\nThese findings have important implications for drug development including for AZD5148.\nSpecifically, our findings reveal that the GTD domain of the TcdB gene has lower variance globally over the last 7 years making it a promising target for a\n*C. difficile* TcdB neutralizing mAb.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection ... - PLOS", "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plospathogens/article/figures?id=10.1371/journal.ppat.1013651", "domain": "journals.plos.org", "snippet": "AZD5148 potently neutralizes TcdB1 and TcdB2 but not TcdB3 in vitro.\n...\nAZD5148 protects against mortality during infection with the TcdB1-producing strain VPI 10463.\n...\nAZD5148 attenuates submucosal edema and inflammation during infection with the TcdB3-producing RT017 strain M68.", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2026-05-19"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Nanobodies against C. difficile TcdA and TcdB reveal unexpected neutralizing epitopes and provide a toolkit for toxin quantitation in vivo", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10621975/", "domain": "ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In contrast, neutralizing epitopes for TcdB were found in multiple domains.\n...\nTcdA and TcdB intoxicate cells via a multistep process: receptor binding and endocytosis, pH-dependent pore formation, translocation of the GTD and APD across the endosomal membrane, autoprocessing and GTD release, and finally, GTD-mediated glucosylation of host GTPases.\n...\nTcdA and TcdB can bind to multiple receptors, but not all receptor binding sites have been defined [13].\nHistorically, receptor binding has been associated with the C-terminal CROPS domain.\n...\nTcdB has been reported to bind four protein receptor classes: chondroitin sulfate proteoglycan 4 (CSPG4); Frizzled (FZD) 1, FZD2, and FZD7; Nectin3; and TFP1 [17–20].\nThe interactions can vary with TcdB subtype, but arguably all bind in a CROPS-independent interaction.\n(The interaction with CSPG4 occurs at the ‘hinge’ where the CROPS moves relative to the other toxin domains and involves some of the N-terminal residues of the CROPS [21].)\n...\nWhile the mechanism for this neutralization is currently unknown, it could suggest a role for CROPS-dependent glycan binding or as-yet unidentified protein receptors for TcdB.\nThe DD, however, was the target of all potent TcdA binding clones as well as multiple potent TcdB binding clones.", "date": "2023-10-07", "last_updated": "2024-10-07"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Receptor binding mechanisms of Clostridioides difficile toxin B and ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9344982/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "More specifically, TcdB exploits the Wnt receptor Frizzled (FZD) members 1, 2, and 7 and chondroitin sulfate proteoglycan 4 (CSPG4) as high-affinity receptors for binding and entering host cells [18, 21-24].\n...\nThe FZD-binding site is located in the middle of the DRBD of TcdB.\nBut besides the traditional protein-protein interactions, TcdB exploits an endogenous fatty acid as a co-receptor, which is completely buried between FZD2 and TcdB through extensive hydrophobic interactions with both proteins, to enhance binding affinity and specificity toward FZD1, 2, and 7 (Figure 1A) [25].\nThis lipid is bound in a hydrophobic groove on the CRD, which is also the docking site for a palmitoleic acid (PAM) lipid modification of Wnt [27].\nTcdB binding would lock this endogenous lipid in the PAM-binding pocket on the CRD, which is therefore unavailable to engage the PAM of Wnt.\nUsing this strategy, TcdB not only gains access to host cells via FZDs, but also inhibits Wnt signaling that may contribute to pathogenesis of CDI.\n...\nNevertheless, our structure reveals that only the first CSPG repeat of CSPG4 (termed Repeat1, residues 410–551) is mainly responsible for TcdB binding, which was also confirmed by a crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) study [18].\nIn spite of its small size, the Repeat1 of CSPG4 binds at a strategic location on TcdB where multiple TcdB domains converge, and it direct interacts with TcdB amino acids spreading across the CPD, DRBD, CROPs, and a hinge region connecting the DRBD and CROPs.\n...\nOn the context of TcdB holotoxin, the binding sites for FZDs and CSPG4 are completely separated and ~78 Å away from each other, which would permit simultaneous binding of both if they are expressed on the same cell surface (Figure 1C).\nHowever, either FZDs or CSPG4 is sufficient to mediate cell entry, as our structure-based mutagenesis studies demonstrate that FZDs and CSPG4 act as independent receptors for TcdB, which is consistent with results from Gerhard and colleagues [13].\n...\nBased on the structure of the TcdB–CRD2 complex, we found that a single mutation at F1597 of TcdB1, a key residue that simultaneously binds CRD2 and the lipid co-receptor, is sufficient to abolish FZD binding [25], which was later confirmed by other groups [12, 13, 31].\n...\nIn summary, our structure and sequence analyses suggest that ~71% of TcdB variants could bind to both FZD and CSPG4; ~17% and ~7% could only use CSPG4 or FZD as their receptor, respectively; while the receptor-binding strategies for the rest ~5% of TcdB variants remain unknown (Figure 2E).", "date": "2021-12-13", "last_updated": "2025-09-10"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Receptor binding mechanisms of Clostridioides difficile toxin B and implications for therapeutics development", "url": "https://febs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/am-pdf/10.1111/febs.16310", "domain": "febs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "The FZD-binding site is located in the middle of \n95\nthe DRBD of TcdB.\n...\ngroove on the CRD, which is also the docking site for a palmitoleic acid (PAM) lipid \n...\nthis strategy, TcdB not only gains access to host cells via FZDs, but also inhibits Wnt ", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-04-13"}], "raw": {"id": "14d8c23a-3784-4449-b1e6-45da67af8cd0"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:46+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q003", "objective": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as RpoB Small Molecule", "anchors": ["RpoB"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q003-1", "query": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis RpoB inhibitors pipeline", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis RpoB inhibitors pipeline", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 7984.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The therapeutic pipeline for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis currently encompasses over 100 investigational agents in Phase II and III clinical development.\nEmerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\n...\n|JAK Inhibitor|Oncostellae (OST-122)|Oral gut-restricted JAK3/TYK2/ARK5 inhibitor|NCT04353791|Phase 1b/2a|Completed Dec 2022; development not pursued further|\n|JAK Inhibitor|Ritlecitinib (PF-06651600)|Oral selective JAK3/TEC family kinase inhibitor|NCT02958865 (VIBRATO)|Phase 2b|Completed May 2021; prioritized for alopecia areata|\n...\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|Zasocitinib (TAK-279)|AI-designed highly selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor|NCT06254950, NCT06764615|Phase 2|Recruiting; estimated completion Aug 2027|\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|Ropsacitinib (PF-06826647)|Selective TYK2 inhibitor|NCT04209556|Phase 2b|Withdrawn with zero enrollment due to portfolio re-prioritization|\n...\n|α4β7 antagonist|Emvistegrast (GS-1427)|Oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor|NCT06290934|Phase 2|Primary completion Oct 2025, final completion Feb 2027|\n...\n|IL-23R antagonist|Icotrokinra|First-in-class targeted oral peptide blocking IL-23 receptor|NCT06049017 (ANTHEM-UC)|Phase 2b|Met primary endpoint; 63.5% response (highest dose) vs 27% placebo|\n...\n|TL1A antagonist|Tulisokibart (MK-7240)|Humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A|NCT04996797 (ARTEMIS-UC), NCT06052059, NCT06651281|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed; Phase 3 ongoing, estimated completion Dec 2029|\n...\n|RIPK1 inhibitor|Eclitasertib (SAR443122)|Highly potent selective oral RIPK1 kinase inhibitor|NCT05588843 (RESOLUTE)|Phase 2|Started Nov 2022; estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\n|MAP3K8/TPL2 inhibitor|Tilpisertib (GS-4875)|Oral MAP3K8/TPL2 inhibitor|NCT04130919, NCT06029972 (PALEKONA)|Phase 2|First study terminated Feb 2021; PALEKONA ongoing, estimated completion 2027|\n...\n|Combination|Guselkumab + Golimumab|IL-23p19 antibody + TNF antagonist|NCT03662542 (VEGA), NCT05242484 (DUET-UC)|Phase 2, Phase 2b|VEGA completed Nov 2021; 83% response with combination.\n|Combination|OD-07656 + Vedolizumab|IRAK4 inhibitor + α4β7 integrin antagonist|NCT06850727|Phase 2a|Started June 2025; estimated completion Nov 2026|\n|Combination|Infliximab + Ustekinumab|TNF antagonist + IL-12/23 p40 antibody|NCT06453317|Phase 2|Started Feb 2025; estimated completion June 2028|\n...\nBrepocitinib (PF-06700841) is an oral dual TYK2/JAK1 inhibitor evaluated alongside ritlecitinib in the same VIBRATO Phase 2b trial (NCT02958865) for moderate-to-severe UC with identical inclusion criteria of patients aged 18–75 years having total MS ≥6, RBS ≥1, and MES ≥2 (38).\n...\nRopsacitinib (PF-06826647) is a selective TYK2 inhibitor that was evaluated in, a Phase 2B double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT with an open-label extension (NCT04209556) designed to assess its safety and efficacy in participants with moderate to severe UC.\n...\nTralokinumab (CAT-354) is a recombinant human monoclonal IgG4 antibody directed against IL-13 that was tested in a Phase IIa randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-arm, multicenter study (NCT01482884) evaluating its efficacy and safety as add-on therapy in patients with active, moderate-to-severe UC.\n...\nAn interim analysis in July 2025 showed lutikizumab achieved numerically higher endoscopic improvement rates compared to adalimumab but failed to demonstrate sufficient differentiation for statistical significance, leading the pharmaceutical company to discontinue monotherapy development in UC.\n...\nRosnilimab is a novel PD-1 agonist IgG1 monoclonal antibody designed to reduce T cell proliferation and inflammatory cytokine secretion by selectively depleting PD-1high expressing T follicular helper, T peripheral helper, and T effector cells while sparing PD-1low cells.\n...\nThe trial is currently in the recruitment Phase with first patient enrollment expected in Q3–2025 and an estimated study completion date of October 2028, therefore no efficacy or safety results are yet available for this novel dual-pathway inhibitor that aims to overcome the therapeutic ceiling observed with single-target approaches in inflammatory bowel disease.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "The IBD Therapeutic Pipeline Is Primed to Produce", "url": "https://practicalgastro.com/2019/04/10/the-ibd-therapeutic-pipeline-is-primed-to-produce/", "domain": "practicalgastro.com", "snippet": "A phase IIb/III trial of MEDI2070 in patients with moderate to severe CD is currently active (NCT03759288) as is a phase II trial of MEDI2070 in patients with UC (NCT03616821).\n...\nSeveral other IL23 specific inhibitors are rapidly entering clinical trials for CD and UC, including mirikizumab (LY3074828) (Eli Lilly), tildrakizumab (Sun Pharma), and guselkumab (Janssen).^9^ Of the three, mirikizumab is farthest along in development with two phase II clinical trials recruiting, one for active CD (SERENITY) and another for moderate to severe UC (NCT02891226, NCT02589665 respectively).\n...\nTofacitinib (Xeljanz) (Pfizer) is a pan-JAK inhibitor with a preference for JAK1 and JAK3 that has demonstrated efficacy in the treatment of ulcerative colitis.^33^ As a result, Tofacitinib was recently approved in the United States and Europe for moderate to severe active ulcerative colitis (UC) based on phase II and phase III trials.\n...\nUpadacitinib (ABT-494) (AbbVie) is a more potent JAK1-selective inhibitor that is currently being investigated for use in Crohn’s disease, ulcerative colitis, rheumatoid arthritis, atopic dermatitis, and psoriatic arthritis.^42^ CELEST (NCT02365649) was a phase II, randomized, double-blinded, placebo-controlled study investigating upadacitinib for patients with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease.", "date": "2019-04-10", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "The therapeutic pipeline for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis currently encompasses over 100 investigational agents in Phase II and III clinical development.\nEmerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\nTumor necrosis factor-like ligand 1A pathway inhibitors demonstrate particularly robust efficacy in early trials, with clinical remission rates exceeding 25% compared to less than 2% for placebo.\n...\n|PSGL-1 agonist|Leiolizumab|Tetravalent agonist IgG1 antibody targeting PSGL-1|NCT06109441|Phase 2a|Started Dec 2023; estimated completion Nov 2026|\n...\n|TL1A antagonist|Tulisokibart (MK-7240)|Humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A|NCT04996797 (ARTEMIS-UC), NCT06052059, NCT06651281|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed; Phase 3 ongoing, estimated completion Dec 2029|\n...\n|Combination|OD-07656 + Vedolizumab|IRAK4 inhibitor + α4β7 integrin antagonist|NCT06850727|Phase 2a|Started June 2025; estimated completion Nov 2026|\n...\nRopsacitinib (PF-06826647) is a selective TYK2 inhibitor that was evaluated in, a Phase 2B double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT with an open-label extension (NCT04209556) designed to assess its safety and efficacy in participants with moderate to severe UC.\n...\nThe same MORF-057 molecule is being evaluated in a double-blind, Phase 2b placebo-controlled RCT (EMERALD-2, NCT05611671) with 282 participants meeting similar inclusion criteria but specifically requiring inadequate response to aminosalicylates, corticosteroids, immunosuppressants, or advanced UC therapies, measuring clinical remission using mMS at 12 weeks as primary endpoint.\n...\nTralokinumab (CAT-354) is a recombinant human monoclonal IgG4 antibody directed against IL-13 that was tested in a Phase IIa randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-arm, multicenter study (NCT01482884) evaluating its efficacy and safety as add-on therapy in patients with active, moderate-to-severe UC.\n...\nRosnilimab is a novel PD-1 agonist IgG1 monoclonal antibody designed to reduce T cell proliferation and inflammatory cytokine secretion by selectively depleting PD-1high expressing T follicular helper, T peripheral helper, and T effector cells while sparing PD-1low cells.\n...\nCU104 is a new oral investigational drug that specifically inhibits IL1β, IL-6, and TNFα (69).\nIt is being studied in a double-blind, placebo-controlled, multi-center Phase 2 RCT (NCT05907330) designed to evaluate its efficacy and safety in patients with moderate to severe UC.\n...\nRO7837195 is a p40xTL1A bispecific antibody developed through a Roche-Pfizer collaboration that simultaneously targets IL-12/IL-23 (via the p40 subunit) and TL1A pathways, currently being evaluated in the Phase IIb SUNCREST trial (NCT06979336) which employs a multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled design with a 12-week induction Phase followed by a 40-week active treatment extension in approximately 224 participants across five dosing arms.\n...\nThe trial is currently in the recruitment Phase with first patient enrollment expected in Q3–2025 and an estimated study completion date of October 2028, therefore no efficacy or safety results are yet available for this novel dual-pathway inhibitor that aims to overcome the therapeutic ceiling observed with single-target approaches in inflammatory bowel disease.\n...\nA study examines tirzepatide, a dual GLP-1/GIP receptor agonist administered subcutaneously, combined with mirikizumab, an IL-23 p19 antibody, in adults with moderately to severely active UC who are also obese or overweight (NCT06937086).\nIt is a Phase 3b, randomized, multicenter, controlled study that began in June 2025 with an estimated completion date of April 2028.\n...\nSeveral mechanistic classes demonstrate particular promise.\nTL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations.\nNext-generation selective JAK1 and TYK2 inhibitors aim to preserve efficacy while mitigating safety concerns through enhanced selectivity.\nGut-restricted therapies including obefazimod, MORF-057, and OST-122 achieve therapeutically relevant tissue concentrations with minimal systemic exposure, potentially optimizing the benefit-risk ratio.\nCombination therapy strategies may prove essential for breaking the therapeutic ceiling, with dual biologic approaches demonstrating 83% clinical response versus 61-75% for monotherapy in controlled trials.\nAdditional promising mechanisms targeting immune checkpoints, RIPK1-mediated necroptosis, and tissue repair pathways offer complementary approaches for patients failing conventional treatments.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Treatment Options in Mild to Moderate Ulcerative Colitis", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4609145/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Biologic therapy for patients with UC includes the anti-TNF agents infliximab, adalimumab, and golimumab and the integrin inhibitor vedolizumab (Figure 4).\n...\n^31^ A second integrin inhibitor, vedolizumab, was developed that is directed against the integrin α~4~β~7~.\n...\nMoreover, vedolizumab was beneficial regardless of the patient’s treatment history, whether that included prior anti-TNF failure, prior anti-TNF loss of response, or no previous anti-TNF therapy.^32^ Vedolizumab, which is administered via intravenous infusion, received FDA approval in 2014 for patients with previously treated moderately to severely active UC.\n...\nA therapy that was designed to meet these needs is budesonide foam, which recently received FDA approval for the induction of remission in patients with mild to moderate distal UC extending up to 40 cm from the anal verge.^1^ This area includes the rectum, which spans the first 10 cm to 15 cm, as well as the sigmoid colon, which spans the next 25 cm to 30 cm.\n...\nSeveral investigational integrin inhibitors are being evaluated, including etrolizumab, which targets the integrin β7, and AMG 181, a gut-specific anti-α~4~β~7~ antibody.\nEtrolizumab demonstrated clinical efficacy in a randomized, controlled, phase 2 trial in patients with moderate to severe UC not responding to conventional therapy.\n...\nSeveral Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors are being evaluated in the treatment of UC.\nThe pan-JAK inhibitor tofacitinib, which has been shown to affect multiple cytokines, demonstrated efficacy as induction therapy in patients with moderate to severe UC.^10^ Tofacitinib is currently being evaluated in phase 3 trials for induction and maintenance of remission in patients with moderately to severely active UC.^11^^,^^12^ Potential safety concerns with tofacitinib include significant immunosuppression, which is associated with a small increased risk of infection.^10^", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "academic.oup.com · ecco-jcc · articleECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Ulcerative Colitis ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/20/7/jjag066/8726617", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Among the most promising alternatives to infliximab and calcineurin inhibitors in ASUC are JAKi^195^.\nSome of the potential advantages of JAKi are rapid absorption and clearance and lack of effect on plasma albumin levels, which may hamper the effectiveness of monoclonal antibodies.^187^ A systematic review of tofacitinib in ASUC included 148 patients and revealed colectomy-free survival of 85% at 30 days, 86% at 90 days, and 69% at 180 days.\n...\nTo date, the main therapeutic third-line options may include one of the three following sequences: cyclosporine followed by infliximab, infliximab followed by cyclosporine, or JAKi [upadacitinib or tofacitinib] following failure of either previous agent.", "date": "2026-07-07", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis - Gastroenterology - Merck Manuals", "url": "https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/inflammatory-bowel-disease-ibd/ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "merckmanuals.com", "snippet": "Suppositories are effective for more distal disease and are usually preferred by patients.\n...\nPatients with inflammation proximal to the sigmoid colon or left-sided disease unresponsive to topical agents should receive a biologic agent (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab, guselkumab, risankizumab, mirikizumab, ustekinumab, vedolizumab) with or without an immunomodulator medication (azathioprine, methotrexate, 6-mercaptopurine) (1, 4, 5).\n...\nFinally, in some patients, a trial of the small-molecule Janus kinase inhibitors (tofacitinib or upadacitinib) or the sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor modulator ozanimod or etrasimod can be considered.\n...\n- Treat mild disease with 5-ASA by rectum and, for proximal disease, by mouth.\n- Treat extensive or severe disease with high-dose glucocorticoids, immunomodulator therapy (eg, azathioprine, 6-mercaptopurine), biologics (eg, adalimumab, infliximab, vedolizumab), tofacitinib, upadacitinib, or ozanimod.", "date": "2026-03-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline", "url": "https://www.merck.com/research/product-pipeline/", "domain": "merck.com", "snippet": "##### Ulcerative colitis\n(NCT07463183) Therapeutic Area: Immunology", "date": "2026-08-18", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis Pipeline: 75+ Therapies & Key Companies", "url": "https://www.delveinsight.com/insights/ulcerative-colitis-clinical-trial-analysis", "domain": "delveinsight.com", "snippet": "DelveInsight’s** “Ulcerative Colitis Pipeline Insight 2026”** report provides comprehensive insights about **70+ companies and 75+ pipeline drugs** in the Ulcerative Colitis Pipeline Landscape.\n...\nObefazimod is an oral small-molecule drug candidate in clinical development for the treatment of moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) and has demonstrated anti-inflammatory activity in preclinical studies and in both Phase IIa and Phase IIb clinical trials.\nCurrently, the drug is in Phase III stage of its clinical trial for the treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\nABBV-668 is under development for the treatment of crohn’s disease, unspecified immunological disorders and ulcerative colitis.\nThe drug candidate acts by targeting receptor interacting serine/threonine protein kinase 1 (RIPK1).\nIt is administered through oral route.\nCurrently, the drug is in Phase II stage of its clinical trial for the treatment of ulcerative colitis.", "date": "2026-03-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "ECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Ulcerative Colitis ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/16/1/2/6390052", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "We recommend tofacitinib for maintaining remission in patients with UC who responded to induction therapy with tofacitinib [strong recommendation, moderate quality of evidence]", "date": "2022-01-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "What are the key players in the ulcerative colitis treatment ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/article/what-are-the-key-players-in-the-ulcerative-colitis-treatment-market", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Small molecule inhibitors, such as tofacitinib and upadacitinib, have further expanded the therapeutic horizon by acting on Janus kinases (JAKs) to modulate inflammatory signaling cascades.\n...\nOther companies highlighted in later reports include Johnson & Johnson’s emphasis on advanced biologics, Bausch Health Companies Inc. with its product line for rectal therapies (e.g., UCERIS® budesonide rectal foam, which is becoming significant in the treatment of mild to moderate distal UC).\n...\nIn addition, pipeline-focused companies like Palatin Technologies are developing next-generation small-molecule candidates such as PL8177 for oral colon delivery and effective targeting of the melanocortin-1 receptor, opening up new therapeutic modalities for UC patients.\n...\n• Additionally, newer candidates in development such as filgotinib (a JAK1 preferential inhibitor) and emerging agents like cobitolimod (a toll-like receptor 9 agonist) are expanding the therapeutic armamentarium.", "date": "2025-03-11", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}], "raw": {"id": "548d945a-e039-43d0-8f44-9ff922196e60"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:53+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q003", "objective": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as RpoB Small Molecule", "anchors": ["RpoB"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q003-2", "query": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis RpoB competitors Phase 2", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis RpoB competitors Phase 2", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 8114.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "ECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Ulcerative Colitis ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/16/1/2/6390052", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "We recommend topical [rectal] 5-ASA at a dose of ≥1 g/d for the induction of remission in active distal colitis [strong recommendation, low-quality evidence]\n...\nWe recommend treatment with anti-tumour necrosis factor [TNF] agents [infliximab, adalimumab, and golimumab] to induce remission in patients with moderate-to-severe UC who have inadequate response or intolerance to conventional therapy [strong recommendation, moderate-quality evidence]\n...\nWe recommend anti-TNF agents [infliximab, adalimumab, or golimumab] for the maintenance of remission in patients with UC who responded to induction therapy with the same drug [strong recommendation, high-quality evidence]\n...\nWe suggest the use of vedolizumab rather than adalimumab for the induction and maintenance of remission in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis [weak recommendation, low level of evidence]\n...\nWe recommend tofacitinib for maintaining remission in patients with UC who responded to induction therapy with tofacitinib [strong recommendation, moderate quality of evidence]", "date": "2022-01-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Reviewing treatments and outcomes in the evolving landscape of ulcerative colitis", "url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00325481.2017.1319730", "domain": "tandfonline.com", "snippet": "The first-line therapy for patients with active, mild-to-moderate forms of distal UC is rectal 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) therapy [Citation22,Citation35].\n...\nRectal corticosteroids are also recommended for patients with mild-to-moderate distal UC [Citation22], but as second-line therapy in patients refractory to rectal 5-ASAs [Citation35].\nOral and rectal corticosteroids have demonstrated efficacy for the induction of remission of mild-to-moderate UC [Citation47] but are not recommended for long-term maintenance of remission of UC [Citation48].\n...\nSecond-generation corticosteroids (budesonide, beclomethasone dipropionate) have high first-pass hepatic metabolism and are associated with fewer corticosteroid-related adverse effects than conventional corticosteroids [Citation50–Citation57].\nBudesonide foam, a formulation approved in 2014 for the induction of remission of active, mild-to-moderate distal UC, has a maximal spread to 40 cm (mean, 25.4 cm) proximal from the rectum to the descending colon [Citation58,Citation59].\n...\nCurrent guidelines make no specific recommendations for or against the use of budesonide foam for the induction of remission of distal UC, but current opinion suggests that budesonide foam should be considered as second-line therapy for patients with UP refractory to treatment with rectal 5-ASA [Citation65].\n...\nThe three TNF-α antagonists currently approved by the US Food and Drug Administration for the treatment of patients with moderate-to-severe UC are infliximab, adalimumab, and golimumab [Citation84].", "date": "2017-07-04", "last_updated": "2026-01-07"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacological Therapy in Inflammatory Bowel Diseases", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10537095/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|Aminosalicylates [26,28,30]|5-ASA|UC|Distal colitis: rectal 5-ASA ≥ 1 g/d Extensive colitis: rectal 5-ASA ≥ 1 g/d + oral 5-ASA ≥ 2 g/d|≥1 g/d >2 g/d|- >4 g/d|\n...\nMirikizumab is a p19-driven antibody against interleukin-23.\nIts efficacy and safety in the treatment of UC were evaluated in the LUCENT-1 (induction) and LUCENT-2 (maintenance) studies.\nClinical remission after 12 and 52 weeks using Mirikizumab was 24.2% and 49.9% against 13.3% and 25.1 in the placebo group, respectively [53].\n...\nFilgotinib is a preferred inhibitor of JAK 1 (blocking potency over JAK 1 five times greater than the others) and has been evaluated in the treatment of UC through the SELECTION trials.\nUnlike most IBD therapies, there are no differences in induction or maintenance doses.", "date": "2023-09-08", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "What are the key players in the ulcerative colitis treatment ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/article/what-are-the-key-players-in-the-ulcerative-colitis-treatment-market", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Small molecule inhibitors, such as tofacitinib and upadacitinib, have further expanded the therapeutic horizon by acting on Janus kinases (JAKs) to modulate inflammatory signaling cascades.\n...\nstands out as a leader largely because of its blockbuster anti‐TNF agent and other biologics that are heavily utilized in inflammatory bowel disease management.\n...\nTheir portfolio includes agents used in IBD treatment, and they have built a reputation for scientific research in this area.\n• Pfizer Inc. is well known for both its conventional and advanced therapeutic agents.\nWith products such as tofacitinib, Pfizer has been influential in pioneering the use of oral JAK inhibitors, which have provided an additional treatment option for patients who have failed biologic therapies or cannot tolerate them.\n...\n• Teva Pharmaceutical Industries Ltd.\nremains a notable competitor with a wide range of generic and innovative treatment options, including biosimilars that have an impact on pricing and market share for UC therapies.\n• Additionally, companies such as Merck & Co. and Shire (now part of Takeda following its acquisition) have contributed to the richer pipeline of UC treatments, often focusing on both existing biologics and emerging novel compounds.\n...\nOther companies highlighted in later reports include Johnson & Johnson’s emphasis on advanced biologics, Bausch Health Companies Inc. with its product line for rectal therapies (e.g., UCERIS® budesonide rectal foam, which is becoming significant in the treatment of mild to moderate distal UC).\nEmerging players such as Janssen Biotech Inc.\n(notably with its FDA approval of STELARA [ustekinumab] for UC) are also key contributors, marking advances in targeting interleukin pathways to achieve better management outcomes.\nIn addition, pipeline-focused companies like Palatin Technologies are developing next-generation small-molecule candidates such as PL8177 for oral colon delivery and effective targeting of the melanocortin-1 receptor, opening up new therapeutic modalities for UC patients.\n...\n• Aminosalicylates continue to form the bedrock of treatment for mild-to-moderate disease.", "date": "2025-03-11", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative colitis - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6487890/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "There are at least 27 new drugs for ulcerative colitis with either recently completed or active trials.^170^ One example is the oral pan-janus kinase inhibitor tofacitinib, which has shown higher rates of clinical remission than placebo in phase 2 studies.\n^171^ Etrolizumab, a subcutaneous monoclonal antibody that blocks the β7 subunit of the heterodimeric integrins α4β7 and αEβ7 achieved higher clinical remission rates than placebo in a phase 2 trial.^172^ An oral anti-α4 integrin therapy (AJM300) significantly increased clinical remission and endoscopic healing in a phase 2 trial.", "date": "2016-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "academic.oup.com · ecco-jcc · articleECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Ulcerative Colitis ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/20/7/jjag066/8726617", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "In treating patients with mildly-to-moderately active distal UC, both per rectal 5-ASA and per rectal corticosteroids, particularly second-generation corticosteroids such as beclomethasone dipropionate and budesonide, have demonstrated efficacy in inducing clinical and endoscopic remission.\n...\nThe use of topical 5-ASA as maintenance therapy in adult patients with distal UC or proctitis was significantly superior in maintaining clinical remission compared with placebo [RR: 2.22; 95% CI: 1.26–3.90].\n...\nTo date, the main therapeutic third-line options may include one of the three following sequences: cyclosporine followed by infliximab, infliximab followed by cyclosporine, or JAKi [upadacitinib or tofacitinib] following failure of either previous agent.", "date": "2026-07-07", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis - Pipeline Insight, 2025", "url": "https://www.researchandmarkets.com/reports/4989296/ulcerative-colitis-pipeline-insight-2025", "domain": "researchandmarkets.com", "snippet": "This “Ulcerative Colitis - Pipeline Insight, 2025” report provides comprehensive insights about 70+ companies and 75+ pipeline drugs in Ulcerative Colitis pipeline landscape.\n...\nRectal application of medical therapy, via suppository or enema, is usually appropriate for isolated distal disease (proctitis); however, a rectal application is usually used in combination with systemic therapy to help target the distal colon and, therefore, decrease tenesmus.\n...\nObefazimod is an oral small-molecule drug candidate in clinical development for the treatment of moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) and has demonstrated anti-inflammatory activity in preclinical studies and in both Phase IIa and Phase IIb clinical trials.\n...\nABBV-668 is under development for the treatment of crohn's disease, unspecified immunological disorders and ulcerative colitis.\nThe drug candidate acts by targeting receptor interacting serine/threonine protein kinase 1 (RIPK1).\nIt is administered through oral route.\nCurrently, the drug is in Phase II stage of its clinical trial for the treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\nAnti-TL1A (TEV-’574) is currently in Phase 2b clinical trials for the treatment of ulcerative colitis (UD) and Crohn's disease (CD), two types of inflammatory bowel disease.\n...\nCurrently, the drug is in Phase II stage of its clinical trial for the treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\nThere are approx. 70+ key companies which are developing the therapies for Ulcerative Colitis.\nThe companies which have their Ulcerative Colitis drug candidates in the most advanced stage, i.e. Phase III include Abivax.\n...\n## The report covers around 75+ products under different phases of clinical development like\n- Late stage products (Phase III)\n- Mid-stage products (Phase II)\n- Early-stage product (Phase I) along with the details of\n- Pre-clinical and Discovery stage candidates\n- Discontinued & Inactive candidates\n...\n## Key Players\n- Oppilan Pharma\n- Genentech\n- Teva Branded Pharmaceutical\n- Boehringer Ingelheim\n- Sorriso Pharmaceuticals\n- Reistone Biopharma\n- Celgene\n- AnaptysBio\n- Rise Therapeutics\n- Merck\n- AltruBio\n- AbbVie\n- Immunic AG\n- Kissei Pharmaceutical Co\n- Lmito Therapeutics\n- Morphic Therapeutic\n- Oncostellae\n- Palatin Technologies\n...\n- VTX002\n- Vixarelimab\n- TEV-48574\n- Spesolimab\n- SOR102\n- SHR0302\n- RPC1063\n- Rosnilimab\n- R-3750\n- MK-7240\n- ALTB-268\n- ABBV-668\n- IMU-838\n- KSP-0243\n- LMT503\n- MORF-057\n- OST-122\n- PL8177", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-17"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis (UC) Pipeline Insight | Drugs, Therapies", "url": "https://www.delveinsight.com/report-store/ulcerative-colitis-uc-pipeline-insight", "domain": "delveinsight.com", "snippet": "DelveInsight’s, “Ulcerative Colitis - Pipeline Insight, 2026” report provides comprehensive insights about 115+ companies and 120+ pipeline drugs in Ulcerative Colitis pipeline landscape.\n...\nThis segment of the Ulcerative Colitis report encloses its detailed analysis of various drugs in different stages of clinical development, including phase II, I, preclinical and Discovery.\n...\nCurrently, the drug is being evaluated in the Phase II stage of its development for the treatment of Ulcerative Colitis.\n- ALTB-268: AltruBio\n...\nCurrently, the drug is being evaluated in the Phase II stage of its development for the treatment of Ulcerative Colitis.\n- OD-001: Odyssey Therapeutics\n...\nCurrently, the drug is being evaluated in the Phase II stage of its development for the treatment of Ulcerative Colitis.\n- PALI-2108: Palisade Bio\n...\nCurrently, the drug is being evaluated in the Phase II stage of its development for the treatment of Ulcerative Colitis.\n- R-3750: Rise Therapeutics\nR-3750, a novel oral immunotherapy, is a synthetic biology-based medicine optimized to selectively target dendritic cells, R-3750 is a first-in-class therapy with a unique mechanism of action with the potential to treat the underlying immunological basis of gastrointestinal inflammatory diseases.\n...\nCurrently, the drug is being evaluated in the Phase I stage of its development for the treatment of Ulcerative Colitis.\n...\nThe report provides insights into different therapeutic candidates in phase II, I, preclinical and discovery stage.", "date": "2025-10-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "The IBD Therapeutic Pipeline is Primed to Produce", "url": "https://practicalgastro.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/IBD-Therapeutic-Pipeline-Is-Primed-To-Produce.pdf", "domain": "practicalgastro.com", "snippet": "Two of the most promising IL23 inhibitors currently in the pipeline, are MEDI2070 (brazikumab) (Allergan) and risankizumab (AbbVie).\n...\nSeveral other IL23 specific inhibitors are rapidly entering clinical trials for CD and UC, including mirikizumab (LY3074828) (Eli Lilly), tildrakizumab (Sun Pharma), and guselkumab\n(Janssen).9 Of the three, mirikizumab is farthest \nalong in development with two phase II clinical trials recruiting, one for active CD (SERENITY) and another for moderate to severe UC (NCT02891226,\nNCT02589665 respectively).\n...\nUC.21 One such therapy is LT-02 (Nestlé Health \nScience), which demonstrated promising early results in clinical trials, including a phase II trial for UC patients refractory to mesalazine.22 This was a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled\n...\n(UC) based on phase II and phase III trials.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-10"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Third European Evidence-based Consensus on Diagnosis and ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/11/7/769/2962457", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Patients with endoscopically documented active distal colitis or proctitis, who fail oral corticosteroids combined with oral and rectal 5-ASA therapy, have refractory disease.\nTherapeutic options include admission for IV steroid therapy, which has been reported to induce remission in a high proportion of patients.^166^ Alternatively, there is open-label evidence, often from retrospective case reviews, supporting the use of salvage medical therapies such as oral or rectal CsA, oral or rectal tacrolimus, or IFX.^167–171^\n...\nOral or rectal 5-ASA is superior to placebo in maintaining remission in UC.\nRectal 5-ASA has equivalent or slightly superior efficacy to oral 5-ASA in distal UC.", "date": "2017-07-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "4090d652-c36a-435a-9eb9-b91927acd18e"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:54+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q003", "objective": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as RpoB Small Molecule", "anchors": ["RpoB"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q003-3", "query": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis RpoB mechanism clinical trial", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis RpoB mechanism clinical trial", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1081.0, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis - 1,154 clinical trials", "url": "https://atlasbyauro.com/trials/condition/ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "atlasbyauro.com", "snippet": "|Mirikizumab Administered at the Same Time as Tirzepatide in Adult Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis and Obesity or Overweight: Phase 3b Study (NCT06937086)|Eli Lilly and Company|2025-06-26|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-12"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Colitis clinical trials", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gg/conditions/colitis", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gg", "snippet": "- A Pilot Study Testing the Safety and Feasibility of Restorative Microbiota Therapy (RMT) in Patients With Refractory Immune-checkpoint Inhibitor-related ColitisRecruitingNCT05726396Phase 21 country\n...\n- Tacrolimus Lipid Suspension for Enema in Adult Subjects with Mild to Moderately Active Left-sided/Distal Ulcerative Colitis Who Had an Inadequate Response to the TreatmentNot yet recruitingNCT06867042Phase 2", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Gut microbiome‐targeted therapies as adjuvant treatments ...", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/jgh.16795", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Thirty-eight RCTs met the inclusion criteria.\nFirstly, we compared the efficacy of MTTs in IBD patients.\nOnly FMT and probiotics were superior to placebo in all outcomes, but FMT ranked best in improving clinical response rate and clinical and endoscopic remission rate, and probiotics ranked second in reducing clinical relapse rate showed significant efficacy, while prebiotics ranked first showed nonsignificant efficacy.", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical outcomes of best practices for the treatment of distal ... - OUCI", "url": "https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/works/4YmAbXPQ/", "domain": "ouci.dntb.gov.ua", "snippet": "Distal ulcerative colitis (DUC), affecting 30–50% of ulcerative colitis (UC) patients, is a subtype characterized by inflammation limited to the rectum and sigmoid colon.\nThe pathogenesis involves a complex interplay of genetic, environmental, and immune factors, with cytokines like TNF-α, IL-1β, IL-6, and IL-17 playing significant roles.\n...\nThis review examined 50 randomized controlled trials (RCTs) that explored pharmacological and non-pharmacological interventions for distal ulcerative colitis, focusing on symptom remission, mucosal healing, and quality-of-life outcomes.\nKey findings revealed that pharmacologic treatments, particularly 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA), corticosteroids, and biologics, effectively achieved remission, with combined therapies beneficial in refractory cases.\n...\nImportantly, trials highlighted that individualized approaches – such as early biologic therapy for patients with moderate-to-severe disease, dose adjustments based on patient demographics, and tailored lifestyle recommendations – enhanced treatment response and patient quality of life.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-01"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Randomised clinical trial: the effectiveness of __Lactobacillus reuteri__ ATCC 55730 rectal enema in children with active distal ulcerative colitis", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/j.1365-2036.2011.04939.x", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Randomised clinical trial: the effectiveness of __Lactobacillus reuteri__ ATCC 55730 rectal enema in children with active distal ulcerative colitis\n...\n**Background** Intestinal microbiota manipulation, one of the pathogenetic components of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), has become an attractive therapy for ulcerative colitis (UC).\n**Aim** To assess in children with active distal UC the effectiveness of __Lactobacillus (L) reuteri__ ATCC 55730 enema on inflammation and cytokine expression of rectal mucosa.\n**Methods** A total of 40 patients (median age: 7.2 years range 6–18) with mild to moderate UC were enrolled in a prospective, randomised, placebo‐controlled study.\nThey received an enema solution containing 10^10^ CFU of __L.\nreuteri__ ATCC 55730 or placebo for 8 weeks, in addition to oral mesalazine.\nClinical endoscopic and histological scores as well as rectal mucosal expression levels of IL‐10, IL‐1β, TNFα and IL‐8 were evaluated at the beginning and at the end of the trial.\n**Results** Thirty‐one patients accomplished the trial (17 males, median age 13 year, range 7–18).\nMayo score (including clinical and endoscopic features) decreased significantly in the __L.\nreuteri__ group (3.2 ± 1.3 vs. 8.6 ± 0.8, __P__<0.01) compared with placebo (7.1 ± 1.1 vs.\n8.7 ± 0.7, NS); furthermore, histological score significantly decrease only in the __L.\nreuteri__ group (0.6 ± 0.5 vs. 4.5 ± 0.6, __P__<0.01) (placebo: 2.9 ± 0.8 vs.\n4.6 ± 0.6, NS).\nAt the post‐trial evaluation of cytokine mucosal expression levels, IL‐10 significantly increased (__P__<0.01) whereas IL‐1β, TNFα and IL‐8 significantly decreased (__P__<0.01) only in the __L.\nreuteri__ group.\n**Conclusions** In children with active distal ulcerative colitis, rectal infusion of __L.\nreuteri__ is effective in improving mucosal inflammation and changing mucosal expression levels of some cytokines involved in the mechanisms of inflammatory bowel disease.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Third European Evidence-based Consensus on Diagnosis and ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/11/7/769/2962457", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "An RCT has assessed the safety and efficacy of eldelumab, a monoclonal antibody against IP-10 in 252 UC patients.\n...\nTacrolimus is a calcineurin inhibitor that acts via a mechanism similar to CsA.\n...\nInfliximab as a single dose [5 mg/kg] is an effective salvage therapy in patients with severe UC refractory to IV steroids.\n...\nPatients with endoscopically documented active distal colitis or proctitis, who fail oral corticosteroids combined with oral and rectal 5-ASA therapy, have refractory disease.\nTherapeutic options include admission for IV steroid therapy, which has been reported to induce remission in a high proportion of patients.^166^ Alternatively, there is open-label evidence, often from retrospective case reviews, supporting the use of salvage medical therapies such as oral or rectal CsA, oral or rectal tacrolimus, or IFX.^167–171^\n...\nIn ULTRA 2, the pivotal RCT of adalimumab in patients with moderate to severe UC, 494 adult patients with endoscopically confirmed moderate/severely active colitis despite treatment with corticosteroids and/or thiopurines, were randomised to receive 160 mg adalimumab at Week 0, 80 mg at Week 2, and then 40 mg every other week from Week 4.\n...\nOral or rectal 5-ASA is superior to placebo in maintaining remission in UC.\nRectal 5-ASA has equivalent or slightly superior efficacy to oral 5-ASA in distal UC.\n...\nNo head-to head, prospective trials are available.", "date": "2017-07-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Medical Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2780076/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "A double-blind comparison of oral versus rectal mesalamine versus combination therapy in the treatment of distal ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Recent advances in the management of distal ulcerative colitis", "url": "https://www.wjgnet.com/2150-5349/full/v1/i2/43.htm", "domain": "wjgnet.com", "snippet": "Infliximab 5 mg/kg induction (0, 2 and 6 wk) followed by maintenance therapy (every 8 wk) offers an effective treatment option for patients with refractory distal UC.\n...\nThe efficacy of MMX mesalazine as maintenance therapy was examined in a more recent multicenter study[37].\nPatients with UC were randomized to receive MMX mesalazine 2.4 g/d once daily, or delayed-release (EUDRAGIT S-coated) mesalazine 2.4 g/d twice daily, administered in a double-dummy fashion for 12 mo.\n...\nThe data from this study indicate that MMX mesalazine 2.4 g/d once daily and delayed-release mesalazine 2.4 g/d twice daily are similarly tolerated and effective in the maintenance of remission of distal UC.\n...\nBudesonide enema is both effective and safe for the treatment of active distal UC[49].\n...\nTrials on adalimumab in UC are ongoing.", "date": "2010-04-06", "last_updated": "2026-08-17"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Second European evidence-based consensus on the ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/6/10/991/504059", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "A double-blind comparison of oral versus rectal mesalamine versus combination therapy in the treatment of distal ulcerative colitis\n...\nCombined oral and enema treatment with Pentasa (mesalazine) is superior to oral therapy alone in patients with extensive mild/moderate active ulcerative colitis: a randomised, double blind, placebo controlled study\n...\nClinical trial: randomized-controlled clinical study comparing the efficacy and safety of a low-volume vs. a high-volume mesalazine foam in active distal ulcerative colitis", "date": "2012-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Budesonide for the Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4989907/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Budesonide is effective in inducing remission in a subset of patients with mild-moderate UC.\nNevertheless, the current evidence suggests inferiority of oral budesonide to 5-aminosalisylates (5-ASA) and systemic steroids, whereas rectal applications are comparable to other rectal steroid preparations but still inferior to rectal 5-ASA.\n...\nSubsequently, two larger, identically designed phase III clinical trials, CORE I and CORE II were conducted.^41,42^ In CORE I, 509 patients with active mild-moderate UC were randomized to four arms; budesonide-MMX 9mg/day, budesonide-MMX 6mg/day, mesalamine 2.4 g/day (Asacol^®^ Warner Chilcott plc.\nDublin, Ireland), or placebo.\nThe primary outcome was combined clinical and endoscopic remission at 8 weeks, which was achieved in 17.9%, 13.2%, and 12.1% of patients treated with budesonide-MMX 9mg, budesonide-MMX 6mg, and mesalamine, respectively, compared to 7.4% in the placebo group.\n...\nThe rates of combined clinical and endoscopic remission were 17.4%, 8.3%, 12.6%, and 4.5% for the budesonide-MMX 9mg, budesonide-MMX 6mg, Entocort, and placebo groups, respectively.\nThe therapeutic advantage compared to placebo was only significant for budesonide-MMX 9 mg (p-value 0.005).\n...\nA pooled analysis of data from CORE I, CORE II, and the study by Rubin *et al*, was recently presented in the updated Cochrane review.^45^ Budesonide-MMX was noted to be significantly superior to placebo for inducing combined clinical and endoscopic remission, 15% vs. 7%, respectively [RR 2.25, 95% CI 1.50 to 3.39.\n...\n|**Sandborn *et al* [2015]** ^50^ **2 identical MC, DB, R trials**|Proctitis or proctosigmoiditis|546|Foam 2mg/25ml BID for 2 weeks, then daily for 4 weeks|Placebo|6 weeks|Combined clinical and endoscopic remission Main results from combined trials: primary outcome was achieved in 41.2% in budesonide vs. 24% in placebo [p <0.001]|\n|**Naganuma *et al* [2015]** ^51^ **MC, DB, R**|Proctitis or proctosigmoiditis|165|Foam 2mg/25ml BID|Budesonide foam 2mg/25ml QD Placebo|6 weeks|Complete mucosal healing [endoscopic subscore 0] Main results: complete mucosal healing in BID budesonide was 46.4% compared to 23.6% for QD [p=0.009], and 5.6% for placebo [p<0.0001].\nRates of clinical remission and rates of endoscopic subscore ≤1 were similar in BID and QD dosing groups|\n...\nThe standard dose in the commercially available rectal budesonide preparations is 2 mg/application, which is the dose utilized in the majority of the clinical trials evaluating rectal budesonide in UC.\n...\n- Budesonide rectal foam (Uceris^®^) received FDA approval on October 7, 2014 for the use in patients with active mild-moderate distal UC (up to 40 cm from the anal verge).\nThe recommended dosage is 2 mg twice daily for 2 weeks, then 2 mg daily for 4 weeks.^17^\n...\nThe role of rectal budesonide is managing distal UC is more established with comparable efficacy to other rectal steroid preparations but yet suggested inferiority to rectal 5-ASA preparations.\nBased on the currently available data, budesonide (oral and rectal preparations) has no role in the maintenance of remission for UC patients.", "date": "2016-06-16", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "7890a267-757b-4c41-9a90-7699d0097468"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:54+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q004", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication MT501, Mirador Therapeutics, Crohn's Disease, Moderately to Severely Active by CDAI and SES-CD", "anchors": ["MT501"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q004-1", "query": "MT501 mechanism of action mechanistic class", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "MT501 mechanism of action mechanistic class", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1270.8, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "MT-501 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/477311c534e0406d8c27a73fc39e6074", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Drug Type Small molecule drug|\n...\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|United States|Mirador Therapeutics, Inc.|26 Jun 2025|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-26"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Ozmosi | MT-501 Drug Profile", "url": "https://pryzm.ozmosi.com/product/33772", "domain": "pryzm.ozmosi.com", "snippet": "Mechanisms of Action: Unknown\nNovel Mechanism: No\nModality: Small Molecule", "date": "2025-06-19", "last_updated": "2025-07-05"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Shimano disc brake MT501/MT520 | buy online - Fitstore24.com", "url": "https://www.fitstore24.com/en/shimano-disc-set-hr-blmt501-brmt5201", "domain": "fitstore24.com", "snippet": "The Shimano disc set HR BLMT501/BRMT520 impresses with SERVO WAVE performance for highest MTB demands.\nThe hydraulic disc brake with its robust 4-piston design ensures riding fun and a safe ride.\n...\nThe Servo Wave brake lever absorbs a lot of brake travel at the beginning of its movement so that the pads can be guided to the brake disc with little lever travel.\nAfter the brake pad has been applied, the power transmission factor increases rapidly.\nThe greater part of the lever travel is thus still available to increase the braking force and to dose it more precisely.\n...\n|Brake type|Hydraulic disc brake|\n...\n|Servo Wave Mechanism|✔|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-30"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "MT501", "url": "https://javadoc.io/doc/com.prowidesoftware/pw-swift-core/SRU2022-9.3.6/com/prowidesoftware/swift/model/mt/mt5xx/MT501.html", "domain": "javadoc.io", "snippet": "SRU2023-10.1.18\n...\nCurrent version\nSRU2022-9.3.6", "date": "2023-10-01", "last_updated": "2025-05-13"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "GF-MT501 Q&A", "url": "https://manuals.answerbase.com/Topic/GF-MT501/Contributors/12660864", "domain": "manuals.answerbase.com", "snippet": "## Browse by Topic\n- User Manual x 21654\n...\n- LED x 1010", "date": "2025-06-14", "last_updated": "2025-06-14"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "HT-MT500/MT501", "url": "https://www.sony.com/electronics/support/res/manuals/C591/C5911008M.pdf", "domain": "sony.com", "snippet": "Rúdhangsugárzó \n...\nKommunikációs rendszer\n3.0-s verziójú vezeték nélküli ", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-04-05"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Maneta de freno de disco hidráulico MTB Shimano MT501", "url": "https://www.cycletyres.es/products/levier-de-frein-shimano-mt501", "domain": "cycletyres.es", "snippet": "## Maneta de freno de disco hidráulico Shimano MT501\nLa\n**maneta de freno Shimano MT501** para **frenos de disco hidráulicos** ha sido diseñada para mntarse en bicicletas urbanas, e-bikes, así como bicicletas de montaña, como pieza nueva o como repuesto en caso de rotura.\nEsta maneta de freno Shimano MT501 corresponde a un\n**sistema de frenos hidráulicos de MTB,** y forma parte de la nueva gama CUES de Shimano.", "date": "2024-01-29", "last_updated": "2025-01-25"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Shimano MT501 + MT520 - Mantel", "url": "https://www.mantel.com/en/shimano-mt501-mt520-disc-brake", "domain": "mantel.com", "snippet": "|Number of pistons in caliper|4 Pistons|\n...\nWith a strong 4-piston design, the Shimano MT501 + MT520 hydraulic disc brakes promote the cyclist's confidence and enjoyment.\n...\nThis means that the pistons move towards the rotors faster at the beginning of the braking movement than when the pads are closer to the rotors.", "date": "2021-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-10-04"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Shimano MT501/520 4-Kolben HR 1700mm", "url": "https://www.rabe-bike.de/en/shimano-mt501-520-4-kolben-hr-1700mm", "domain": "rabe-bike.de", "snippet": "The hydraulic disc brake with four-piston caliper offers a wide range of applications from trekking to cross country and trail riding.\n...\nWith the two-finger lever, which already features Shimano's Servo Wave technology, the MT501/MT520 offers very well controllable braking performance.\nFour counter-rotating pistons with different diameters result in a large contact surface and uniform development of the braking force.\n...\n|Piston number|4|\n|Brake System|hydraulic|", "date": "2025-07-11", "last_updated": "2025-07-20"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Tested: Shimano BL-MT501/BR-MT520 - BikeMag", "url": "https://www.bikemag.com/mountain-bike-gear/tested-shimano-bl-mt501-br-mt520-145-per-brake", "domain": "bikemag.com", "snippet": "The BL-MT501/BR-MT520 brakes are sold as a full brake with the BR MT520 caliper and the BL MT501 lever.\nAfter some research, it was confirmed by Shimano that the MT501 lever is identical to the M6000 lever, better known as “Deore,” which we’ve seen helping bring the price down on plenty of high-end bikes.", "date": "2019-12-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}], "raw": {"id": "5f6d08ac-72f4-4fee-ab79-624a27bb2b27"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:55+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q004", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication MT501, Mirador Therapeutics, Crohn's Disease, Moderately to Severely Active by CDAI and SES-CD", "anchors": ["MT501"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q004-2", "query": "MT501 Crohn's disease target pathway", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "MT501 Crohn's disease target pathway", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 9691.3, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "MT-501 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/477311c534e0406d8c27a73fc39e6074", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Active Indication Colitis, UlcerativeCrohn Disease|\n...\n|Crohn Disease|Phase 2|United States|Mirador Therapeutics, Inc.|26 Jun 2025|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-26"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "MT-501 for Inflammatory Bowel Disease (ASCEND-IBD Trial)", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-2-inflammatory-bowel-diseases-5-2025-6303d", "domain": "withpower.com", "snippet": "This trial tests a new treatment, MT-501, for individuals with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), specifically Crohn's Disease or Ulcerative Colitis.\n...\nUnlike traditional treatments, which often rely on suppressing the immune system, MT-501 targets specific inflammatory pathways, potentially reducing side effects and increasing effectiveness.\nThis precision in targeting inflammation may lead to faster symptom relief and improved long-term outcomes for patients.\n...\nStudies have shown that MT-501 may benefit people with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), including Crohn's Disease and Ulcerative Colitis.\nEarly findings suggest that MT-501 can reduce gut swelling, potentially leading to fewer symptoms.\nResearch indicates that the treatment targets specific body parts responsible for this swelling, possibly offering better disease control than some current treatments.\n...\nIt aims to determine the safety and effectiveness of this investigational drug in adults with either Crohn's Disease or Ulcerative Colitis that is moderately to severely active.", "date": "2021-10-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for luminal and perianal Crohn's disease management - Luisa Bertin, Martina Crepaldi, Miriana Zanconato, Greta Lorenzon, Daria Maniero, Caterina de Barba, Erica Bonazzi, Sonia Facchin, Marco Scarpa, Cesare Ruffolo, Imerio Angriman, Andrea Buda, Fabiana Zingone, Brigida Barberio, Edoardo Vincenzo Savarino, 2024", "url": "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/17562848241303651", "domain": "journals.sagepub.com", "snippet": "Some of these drugs target well-known pathways, like JAK inhibitors and IL-23 blockers, while others focus on newer areas, such as specific receptors or molecules involved in inflammation.\n...\nMirikizumab, a humanized IgG4 monoclonal antibody targeting the p19 component of IL-23, offers both IV and SC administration options.\n...\nUpon activation, this initiates a cascade of signaling events that regulate various biological processes, including lymphocyte migration, endothelial cell permeability, angiogenesis, cell differentiation, proliferation, survival, and apoptosis.^32^ S1P receptors regulate the influx of leukocytes from primary and secondary lymphoid organs into the intestinal wall, thereby contributing to the maintenance of intestinal inflammation.\n...\n^43^ Blocking the activation of this pathway halts the activity of several chemokines involved in mediating inflammation.\nSeveral pan-JAK inhibitors and selective JAK inhibitors, including the newer TYK2 inhibitors, are in various stages of development for IBD.\n...\n### LMT503\nLMT503 is a small molecule designed to treat IBD by modulating macrophage polarization.^146^ Macrophages play a crucial role in immune response and tissue homeostasis by switching between pro-inflammatory (M1) and anti-inflammatory (M2) states.\nLMT503 reprograms macrophage metabolism, shifting them from an M1-like inflammatory state to an M2-like anti-inflammatory state.\nIn vitro studies showed that LMT503 suppresses M1 markers (e.g., iNOS) and enhances M2 markers (e.g., Arg-1).", "date": "2024-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "MT-501 - AssetMemo", "url": "https://assetmemo.bio/drug/mt-501", "domain": "assetmemo.bio", "snippet": "Phase 1 / first-in-human studies establish safety, tolerability, and dosing — they are not designed to demonstrate efficacy, so no evidence signal is assigned yet.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-23"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Phase 2 Study of MT-501 and MT-201 for Active Crohn's Disease or ...", "url": "https://aidyhq.com/clinical-trials/nct07113522", "domain": "aidyhq.com", "snippet": "This Phase 2 platform study is assessing the safety, activity, drug levels, and biological effects of MT-501 and MT-201 in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis.\n...\n### What participants may receive or do\n- MT-501: Participants assigned to either the Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis MT-501 group receive MT-501 tablets.\n...\nThis is a Phase 2, multicenter platform study with three experimental groups: MT-501 tablets for Crohn’s disease, MT-501 tablets for ulcerative colitis, and intravenous MT-201 with standard of care for Crohn’s disease.\n...\nThis is a Phase 2, multicenter, platform study in adult participants with IBD (moderately to severely active Crohn's Disease or Ulcerative Colitis).\nThe primary goal of this study is to assess the safety and efficacy of multiple investigational drugs.\nThe purpose of this platform study is to assess the safety, efficacy, pharmacokinetics (PK), and pharmacodynamics (PD) to evaluate multiple oral or parenteral experimental therapies for moderately to severely active Crohn's Disease or Ulcerative Colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "MT-501 for Safety in Healthy Subjects · Info for Participants", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-1-healthy-subjects-hs-11-2024-26a90", "domain": "withpower.com", "snippet": "The main goal of this research is to determine if MT-501 is safe and effective for people with moderate to severe forms of these digestive ...", "date": "2021-10-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "A Phase 2 Study to Evaluate Therapies for Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://bridgemd.health/study/NCT07113522", "domain": "bridgemd.health", "snippet": "MT-501 is an investigational medicine, given as a pill taken by mouth, being studied as a potential treatment for inflammatory bowel disease (ibd).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-06"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohn's Disease Enteritis: Pathophysiological Mechanisms and ...", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/sji.70129", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "The progression of the disease is driven by complex cytokine and chemokine networks (e.g., TNF-α, IL-6, IL-17), epithelial barrier dysfunction and microbial dysbiosis, all of which contribute to chronic inflammation and mucosal damage.\n...\nUnlike monoclonal antibodies that target single cytokines, JAK inhibitors target the intracellular JAK–STAT signalling pathways—specifically JAK1—thereby modulating the downstream signalling of multiple pro-inflammatory cytokines simultaneously, including IL-6, IFN-γ and IL-23 [26, 27].\nThis mechanistic ability to broadly suppress inflammatory signal transduction offers a potent alternative for patients with refractory disease [27-29].\nWhile other agents in this class, such as filgotinib, have shown variable results in clinical trials depending on patient heterogeneity and trial design, the success of JAK1 inhibition highlights the importance of intracellular signalling nodes as therapeutic targets in CD [25, 30].\n...\nCrohn's disease (CD) enteritis, a primary manifestation of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) affecting the small intestine, results from dysregulated interactions among intestinal epithelial cells (IECs), the mucosal immune system and the gut microbiota, leading to chronic, transmural inflammation (Figure 1) [31].\n...\nDuring the inflammatory flares characteristic of CD, lymphotoxin beta receptor (LTβR) signalling in IECs induces the production of chemokines such as CXCL1 and CXCL2, which promote neutrophil recruitment to sites of inflammation [36].\n...\nIncreased epithelial permeability permits microbial translocation into the lamina propria, initiating innate immune activation.\nMacrophages polarize towards a pro-inflammatory M1 phenotype, producing TNF-α, IL-1β and IL-6 via NF-κB and MAPK signalling pathways, whereas M2 macrophages secrete IL-10 and PGE2 to promote tissue repair and resolution.\n...\nThe canonical Th1 response is driven by the cytokine IL-12, which signals through the STAT4 transcription factor to induce the master regulator T-bet, leading to the secretion of interferon-gamma (IFN-γ) [52, 53].\nIn contrast, the Th17 phenotype is promoted by cytokines such as IL-6 and TGF-β for initial differentiation and is critically maintained by IL-23, which signals via STAT3 to induce the master regulator RORγt.\n...\nCytokine and chemokine networks drive intestinal inflammation in Crohn's Disease (CD), mediating mucosal damage and immune cell recruitment [84, 85].\nPro-inflammatory mediators, including interleukin-1β (IL-1β), tumour necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), interleukin-6 (IL-6) and interleukin-17 (IL-17), amplify inflammatory cascades and disrupt homeostasis.\nIL-1β, produced by monocytes, induces fever and increases tight junction (TJ) permeability via nuclear factor-kappa B (NF-κB) activation and claudin-3 downregulation, promoting barrier dysfunction and immune cell infiltration [86, 87].\n...\nTherapies targeting cytokines, such as TNF-α blockade, IL-6 trans-signalling inhibitors (e.g., olamkicept) and JAK inhibitors (e.g., tofacitinib), reduce inflammation and restore mucosal integrity in CD [85, 106, 107].\nThe interplay among IL-1β, TNF-α, IL-6, IL-17 and JAK/STAT and NF-κB pathways underscores their role in CD and their therapeutic potential for the disease.\n...\n|TNF-α|Macrophages, monocytes, Th1 cells, ILCs|TNFR1, TNFR2; NF-κB, MAPK and apoptosis signalling|Drives a broad pro-inflammatory cascade; induces epithelial cell apoptosis and disrupts tight junctions, increasing permeability; promotes immune cell recruitment.|Neutralizing antibodies (e.g., infliximab, adalimumab) directly block TNF-α activity.|[93, 94]|\n|IL-12|Dendritic cells, macrophages|IL-12R (IL-12Rβ1/IL-12Rβ2); JAK2/TYK2, STAT4|Crucial for the differentiation of naïve T cells into Th1 cells; promotes IFN-γ production, driving cell-mediated cytotoxicity and macrophage activation.|p40 subunit inhibition (e.g., ustekinumab) blocks both IL-12 and IL-23 signalling.|[92, 95]|\n|IFN-γ|Th1 cells, NK cells, ILC1s|IFNGR1/IFNGR2; JAK1/JAK2, STAT1|Signature Th1 cytokine; activates macrophages, upregulates MHC expression and impairs intestinal epithelial barrier function by increasing apoptosis and altering tight junction proteins.|Not a direct target, but production is reduced by anti-IL-12/23 and JAK inhibitors.|[96, 97]|\n...\nConventional pharmacological therapies, comprising corticosteroids and immunosuppressants, remain foundational for inducing and maintaining remission [212].\n...\nUstekinumab targets the p40 subunit shared by both IL-12 and IL-23.\nThis dual inhibition is critical because it simultaneously disrupts two key pathogenic axes in CD: the IL-12/STAT4 pathway, which drives Th1 cell differentiation and IFN-γ production and the IL-23/STAT3 pathway, which is essential for the stabilization and expansion of pathogenic Th17 cells [233-237].\n...\nJanus kinase (JAK) inhibitors are small molecules that interfere with the intracellular JAK–STAT signalling pathway, which is utilized by numerous pro-inflammatory cytokine receptors.\nUpadacitinib, a selective JAK1 inhibitor, preferentially blocks signalling from key cytokines implicated in CD, such as IL-6, IL-23 and IFN-γ.", "date": "2026-06-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancements in Targeted Therapies for the Management of ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11226209/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "These medicines include biologics like anti-TNF agents, interleukin inhibitors, and small molecule inhibitors; they work by altering the modulation of immune responses and reducing inflammation.\n...\nNAT1 further binds to PPAR-gamma (peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma), a nuclear hormone receptor, brings its conformational change, and leads its migration from the cytoplasm to the nucleus where it starts the domino effect of turning down the inflammatory casket [14].\n...\nDRIP interacts with PPAR-gamma to form protein dimers.\nThe consumption of PPAR-gamma in this process downregulates the PPAR-gamma response element (PPRE) down the line that ultimately sends the signal to switch off the inflammatory casket primarily by downregulating the nuclear factor kappa B (NF-kappaB), activated protein kinase (MAPK), ceasing COX-2 activity.\nThis manifests as reduced cytokines and prostaglandin availability, which are vitals for the inflammation [11,14].\n...\nThe main mechanism of corticosteroids in the treatment and prevention of CD remission is to put a halt to the transcription of interleukin and arachidonic acid metabolism, thus preventing the indispensable steps in the casket of inflammation and triggering lymphocyte apoptosis in the gut [13].\n...\nThe goal of immunosuppressive therapy for the treatment of CD is to decrease the gut inflammatory process.\nDue to the pathophysiology of CD, a dysregulated, overactive immune response with an excess of pro-inflammatory cytokines has led to the relative importance of immunosuppressive therapy.\nThese drugs work by reducing the function of immune cells.\n...\nThe mechanism of action of methotrexate is to inhibit dihydrofolate reductase.\nDihydrofolate reductase plays a pivotal role in the metabolism of folic acid, thus ultimately inhibiting the production of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA), ribonucleic acid (RNA), and protein [15,19].\n...\nThis cyclosporine is an immunomodulator that targets T-cell activation by halting cytokine production.\nIt inhibits calcineurin.\n...\nTherefore, TNF therapy provides relief in such patients by multiple mechanisms.\nNullification of TNF-α, reverse signaling, apoptosis, and cytotoxicity are the major mechanisms employed by it.\n...\nThey bind TNF-α, blocking further upregulation of pro-inflammatory molecules and blocking the production of cytokines [16,21-22].\n...\nAnti-integrin agents are similar to their working TNF-α inhibitors in a way that it is specifically designed to target the molecules that result in triggering the inflammatory casket in the intestinal mucosa.\nHowever, unlike TNF-α inhibitors, its target is integrin responsible for leukocyte trafficking [15,16].\n...\nThe mechanism of action of this drug is directly on α4β7 integrin, without disturbing α4β1 integrin [15,16].\n...\n|Natalizumab|Alpha-4 integrin|Humanized monoclonal antibody against the alpha-4 subunit of integrin molecules, thereby blocking adhesion and migration of leukocytes in the gut.|Moderate to severe|Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), skin rash, gastrointestinal symptoms including nausea and gastroenteritis [18,19,21].|\n|Risankizumab|Interleukin-23|Target inhibition of IL-23|Moderate to severe|Hypersensitivity reactions, upper respiratory tract infection, inflammation, nasopharyngitis [27].|\n...\n|Vedolizumab|α4β7 integrin|Humanized monoclonal antibody that binds to the α4β7 integrin and blocks the interaction of α4β7 integrin with mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule-1 (MAdCAM-1)|Moderate to severe|Immunologic: antibody development, headaches, arthralgias, nasopharyngitis, pruritis, and gastrointestinal including nausea [30].|\n...\nRisankizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that represents the first and only targeted selective inhibitor of interleukin (IL)-23, targeting the p19 subunit, thereby modulating the inflammatory cascade implicated in CD.\nRizankizumab disrupts IL-23-driven immune response, thereby alleviating intestinal inflammation, reducing disease activity, and improving clinical outcomes in patients with CD [32].\n...\nUpadacitinib is an inhibitor of the JAK-1 enzyme, selectively blocking it, and thereby interfering with JAK-STAT signaling pathways that are linked to the immune responses, which play a role in the pathogenesis of CD.\nBy preventing the synthesis of inflammatory cytokines, inflammation diminishes while the gastrointestinal immune system recovers homeostasis, eventually resulting in an improvement of the condition and better clinical results for CD patients [39].\n...\nEfforts are underway to create novel drugs targeting pathways, including α4β7 integrin, JAK, and IL-12/IL-23.\nAs an example, the fully human monoclonal antibody ustekinumab (UST) blocks the interaction between the common receptor IL-12Rβ1 and the IL-12/IL-23 p40 subunit.", "date": "2024-06-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Janus Kinase Antagonists and Other Novel Small Molecules for the Treatment of Crohn's Disease - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28838419/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Treatments for Crohn's disease continue to evolve from the traditional biologics to novel small molecules, with targeted mechanisms directed toward pathways that are dysregulated in Crohn's disease.\nThere are multiple emerging mechanisms of action, including Janus kinase inhibition, Smad7 inhibition, and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators, that are administered as oral medications, and small molecules represent the next generation of therapies for Crohn's disease.", "date": "2017-09-04", "last_updated": "2025-04-04"}], "raw": {"id": "15e40bac-a11a-41db-be6c-e3d8d0ef709c"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:04+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q005", "objective": "SOR102 safety findings and adverse events in Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["SOR102", "SOR102-101"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q005-1", "query": "SOR102 Crohn's disease safety adverse events", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "SOR102 Crohn's disease safety adverse events", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 653.3, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Safely targeting combination TNF and IL-23 using a gut-restricted ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13263963/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Overall, SOR102 was well tolerated in both healthy participants and patients with UC, with a low incidence of treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs), most of which were mild to moderate in severity.\nIn part 1, TEAEs were reported in 3/24 (13%) participants receiving SOR102 compared with 2/8 (25%) receiving placebo.\nNo TEAEs were observed in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in 7/16 (44%) patients treated with SOR102 and 3/6 (50%) patients receiving placebo.\nOnly 1 severe TEAE was reported, a flare of UC requiring hospitalization, which was not considered related to the study drug.\nImportantly, no clinically meaningful effects of SOR102 were observed on electrocardiograms, vital signs, physical examination findings, or safety laboratory parameters at any dose or dosing frequency evaluated.\nThis trial also critically demonstrated proof-of-concept for the therapeutic target and delivery mechanism: from a PK perspective, no detectable serum or urine concentrations of SOR102, SOR102-TNF, or SOR102-IL-23 were identified across study parts, suggesting minimal systemic drug exposure.\n...\nIn this early-phase study, anti-drug antibodies (ADAs) were detected in 2/8 (25%) subjects in part 2 and 6/16 (38%) patients in part 3.\nNotably, some ADA positivity was present at baseline, and most treatment-emergent ADA titers were low.\nDespite no apparent short-term impact, the relatively high ADA rates warrant further evaluation for potential effects on long-term efficacy, potential neutralization of drug effects, durability of response, and potential for inducing hypersensitivity reactions in later phase trials.\n...\nIn summary, SOR102 demonstrated a favorable safety and tolerability profile across early-phase evaluation, with no signal of systemic toxicity or immunogenicity.\n...\nTo date, no major safety concerns have emerged; however, immunogenicity and the long-term consequences of dual-pathway immunosuppression will require careful evaluation in ongoing and future studies (11).", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "PHASE 1B STUDY OF SOR102, A NOVEL, ORALLY ...", "url": "https://eposters.ddw.org/ddw/2025/ddw-2025/4155984/vipul.jairath.phase.1b.study.of.sor102.a.novel.orally.delivered.bispecific.html?f=menu=6*browseby=8*sortby=2*ce_id=2855*ot_id=31400*marker=5698", "domain": "eposters.ddw.org", "snippet": "TEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1).\nMost were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\nOne serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm.\n...\nThere were no significant differences in FC and CRP between the treatment groups.\n**Conclusion**: SOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\nEfficacy across multiple endpoints was observed in the SOR102 BID arm compared to the QD and placebo arms, despite the relatively short duration of treatment.\nThese results warrant further clinical development of SOR102.\n...\nTEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1).\nMost were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\n...\n**Conclusion**: SOR102 was safe and well tolerated.", "date": "2025-03-31", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral Administration of SOR102 Delivers Anti-TNF/IL-23 ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-Week-2025-Poster-v4.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "22 patients were randomized (ITT; Table \n1), 17 patients completed the study Per \nProtocol (QD n=6, BID n=5, Placebo n=6).\nReasons for discontinuation: worsening of \nUC (n=2), study schedule non-compliance \n(n=2) and consent withdrawal (n=1).\n...\nNo clinically significant changes in safety\nlaboratory \nparameters, \ndose-limiting \ntoxicities, \nor \nevents \nsuggestive \nof \npronounced immunosuppression\n•\nAll but one TEAE were mild to moderate\n•\n1 severe and serious TEAE of worsening \nUC requiring hospitalization was not \nconsidered related to treatment.\nThis\nTEAE occurred during follow-up in a \nSOR102 BID patient\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\n...\nSOR102 BID demonstrated strong and consistent activity across multiple clinical and tissue PD endpoints in patients with UC.\n...\nHigh and sustained levels of active monomers in feces confirmed\nSOR102 is efficiently cleaved by trypsin and cytokine-binding activity is maintained after oral dosing in patients with UC.\n...\n4 QD and 2 BID patients developed ADA responses after SOR102 dosing (anti-SOR102-TNF only, n=1; anti-SOR102-IL-23 only, n=2; ADA\nagainst both monomers n=3).\nMost titers were low (≤1:240) and not associated with safety or clinical response5", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-02-25"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Journal Scan_Jan 2026", "url": "https://www.ibdenc.org/journalscan-jan2026", "domain": "ibdenc.org", "snippet": "A novel oral bispecific antibody (SOR102) targeting TNF and IL-23 shows a favourable safety profile with encouraging early efficacy in ulcerative colitis.\n...\nSOR102 is an oral bispecific antibody targeting TNF and IL-23p19 developed for ulcer ative colitis.\nThis phase 1, double blind, placebo-controlled trial evaluated its safety, pharmacokinetics, and preliminary efficacy in healthy participants and patients with active ulcerative colitis.\nAcross single and multiple ascending doses, SOR102 was generally well tolerated, with mostly mild to moderate adverse events and no clinically significant safety signals.\nIn patients with ulcerative colitis treated for 42 days, clinical responses were more frequent with SOR102 than placebo, particularly with twice-daily dosing.\nSymptomatic remission was achieved in over half of patients receiving twice-daily SOR102, while no placebo-treated patients reached remission.\nAlthough one severe disease exacerbation occurred, it was not considered treatment related.\nOverall, the favourable safety profile and early signs of clinical bene fit support further clinical development of SOR102 as a potential oral therapy for ulcerative colitis.\n...\nNo significant differences were observed in corticosteroid-free remission, clinical response, hospitalization, surgery, adverse events, or loss of response.\nOverall, the findings suggest that continuing 5-ASA alongside advanced therapies provides no clear therapeutic benefit or safety advantage, supporting consideration of 5-ASA dis continuation in patients with ulcerative colitis who have escalated to biologic or advanced treatments.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-04"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR-102 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c4ffffc6839e4416b41bd1cd9d28f2d9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Active Indication Colitis, UlcerativeCrohn Disease|\n...\nSOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3).\n...\nIn part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\nNo TEAEs occurred in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nThe favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\nKey Highlights:\n...\n• Safety and Tolerability: SOR102 was dosed orally and was safe and well-tolerated in both healthy participants and UC patients.\nNo unexpected adverse events were observed.", "date": "2025-01-17", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Sorriso Pharmaceuticals, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/91d3bdda199f847ec2b338400a1a78ff", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "SOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3).\n...\nIn part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\nNo TEAEs occurred in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nThree (43%) patients in the once-daily group, five (56%) in the twice-daily group, and none in the placebo group achieved symptomatic remission by day 42.\n...\nThe favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\nKey Highlights:\n...\n• Safety and Tolerability: SOR102 was dosed orally and was safe and well-tolerated in both healthy participants and UC patients.\nNo unexpected adverse events were observed.\n...\n|SOR-102 ( IL-23 x IL-23p19 x TNF-α )|Colitis, Ulcerative More|Phase 2|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Anti-tumor necrosis factor-like ligand 1A and bispecific antibod - Apollo", "url": "https://api.repository.cam.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/f38d88f6-3633-401f-b149-325481457cca/content", "domain": "api.repository.cam.ac.uk", "snippet": "Treatment-emergent\nadverse events\noccurred in 48% of\npatients during induction\nand 59% during\nmaintenance with\noverall incidences\nsimilar to placebo; the\nmost common were\nnausea, urinary tract\ninfection, anemia, and\n...\nTulisokibart was well\ntolerated.\n46% reported\nmild/moderate\ninfections; two serious\ninfections were\nunrelated and resolved.\nAt week 50, infection\noccurred in 63%\n(100 mg) and 61%\n(250 mg) groups, none\nserious.\nNo dose-\n...\nIn addition, another molecule, SOR102, consists of V900, a humanized llama- derived domain antibody targeting the p19 subunit of\nIL-23.\nIt is also engineered for resistance to intestinal proteases, fused with V565, a previously characterized domain antibody against TNF-α.\nThe two domains are linked via a central protease-cleavable linker, forming a single molecule designed for oral administration.\nThis molecule is currently being tested in a phase I trial for both healthy volunteers and patients with UC.\nThe phase I trial is seeking to assess safety, tolerability, and pharmacokinetics using ascending doses.\nInitial findings suggest that SOR102 has a manageable safety profile and favorable pharmacodynamics, though more data are expected soon [12].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-20"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Sorriso Pharmaceuticals Presents Positive SOR102 Phase ...", "url": "https://magnify.cnsi.ucla.edu/2025/02/27/february-27-2025-sorriso-pharmaceuticals-presents-positive-sor102-phase-1b-ulcerative-colitis-data-at-ecco-conference/", "domain": "magnify.cnsi.ucla.edu", "snippet": "𝐊𝐞𝐲 𝐇𝐢𝐠𝐡𝐥𝐢𝐠𝐡𝐭𝐬 𝐨𝐟 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐏𝐡𝐚𝐬𝐞 𝟏𝐛 𝐃𝐚𝐭𝐚:\n- 𝐒𝐚𝐟𝐞𝐭𝐲 𝐚𝐧𝐝 𝐓𝐨𝐥𝐞𝐫𝐚𝐛𝐢𝐥𝐢𝐭𝐲: SOR102 exhibited a favorable safety profile, with adverse events predominantly mild to moderate in severity; no new safety signals were observed.", "date": "2025-02-27", "last_updated": "2026-07-11"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "PowerPoint-Präsentation", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-2024-presentation.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "SOR102-101 (NCT06080048) is a Phase 1, first in human study that consists of 3 parts.\nParts 1 and 2 enrolled healthy subjects (HS) age 18-55 years and evaluated single ascending (SAD) and multiple doses (MD) of SOR102, respectively.\n...\nSOR102 is Safe and Well-Tolerated\nNo safety or tolerability concerns noted at single doses up to 3,645mg or multiple doses of 1,215mg BID\n-No serious adverse events\n-No clinically significant laboratory, ECG, vitals, or physical exam findings\n-No subjects discontinued \nPart 1: One TEAE considered related to study drug (Cohort #1)\nPart 2: No adverse events\nCohort / Dose\nEvent\nDuration\nSeverity\nCausality\nC1 / 135mg\nDiarrhea\n~17 hours\nMild\nProbably related\nC1 / 135mg\nDysmenorrhea\n~2.5 days\nModerate\nNot related\nC1 / 135mg\nFlatulence\n~12 hours\nMild\nNot related\nC2 / Placebo\nHeadache\n~4 hours\nMild\nNot related\nC2 / Placebo\nCatheter site pain\n~5 hours\nModerate\nNot related\nC2 / 405mg\nDysmenorrhea\n~12 hours\nModerate\nNot related\nTEAE: treatment-emergent adverse event\n...\nSystemic Exposure Of SOR102 And Its Monomers Was Not \nObserved; Low-Level ADA Signal in 3 of 40 Samples Tested \n...\nNo detectable levels of SOR102 or monomers in serum in Parts 1 and 2\n...\nMost samples negative for ADA \n•\nLow level assay signal detected in:\n•\n2 of 20 samples for SOR102-IL-23 (1 pre-, 1 post-dose from a single subject)\n•\n...\n•\nSOR102 was well tolerated at all tested doses.\n•\nSystemic exposure of SOR102 and its monomers was not observed, supporting its gut selectivity.\n•\nLow levels of intact SOR102 and high levels of active SOR102 monomers in faeces confirm that SOR102 is efficiently cleaved and maintains activity after oral dosing, as designed.\n...\nLow-level ADA positive signal observed in 2 subjects\n•", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-05-11"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "gutflix.eu › search › dIMPROVEMENT IN INFLAMMATORY BIOMARKER LEVELS THROUGH WEEK 12 ...", "url": "https://gutflix.eu/search/d/53f9ac8e-743b-11ee-bede-0242ac140004", "domain": "gutflix.eu", "snippet": "Ninety patients (16%) developed acne during iJAK treatment: 69% were mild, with only 4 severe cases (4%).\nAcne occurred early (median onset 42 days, IQR 7-122), predominantly on the face (56%), trunk (13%) or both (31%).\n...\nAcne developed in 26%, 6% and 6% of upadacitinib, tofacitinib and filgotinib treated patients respectively (p=0.001).\nMultivariate analysis showed that acne development was associated with upadacitinib (OR 10.28;IC95%=4,7–22,6; p<0,001) and history of acne (OR 3.52; IC95%=1,5–8,3; p=0,004), while older age (>35 years, OR 0.31;\n...\nAcne occurred in a relevant proportion of iJAK-treated patients, was generally mild and manageable with dermatological care, and rarely required treatment discontinuation.\nRisk factors for iJAK-associated acne included use of upadacitinib (probably reflecting a greater affinity for JAK1) and history of acne, while older age and Crohn’s disease were protective.\nGastroenterologists should be aware of both likelihood and management of this specific adverse event when considering iJAK therapy.\n...\nAll 42 enrolled participants completed the study.\nSeven adverse events (AE) were observed, all occurring in Part 1.\nAE severity was mild or moderate.\nThere was one treatment-related AE (loose stools) of mild intensity and 17 hours duration occurring in the lowest dose cohort.\nSOR102 and its monomers were not detected in serum.\n...\nSOR102 was well tolerated at all tested doses.\nSystemic exposure of SOR102 and its monomers was not observed, supporting its gut selectivity.\n...\nIn the combined GUS group, serious adverse events (SAEs, n[%]: 35[15.9]) and serious infections (12[5.5]) were infrequent.\nMost infections were not serious, mild to moderate in severity, and most resolved without drug withdrawal.\nNo cases of active tuberculosis, opportunistic infections, anaphylactic/serum sickness reactions, major adverse cardiovascular event, or death occurred during the LTE.\n...\nDurable clinical and endoscopic efficacy was maintained over time through Week 144 of the LTE.\nThe safety profile of GUS was consistent with that of the approved indications.\nMost infections were not serious and did not result in discontinuation, while incidence rates of SAEs and serious infections were generally low.", "date": "2023-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "82aed1d6-c491-44c7-be43-4d26541a2ce2"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:05+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q004", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication MT501, Mirador Therapeutics, Crohn's Disease, Moderately to Severely Active by CDAI and SES-CD", "anchors": ["MT501"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q004-3", "query": "MT501 inflammatory bowel disease competitors same mechanism", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "MT501 inflammatory bowel disease competitors same mechanism", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 10013.5, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory bowel disease treatment: Mechanisms ...", "url": "https://www.spandidos-publications.com/10.3892/ijmm.2026.5961", "domain": "spandidos-publications.com", "snippet": "The mechanism of action primarily involves inhibiting natural killer cell activity, suppressing the cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways of arachidonic acid metabolism, thereby decreasing prostaglandin and leukotriene levels, inhibiting th\n...\nMethotrexate (MTX) is a dihydrofolate reductase\ninhibitor that exerts therapeutic effects by inhibiting cell\n...\nMonoclonal antibodies targeting TNF-α (infliximab\nand adalimumab) are used in IBD management (82), decreasing inflammation, promoting mucosal healing and decreasing the need for surgery (83).\nTNF-α serves a key role in intestinal inflammation during IBD, promoting the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines, increasing intestinal permeability, activating immune cells and \nfacilitating inflammatory cell migration (84).\nMechanistically, anti-TNF-α neutralizes soluble and transmembrane TNF-α, decreases NF-κB activation (85) and diminishes downstream cytokine cascades (IL-1β, IL-6 and IL-12/23) (86).\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody\ntargeting α4β7 integrin, exerting selective immunosuppression by\ninhibiting lymphocyte homing to the intestinal mucosa (93).\nα4β7 integrin serves a key role in the trafficking of gut-specific lymphocytes.\nIt binds the intestinal mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule-1 (MAdCAM-1), thereby mediating lymphocyte migration into intestinal tissue (94).\n...\nUstekinumab is a monoclonal antibody targeting the\np40 subunit common to IL-12 and IL-23, indicated for the treatment\nof moderate-to-severe CD and UC (100).\n...\nUstekinumab inhibits IL-12- and IL-23-mediated\nsignaling and cytokine cascades by blocking the binding of IL-12\nand IL-23 to the IL-12Rβ1 receptor protein on target cell surfaces\n(102).\n...\nJAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib and\nfilgotinib) block JAK-STAT signaling downstream of multiple\ncytokine receptors (including IL-2, IL-4, IL-6, IL-7 and IL-12/23),\nproducing bro\nad immunomodulation (121).\n...\nozelamod) bind S1P receptors on lymphocyte surfaces, functionally\ninhibiting lymphocyte migration out of lymphoid tissue.", "date": "2026-10-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacological Therapy in Inflammatory Bowel Diseases", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10537095/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "New therapeutic perspectives emerged with biological therapy, initially with anti-TNF, followed by anti-integrins and anti-interleukins.\nDespite the different mechanisms of action, there are similarities between the general rates of effectiveness.\nThese similar results were also evidenced in JAK inhibitors and S1p modulators, the last therapeutic classes approved for the treatment of IBD.\n...\nAction of salicylates and glucocorticoids in IBD.\n5-ASA acts on pro-inflammatory factors such as molecular adhesion, Natural Killer cell activation, intestinal permeability, nuclear factor kappa B activation, IL-1β, IL-2, Interferon gamma, prostaglandins, and leukotrienes.\n...\nAnti-integrins are molecules expressed by leukocytes that bind to adhesion molecules (CAM) and allow the trafficking of these cells [85].\n...\nTofacitinib was the first small molecule released for the treatment of UC, it is a molecule that acts on all JAK, mainly on JAK 1 and 3.\n...\nS1p modulators act as functional antagonists in lymphocytic receptors, inhibiting lymphocytes dependent on this receptor from leaving secondary lymph nodes for peripheral blood, reducing the circulating number of these cells.", "date": "2023-09-08", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Competitive Landscape Analysis 2026 | Eureka", "url": "https://eureka.patsnap.com/blog/life-science/inflammatory-bowel-diseases-global-competitive-landscape-report-2026/", "domain": "eureka.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Moreover, inflammatory Bowel Diseases (IBD) — covering ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD) — anchor 1,837 active drug programs, 7,198 patent filings since January 2023, and 1,244 ongoing Phase 2 and Phase 3 trials.\nNotably, 2025-2026 has produced an exceptional wave of approvals including Janssen’s oral peptide IL-23 antagonist Icotrokinra (March 2026), Lilly’s Mirikizumab (Omvoh) for Crohn’s expansion, and multiple Chinese-developed JAK and IL-23 mAb approvals.\nSpecifically, the late-stage IBD landscape is bifurcated between injectable biologics — AbbVie’s Skyrizi (Risankizumab) and Rinvoq (Upadacitinib), Janssen’s Stelara (Ustekinumab) and Tremfya (Guselkumab), Takeda’s Entyvio (Vedolizumab), Pfizer’s Etrasimod (Velsipity), and BMS’s Zeposia (Ozanimod) — and the emerging oral biologic and JAK1-selective wave.\nMoreover, sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) modulators have established a third oral mechanism alongside JAK and TYK2 inhibitors.\nHowever, the strategic outlook is increasingly defined by oral peptide IL-23 antagonists and selective JAK1 inhibitors rather than next-generation TNF or anti-integrin biologics.\n...\nFurthermore, microbiome therapeutics, mesenchymal stem cell therapy, and IL-23 / TL1A combination biology extend the long-term modality horizon.\n...\nAcross the 200 sampled IBD programs, small molecules dominate with 114 entries (oral JAK, TYK2, S1P modulators), followed by 31 monoclonal antibodies, 6 herbal medicines, 4 mesenchymal stem cell therapies, 3 synthetic peptides, 3 cyclic peptides, 3 bispecific antibodies, 3 probiotics, and 3 polymers.\n...\n|AbbVie|US|Tier 1 — Leader|Risankizumab (Skyrizi) + Upadacitinib (Rinvoq)|Both approved across UC and CD|\n|Janssen (J&J)|US/Belgium|Tier 1 — Leader|Ustekinumab + Guselkumab + Icotrokinra|Tremfya UC/CD approvals; Icotrokinra approval 2026-03|\n|Takeda|Japan|Tier 1 — Leader|Vedolizumab (Entyvio)|Anti-α4β7 mAb; gut-selective leader|\n|Eli Lilly|US|Tier 1 — Leader|Mirikizumab (Omvoh)|UC approval 2023; Crohn’s expansion 2025|", "date": "2026-05-14", "last_updated": "2026-09-21"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Understanding the therapeutic toolkit for inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://s29.q4cdn.com/642306244/files/doc_downloads/2025/Publications/Danese-2025.pdf", "domain": "s29.q4cdn.com", "snippet": "Subsequent advancements led to the development of new biologic agents with distinct mechanisms of action: vedolizumab, an anti-integrin α4β7 agent29,30; ustekinumab, which inhibits IL-12 and\nIL-2331,32; and risankizumab and mirikizumab, which specifically target\n...\nand tailored lifestyle modifications to holistically address individual patient needs.\n...\naminosalicylates, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, biologic agents \n(anti-tumour necrosis factor agents, anti-integrins, anti-interleukins),\nand small molecules such as JAK inhibitors and S1P modulators.\n• Emerging therapies for IBD, including anti-IL-23, JAK inhibitors, TL1A \ninhibitors and RIPK1 inhibitors, offer targeted approaches to mitigate inflammation, expanding options for patients with IBD.\n...\nCyclosporine \nA and tacrolimus are calcineurin inhibitors that work by blocking the nuclear translocation of the transcription factor NFAT, thereby\n­preventing the initiation of T cell cytokine transcription76.\n...\nMany \nIBD treatments focus on inhibiting pro-inflammatory cytokines, which is particularly evident with anti-tumour necrosis factor (TNF) agents (such as infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab pegol and golimumab), which are approved, and investigational therapies (such as V565 and tulinercept).\nOther therapies target specific interleukins, including: lutikizumab (targets IL-1α/β);\naldesleukin and efavaleukin alfa (targets IL-2); PF-04236921 (inhibits IL-6); \nand olamkicept (targets IL-6 receptor).\nAdditionally, lusvertikimab antagonizes the IL-7 receptor and GSK1070806 inhibits IL-18.\n...\nAnti-TNF therapies bind to both soluble and transmembrane TNF, inhib- iting its interaction with receptors and therefore regulating various signalling pathways involved in IBD pathophysiology105,106.\n...\n• AMT101 (gut-selective IL-10 fusion protein)", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-08-10"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "www.frontiersin.org › journals › pharmacologyFrontiers | Biologic and small-molecule therapies for ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2026.1948258/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Three selective interleukin (IL)-23p19 antagonists have been approved for both UC and CD; two sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators and a second-generation Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitor have entered routine practice; subcutaneous formulations have altered delivery; and the first formal head-to-head trials, together with placebo-controlled trials incorporating an active reference arm, have begun to replace indirect comparison with direct evidence.\n...\nThree further strategies have generated late-phase data: anti-TL1A antibodies, which target a pathway not previously addressed pharmacologically in IBD; the miR-124 enhancer obefazimod; and the oral IL-23 receptor antagonist peptide icotrokinra.", "date": "2026-07-25", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Competitor Landscape", "url": "https://marketintelo.com/report/inflammatory-bowel-disease-drugs-market", "domain": "marketintelo.com", "snippet": "The regulatory approval of next-generation biologics targeting novel inflammatory pathways - including JAK inhibitors, integrin antagonists, and S1P receptor modulators - has substantially broadened the therapeutic arsenal.\n...\nJAK-STAT inhibitors including tofacitinib and upadacitinib demonstrate oral bioavailability combined with targeted intracellular signaling inhibition, achieving clinical remission rates of 48-56% in moderate-to-severe disease compared to 22-28% with conventional therapies.\nIntegrin antagonists such as vedolizumab provide selective gut-homing lymphocyte targeting, substantially reducing systemic immunosuppression and opportunistic infection risk.\nSphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators represent an emerging frontier, with ozanimod demonstrating 41% remission rates while preserving systemic immunity.\nFurthermore, dual-mechanism agents combining TNF inhibition with IL-23 pathway targeting are entering late-stage development, with interim data suggesting remission rates exceeding 58-62% in treatment-naive cohorts.\n...\nPatients presenting with pure colonic ulcerative colitis frequently receive integrin antagonists or JAK inhibitors as first-line biologic therapy, leveraging gut-selective mechanisms minimizing systemic immunosuppression.", "date": "2025-08-24", "last_updated": "2026-09-22"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9752289/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Among the most promising molecules are anti‐IL‐23, JAK‐i\n...\nMolecules at a more preliminary stage of development (phase 1 and 2), one of which may 1 day offer an optimal benefit‐risk ratio, will also be presented as well as their respective mechanisms of action.\n...\nThe mechanisms targeted by these new therapeutic options include anti‐IL, new JAK inhibitors, therapies interfering with T‐cell trafficking such as anti‐integrins (preventing the migration of lymphocytes from the blood vessels to the gut) or sphingosine‐1‐phosphate (S1P) modulators (blocking lymphocytes in the lymph nodes) and toll like receptor (TLR) agonist, among many others.\n...\nIL‐23 is a pro‐inflammatory cytokine, composed of 2 subunits (p19, specific to IL‐23 and p40, common to IL‐12), playing a key role in IBD.\n...\ns are small molecules, having the advantage of being per os and not associated with antidrug antibodies development, acting downstream of cytokines.\n...\n^52^ , ^53^ Filgotinib, upadacitinib and ivarmacitinib are three other JAK‐inhibitors that have been developed in IBD.", "date": "2022-09-16", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "academic.oup.com · ecco-jcc · articleInflammatory bowel disease therapies and demyelinating ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/20/1/jjaf215/8361737", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "^18^ They have emerged as therapeutic targets across a range of IMIDs due to their role in regulating immune cell trafficking, particularly by controlling the egress of lymphocytes from lymph nodes into the circulation.\n...\nOzanimod, which selectively targets S1P1 and S1P5, was designed to maintain immunomodulatory efficacy while reducing cardiovascular risks.\n...\nAnti-leukocyte trafficking agents act by blocking integrins involved in lymphocyte adhesion and migration across endothelial barriers, thereby limiting immune cell infiltration into target tissues.^29^ This mechanism underlies their use in IBD and demyelinating disorders, where aberrant leukocyte trafficking contributes to chronic inflammation.\n...\nNatalizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that targets the α4 subunit of integrins, blocking both α4β1–VCAM-1 and α4β7–MAdCAM-1 interactions.\nBy inhibiting these adhesion pathways, it prevents lymphocyte trafficking across the endothelium into the central nervous system (CNS) and gastrointestinal tract.\nIn contrast, vedolizumab selectively inhibits the α4β7–MAdCAM-1 interaction, restricting its activity to the intestinal mucosa, while carotegrast methyl (AJM300) is an oral, reversible α4-integrin antagonist that transiently blocks VCAM-1 and MAdCAM-1 binding, producing a functionally gut-selective effect with minimal systemic immune suppression.\n...\nVedolizumab, with its gut-selective mechanism and favorable safety profile, may be used in combination with approved MS therapies for patients requiring treatment of both IBD and demyelinating disease, with the exception of natalizumab due to overlapping mechanisms of action.\n...\nThey exert their immunomodulatory effects primarily by inhibiting purine synthesis and lymphocyte proliferation.", "date": "2026-01-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "A Gastroenterologist's guide to drug interactions of small molecules for inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ueg2.12559", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Currently approved small molecule drugs exert their effects by either of two main mechanisms– Janus kinase inhibition or Sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulation.\n...\nTofacitinib is an inhibitor of the JAK family and is approved for use in moderately to severely active UC.^21^ Tofacitinib is mainly metabolized by CYP3A4 and is also a PGP substrate.\n...\nUpadacitinib is a selective inhibitor of the JAK family, targeting primarily JAK1 and JAK2, and is approved for use in moderately to severely active UC and moderately to severely active Crohn's disease.^23^ Upadacitinib is a non-sensitive CYP3A4 substrate.^33^ As a result, it is modestly influenced by strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as grapefruit juice or Voriconazole, which increase the AUC of Upadacitinib by 75%.\n...\nFilgotinib is an adenosine triphosphate (ATP) competitive and reversible inhibitor of the JAK family^25^ that is approved for the treatment of moderately to severely active UC.\n...\nSphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulator drugs bind to the S1P receptor on lymphocytes and are then internalized by the cell.\nThis interferes with lymphocyte mobilization and exit from draining lymph nodes in the tissue.", "date": "2024-06-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "The Expanding Therapeutic Armamentarium for Inflammatory Bowel Disease: How to Choose the Right Drug[s] for Our Patients?", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/12/1/105/4093864?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "For many years, the treatment of patients with moderate-to-severely active ulcerative colitis [UC] and Crohn’s disease [CD] has been dominated by the tumour necrosis factor [TNF] antagonists.\nHowever, two new classes of biologic drugs, the integrin-antagonists for both UC and CD, and the interleukin [IL]-12/23-antagonist ustekinumab for CD, are now available.\n...\nRecruitment of white blood cells from the circulation to the gut results from complex interactions between integrins on leukocyte cell surfaces and other adhesion molecules on intestinal microvascular endothelial cells.\n4 Anti-adhesion molecules antagonise this interaction by targeting either a shared epitope of the α4β7 integrin heterodimer [vedolizumab, Takeda; AMG 181, Amgen] or either of its individual subunits: α4 in the case of natalizumab [Biogen] and AJM300 [Ajinomoto Co.\nInc.]; and β7 for etrolizumab [Genentech].\nNatalizumab and AJM300 also target α4β1, the ligand of vascular cell adhesion molecule [VCAM]-1, whereas etrolizumab also targets αEβ7, preventing the latter integrin from binding E-cadherin, which is expressed on epithelial cells.\nAnother approach to interfere with leukocyte trafficking to the gut is to block mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule [MAdCAM]-1, the ligand of α4β7 that is specifically expressed on the gut endothelium [PF-00547659; Pfizer] [Figure 2].\n4\n...\nInterleukin [IL]-12 and -23 are expressed by inflammatory myeloid cells and share a common p40 subunit.\nThese cytokines play a central role in T cell-mediated responses in inflammation by influencing the development of T\n...\n16,17 Ustekinumab [Janssen] and briakinumab [Abbvie] are directed against the shared p40 subunit of IL-12 and -23.\n...\n18 MEDI2070 [Amgen], tildrakisumab [Sun Pharma], and risankisumab [Boehringer Ingelheim/Eli Lilly, recently licensed by AbbVie] target the p19 subunit which is specific for IL-23 [Figure 2].\n...\nTofacitinib [Pfizer] is an orally administered selective inhibitor of JAKs, inhibiting mainly JAK1 and JAK3, and to a lesser extent JAK2 and TYK2.\n32,33 Filgotinib [Galapagos], JNJ-54781532 [Johnson & Johnson, NCT01959282], and ABT-494 [Abbvie] are oral JAK inhibitors with improved selectivity for JAK1 [Figure 2].\n...\n42 The latter process is governed by interactions between S1P1 receptors on lymphocytes and their ligand S1P which is differentially expressed on the cell surface of lymphatic endothelial cells.\nUpon stimulation, the cell surface-associated S1P receptors become phosphorylated and are internalised and degraded, leading to sequestration of B and T lymphocytes in the peripheral lymphoid organs and reduced trafficking of these cells to inflamed tissue [Figure 2].\n...\nThe cytokine transforming growth factor [TGF]-β1 exhibits potent anti-inflammatory properties by inhibiting T cell proliferation and differentiation, macrophage activation, and dendritic cell maturation.\nThese anti-inflammatory properties are downregulated by mothers against decapentaplegic homologue 7 [SMAD7], a protein that is consistently elevated in CD patients with active inflammation.\n49 The oral antisense oligonucleotide mongersen [Celgene] binds to SMAD *7* messenger RNA and specifically causes its degradation by cellular ribonucleases.\nThe subsequent restoration of TGF-β signalling decreases the inflammatory cascade by restoring endogenous anti-inflammatory processes [Figure 2].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20"}], "raw": {"id": "ef7b7698-f446-4274-b383-36d5789e1dc3"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:05+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q005", "objective": "SOR102 safety findings and adverse events in Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["SOR102", "SOR102-101"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q005-3", "query": "SOR102 ulcerative colitis individual adverse events infections deaths", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "SOR102 ulcerative colitis individual adverse events infections deaths", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1009.9, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Sorriso Pharmaceuticals, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "url": "https://synapse-patsnap-com.libproxy1.nus.edu.sg/organization/91d3bdda199f847ec2b338400a1a78ff", "domain": "synapse-patsnap-com.libproxy1.nus.edu.sg", "snippet": "Login to view more data\n...\nIn part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\nNo TEAEs occurred in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nThe favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-09"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Safely targeting combination TNF and IL-23 using a gut-restricted ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13263963/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Overall, SOR102 was well tolerated in both healthy participants and patients with UC, with a low incidence of treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs), most of which were mild to moderate in severity.\nIn part 1, TEAEs were reported in 3/24 (13%) participants receiving SOR102 compared with 2/8 (25%) receiving placebo.\nNo TEAEs were observed in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in 7/16 (44%) patients treated with SOR102 and 3/6 (50%) patients receiving placebo.\nOnly 1 severe TEAE was reported, a flare of UC requiring hospitalization, which was not considered related to the study drug.\nImportantly, no clinically meaningful effects of SOR102 were observed on electrocardiograms, vital signs, physical examination findings, or safety laboratory parameters at any dose or dosing frequency evaluated.\n...\nIn this early-phase study, anti-drug antibodies (ADAs) were detected in 2/8 (25%) subjects in part 2 and 6/16 (38%) patients in part 3.\n...\nIn summary, SOR102 demonstrated a favorable safety and tolerability profile across early-phase evaluation, with no signal of systemic toxicity or immunogenicity.", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "PowerPoint Presentation", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/DDW-2025-poster.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "Data are n (%), mean (SD), or median (min, max); FCAL=fecal calprotectin; CRP=C-reactive protein\n* One patient received adalimumab, and one patient received mirikizumab, both in clinical trials\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\n...\nADA titers were low in most patients and not associated with safety or decreased clinical response.\n...\nTable 2.\nTreatment Emergent Adverse Events\nPlacebo\n...\nSOR102 \n...\nSOR102 \n810mg BID (N=9)\nTotal\n(N=22)\nTotal Number of TEAEs\n5\n2\n6\n13\nNumber of patients with ≥1 TEAE\n(50)\n2 (29)\n5 (56)\n10 (46)\nNumber of patients with TEAEs \nleading to study discontinuation\n0 (0)\n0 (0)\n2 (22)\n2 (9)\nNumber of patients with ≥1 SAE\n0 (0)\n0 (0)\n1 (11)1\n1 (5)\n...\n12 (55)\n1. Worsening of UC that led to hospitalization 2.\nAbdominal pain of mild severity\n...\nTreatment emergent adverse events (TEAEs) were reported in 57%, \n29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 \n810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 2).\nMost were mild-to-\nmoderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\nOne unrelated serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-05-11"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral Administration of SOR102 Delivers Anti-TNF/IL-23 ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-Week-2025-Poster-v4.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "22 patients were randomized (ITT; Table \n1), 17 patients completed the study Per \nProtocol (QD n=6, BID n=5, Placebo n=6).\nReasons for discontinuation: worsening of \nUC (n=2), study schedule non-compliance \n(n=2) and consent withdrawal (n=1).\n...\nNo clinically significant changes in safety\nlaboratory \nparameters, \ndose-limiting \ntoxicities, \nor \nevents \nsuggestive \nof \npronounced immunosuppression\n•\nAll but one TEAE were mild to moderate\n•\n1 severe and serious TEAE of worsening \nUC requiring hospitalization was not \nconsidered related to treatment.\nThis\nTEAE occurred during follow-up in a \nSOR102 BID patient\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\n...\nSOR102 BID demonstrated strong and consistent activity across multiple clinical and tissue PD endpoints in patients with UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-02-25"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "gutflix.eu › search › dIMPROVEMENT IN INFLAMMATORY BIOMARKER LEVELS THROUGH WEEK 12 ...", "url": "https://gutflix.eu/search/d/53f9ac8e-743b-11ee-bede-0242ac140004", "domain": "gutflix.eu", "snippet": "All 42 enrolled participants completed the study.\nSeven adverse events (AE) were observed, all occurring in Part 1.\nAE severity was mild or moderate.\nThere was one treatment-related AE (loose stools) of mild intensity and 17 hours duration occurring in the lowest dose cohort.\n...\nSOR102 was well tolerated at all tested doses.\nSystemic exposure of SOR102 and its monomers was not observed, supporting its gut selectivity.\n...\nClinical remission rates at Wk48 were 77.0–83.5% among Wk12 GUS induction clinical responders and 55.6–58.3% among pts not in clinical response at Wk12, and PRO-2 remission rates were 71.3–75.8% and 46.7–49.0%, respectively (**Table**).", "date": "2023-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Journal Scan_Jan 2026", "url": "https://www.ibdenc.org/journalscan-jan2026", "domain": "ibdenc.org", "snippet": "SOR102 is an oral bispecific antibody targeting TNF and IL-23p19 developed for ulcer ative colitis.\nThis phase 1, double blind, placebo-controlled trial evaluated its safety, pharmacokinetics, and preliminary efficacy in healthy participants and patients with active ulcerative colitis.\nAcross single and multiple ascending doses, SOR102 was generally well tolerated, with mostly mild to moderate adverse events and no clinically significant safety signals.\nIn patients with ulcerative colitis treated for 42 days, clinical responses were more frequent with SOR102 than placebo, particularly with twice-daily dosing.\nSymptomatic remission was achieved in over half of patients receiving twice-daily SOR102, while no placebo-treated patients reached remission.\nAlthough one severe disease exacerbation occurred, it was not considered treatment related.\nOverall, the favourable safety profile and early signs of clinical bene fit support further clinical development of SOR102 as a potential oral therapy for ulcerative colitis.\n...\nNo significant differences were observed in corticosteroid-free remission, clinical response, hospitalization, surgery, adverse events, or loss of response.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-04"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Interleukin-23 Inhibitors in Inflammatory Bowel Disease - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13212390/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The most reported adverse events were nasopharyngitis, upper respiratory tract infections, headache, and injection-site reactions, which were generally mild to moderate and comparable to placebo rates.\nSerious adverse events and treatment discontinuations were infrequent, and no clear dose-related safety signals emerged.\nRates of serious infections remained low, with no meaningful increase in opportunistic infections or tuberculosis reactivation observed across programs.\n...\nSevere adverse events were reported in only 15 patients (5.2%), mainly related to SARS-CoV-2 infections or UC worsening.\n...\nEven more interestingly, SOR102 is an orally administered bispecific antibody that targets both TNFα and IL-23 [65].\nIn a phase I trial, the drug was administered to 48 subjects including healthy individuals and UC patients.\nThe drug showed a favorable safety profile, with mild diarrhea and dysmenorrhea being the main side effects.\nTwo of the seven patients with UC experienced a disease worsening.\n...\nSafety and pharmacokinetics of SOR102, an oral bispecific inhibitor of TNF and interleukin-23 in healthy participants and patients with ulcerative colitis: a first-in-human, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 1 trial.", "date": "2026-04-21", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Interleukin-23p19 Inhibitors in Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12942054/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Similarly, SOR102 is an oral bispecific inhibitor of TNF and IL-23p19, which has been tested in a phase 1 trial involving patients with UC [40].\n...\nA meta-analysis focusing on adverse events found no increase in overall adverse events versus placebo and even lower rates of serious adverse events, serious infections, and adverse event-related discontinuations in both CD and UC, while maintaining neutral infection and malignancy risks [60].\n...\nOverall, the safety profile of IL-23p19 inhibitors looks promising (Table 2), with most adverse events reported during clinical trials being mild to moderate in severity.\nUpper respiratory tract infections are the most frequently reported side effects for risankizumab and guselkumab, whereas injection site reactions are most commonly reported for mirikizumab [70,71,72].", "date": "2026-02-14", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "5.2 Il-23 Inhibitors In...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00781-1?error=cookies_not_supported&code=22180240-68d1-410d-9ca0-799741b2cf84", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "Across trials, IL-23 inhibitors displayed a favorable safety profile, with low rates of serious adverse events, infections, and major cardiovascular events, supporting their use in long-term disease management.\n...\nThe most reported adverse events were nasopharyngitis, upper respiratory tract infections, headache, and injection-site reactions, which were generally mild to moderate and comparable to placebo rates.\nSerious adverse events and treatment discontinuations were infrequent, and no clear dose-related safety signals emerged.\nRates of serious infections remained low, with no meaningful increase in opportunistic infections or tuberculosis reactivation observed across programs.\nImportantly, no new safety concerns were identified during long-term extension studies, supporting the durability of the safety profile with sustained exposure [33].\n...\nSevere adverse events were reported in only 15 patients (5.2%), mainly related to SARS-CoV-2 infections or UC worsening.\nMajor adverse cardiovascular events (MACE) were observed in 0.7% of patients, suggesting the treatment's safety [23].\n...\nSerious adverse event surveillance across the maintenance and induction phases reveals that mirikizumab consistently demonstrates the lowest incidence of serious adverse events among IL-23p19 inhibitors, with safety advantages for infections and malignancy in older populations [58].\n...\nEven more interestingly, SOR102 is an orally administered bispecific antibody that targets both TNFα and IL-23 [65].\n...\nThe drug showed a favorable safety profile, with mild diarrhea and dysmenorrhea being the main side effects.\nTwo of the seven patients with UC experienced a disease worsening.", "date": "2026-04-21", "last_updated": "2026-09-20"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Phase 1b study of SOR102, a novel, orally delivered ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/ECCO-2025-presentation.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "To evaluate the safety and tolerability of multiple doses of SOR102 in patients\naged 18 to 75 years with mild to severe \n...\nDiscontinuations: 1 discontinued after 5 days of dosing (QD); 1 had evidence of improvement (BID); \n3 were treatment failures (BID) with significant disease burden at baseline\n...\n4 Discontinued study (44.4%)\n...\nschedule (11.1%)\n- 1 Withdraw consent (11.1%)\n- 2 Other* (22.2%)\n*worsening of UC\n...\nSafety Analysis Set\n...\nSOR102 \n...\n(N=9)\nTotal \n(N=22)\nTotal Number of TEAEs\n5\n2\n6\n13\nNumber of patients with ≥1 TEAE\n3 (50)\n2 (29)\n5 (56)\n10 (46)\nNumber of patients with TEAEs leading to study discontinuation\n0 (0)\n0 (0)\n2 (22)\n2 (9)\nNumber of patients with ≥1 SAE\n...\nNumber of patients with ≥1 TEAE by maximum severity\n...\n12 (55)\n1. Worsening of UC that led to hospitalization\n2. Abdominal pain of mild severity\n...\n• SOR102 was safe and well tolerated", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-14"}], "raw": {"id": "e8ad412e-7829-4f5f-8acc-f9ead02523d6"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:06+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q005", "objective": "SOR102 safety findings and adverse events in Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["SOR102", "SOR102-101"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q005-4", "query": "SOR102 Grade 3 adverse events 810 mg", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "SOR102 Grade 3 adverse events 810 mg", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1268.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety and pharmacokinetics of SOR102, an oral bispecific ...", "url": "https://pure.amsterdamumc.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/173141909/Safety-and-pharmacokinetics-of-sor102-an-oral-bispecific-inhibitor-of-tnf-and-interleukin-23-in-healthy-participants-an.pdf", "domain": "pure.amsterdamumc.nl", "snippet": "In part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe\n[the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other \nTEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\n...\nIn part 1, any treatment emergent adverse event (TEAE) occurred in three (13%) of 24 healthy participants who received single doses of SOR102 compared with two (25%) of eight patients who received placebo.\n...\nSOR102 405 mg dose group.\n...\nNo treatment emergent serious adverse event occurred during parts 1 or 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of \n16 patients who received SOR102 and three (50%) of \nsix patients who received placebo (table 3).\nTwo (13%) of 16 patients treated with SOR102 (both in the twice-daily\nSOR102 group) had TEAEs of worsening ulcerative colitis that led to study discontinuation.\nAll (77%) but one TEAE in patients with at least one TEAE were mild to moderate.\nOne mild TEAE of upper abdominal pain in a patient who received twice-daily SOR102 was considered possibly related to treatment.\nThis patient also had a treatment emergent serious adverse event of worsening ulcerative colitis during the follow-up period that required hospital­ isation, and which led to study withdrawal.\nThis serious adverse event (also considered a severe TEAE) was considered unrelated to study drug.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-16"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR-102 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c4ffffc6839e4416b41bd1cd9d28f2d9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "In part 3, seven patients with ulcerative colitis were randomly assigned to receive SOR102 810 mg once-daily, nine to receive the same dose twice-daily, and six to receive placebo.\n...\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\n...\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nThree (43%) patients in the once-daily group, five (56%) in the twice-daily group, and none in the placebo group achieved symptomatic remission by day 42.\n...\nIn Sorriso’s Phase 1b study, six patients received placebo, seven received 810 mg of SOR102 once per day, and nine received 810 mg twice per day.", "date": "2025-01-17", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "PHASE 1B STUDY OF SOR102, A NOVEL, ORALLY ...", "url": "https://eposters.ddw.org/ddw/2025/ddw-2025/4155984/vipul.jairath.phase.1b.study.of.sor102.a.novel.orally.delivered.bispecific.html?f=menu=6*browseby=8*sortby=2*ce_id=2855*ot_id=31400*marker=5698", "domain": "eposters.ddw.org", "snippet": "TEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1).\nMost were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\nOne serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm.\n...\nTEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1).\nMost were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).", "date": "2025-03-31", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Phase 1b study of SOR102, a novel, orally delivered ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/ECCO-2025-presentation.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "6-week SOR102 treatment: 810 mg QD, 810 mg BID, or placebo BID\n...\nSafety Analysis Set\n...\nSOR102 \n810mg QD \n(N=7)\nSOR102 \n810mg BID \n(N=9)\nTotal \n(N=22)\nTotal Number of TEAEs\n5\n2\n6\n13\nNumber of patients with ≥1 TEAE\n3 (50)\n2 (29)\n5 (56)\n10 (46)\nNumber of patients with TEAEs leading to study discontinuation\n0 (0)\n0 (0)\n2 (22)\n2 (9)\nNumber of patients with ≥1 SAE\n0 (0)\n0 (0)\n1 (11)\n1 (5)\n...\nNumber of patients with ≥1 TEAE by maximum severity\nMild\n1 (17)\n1 (14)\n2 (22)\n4 (18)\nModerate\n2 (33)\n...\nSevere\n...\n12 (55)\n1. Worsening of UC that led to hospitalization\n2. Abdominal pain of mild severity", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-14"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "OP31 Phase 1b study of SOR102, a novel, orally delivered ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/19/Supplement_1/i62/7966975", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Pts with Mayo endoscopy score (ES) ≥2, rectal bleeding score (RBS) ≥1 and stool frequency score (SFS) ≥1 were randomized 1:1:1 to receive SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo for 6 weeks.\n...\nTwenty-two pts were randomized (SOR102 810mg BID=9; SOR102 810mg QD=7; placebo=6); 17 pts completed the study.\n...\nTEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of pts receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1); most were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\nOne serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization occurred in the SOR102 810mg BID arm.\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.", "date": "2025-01-22", "last_updated": "2026-09-06"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Journal Scan_Jan 2026", "url": "https://www.ibdenc.org/journalscan-jan2026", "domain": "ibdenc.org", "snippet": "Across single and multiple ascending doses, SOR102 was generally well tolerated, with mostly mild to moderate adverse events and no clinically significant safety signals.\n...\nAlthough one severe disease exacerbation occurred, it was not considered treatment related.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-04"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Pooled safety analysis and management of sotorasib-related ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/oncolo/article/30/1/oyae356/7976610", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "TRAEs were reported in 388 (70.7%) patients (Table 2).\nGrade 1 and grade 2 TRAEs were reported in 124 (22.6%) and 117 (21.3%) patients, respectively, with grade ≥3 events occurring in 147 (26.8%) patients.\n...\nOverall, grades 1, 2, and 3 treatment-related diarrhea events occurred in 78 (14.2%), 53 (9.7%), and 40 (7.3%) patients, respectively (Table 3).\n...\nGrade ≥3 events were managed with dose modification; a subset of these patients resumed sotorasib at 960 mg after treatment interruption or prior dose reduction at investigator discretion.\n...\nSotorasib-related diarrhea, including grade 3, was effectively managed with dose interruption or dose reduction with or without antidiarrheal medications in more than 90% of events; no grade 4 or fatal diarrhea event was reported.", "date": "2025-01-17", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Sotorasib in KRAS p.G12C–Mutated Advanced Pancreatic Cancer | NEJM", "url": "https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2208470", "domain": "nejm.org", "snippet": "In phase 2, patients received sotorasib at a dose of 960 mg orally once daily.\n...\nTreatment-related adverse events of any grade were reported in 16 patients (42%); 6 patients (16%) had grade 3 adverse events.\nNo treatment-related adverse events were fatal or led to treatment discontinuation.\n...\nOverall, 24 patients (63%) had adverse events of grade 3 or higher.\nAdverse events of any grade that were considered by the investigators to be related to treatment were reported in 16 patients (42%); 6 of these events (16%) were grade 3 in severity (Table 3).\nThe most common grade 3 treatment-related adverse events were diarrhea and fatigue (in 2 patients [5%] each).\n...\nAmong these events, grade 3 diarrhea that resolved in 4 days and grade 2 or 3 diarrhea that resolved in 16 days were observed in 2 patients (5%).\n...\n|Grade ≥3|9 (75)|15 (58)|24 (63)|0|6 (23)|6 (16)|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Safely targeting combination TNF and IL-23 using a gut-restricted ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13263963/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Overall, SOR102 was well tolerated in both healthy participants and patients with UC, with a low incidence of treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs), most of which were mild to moderate in severity.\nIn part 1, TEAEs were reported in 3/24 (13%) participants receiving SOR102 compared with 2/8 (25%) receiving placebo.\nNo TEAEs were observed in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in 7/16 (44%) patients treated with SOR102 and 3/6 (50%) patients receiving placebo.\nOnly 1 severe TEAE was reported, a flare of UC requiring hospitalization, which was not considered related to the study drug.\nImportantly, no clinically meaningful effects of SOR102 were observed on electrocardiograms, vital signs, physical examination findings, or safety laboratory parameters at any dose or dosing frequency evaluated.\n...\nIn this early-phase study, anti-drug antibodies (ADAs) were detected in 2/8 (25%) subjects in part 2 and 6/16 (38%) patients in part 3.\n...\nIn summary, SOR102 demonstrated a favorable safety and tolerability profile across early-phase evaluation, with no signal of systemic toxicity or immunogenicity.", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral Administration of SOR102 Delivers Anti-TNF/IL-23 ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-Week-2025-Poster-v4.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "No clinically significant changes in safety\nlaboratory \nparameters, \ndose-limiting \ntoxicities, \nor \nevents \nsuggestive \nof \npronounced immunosuppression\n•\nAll but one TEAE were mild to moderate\n•\n1 severe and serious TEAE of worsening \nUC requiring hospitalization was not \nconsidered related to treatment.\nThis\nTEAE occurred during follow-up in a \nSOR102 BID patient", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-02-25"}], "raw": {"id": "b4815542-3a46-491b-9823-045c6da4fe56"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:07+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q002", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q002-2", "query": "AZD5148 TcdB antibody competitors licensing", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "AZD5148 TcdB antibody competitors licensing", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 26607.2, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d6acfad0-c39d-44cd-90d2-d4edff505376", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "Despite these advancements—and the approval of numerous mAbs for other indications—few anti-bacterial mAbs have been approved for clinical use, which is limited to obiltoxaximab (Anthim) and raxibacumab (Abthrax) for treatment of *Bacillus anthracis* toxin exposure, and bezlotoxumab (Zinplava^®^) for *Clostridioides difficile* infection of the gastrointestinal tract [11,12,13].\n...\nMedarex was the first to report anti-toxin mAbs, which were later licensed by Merck [23].\nTwo fully human IgG1κ mAbs, anti-TcdA (CDA1/actoxumab) and TcdB (CDB1/bezlotoxumab) were selected for in vitro potency, and efficacy in a lethal intoxication hamster model [24].\n...\nBezlotoxumab (Zinplava^®^) was approved as a single intravenous infusion of 10 mg/kg in the USA and European Union for prevention of rCDI in individuals at high risk for CDI and receiving antibacterial treatment [23].\n...\nAstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\n...\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad Review of the Field", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13337890/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Medarex was the first to report anti-toxin mAbs, which were later licensed by Merck [23].\nTwo fully human IgG1κ mAbs, anti-TcdA (CDA1/actoxumab) and TcdB (CDB1/bezlotoxumab) were selected for in vitro potency, and efficacy in a lethal intoxication hamster model [24].\n...\nAstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\n...\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-07-18"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Clostridium Difficile Infections Pipeline Set - openPR.com", "url": "https://www.openpr.com/news/4491579/clostridium-difficile-infections-pipeline-set", "domain": "openpr.com", "snippet": "Key Clostridium Difficile Infections Companies are Acurx Pharmaceuticals, Adiso Therapeutics, Summit Therapeutics, Finch Therapeutics, Vedanta Biosciences, and others.\n...\nLeading companies such as Vedanta Biosciences, Acurx Pharmaceuticals, Summit Therapeutics, Finch Therapeutics Group, and Adiso Therapeutics are actively advancing novel drug candidates aimed at addressing recurrence, antimicrobial resistance, and poor treatment durability-key unmet needs that continue to challenge existing Clostridium Difficile Infections management strategies.\n...\n* Strategic collaborations and licensing agreements are accelerating innovation.\n...\n* In April 2026, AstraZeneca announced results of a Phase IIb, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled Study to Evaluate the Efficacy and Safety of AZD5148 for Prevention of Recurrence of Clostridioides Difficile Infection in Individuals 18 Years of Age and Above\n...\nAdditionally, bezlotoxumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting C. difficile toxin B, has been approved to prevent recurrence in high-risk patients.", "date": "2026-04-27", "last_updated": "2026-04-29"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12594360/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The TcdB-targeting monoclonal antibody (mAb) bezlotoxumab (Zinplava) was indicated to reduce CDI recurrence in patients 18 years of age or older who are receiving antibacterial drug treatment for CDI and are at high risk for CDI recurrence.\n...\nAZD5148 is a humanized anti-TcdB mAb that neutralizes toxin activity by blocking the delivery of the enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into host cells.\n...\nFurthermore, the addition of the anti-TcdA mAb, PA50, provided no additional protective benefit.\n...\nWe previously described PA41 (now called AZD5148), a humanized mAb that binds a highly conserved TcdB GTD epitope and prevents the delivery of the GTD enzymatic cargo into the host cytoplasm [25,26].\n...\nIn conclusion, our data show that AZD5148 provides robust protection in a mouse model of CDI caused by diverse *C. difficile* strains.", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2025-11-12"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12614991/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nHerein, we showed that the AZD5148 epitope is highly conserved across all geographic regions and sequence types and has a higher average conservation frequency in its binding site (99.58%) when compared with bezlotoxumab (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes.\n...\nAn anti-TcdB monoclonal antibody (mAb), Zinplava (bezlotoxumab), was approved in 2016 and recently withdrawn from the market by the sponsor [5].\n...\nWe recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16].\n...\nWe further assessed the effect of amino acid (AA) substitutions of key ribotypes (RT) on the neutralization by AZD5148 and compared that with a licensed anti-TcdB mAb.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-08-09"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Search for patents - Research and innovation - European Union", "url": "https://research-and-innovation.ec.europa.eu/knowledge-publications-tools-and-data/patents-and-licencing/search-patents_en", "domain": "research-and-innovation.ec.europa.eu", "snippet": "Search patents and technologies categorised by name, JRC reference number, IPR status, science area, keyword and JRC institute.", "date": "2018-08-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40810427/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** Herein, we showed that the AZD5148 epitope is highly conserved across all geographic regions and sequence types and has a higher average conservation frequency in its binding site (99.58%) when compared with bezlotoxumab (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes.", "date": "2025-11-14", "last_updated": "2025-12-02"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "AZD5148 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/578bdd03a2f245e1babb56c500ca552e", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Drug Type\nMonoclonal antibody\n...\nTarget\ntoxB\n...\nActive Indication\nClostridium difficile infection\n...\nOriginator Organization\nAstraZeneca PLC\nActive Organization\nAstraZeneca PLC\nInactive Organization\nLicense Organization", "date": "2025-05-08", "last_updated": "2025-10-25"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "P-1055. Anti-Toxin B Neutralizing Monoclonal Antibody AZD5148 ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11778635/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Because toxin B is a clinically validated target for CDI, we have generated an anti-toxin B neutralizing monoclonal antibody (mAb) AZD5148.\nWe previously demonstrated that AZD5148 administered at 10, 1 and 0.5 mg/kg prevents severe disease development in a gnotobiotic piglet model, which recapitulates the clinical signs and gross pathology observed in human CDI, we compared the protective efficacy of AZD5148 with the clinically approved anti-toxin B mAb bezlotoxumab.\n...\nNo significant difference was observed between the c-IgG and bezlotoxumab groups.\nIn contrast, AZD5148 reduced signs of disease severity as measured by diarrhea scores, gross pathology scoring of descending and spiral colons and damage to the gut epithelium along with inflammation.\n...\nThese data suggest that AZD5148 may offer some therapeutic benefit in preventing CDI in humans.", "date": "2025-01-29", "last_updated": "2026-09-01"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/our-therapy-areas/pipeline.html", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD5148\n** *reduction of C. diff recurrence*\n#### AZD5148 - Phase II\nClose\n- **Mechanism:** anti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb\n- **Area under investigation:** reduction of C. diff recurrence\n- **Molecule size:** Large molecule", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "f4565f24-48fd-4c05-ad17-0d843a7c9f1c"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:08+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q005", "objective": "SOR102 safety findings and adverse events in Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["SOR102", "SOR102-101"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q005-5", "query": "SOR102 900 mg 42-day safety results", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "SOR102 900 mg 42-day safety results", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 856.0, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR-102 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c4ffffc6839e4416b41bd1cd9d28f2d9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "SOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3).\n...\nIn part 3, patients with mildly-to-severely active ulcerative colitis were randomly assigned (1:1:1) to once-daily or twice-daily SOR102 or placebo for 42 days.\nThe primary objective was to evaluate the safety and tolerability of SOR102.\n...\nFrom Oct 9, 2023, to Oct 16, 2024, 157 participants were assessed for eligibility, of whom 93 were excluded and 64 were randomly assigned to either SOR102 (n=48) or placebo (n=16) across all three parts of the study.\n...\nIn part 3, seven patients with ulcerative colitis were randomly assigned to receive SOR102 810 mg once-daily, nine to receive the same dose twice-daily, and six to receive placebo.\nIn part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\nNo TEAEs occurred in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\nIn part 3, by day 42, three (43%) of seven participants in the SOR102 once-daily group, five (56%) of nine patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group, and one (17%) of six patients in the placebo group had achieved a Mayo score clinical response, while two (29%) patients in the once-daily group, six (67%) in the twice-daily group, and two (33%) in the placebo group achieved a modified Mayo score clinical response.\nThree (43%) patients in the once-daily group, five (56%) in the twice-daily group, and none in the placebo group achieved symptomatic remission by day 42.\n...\nThe favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\n• Safety and Tolerability: SOR102 was dosed orally and was safe and well-tolerated in both healthy participants and UC patients.\nNo unexpected adverse events were observed.\n...\nCollectively, these results showed that oral SOR102, administered twice daily for six weeks, demonstrated strong and consistent activity across multiple clinical and pharmacodynamic endpoints.", "date": "2025-01-17", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety and pharmacokinetics of SOR102, an oral bispecific ...", "url": "https://pure.amsterdamumc.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/173141909/Safety-and-pharmacokinetics-of-sor102-an-oral-bispecific-inhibitor-of-tnf-and-interleukin-23-in-healthy-participants-an.pdf", "domain": "pure.amsterdamumc.nl", "snippet": "In part 3, patients with mildly-to-severely active ulcerative colitis were randomly assigned (1:1:1) to once-daily or twice-daily SOR102 or placebo for 42 days.\n...\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\n...\n[the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other \nTEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\nIn part 3, by day 42, three (43%) of seven participants in the SOR102 once-daily group, five (56%) of nine patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group, and one (17%) of six patients in the placebo group had achieved a Mayo score clinical response, while two (29%) patients in the once-daily group, six (67%) in the twice-daily group, and two (33%) in the placebo group achieved a modified Mayo score clinical response.\n...\nIn part 3, \n18 patients with ulcerative colitis were randomly \nassigned (1:1:1) to receive once-daily or twice-daily\nSOR102 810 mg or placebo for 42 days.\n...\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of \n16 patients who received SOR102 and three (50%) of \nsix patients who received placebo (table 3).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-16"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Genomeditech(Shanghai) Co.LTD", "url": "https://en.genomeditech.com/news/detail?id=342", "domain": "en.genomeditech.com", "snippet": "Phase I clinical data demonstrated favorable safety and preliminary efficacy, with a 56% clinical response rate, and a Phase II study is planned.\n...\nIn Part 3 of the study, on Day 42, the clinical response rates based on the Mayo score were 43% (3/7) and 56% (5/9) in the once-daily and twice-daily SOR102 dosing groups, respectively.\nThe modified Mayo response rates were 29% (2/7) and 67% (6/9), and the symptom remission rates were 43% (3/7) and 56% (5/9).\nIn contrast, the corresponding rates in the placebo group were only 17% (1/6), 33% (2/6), and 0%, respectively.\nIn addition, two patients (2/9) in the twice-daily dosing group achieved clinical remission accompanied by endoscopic improvement.\nPreliminary clinical data demonstrate that this oral bispecific antibody not only shows early signs of efficacy but also exhibits a favorable safety profile.\nSorriso Pharmaceuticals stated that a Phase II clinical trial is planned to be initiated later this year, with further efficacy data anticipated.\n...\nIn Part 3 of the study, on Day 42, the clinical response rates based on the Mayo score were 43% (3/7) and 56% (5/9) in the once-daily and twice-daily SOR102 dosing groups, respectively.", "date": "2026-02-06", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral Administration of SOR102 Delivers Anti-TNF/IL-23 ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-Week-2025-Poster-v4.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "22 patients were randomized (ITT; Table \n1), 17 patients completed the study Per \nProtocol (QD n=6, BID n=5, Placebo n=6).\n...\nNo clinically significant changes in safety\nlaboratory \nparameters, \ndose-limiting \ntoxicities, \nor \nevents \nsuggestive \nof \npronounced immunosuppression\n•\nAll but one TEAE were mild to moderate\n•\n1 severe and serious TEAE of worsening \nUC requiring hospitalization was not \nconsidered related to treatment.\nThis\nTEAE occurred during follow-up in a \nSOR102 BID patient\n...\nA. Log2 fold-change (Log2FC) of cytokine protein concentrations in biopsy homogenates from baseline to end of treatment (day 42, EOT).\nSerum cytokines were examined weekly and showed no consistent changes over the treatment period.\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\n...\nSOR102 BID demonstrated strong and consistent activity across multiple clinical and tissue PD endpoints in patients with UC.\n...\nHigh and sustained levels of active monomers in feces confirmed\nSOR102 is efficiently cleaved by trypsin and cytokine-binding activity is maintained after oral dosing in patients with UC.\n...\nLevels of SOR102 monomers in sigmoid colonic tissue increased with oral dose, with minimal systemic exposure.\n...\nMost titers were low (≤1:240) and not associated with safety or clinical response5", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-02-25"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Phase 1b study of SOR102, a novel, orally delivered ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/ECCO-2025-presentation.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "Part 1 (Single Ascending Dose) and Part 2 (Multiple Dose) in healthy volunteers showed no safety or tolerability\nconcerns at SOR102 doses above Phase 1b dose levels; results were presented previously2\n...\n6-week SOR102 treatment: 810 mg QD, 810 mg BID, or placebo BID\n...\nSigmoidoscopy \nand Biopsies\nDay 39 to Day 42\nDay 1\nDay 7\nDay 14\nDay 21\nDay 28\nDay 42\n(6 weeks)\n...\nPrimary Objective\nTo evaluate the safety and tolerability of multiple doses of SOR102 in patients\naged 18 to 75 years with mild to severe \nactive Ulcerative Colitis (UC)\n...\nDiscontinuations: 1 discontinued after 5 days of dosing (QD); 1 had evidence of improvement (BID); \n3 were treatment failures (BID) with significant disease burden at baseline\n...\n• SOR102 was safe and well tolerated\n• In most samples tested, there were no detectable levels of \nSOR102 or monomers in serum\n• ADA titers were low in most patients and not associated with \ndecreased clinical response \n• Efficacy across multiple endpoints was observed in the SOR102 \nBID arm compared to QD and placebo, despite the short treatment duration", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-14"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Sorriso Pharmaceuticals, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/91d3bdda199f847ec2b338400a1a78ff", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "In part 3, patients with mildly-to-severely active ulcerative colitis were randomly assigned (1:1:1) to once-daily or twice-daily SOR102 or placebo for 42 days.\nThe primary objective was to evaluate the safety and tolerability of SOR102.\n...\nIn part 3, seven patients with ulcerative colitis were randomly assigned to receive SOR102 810 mg once-daily, nine to receive the same dose twice-daily, and six to receive placebo.\nIn part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\nNo TEAEs occurred in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\nIn part 3, by day 42, three (43%) of seven participants in the SOR102 once-daily group, five (56%) of nine patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group, and one (17%) of six patients in the placebo group had achieved a Mayo score clinical response, while two (29%) patients in the once-daily group, six (67%) in the twice-daily group, and two (33%) in the placebo group achieved a modified Mayo score clinical response.\nThree (43%) patients in the once-daily group, five (56%) in the twice-daily group, and none in the placebo group achieved symptomatic remission by day 42.\n...\nThe favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\n• Safety and Tolerability: SOR102 was dosed orally and was safe and well-tolerated in both healthy participants and UC patients.\nNo unexpected adverse events were observed.\n...\nCollectively, these results showed that oral SOR102, administered twice daily for six weeks, demonstrated strong and consistent activity across multiple clinical and pharmacodynamic endpoints.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "PowerPoint-Präsentation", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-2024-presentation.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "SOR102-101 (NCT06080048) is a Phase 1, first in human study that consists of 3 parts.\nParts 1 and 2 enrolled healthy subjects (HS) age 18-55 years and evaluated single ascending (SAD) and multiple doses (MD) of SOR102, respectively.\n...\nSOR102 is Safe and Well-Tolerated\nNo safety or tolerability concerns noted at single doses up to 3,645mg or multiple doses of 1,215mg BID\n-No serious adverse events\n-No clinically significant laboratory, ECG, vitals, or physical exam findings\n-No subjects discontinued \nPart 1: One TEAE considered related to study drug (Cohort #1)\nPart 2: No adverse events\nCohort / Dose\nEvent\nDuration\nSeverity\n...\nC1 / 135mg\nDiarrhea\n~17 hours\nMild\nProbably related\nC1 / 135mg\nDysmenorrhea\n~2.5 days\nModerate\nNot related\nC1 / 135mg\nFlatulence\n~12 hours\nMild\nNot related\nC2 / Placebo\nHeadache\n~4 hours\nMild\nNot related\nC2 / Placebo\nCatheter site pain\n~5 hours\nModerate\nNot related\nC2 / 405mg\nDysmenorrhea\n~12 hours\nModerate\nNot related\n...\nSystemic Exposure Of SOR102 And Its Monomers Was Not \nObserved; Low-Level ADA Signal in 3 of 40 Samples Tested \n...\nNo detectable levels of SOR102 or monomers in serum in Parts 1 and 2\n...\nMost samples negative for ADA \n...\nefficiently cleaved after oral dosing\n...\n• After Day 3, all stool samples tested in subjects receiving SOR102 demonstrated detectable levels of monomers\n...\n•\nSOR102 was well tolerated at all tested doses.\n•\nSystemic exposure of SOR102 and its monomers was not observed, supporting its gut selectivity.\n•\nLow levels of intact SOR102 and high levels of active SOR102 monomers in faeces confirm that SOR102 is efficiently cleaved and maintains activity after oral dosing, as designed.\n...\nIn Part 2 (MD), monomer faecal concentrations peaked after Day 2 and were detected in all stool samples tested after Day 3.\n...\nLow-level ADA positive signal observed in 2 subjects", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-05-11"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety and pharmacokinetics of SOR102, an oral bispecific inhibitor ...", "url": "https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/works/986OAB8a/", "domain": "ouci.dntb.gov.ua", "snippet": "# Safety and pharmacokinetics of SOR102, an oral bispecific inhibitor of TNF and interleukin-23 in healthy participants and patients with ulcerative colitis: a first-in-human, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 1 trial", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-14"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR102 for Ulcerative Colitis", "url": "https://www.docwirenews.com/post/novel-bispecific-antibody-shows-promise-for-ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "docwirenews.com", "snippet": "In a first-in-human phase 1 trial, SOR102, an oral bispecific inhibitor of tumor necrosis factor and IL-23p19, demonstrated a favorable safety profile and encouraging evidence of efficacy among patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThis double-blind, placebo-controlled trial had three parts and enrolled 64 participants, both healthy individuals in parts 1 and 2 and patients with active ulcerative colitis in part 3.\n...\nIn part 3, patients with mildly to severely active ulcerative colitis were randomly assigned to receive 810 mg SOR102 once daily (n=7), 810 mg SOR102 twice daily (n=9), or placebo (n=6) for 42 days.\nThe primary end point of the study was safety and tolerability of SOR102.\n...\nAcross all parts, 48 participants received SOR102 and 16 received placebo.\nDuring part 1, 13%  of participants who received SOR102 (2 in the 135-mg arm and 1 in the 405-mg arm) experienced a treatment-emergent adverse event (TEAE) compared with 25% who received placebo.\nOnly one of the TEAEs (mild diarrhea) was deemed to be treatment related.\nNo TEAEs were reported during part 2.\nDuring part 3, 44% of the 16 patients who received SOR102 (5 in the twice-daily arm and 2 in the once-daily arm) experienced at least one TEAE compared with 50% of patients who received the placebo.\nTwo patients in the twice-daily arm experienced worsening of ulcerative colitis that led to treatment discontinuation.\nOf those two patients, one experienced severe worsening of ulcerative colitis, but it was not deemed to be treatment related, and it was the only serious TEAE reported.\nIn part 3 of the trial, by day 42, 43% of patients in the once-daily arm, 56% in the twice-daily arm, and 17% in the placebo arm had achieved a Mayo score clinical response.\nAccording to the modified Mayo score, 29%, 67%, and 33% of patients, respectively, achieved a clinical response.\nBy day 42, 43% of patients in the once-daily arm, 56% in the twice-daily arm, and none of the patients in the placebo arm had achieved symptomatic remission.\nStudy authors concluded that these findings “support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.”", "date": "2026-05-06", "last_updated": "2026-08-06"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR-102 | MedPath", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/drug/a31e54db33d16c00?tab=news&page=1", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "Sorriso Pharmaceuticals' SOR102, an oral dual-acting biologic, demonstrated a favorable safety and tolerability profile in a Phase 1b trial for ulcerative colitis.", "date": "2024-12-21", "last_updated": "2025-05-27"}], "raw": {"id": "acb7b297-3313-40e9-9f9f-4f6371b5bc4e"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:08+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q005", "objective": "SOR102 safety findings and adverse events in Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis", "anchors": ["SOR102", "SOR102-101"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q005-2", "query": "SOR102-101 registry identifier safety", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "SOR102-101 registry identifier safety", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 3728.2, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "PowerPoint-Präsentation", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-2024-presentation.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "Objectives of Parts 1 and 2:\n• To evaluate the safety and tolerability of \nSAD (Part 1) and MD (Part 2) of SOR102\n...\nSOR102-101 (NCT06080048) is a Phase 1, first in human study that consists of 3 parts.\nParts 1 and 2 enrolled healthy subjects (HS) age 18-55 years and evaluated single ascending (SAD) and multiple doses (MD) of SOR102, respectively.\n...\nSOR102 is Safe and Well-Tolerated\nNo safety or tolerability concerns noted at single doses up to 3,645mg or multiple doses of 1,215mg BID\n-No serious adverse events\n-No clinically significant laboratory, ECG, vitals, or physical exam findings\n-No subjects discontinued \nPart 1: One TEAE considered related to study drug (Cohort #1)\nPart 2: No adverse events\n...\nSystemic Exposure Of SOR102 And Its Monomers Was Not \nObserved; Low-Level ADA Signal in 3 of 40 Samples Tested \n...\nNo detectable levels of SOR102 or monomers in serum in Parts 1 and 2\n...\n•\nSOR102 was well tolerated at all tested doses.\n•\nSystemic exposure of SOR102 and its monomers was not observed, supporting its gut selectivity.\n•\nLow levels of intact SOR102 and high levels of active SOR102 monomers in faeces confirm that SOR102 is efficiently cleaved and maintains activity after oral dosing, as designed.\n...\nLow-level ADA positive signal observed in 2 subjects\n...\nPart 3, a Phase 1b study in patients with mild to severe ulcerative colitis, is ongoing.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-05-11"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Sorriso Pharmaceuticals, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/91d3bdda199f847ec2b338400a1a78ff", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "NCT06080048\n/ CompletedPhase 1\nA Ph I Randomized Double-Blind First-in-Human Single Ascending Dose & Multiple Dose Study to Investigate the Safety Tolerability and Pharmacokinetics of SOR102 in Healthy Adult Participants and Patient Volunteers With Mild to Severe Ulcerative Colitis\nSOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3).\n...\nThe primary objective was to evaluate the safety and tolerability of SOR102.\nSafety analyses were done on all participants or patients who received at least one dose of study drug.\nThis trial is registered with ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT06080048 and is complete.\n...\nFrom Oct 9, 2023, to Oct 16, 2024, 157 participants were assessed for eligibility, of whom 93 were excluded and 64 were randomly assigned to either SOR102 (n=48) or placebo (n=16) across all three parts of the study.\n...\nIn part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\n...\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nKey Highlights:\n...\n• Safety and Tolerability: SOR102 was dosed orally and was safe and well-tolerated in both healthy participants and UC patients.\nNo unexpected adverse events were observed.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "A Clinical Trial to Assess the Safety of SOR102 in Healthy ...", "url": "https://ctv.veeva.com/study/a-clinical-trial-to-assess-the-safety-of-sor102-in-healthy-participants-and-patients-with-ulcerative", "domain": "ctv.veeva.com", "snippet": "# A Clinical Trial to Assess the Safety of SOR102 in Healthy Participants and Patients With Ulcerative Colitis\n...\nStatus and phase\nCompleted\nPhase 1\nConditions\nUlcerative Colitis\n...\nIdentifiers\nNCT06080048\nSOR102-101\n...\nSOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3).\nEnrollment\n22 patients\n...\n18 to 75 years old\n...\nKey Exclusion Criteria:\n- Any diagnosis of IBD except for UC.\n- History of fistula(e), strictures or surgery, known intestinal obstruction, or diagnosis of toxic megacolon.\n- Concurrent use of any biologic drug.\n- Prior primary efficacy failure or secondary loss of response to more than one biologic or new small molecule therapy (i.e., JAK inhibitors or S1P receptor modulators) indicated for the treatment of UC.\nThis does not include prior discontinuation due to drug intolerance.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-03"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Phase 1b study of SOR102, a novel, orally delivered ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/ECCO-2025-presentation.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "SOR102-1011: Phase 1b Study Design\nRandomized, Double-Blinded, Placebo-Controlled in Mild to Severe UC\n\nSOR102-101 was a 3-part FIH study\nPart 1 (Single Ascending Dose) and Part 2 (Multiple Dose) in healthy volunteers showed no safety or tolerability\nconcerns at SOR102 doses above Phase 1b dose levels; results were presented previously2\n\nPart 3 was a Phase 1b, randomized, placebo-controlled, double-blinded, double-dummy, multiple dose exploratory study\n...\nPrior primary efficacy failure or secondary loss of response to >1 biologic or new small molecule therapy or\n...\n6-week SOR102 treatment: 810 mg QD, 810 mg BID, or placebo BID\n...\nPrimary Objective\nTo evaluate the safety and tolerability of multiple doses of SOR102 in patients\naged 18 to 75 years with mild to severe \nactive Ulcerative Colitis (UC)\n...\nConclusions\n• SOR102 was safe and well tolerated\n• In most samples tested, there were no detectable levels of \nSOR102 or monomers in serum\n• ADA titers were low in most patients and not associated with \ndecreased clinical response \n• Efficacy across multiple endpoints was observed in the SOR102 \nBID arm compared to QD and placebo, despite the short treatment duration\n• Further clinical development of SOR102 is warranted", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-14"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR-102 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c4ffffc6839e4416b41bd1cd9d28f2d9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "NCT06080048\n/ CompletedPhase 1\nA Ph I Randomized Double-Blind First-in-Human Single Ascending Dose & Multiple Dose Study to Investigate the Safety Tolerability and Pharmacokinetics of SOR102 in Healthy Adult Participants and Patient Volunteers With Mild to Severe Ulcerative Colitis\nSOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3).\n...\nWe aimed to assess the safety, pharmacokinetics, clinical, and biological activity of SOR102 in healthy participants and in patients with active ulcerative colitis.\n...\nThis phase 1 first-in-human, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, trial was done in the UK, Ukraine, and Georgia in clinical research centres.\n...\nThe primary objective was to evaluate the safety and tolerability of SOR102.\nSafety analyses were done on all participants or patients who received at least one dose of study drug.\nThis trial is registered with ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT06080048 and is complete.\n...\nFrom Oct 9, 2023, to Oct 16, 2024, 157 participants were assessed for eligibility, of whom 93 were excluded and 64 were randomly assigned to either SOR102 (n=48) or placebo (n=16) across all three parts of the study.\n...\nIn part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\nNo TEAEs occurred in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nThe randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial enrolled 42 healthy participants assigned to single ascending doses and multiple doses of SOR102 or placebo, as well as 22 patients with ulcerative colitis who were randomized to receive SOR102 one- or twice-daily, or placebo for 6 weeks.\nThe study assessed safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetics, pharmacodynamics, immunogenicity and preliminary efficacy.\n...\nKey Highlights:\n...\n• Safety and Tolerability: SOR102 was dosed orally and was safe and well-tolerated in both healthy participants and UC patients.\nNo unexpected adverse events were observed.\n...\n|NCT06080048 (SOR102-101, NEWS) Manual|Phase 1|Colitis, Ulcerative|22|High-dose SOR102SOR102 (ITT)|bmkefhuyvb(ekqxtmfpvy) = anvvkrslca yzltjrmvir (bkelhyedur ) View more|Positive|27 Feb 2025|", "date": "2025-01-17", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "PowerPoint Presentation", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/DDW-2025-poster.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "The Phase 1, first-in-human study (SOR102-101; NCT06080048) had 3 parts.\nParts 1 and \n2 enrolled healthy subjects (results presented previously).\nHere, we present safety and efficacy results for Part 3, a\nPhase 1b randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study in patients with mild to severe UC.\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\n...\nIn most samples tested, there were no detectable levels of SOR102 or monomers in serum\n...\nADA titers were low in most patients and not associated with safety or decreased clinical response.\n...\nEfficacy across multiple endpoints was observed in the SOR102 BID arm compared to QD and placebo, despite the short treatment duration.\n...\nSOR102 810mg QD, or placebo for 6 weeks.\n...\nMost ADA titers were low (<1:240) and not associated with safety or clinical response\n...\nTreatment emergent adverse events (TEAEs) were reported in 57%, \n29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 \n810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 2).\nMost were mild-to-\nmoderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\nOne unrelated serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-05-11"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "NCT06080048 - A Clinical Trial to Assess the Safety of SOR102 in Healthy Participants and Patients With Ulcerative Colitis", "url": "https://www.syfrah.com/trial/NCT06080048", "domain": "syfrah.com", "snippet": "SOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3).\n...\n90\nOn Track\n...\nEnrollment\n22\nparticipants targeted\nTarget at P25-P50 for phase_1\n...\nCompleted\nStarted Oct 2023\n...\n2 countries\n2 active sites\nStatus\ncompleted\n...\n### Primary Outcomes (1)\n- Treatment Emergent Adverse Events following oral dosing\nAfter multiple oral doses administered BID or QD, number of subjects with AEs in the SOR102 dose level cohorts compared with the oral placebo group\nUp to 6 weeks in Part 3 or at the time of study discontinuation\n...\n- SOR102 concentrations following oral dosing\n...\n### You may qualify if:\n- Male or females, of any ethnic origin.\n- Established diagnosis of UC by standard criteria for \\>3 months.\n- Disease evaluable by sigmoidoscopy.\n- Mildly to severely active UC as determined by central reader in combination with other assessments of disease\n### You may not qualify if:\n- Any diagnosis of IBD except for UC.\n- History of fistula(e), strictures or surgery, known intestinal obstruction, or diagnosis of toxic megacolon.\n- Concurrent use of any biologic drug.\n- Prior primary efficacy failure or secondary loss of response to more than one biologic or new small molecule therapy (i.e., JAK inhibitors or S1P receptor modulators) indicated for the treatment of UC.\nThis does not include prior discontinuation due to drug intolerance.\n...\nArensia Exploratory Medicine\nTbilisi, Georgia\nLocation\nArensia Exploratory Medicine\nKyiv, Ukraine", "date": "2023-10-12", "last_updated": "2026-09-11"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral Administration of SOR102 Delivers Anti-TNF/IL-23 ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-Week-2025-Poster-v4.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "22 patients were randomized (ITT; Table \n1), 17 patients completed the study Per \nProtocol (QD n=6, BID n=5, Placebo n=6).\n...\nNo clinically significant changes in safety\nlaboratory \nparameters, \ndose-limiting \ntoxicities, \nor \nevents \nsuggestive \nof \npronounced immunosuppression\n•\nAll but one TEAE were mild to moderate\n•\n1 severe and serious TEAE of worsening \nUC requiring hospitalization was not \nconsidered related to treatment.\nThis\nTEAE occurred during follow-up in a \nSOR102 BID patient\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\n...\nSOR102 BID demonstrated strong and consistent activity across multiple clinical and tissue PD endpoints in patients with UC.\n...\nstability to intestinal and inflammatory proteases, enabling oral dosing.2 The Phase 1, first-in-human study (SOR102-101; NCT06080048) had 3 parts.\nParts 1 and 2 enrolled healthy subjects (results presented previously).3 Part 3 was a Phase 1b randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study in patients with mild to severe UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-02-25"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "A Clinical Trial to Assess the Safety of SOR102 in Healthy Participants and Patients With Ulcerative Colitis", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT06080048", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "SOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3).\n## Conditions\n- Ulcerative Colitis\n...\n- **DRUG**\nSOR102 SOR102 capsules\n- **DRUG**\nPlacebo Matching placebo capsules\n...\n## Eligibility\n- **Min Age**\n18 Years\n- **Max Age**\n75 Years\n- **Sex**\nALL\n- **Healthy Volunteers**\nYes\n## Timeline & Regulatory\n...\n2023-10-24\n- **Primary Completion**\n2024-10-16\n- **Completion**\n2024-11-08", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "PHASE 1B STUDY OF SOR102, A NOVEL, ORALLY ...", "url": "https://eposters.ddw.org/ddw/2025/ddw-2025/4155984/vipul.jairath.phase.1b.study.of.sor102.a.novel.orally.delivered.bispecific.html?f=menu=6*browseby=8*sortby=2*ce_id=2855*ot_id=31400*marker=5698", "domain": "eposters.ddw.org", "snippet": "The Phase 1, first-in-human study (SOR102-101; NCT06080048) had 3 parts.\nParts 1 and 2 enrolled healthy subjects.\nHere, we present results for Part 3, a Phase 1b randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study in patients with mild to severe UC.\n...\nThe primary objective was safety and tolerability of SOR102.\n...\nTEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1).\nMost were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\n...\n**Conclusion**: SOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\nEfficacy across multiple endpoints was observed in the SOR102 BID arm compared to the QD and placebo arms, despite the relatively short duration of treatment.\n...\n**Conclusion**: SOR102 was safe and well tolerated.", "date": "2025-03-31", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}], "raw": {"id": "b78fd599-4521-4177-9ca6-27031ad52f1a"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:08+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q006", "objective": "Moderately to Severely Active Crohn's Disease Moderately to Severely Active Crohn’s Disease Refractory to Systemic Therapies Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis Refractory to Systemic Therapies Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis with inadequate response, loss of response, or intolerance to ≥1 biologic or novel oral with biologic-like activity competing agents in the same mechanistic class as JNJ-4804 Co Antibody Therapy, including Antibody Cocktail Monoclonal Antibody and IL-23 IL23A TNF-α Tumor Necrosis Factor Alpha therapies", "anchors": ["JNJ-4804"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q006-3", "query": "JNJ-4804 same mechanistic class IBD pipeline", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "JNJ-4804 same mechanistic class IBD pipeline", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2500.3, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Johnson & Johnson investigational co-antibody therapy JNJ-4804 ...", "url": "https://www.investor.jnj.com/investor-news/news-details/2026/Johnson--Johnson-investigational-co-antibody-therapy-JNJ-4804-shows-potential-to-raise-the-bar-for-clinical-efficacy-in-treating-refractory-inflammatory-bowel-disease/default.aspx?_sp=75fe9bba-8c8e-4470-a97f-efa1931f0f46", "domain": "investor.jnj.com", "snippet": "*JNJ-4804 demonstrated highest rates of clinical and endoscopic outcomes compared to golimumab and guselkumab in patients with ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes*\n*JNJ-4804 is the first and only fixed dose co-antibody designed to deliver molecular synergy in IBD by blocking the complementary IL-23 and TNF pathways*\n*Data from Phase 2b DUET studies show potential to address a critical unmet need and support advancement to Phase 3 trials*\nCHICAGO, May 5, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Johnson & Johnson (NYSE: JNJ) today announced Phase 2b data from two studies evaluating JNJ-4804, an investigational co-antibody therapy targeting both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) or Crohn's disease (CD) that is refractory to systemic therapies.\n...\n\"By targeting the orthogonal pathways of IL-23 and TNFα, JNJ-4804 yields a synergistic therapeutic approach.\nThe results from the DUET studies highlight its potential to change the treatment landscape.\"\nBased on results from the Phase 2b DUET studies, Johnson & Johnson will be initiating the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-CD trial in adults with moderately to severely active CD as well as the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-UC trial in adults with moderately to severely active UC.\n...\n** The DUET-UC (ulcerative colitis) and DUET-CD (Crohn's disease) studies are randomized, double-blind, dose-ranging Phase 2b studies comparing JNJ-4804 with placebo and active comparators, including guselkumab and golimumab.\n...\n** JNJ-4804 is an investigational co-antibody therapy designed to target both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), two inflammatory pathways involved in chronic immune-mediated diseases.\nJNJ-4804 is a fixed-dose combination of two proven therapies, guselkumab and golimumab, in a single, subcutaneous (SC) injection.\nJNJ-4804 is being studied in the Phase 2b DUET clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease and the Phase 2a AFFINITY study in active psoriatic arthritis (PsA).", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "News Details - JNJ Investor Relations", "url": "https://www.investor.jnj.com/investor-news/news-details/2026/Johnson--Johnson-investigational-co-antibody-therapy-JNJ-4804-shows-potential-to-raise-the-bar-for-clinical-efficacy-in-treating-refractory-inflammatory-bowel-disease/default.aspx?_sp=649ecbb8-bb9f-4dba-8dc0-824bc2fff7e2", "domain": "investor.jnj.com", "snippet": "*JNJ-4804 demonstrated highest rates of clinical and endoscopic outcomes compared to golimumab and guselkumab in patients with ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes*\n*JNJ-4804 is the first and only fixed dose co-antibody designed to deliver molecular synergy in IBD by blocking the complementary IL-23 and TNF pathways*\n*Data from Phase 2b DUET studies show potential to address a critical unmet need and support advancement to Phase 3 trials*\nCHICAGO, May 5, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Johnson & Johnson (NYSE: JNJ) today announced Phase 2b data from two studies evaluating JNJ-4804, an investigational co-antibody therapy targeting both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) or Crohn's disease (CD) that is refractory to systemic therapies.\n...\n\"By targeting the orthogonal pathways of IL-23 and TNFα, JNJ-4804 yields a synergistic therapeutic approach.\nThe results from the DUET studies highlight its potential to change the treatment landscape.\"\nBased on results from the Phase 2b DUET studies, Johnson & Johnson will be initiating the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-CD trial in adults with moderately to severely active CD as well as the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-UC trial in adults with moderately to severely active UC.\n...\n** The DUET-UC (ulcerative colitis) and DUET-CD (Crohn's disease) studies are randomized, double-blind, dose-ranging Phase 2b studies comparing JNJ-4804 with placebo and active comparators, including guselkumab and golimumab.\n...\n** JNJ-4804 is an investigational co-antibody therapy designed to target both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), two inflammatory pathways involved in chronic immune-mediated diseases.\nJNJ-4804 is a fixed-dose combination of two proven therapies, guselkumab and golimumab, in a single, subcutaneous (SC) injection.\nJNJ-4804 is being studied in the Phase 2b DUET clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease and the Phase 2a AFFINITY study in active psoriatic arthritis (PsA).", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Johnson & Johnson investigational co-antibody therapy JNJ-4804 ...", "url": "https://www.investor.jnj.com/investor-news/news-details/2026/Johnson--Johnson-investigational-co-antibody-therapy-JNJ-4804-shows-potential-to-raise-the-bar-for-clinical-efficacy-in-treating-refractory-inflammatory-bowel-disease/default.aspx?_sp=e3b61bd0-dde7-4baf-b9aa-d1c78da2ab25", "domain": "investor.jnj.com", "snippet": "*JNJ-4804 is the first and only fixed dose co-antibody designed to deliver molecular synergy in IBD by blocking the complementary IL-23 and TNF pathways*\n*Data from Phase 2b DUET studies show potential to address a critical unmet need and support advancement to Phase 3 trials*\nCHICAGO, May 5, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Johnson & Johnson (NYSE: JNJ) today announced Phase 2b data from two studies evaluating JNJ-4804, an investigational co-antibody therapy targeting both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) or Crohn's disease (CD) that is refractory to systemic therapies.\n...\n\"By targeting the orthogonal pathways of IL-23 and TNFα, JNJ-4804 yields a synergistic therapeutic approach.\nThe results from the DUET studies highlight its potential to change the treatment landscape.\"\nBased on results from the Phase 2b DUET studies, Johnson & Johnson will be initiating the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-CD trial in adults with moderately to severely active CD as well as the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-UC trial in adults with moderately to severely active UC.\n...\n** The DUET-UC (ulcerative colitis) and DUET-CD (Crohn's disease) studies are randomized, double-blind, dose-ranging Phase 2b studies comparing JNJ-4804 with placebo and active comparators, including guselkumab and golimumab.\n...\n** JNJ-4804 is an investigational co-antibody therapy designed to target both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), two inflammatory pathways involved in chronic immune-mediated diseases.\nJNJ-4804 is a fixed-dose combination of two proven therapies, guselkumab and golimumab, in a single, subcutaneous (SC) injection.\nJNJ-4804 is being studied in the Phase 2b DUET clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease and the Phase 2a AFFINITY study in active psoriatic arthritis (PsA).", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-08-14"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "efficacy and safety of the first co-antibody therapy, jnj- ...", "url": "https://www.jnjmedicalconnect.com/media/attestation/congresses/immunology/2026/ddw/efficacy-and-safety-of-the-first-coantibody-therapy-jnj78934804-in-patients-with-moderately-to-sever.pdf", "domain": "jnjmedicalconnect.com", "snippet": "JNJ-4804 (JNJ-78934804; guselkumab and golimumab fixed-dose combination), the first co-antibody therapy in IBD, aims to address the high unmet need in patients with disease refractory to current systemic therapies\nIn the Phase 2a VEGA study in ulcerative colitis,1,2 guselkumab and golimumab combination therapy showed higher efficacy over either monotherapy\nThis combination was informed by preclinical evidence of complementary and distinct IL-23 and TNF-α activity, with molecular synergy demonstrated\nin VEGA\n...\nCombination associated with reduced inflammation and restoration of epithelial repair3\nMolecular synergy observed in VEGA\n...\nThe Phase 2b DUET-UC trial (NCT05242484) evaluated the efficacy and safety of JNJ-4804 compared with the component monotherapies in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis refractory to systemic therapies\n...\nIn patients with disease refractory to two or more systemic therapy mechanisms, high-dose \nJNJ-4804 exceeded the additive effects of golimumab and guselkumab across all key clinical, endoscopic, and histologic-endoscopic endpoints\n...\nJNJ-4804, the first co-antibody therapy in development for IBD, delivered clinically meaningful efficacy exceeding that of the monotherapies in patients with treatment-refractory disease\nBuilding on the molecular synergy seen in VEGA, the DUET-UC results support advancement to Phase 3 in patients with disease refractory to systemic therapies, a population with high unmet need\nIn the overall population, efficacy of high-dose JNJ-4804 was superior to golimumab and numerically greater than guselkumab", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-14"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "golimumab/guselkumab (JNJ-4804) / J&J", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=88cea786-38b9-44cc-991d-4a77387520d6", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## EFFICACY AND SAFETY OF THE FIRST CO-ANTIBODY THERAPY, JNJ-78934804, IN PATIENTS WITH MODERATELY TO SEVERELY ACTIVE ULCERATIVE COLITIS REFRACTORY TO TWO OR MORE SYSTEMIC THERAPY MECHANISMS (UEGW 2026) - \"JNJ-4804, the first co-antibody therapy in development for IBD, delivered substantial and clinically meaningful efficacy in pts with UC refractory to ≥2 ST mechanisms. These benefits exceeded those of GOL or GUS alone. The JNJ-4804 safety profile in this subpopulation was similar to the monotherapies.\"\n...\n## EFFICACY AND SAFETY OF THE FIRST CO-ANTIBODY THERAPY, JNJ-78934804, IN PATIENTS WITH MODERATELY TO SEVERELY ACTIVE CROHN'S DISEASE REFRACTORY TO TWO OR MORE SYSTEMIC THERAPY MECHANISMS (UEGW 2026) - \"In the treatment-refractory subpopulation of pts with inadequate response to ≥2 ST mechanisms, JNJ-4804, the first co‑antibody therapy in development for IBD, delivered substantial and clinically meaningful efficacy, with benefits that exceeded those of GOL and GUS. The JNJ-4804 safety profile in this subpopulation was similar to the monotherapies. These results support advancement to Phase 3 in the rapidly growing population of patients with disease refractory to STs.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Johnson & Johnson Advances Dual-Pathway Strategy in Refractory IBD | BioPharm International", "url": "https://www.biopharminternational.com/view/johnson-johnson-advances-dual-pathway-strategy-in-refractory-ibd", "domain": "biopharminternational.com", "snippet": "### Key Takeaways\n- A fixed-dose engineered co-antibody approach aims to simultaneously suppress TNF-driven inflammation and IL-23/Th17 mucosal immune activation to address incomplete control seen with single-pathway biologics.\n...\n- Further development depends on Phase 3 trials to validate durability, safety, and benefit–risk across broader UC/CD populations within a rapidly evolving IBD biologics landscape anchored by IL-23 axis efficacy.\n...\nJohnson & Johnson reported Phase 2b results for its investigational dual-pathway co-antibody JNJ-4804, showing improved clinical remission and endoscopic outcomes in patients with refractory inflammatory bowel disease.\nJohnson & Johnson has reported Phase 2b results for its investigational co-antibody therapy JNJ-4804, suggesting potential clinical benefit in patients with highly refractory inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), including ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD).\n...\nJNJ-4804 is designed to simultaneously inhibit interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-alpha, two key inflammatory drivers in IBD pathogenesis.\nThe therapy combines mechanisms of guselkumab and golimumab into a single fixed-dose co-antibody intended to enhance immune modulation.^1^\n...\nThe Phase 2b DUET-UC and DUET-CD studies evaluated JNJ-4804 in patients with moderately to severely active UC and CD who had inadequate response to prior biologics or systemic therapies.\n...\nAcross cohorts, JNJ-4804 demonstrated higher clinical remission and endoscopic response rates compared with golimumab monotherapy and numerically higher outcomes versus guselkumab alone.\n...\nThe development of JNJ-4804 reflects a broader immunology strategy aimed at targeting multiple inflammatory pathways simultaneously.\nTNF drives systemic inflammation and tissue injury, while IL-23 contributes to Th17-mediated mucosal immune activation, both central to IBD pathophysiology.^4^\nBy combining these mechanisms, dual-pathway inhibition is designed to address complementary biological drivers of disease that may not be fully suppressed by single agents.\n...\nJohnson & Johnson maintains a significant immunology portfolio, including guselkumab and golimumab, which form the mechanistic foundation of JNJ-4804.\nIf successful in later-stage trials, the co-antibody could represent a novel engineered combination biologic approach in gastroenterology.^1^\nJNJ-4804 remains investigational, and further Phase 3 evaluation will be required to confirm efficacy and safety across broader patient populations.", "date": "2026-05-08", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "EFFICACY AND SAFETY OF THE FIRST CO-ANTIBODY THERAPY, JNJ ...", "url": "https://www.jnjmedicalconnect.com/media/attestation/congresses/immunology/2026/ddw/efficacy-and-safety-of-the-first-coantibody-therapy-jnj78934804-in-patients.pdf", "domain": "jnjmedicalconnect.com", "snippet": "JNJ-4804 (JNJ-78934804; guselkumab and golimumab fixed-dose combination), the first co-antibody therapy in IBD, aims to address the high unmet need in patients with disease refractory to current systemic therapies\nObjective: The Phase 2b DUET-CD trial (NCT05242471) evaluated the efficacy and safety of JNJ-4804 compared with the component monotherapies in patients with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease refractory to systemic therapies\nIn the Phase 2a VEGA study in ulcerative colitis,1,2 guselkumab and golimumab combination therapy showed higher efficacy over either monotherapy\n...\nCombination associated with reduced inflammation and restoration of epithelial repair3\nMolecular synergy observed in VEGA\n...\nIn patients with disease refractory to two or more systemic therapy mechanisms, high-dose JNJ-4804 exceeded golimumab and guselkumab, with more than additive benefits in high-bar endoscopic and deep remission outcomes\n...\nThe safety profile of JNJ-4804 through 48 weeks was consistent with the well-established safety profiles of the component monotherapies​\nBuilding on the molecular synergy seen in VEGA, the DUET-CD results support advancement to Phase 3 in the rapidly growing population of patients with disease refractory to systemic therapies\n...\nJNJ-4804, the first co-antibody therapy in development for IBD, demonstrated clinically meaningful efficacy exceeding that of the monotherapies in patients with treatment-refractory Crohn’s disease", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-10"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "J&J pushes dual-antibody IBD therapy into Phase 3 ...", "url": "https://www.biospace.com/drug-development/j-j-pushes-dual-antibody-ibd-therapy-into-phase-3-despite-mid-stage-fails", "domain": "biospace.com", "snippet": "## Johnson & Johnson plans to advance the co-antibody therapy, which combines its IL-23 blocker Tremfya and the TNF-alpha inhibitor Simponi, into late-stage testing for Crohn’s disease and ulcerative colitis.\nJohnson & Johnson’s investigational antibody combo failed to significantly improve remission and response in a pair of mid-stage trials of inflammatory bowel diseases, but that isn’t stopping the pharma from forging ahead to pivotal testing.\nThe investigational co-antibody therapy, dubbed JNJ-4804, is a fixed-dose combination of the pharma’s anti-IL-23 therapy Tremfya and TNF-alpha blocker Simponi.\nCombining both molecules in one therapy “yields a synergistic therapeutic approach” for patients resistant to monotherapies, Esi Lamousé Smith, vice president of J&J’s Gastroenterology Disease Area, said in a Tuesday release.\n...\nIn the Phase 2b DUET-CD study, high-dose JNJ-4804 showed significantly higher clinical remission and endoscopic response rates versus monotherapy with Simponi, but not versus Tremfya.\n...\nThe mid-stage DUET-UC study, which focused on ulcerative colitis, reached similar outcomes—JNJ-4804 elicited significantly greater clinical remission than Simponi but not Tremfya.\n...\nIn these populations, according to the pharma, JNJ-4804 elicited “meaningful improvements across multiple clinical and endoscopic measures.”\nThese signals warrant further development, according to J&J, which on Tuesday said it would launch the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-CD study in Crohn’s disease and DUET ENCORE-UC study in ulcerative colitis.\n“Although the DUET studies missed the primary endpoint, we do believe the positive trends support the idea that combination approaches are needed to raise the efficacy bar in IBD,” William Blair analysts wrote to investors on Tuesday.\n...\nJ&J hasn’t yet specified its target population for the DUET ENCORE programs, nor has it said when the studies are set to start.\n...\n- Annalee Armstrong\n- Annalee Armstrong", "date": "2026-05-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Selected Innovative Medicines in Development as of April 14 ...", "url": "https://s203.q4cdn.com/636242992/files/doc_financials/2026/q1/JNJ-Pipeline-1Q26.pdf", "domain": "s203.q4cdn.com", "snippet": "JNJ-4804 Co-antibody Therapy \nJNJ-4804 Co-antibody Therapy \nJNJ-4804 Co-antibody Therapy \nUlcerative Colitis\nPsoriatic Arthritis\nCrohn's Disease\n...\n*This is not a fully exhaustive list of all pipeline programs and assets.\nThe pipeline includes assets currently progressing through clinical trials as well as those under review by regulatory bodies.\nInclusion in the pipeline is based on the current status of these\nprograms and assets and does not guarantee continued investment.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-27"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Combination therapy could improve outcomes in the most ...", "url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-05-combination-therapy-outcomes-difficult-patients.html", "domain": "medicalxpress.com", "snippet": "\"By combining two mechanisms of action, we're seeing efficacy that appears to be additive—without increasing safety risk.\"\nThe findings come from two parallel Phase IIb trials—DUET-Crohn's and DUET-UC—testing JNJ-78934804 (JNJ-4804), a new combination fixed-dose co-antibody therapy designed to target two inflammatory pathways simultaneously.\nTaken alone, each drug—guselkumab and golimumab—targets a different protein that drives inflammation.\nBlocking both pathways at once, the combination aims to achieve deeper, more lasting results.\n...\nBoth trials enrolled patients with moderate-to-severely active disease who had already failed one or more therapies, including biologics and novel oral agents.\nFor those patients who had failed two or more prior treatments, high-dose JNJ-4804 showed improved outcomes compared with golimumab and similar efficacy to guselkumab across key measures of remission and endoscopic improvement at 48 weeks.\nThe trials shared the same design.\nPatients were randomized to receive either a placebo, one of two existing biologics—golimumab, an anti-TNF therapy, or guselkumab, an anti-IL-23 therapy—or JNJ-4804, which combines both, at one of three doses.\n...\nIn the Crohn's disease trial, patients receiving high-dose JNJ-4804 were significantly more likely to achieve clinical remission and endoscopic response at 48 weeks than those receiving golimumab alone.\n...\nResults in the ulcerative colitis trial followed a similar pattern.\nHigh-dose JNJ-4804 significantly outperformed golimumab on the primary endpoint of clinical remission at 48 weeks, and the combination's advantage was again most pronounced in patients who had failed two or more prior treatments.\n...\nThe safety profile of JNJ-4804 was comparable to either monotherapy used alone.\nSerious adverse events were uncommon across both trials and primarily gastrointestinal in nature.\nBased on these results, the researchers say the combination is ready to advance to Phase III trials.", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-13"}], "raw": {"id": "030d1660-59bc-4fc7-a91c-4d4cbccf910e"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:11+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q006", "objective": "Moderately to Severely Active Crohn's Disease Moderately to Severely Active Crohn’s Disease Refractory to Systemic Therapies Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis Refractory to Systemic Therapies Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis with inadequate response, loss of response, or intolerance to ≥1 biologic or novel oral with biologic-like activity competing agents in the same mechanistic class as JNJ-4804 Co Antibody Therapy, including Antibody Cocktail Monoclonal Antibody and IL-23 IL23A TNF-α Tumor Necrosis Factor Alpha therapies", "anchors": ["JNJ-4804"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q006-4", "query": "JNJ-4804 competing IL-23 TNF-α antibody Crohn's disease ulcerative colitis", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "JNJ-4804 competing IL-23 TNF-α antibody Crohn's disease ulcerative colitis", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2207.4, "n_results": 3, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "JNJ-4804 demonstrated highest rates of clinical ...", "url": "https://www.jnj.com/media-center/press-releases/johnson-johnson-investigational-co-antibody-therapy-jnj-4804-shows-potential-to-raise-the-bar-for-clinical-efficacy-in-treating-refractory-inflammatory-bowel-disease", "domain": "jnj.com", "snippet": "## JNJ-4804 demonstrated highest rates of clinical and endoscopic outcomes compared to golimumab and guselkumab in patients with ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes JNJ-4804 is the first and only fixed dose co-antibody designed to deliver molecular synergy in IBD by blocking the complementary IL-23 and TNF pathways Data from Phase 2b DUET studies show potential to address a critical unmet need and support advancement to Phase 3 trials\n...\n**Chicago, Ill., (May 5, 2026) **– Johnson & Johnson (NYSE: JNJ) today announced Phase 2b data from two studies evaluating JNJ-4804, an investigational co-antibody therapy targeting both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD) that is refractory to systemic therapies.\n...\nIn both studies at Week 48, JNJ-4804 showed meaningful improvements across multiple clinical and endoscopic measures for a subpopulation of patients who are considered highly refractory and have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes.\n...\nIn the overall population, JNJ-4804^a^ demonstrated higher clinical remission^b^ rates (50.8%) and endoscopic response^c ^rates (38.1%) versus golimumab (25.4% for clinical remission, 19.8% for endoscopic response) at Week 48.\nThe JNJ-4804 rates were also numerically higher than the rates achieved with guselkumab (42.5% for clinical remission and 33.9% for endoscopic response).\nIn the highly refractory subpopulation of patients with CD who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes, JNJ-4804 showed clinically meaningful improvements at Week 48 across multiple clinical and endoscopic endpoints compared to golimumab and guselkumab monotherapies, and placebo.\nClinical remission rates for JNJ-4804 were nearly double the highest comparator and more than 60% higher in endoscopic response rate.\n...\nIn the overall population, JNJ-4804^a^ demonstrated superior clinical remission^d^ rates compared to golimumab, with 41.0% of JNJ-4804-treated patients achieving clinical remission at Week 48 versus golimumab (11.5%), and numerically higher rates than guselkumab (34.0%).\nIn the highly refractory subpopulation of patients with UC who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes, JNJ-4804 showed clinically meaningful improvements at Week 48 across multiple clinical and endoscopic endpoints compared to golimumab and guselkumab monotherapies, and placebo, with the clinical remission rate for JNJ-4804 being almost 60% higher than the closest comparator.\n...\nIn both studies, safety findings were generally consistent with the known profiles of the component monotherapies.\n...\n“By targeting the orthogonal pathways of IL-23 and TNFα, JNJ-4804 yields a synergistic therapeutic approach.\nThe results from the DUET studies highlight its potential to change the treatment landscape.”\nBased on results from the Phase 2b DUET studies, Johnson & Johnson will be initiating the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-CD trial in adults with moderately to severely active CD as well as the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-UC trial in adults with moderately to severely active UC.\n...\nThe DUET-UC (ulcerative colitis) and DUET-CD (Crohn’s disease) studies are randomized, double-blind, dose-ranging Phase 2b studies comparing JNJ-4804 with placebo and active comparators, including guselkumab and golimumab.\n...\nJNJ-4804 is an investigational co-antibody therapy designed to target both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), two inflammatory pathways involved in chronic immune-mediated diseases.\nJNJ-4804 is a fixed-dose combination of two proven therapies, guselkumab and golimumab, in a single, subcutaneous (SC) injection.\nJNJ-4804 is being studied in the Phase 2b DUET clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease and the Phase 2a AFFINITY study in active psoriatic arthritis (PsA).\n...\nDeveloped by Johnson & Johnson, TREMFYA is the first fully-human, dual-acting monoclonal antibody designed to neutralize inflammation at the cellular source by blocking IL-23 and binding to CD64 (a receptor on cells that produce IL-23).", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "J&J pushes dual-antibody IBD therapy into Phase 3 ...", "url": "https://www.biospace.com/drug-development/j-j-pushes-dual-antibody-ibd-therapy-into-phase-3-despite-mid-stage-fails", "domain": "biospace.com", "snippet": "## Johnson & Johnson plans to advance the co-antibody therapy, which combines its IL-23 blocker Tremfya and the TNF-alpha inhibitor Simponi, into late-stage testing for Crohn’s disease and ulcerative colitis.\nJohnson & Johnson’s investigational antibody combo failed to significantly improve remission and response in a pair of mid-stage trials of inflammatory bowel diseases, but that isn’t stopping the pharma from forging ahead to pivotal testing.\nThe investigational co-antibody therapy, dubbed JNJ-4804, is a fixed-dose combination of the pharma’s anti-IL-23 therapy Tremfya and TNF-alpha blocker Simponi.\nCombining both molecules in one therapy “yields a synergistic therapeutic approach” for patients resistant to monotherapies, Esi Lamousé Smith, vice president of J&J’s Gastroenterology Disease Area, said in a Tuesday release.\nData presented Tuesday at Digestive Disease Week 2026 in Chicago, however, haven’t borne that out.\nIn the Phase 2b DUET-CD study, high-dose JNJ-4804 showed significantly higher clinical remission and endoscopic response rates versus monotherapy with Simponi, but not versus Tremfya.\nThis study focused on patients with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease with poor responses or intolerance to at least one prior line of systemic therapy.\nThe mid-stage DUET-UC study, which focused on ulcerative colitis, reached similar outcomes—JNJ-4804 elicited significantly greater clinical remission than Simponi but not Tremfya.\nEndoscopic improvement, which in this study was set as a secondary endpoint, was “numerically higher” in those treated with the combo therapy versus Tremfya or Simponi alone.\nIn a news release shared alongside these abstracts, however, J&J homed in on patients “who are considered highly refractory”—those who have shown suboptimal response to at least two classes of systemic therapies.\nIn these populations, according to the pharma, JNJ-4804 elicited “meaningful improvements across multiple clinical and endoscopic measures.”\nThese signals warrant further development, according to J&J, which on Tuesday said it would launch the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-CD study in Crohn’s disease and DUET ENCORE-UC study in ulcerative colitis.\n“Although the DUET studies missed the primary endpoint, we do believe the positive trends support the idea that combination approaches are needed to raise the efficacy bar in IBD,” William Blair analysts wrote to investors on Tuesday.\n“However, with the signal being driven by the most refractory patients, we believe it also highlights the importance of targeting the right subpopulation,” they added.\nJ&J hasn’t yet specified its target population for the DUET ENCORE programs, nor has it said when the studies are set to start.\n...\n- Annalee Armstrong\n- Annalee Armstrong", "date": "2026-05-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Study highlights breakthrough in overcoming diminishing returns of sequential IBD therapies", "url": "https://www.news-medical.net/news/20260505/Study-highlights-breakthrough-in-overcoming-diminishing-returns-of-sequential-IBD-therapies.aspx", "domain": "news-medical.net", "snippet": "For patients with Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis who have exhausted other treatment options, a new combination therapy is showing results that offer hope for one of medicine's most treatment-resistant populations, according to findings from two studies to be presented today at Digestive Disease Week® (DDW) 2026.\n...\n> By combining two mechanisms of action, we're seeing efficacy that appears to be additive - without increasing safety risk.\"\n...\nThe findings come from two parallel Phase 2b trials - DUET-Crohn's and DUET-UC - testing JNJ-78934804 (JNJ-4804), a new combination fixed-dose co-antibody therapy designed to target two inflammatory pathways simultaneously.\nTaken alone, each drug - guselkumab and golimumab - targets a different protein that drives inflammation.\nBlocking both pathways at once, the combination aims to achieve deeper, more lasting results.\n...\nBoth trials enrolled patients with moderate-to-severely active disease who had already failed one or more therapies, including biologics and novel oral agents.\nFor those patients who had failed two or more prior treatments, high-dose JNJ-4804 showed improved outcomes compared with golimumab and similar efficacy to guselkumab across key measures of remission and endoscopic improvement at 48 weeks.\n...\nThe trials shared the same design.\nPatients were randomized to receive either a placebo, one of two existing biologics - golimumab, an anti-TNF therapy, or guselkumab, an anti-IL-23 therapy - or JNJ-4804, which combines both, at one of three doses.\nThe Crohn's trial enrolled 693 patients; the UC trial enrolled 572.\n...\nIn the Crohn's disease trial, patients receiving high-dose JNJ-4804 were significantly more likely to achieve clinical remission and endoscopic response at 48 weeks than those receiving golimumab alone.\nThe advantage was clearest in the most treatment-resistant patients - those who had failed two or more prior therapies.\nIn that subgroup, clinical remission rates with high-dose JNJ-4804 were more than 20 percentage points higher than guselkumab in this subgroup, and nearly 40 percentage points higher than placebo.\nResults in the ulcerative colitis trial followed a similar pattern.\nHigh-dose JNJ-4804 significantly outperformed golimumab on the primary endpoint of clinical remission at 48 weeks, and the combination's advantage was again most pronounced in patients who had failed two or more prior treatments.\nIn that subgroup, clinical remission rates ran more than 20 percentage points higher than guselkumab and nearly 20 percentage points higher than placebo, with similarly meaningful gaps in endoscopic improvement and histologic remission, a measure of healing at the tissue level.\nThe safety profile of JNJ-4804 was comparable to either monotherapy used alone.\nSerious adverse events were uncommon across both trials and primarily gastrointestinal in nature.\nBased on these results, the researchers say the combination is ready to advance to Phase 3 trials.\nBoth studies were sponsored by Johnson & Johnson.", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}], "raw": {"id": "36d7fd7c-52e7-4452-be72-79290d6bb568"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:13+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q006", "objective": "Moderately to Severely Active Crohn's Disease Moderately to Severely Active Crohn’s Disease Refractory to Systemic Therapies Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis Refractory to Systemic Therapies Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis with inadequate response, loss of response, or intolerance to ≥1 biologic or novel oral with biologic-like activity competing agents in the same mechanistic class as JNJ-4804 Co Antibody Therapy, including Antibody Cocktail Monoclonal Antibody and IL-23 IL23A TNF-α Tumor Necrosis Factor Alpha therapies", "anchors": ["JNJ-4804"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q006-2", "query": "JNJ-4804 IL-23 TNF-α dual antibody competing agents IBD", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "JNJ-4804 IL-23 TNF-α dual antibody competing agents IBD", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 4823.9, "n_results": 8, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "JNJ-4804 demonstrated highest rates of clinical ...", "url": "https://www.jnj.com/media-center/press-releases/johnson-johnson-investigational-co-antibody-therapy-jnj-4804-shows-potential-to-raise-the-bar-for-clinical-efficacy-in-treating-refractory-inflammatory-bowel-disease", "domain": "jnj.com", "snippet": "## JNJ-4804 demonstrated highest rates of clinical and endoscopic outcomes compared to golimumab and guselkumab in patients with ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes JNJ-4804 is the first and only fixed dose co-antibody designed to deliver molecular synergy in IBD by blocking the complementary IL-23 and TNF pathways Data from Phase 2b DUET studies show potential to address a critical unmet need and support advancement to Phase 3 trials\n...\n**Chicago, Ill., (May 5, 2026) **– Johnson & Johnson (NYSE: JNJ) today announced Phase 2b data from two studies evaluating JNJ-4804, an investigational co-antibody therapy targeting both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD) that is refractory to systemic therapies.\n...\nIn both studies at Week 48, JNJ-4804 showed meaningful improvements across multiple clinical and endoscopic measures for a subpopulation of patients who are considered highly refractory and have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes.\n...\nIn the overall population, JNJ-4804^a^ demonstrated higher clinical remission^b^ rates (50.8%) and endoscopic response^c ^rates (38.1%) versus golimumab (25.4% for clinical remission, 19.8% for endoscopic response) at Week 48.\nThe JNJ-4804 rates were also numerically higher than the rates achieved with guselkumab (42.5% for clinical remission and 33.9% for endoscopic response).\nIn the highly refractory subpopulation of patients with CD who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes, JNJ-4804 showed clinically meaningful improvements at Week 48 across multiple clinical and endoscopic endpoints compared to golimumab and guselkumab monotherapies, and placebo.\nClinical remission rates for JNJ-4804 were nearly double the highest comparator and more than 60% higher in endoscopic response rate.\n...\nIn the overall population, JNJ-4804^a^ demonstrated superior clinical remission^d^ rates compared to golimumab, with 41.0% of JNJ-4804-treated patients achieving clinical remission at Week 48 versus golimumab (11.5%), and numerically higher rates than guselkumab (34.0%).\nIn the highly refractory subpopulation of patients with UC who have had inadequate response to two or more systemic therapy classes, JNJ-4804 showed clinically meaningful improvements at Week 48 across multiple clinical and endoscopic endpoints compared to golimumab and guselkumab monotherapies, and placebo, with the clinical remission rate for JNJ-4804 being almost 60% higher than the closest comparator.\n...\nIn both studies, safety findings were generally consistent with the known profiles of the component monotherapies.\n...\n“By targeting the orthogonal pathways of IL-23 and TNFα, JNJ-4804 yields a synergistic therapeutic approach.\n...\nThe DUET-UC (ulcerative colitis) and DUET-CD (Crohn’s disease) studies are randomized, double-blind, dose-ranging Phase 2b studies comparing JNJ-4804 with placebo and active comparators, including guselkumab and golimumab.\n...\nJNJ-4804 is an investigational co-antibody therapy designed to target both interleukin-23 (IL-23) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), two inflammatory pathways involved in chronic immune-mediated diseases.\nJNJ-4804 is a fixed-dose combination of two proven therapies, guselkumab and golimumab, in a single, subcutaneous (SC) injection.", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "EFFICACY AND SAFETY OF THE FIRST CO-ANTIBODY THERAPY, JNJ ...", "url": "https://www.jnjmedicalconnect.com/media/attestation/congresses/immunology/2026/ddw/efficacy-and-safety-of-the-first-coantibody-therapy-jnj78934804-in-patients.pdf", "domain": "jnjmedicalconnect.com", "snippet": "JNJ-4804 (JNJ-78934804; guselkumab and golimumab fixed-dose combination), the first co-antibody therapy in IBD, aims to address the high unmet need in patients with disease refractory to current systemic therapies\nObjective: The Phase 2b DUET-CD trial (NCT05242471) evaluated the efficacy and safety of JNJ-4804 compared with the component monotherapies in patients with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease refractory to systemic therapies\nIn the Phase 2a VEGA study in ulcerative colitis,1,2 guselkumab and golimumab combination therapy showed higher efficacy over either monotherapy\n...\nCombination associated with reduced inflammation and restoration of epithelial repair3\n...\nInadequate response or intolerance to ≥1 systemic therapy mechanism (anti-TNF, IL-12/23, IL-23p19, integrin, or JAK inhibitors)\n...\nIn patients with disease refractory to two or more systemic therapy mechanisms, high-dose JNJ-4804 exceeded golimumab and guselkumab, with more than additive benefits in high-bar endoscopic and deep remission outcomes\nIn the overall population, efficacy of high-dose JNJ-4804 was superior to golimumab and numerically greater than guselkumab\nThe safety profile of JNJ-4804 through 48 weeks was consistent with the well-established safety profiles of the component monotherapies​\n...\nJNJ-4804, the first co-antibody therapy in development for IBD, demonstrated clinically meaningful efficacy exceeding that of the monotherapies in patients with treatment-refractory Crohn’s disease", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-10"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "efficacy and safety of the first co-antibody therapy, jnj- ...", "url": "https://www.jnjmedicalconnect.com/media/attestation/congresses/immunology/2026/ddw/efficacy-and-safety-of-the-first-coantibody-therapy-jnj78934804-in-patients-with-moderately-to-sever.pdf", "domain": "jnjmedicalconnect.com", "snippet": "JNJ-4804 (JNJ-78934804; guselkumab and golimumab fixed-dose combination), the first co-antibody therapy in IBD, aims to address the high unmet need in patients with disease refractory to current systemic therapies\nIn the Phase 2a VEGA study in ulcerative colitis,1,2 guselkumab and golimumab combination therapy showed higher efficacy over either monotherapy\nThis combination was informed by preclinical evidence of complementary and distinct IL-23 and TNF-α activity, with molecular synergy demonstrated\nin VEGA\n...\nCombination associated with reduced inflammation and restoration of epithelial repair3\n...\nThe Phase 2b DUET-UC trial (NCT05242484) evaluated the efficacy and safety of JNJ-4804 compared with the component monotherapies in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis refractory to systemic therapies\n...\nIn patients with disease refractory to two or more systemic therapy mechanisms, high-dose \nJNJ-4804 exceeded the additive effects of golimumab and guselkumab across all key clinical, endoscopic, and histologic-endoscopic endpoints\nThe safety profile of JNJ-4804 through 48 weeks was consistent with the well-established safety profiles of the component monotherapies​\n...\nJNJ-4804, the first co-antibody therapy in development for IBD, delivered clinically meaningful efficacy exceeding that of the monotherapies in patients with treatment-refractory disease\nBuilding on the molecular synergy seen in VEGA, the DUET-UC results support advancement to Phase 3 in patients with disease refractory to systemic therapies, a population with high unmet need\nIn the overall population, efficacy of high-dose JNJ-4804 was superior to golimumab and numerically greater than guselkumab", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-14"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease Market to Experience Strong ...", "url": "https://news.futunn.com/en/post/76868809/inflammatory-bowel-disease-market-to-experience-strong-uptake-during-the", "domain": "news.futunn.com", "snippet": "- Growing Adoption of Advanced Biologic Therapies: The widespread use of biologics targeting TNF-α, IL-12/23, IL-23, and integrins has transformed IBD treatment.\nTheir superior efficacy in achieving long-term remission is driving higher treatment uptake and market value.\n...\n- The IBD treatment landscape is becoming increasingly competitive with the introduction of several advanced biologic therapies.\nIL-23 inhibitors, including Mirikizumab (OMVOH), Risankizumab (SKYRIZI), and Guselkumab (TREMFYA), are competing alongside well-established TNF inhibitors such as Adalimumab (HUMIRA) and Infliximab (REMICADE).\n...\n- Among the IL-23 inhibitors, Risankizumab (SKYRIZI) and Guselkumab (TREMFYA) are emerging as major competitors, each seeking to strengthen its position in the moderate-to-severe IBD market and capitalize on the growing demand for targeted biologic therapies.", "date": "2026-07-30", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "JNJ-4804 Doubles Remission in Refractory Crohn's - allsci.com", "url": "https://allsci.com/news/clinical-trials/johnson-johnsons-jnj-4804-doubles-clinical-remission-in-phase-iib-refractory-crohns-disease-trial/", "domain": "allsci.com", "snippet": "Johnson & Johnson (NYSE: JNJ) reported Phase IIb data showing that JNJ-4804 (also known as JNJ-78934804), its investigational co-antibody combining guselkumab and golimumab, produced clinical remission rates roughly double those of golimumab monotherapy at Week 48 in patients with refractory Crohn's disease.\nThere were also comparable directional advantages in ulcerative colitis, prompting the company to announce plans for two Phase III trials.\n...\nThe DUET-CD and DUET-UC studies are randomized, double-blind, dose-ranging Phase IIb trials comparing JNJ-4804 against placebo and active comparators — golimumab and Tremfya (guselkumab) — in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis who had inadequate response or intolerance to one or more systemic therapy classes.\n...\nThe rationale for the JNJ-4804 inflammatory bowel disease program rests on the observation that IBD involves at least two non-redundant inflammatory axes.\nTNF-α drives acute mucosal inflammation and is the target of the most widely used biologic class in IBD, while IL-23 governs downstream Th17-mediated chronic inflammation.\nBlocking one pathway while the other remains active may explain the loss of response that is common with monotherapy over time.\nJNJ-4804 delivers a fixed-dose combination of guselkumab and golimumab in a single subcutaneous injection, aiming to achieve simultaneous pathway suppression without requiring separate dosing of two agents.\n...\nIn the overall population, the JNJ-4804 high-dose group achieved clinical remission in 50.8% of patients, compared with 25.4% for golimumab and 42.5% for guselkumab.\nEndoscopic response rates followed a similar pattern: 38.1% for JNJ-4804 versus 19.8% for golimumab and 33.9% for guselkumab.\n...\nIn that population, the company said clinical remission rates for JNJ-4804 were nearly double the highest comparator rate, and endoscopic response was more than 60% higher than the next best arm.\n...\nGolimumab is an established TNF inhibitor approved in other inflammatory conditions, and its performance in this trial provides a direct benchmark for the incremental contribution of the IL-23 component.\nThe roughly 25-percentage-point difference in clinical remission between JNJ-4804 and golimumab in the overall CD population is directionally consistent with the hypothesis that dual-pathway blockade adds meaningful efficacy in patients where TNF inhibition alone is insufficient.\n...\nJNJ-4804 ulcerative colitis remission rates reached 41.0% in the overall population, compared with 11.5% for golimumab and 34.0% for guselkumab.\n...\nThe refractory IBD treatment landscape has expanded considerably over the past decade, with approved options now spanning TNF inhibitors, IL-12/23 inhibitors, IL-23 p19 inhibitors, integrin inhibitors, and JAK inhibitors.\n...\nThe Johnson & Johnson antibody therapy approach differs from combination regimens that are administered as separate agents — such as the investigational use of vedolizumab plus an anti-TNF — by delivering both components in a single fixed-dose subcutaneous injection.\n...\nGuselkumab, one of JNJ-4804's components, already holds FDA approval for moderately to severely active UC and CD under the Tremfya brand, which means the IL-23 contribution to JNJ-4804's efficacy is not entirely novel.\n...\nJohnson & Johnson said it will initiate the Phase III DUET ENCORE-CD trial in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease and the Phase III DUET ENCORE-UC trial in adults with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis, with the Phase IIb DUET data serving as the basis for advancing to registration-enabling studies.", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-08-10"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "J&J pushes dual-antibody IBD therapy into Phase 3 ...", "url": "https://www.biospace.com/drug-development/j-j-pushes-dual-antibody-ibd-therapy-into-phase-3-despite-mid-stage-fails", "domain": "biospace.com", "snippet": "## Johnson & Johnson plans to advance the co-antibody therapy, which combines its IL-23 blocker Tremfya and the TNF-alpha inhibitor Simponi, into late-stage testing for Crohn’s disease and ulcerative colitis.\nJohnson & Johnson’s investigational antibody combo failed to significantly improve remission and response in a pair of mid-stage trials of inflammatory bowel diseases, but that isn’t stopping the pharma from forging ahead to pivotal testing.\nThe investigational co-antibody therapy, dubbed JNJ-4804, is a fixed-dose combination of the pharma’s anti-IL-23 therapy Tremfya and TNF-alpha blocker Simponi.\nCombining both molecules in one therapy “yields a synergistic therapeutic approach” for patients resistant to monotherapies, Esi Lamousé Smith, vice president of J&J’s Gastroenterology Disease Area, said in a Tuesday release.\n...\nIn the Phase 2b DUET-CD study, high-dose JNJ-4804 showed significantly higher clinical remission and endoscopic response rates versus monotherapy with Simponi, but not versus Tremfya.\nThis study focused on patients with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease with poor responses or intolerance to at least one prior line of systemic therapy.\nThe mid-stage DUET-UC study, which focused on ulcerative colitis, reached similar outcomes—JNJ-4804 elicited significantly greater clinical remission than Simponi but not Tremfya.\nEndoscopic improvement, which in this study was set as a secondary endpoint, was “numerically higher” in those treated with the combo therapy versus Tremfya or Simponi alone.\nIn a news release shared alongside these abstracts, however, J&J homed in on patients “who are considered highly refractory”—those who have shown suboptimal response to at least two classes of systemic therapies.\nIn these populations, according to the pharma, JNJ-4804 elicited “meaningful improvements across multiple clinical and endoscopic measures.”\nThese signals warrant further development, according to J&J, which on Tuesday said it would launch the Phase 3 DUET ENCORE-CD study in Crohn’s disease and DUET ENCORE-UC study in ulcerative colitis.\n“Although the DUET studies missed the primary endpoint, we do believe the positive trends support the idea that combination approaches are needed to raise the efficacy bar in IBD,” William Blair analysts wrote to investors on Tuesday.\n...\nJ&J hasn’t yet specified its target population for the DUET ENCORE programs, nor has it said when the studies are set to start.\n...\n- Annalee Armstrong", "date": "2026-05-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Study highlights breakthrough in overcoming diminishing returns of sequential IBD therapies", "url": "https://www.news-medical.net/news/20260505/Study-highlights-breakthrough-in-overcoming-diminishing-returns-of-sequential-IBD-therapies.aspx", "domain": "news-medical.net", "snippet": "For patients with Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis who have exhausted other treatment options, a new combination therapy is showing results that offer hope for one of medicine's most treatment-resistant populations, according to findings from two studies to be presented today at Digestive Disease Week® (DDW) 2026.\n...\n> By combining two mechanisms of action, we're seeing efficacy that appears to be additive - without increasing safety risk.\"\n...\nThe findings come from two parallel Phase 2b trials - DUET-Crohn's and DUET-UC - testing JNJ-78934804 (JNJ-4804), a new combination fixed-dose co-antibody therapy designed to target two inflammatory pathways simultaneously.\nTaken alone, each drug - guselkumab and golimumab - targets a different protein that drives inflammation.\nBlocking both pathways at once, the combination aims to achieve deeper, more lasting results.\n...\nBoth trials enrolled patients with moderate-to-severely active disease who had already failed one or more therapies, including biologics and novel oral agents.\nFor those patients who had failed two or more prior treatments, high-dose JNJ-4804 showed improved outcomes compared with golimumab and similar efficacy to guselkumab across key measures of remission and endoscopic improvement at 48 weeks.\n...\nThe trials shared the same design.\nPatients were randomized to receive either a placebo, one of two existing biologics - golimumab, an anti-TNF therapy, or guselkumab, an anti-IL-23 therapy - or JNJ-4804, which combines both, at one of three doses.\nThe Crohn's trial enrolled 693 patients; the UC trial enrolled 572.\n...\nIn the Crohn's disease trial, patients receiving high-dose JNJ-4804 were significantly more likely to achieve clinical remission and endoscopic response at 48 weeks than those receiving golimumab alone.\nThe advantage was clearest in the most treatment-resistant patients - those who had failed two or more prior therapies.\nIn that subgroup, clinical remission rates with high-dose JNJ-4804 were more than 20 percentage points higher than guselkumab in this subgroup, and nearly 40 percentage points higher than placebo.\nResults in the ulcerative colitis trial followed a similar pattern.\nHigh-dose JNJ-4804 significantly outperformed golimumab on the primary endpoint of clinical remission at 48 weeks, and the combination's advantage was again most pronounced in patients who had failed two or more prior treatments.\nIn that subgroup, clinical remission rates ran more than 20 percentage points higher than guselkumab and nearly 20 percentage points higher than placebo, with similarly meaningful gaps in endoscopic improvement and histologic remission, a measure of healing at the tissue level.\nThe safety profile of JNJ-4804 was comparable to either monotherapy used alone.\nSerious adverse events were uncommon across both trials and primarily gastrointestinal in nature.\nBased on these results, the researchers say the combination is ready to advance to Phase 3 trials.", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Johnson & Johnson's quest to develop effective IBD treatments", "url": "https://www.jnj.com/innovation/inside-johnson-johnson-decades-long-quest-to-develop-effective-treatments-for-ibd", "domain": "jnj.com", "snippet": "## Inflammatory bowel disease doesn’t have a cure—yet. But the recent FDA approval of a promising medication is the latest in a long line of treatments from Johnson & Johnson that are helping patients with IBD.\n...\nIn people with IBD, cytokines often use two different pathways, known as TNF (tumor necrosis factor) and IL-23 (interleukin-23), to activate the immune system.\n...\nA Johnson & Johnson biologic became the first anti-TNF antibody approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to treat Crohn’s disease in the U.S. It’s approved for use against Crohn’s in the EU one year later.\n...\nJohnson & Johnson developed another therapy that relieves IBD by stopping inflammation in the gut.\nInstead of focusing on the TNF pathway, it focuses on IL-23 as well as IL-12, another protein that is involved in inflammatory and immune responses.\n...\nJohnson & Johnson researchers began investigating combination therapy, the use of two different medications at once, to improve IBD outcomes.\nPatients with moderate to severe UC were treated with a rheumatoid arthritis treatment, which works on the TNF pathway, as well as an IL-23 medication.\n...\nA Johnson & Johnson treatment, known as a dual-acting monoclonal antibody, was newly FDA-approved for both moderately to severely active UC and Crohn’s disease.\nClinical trials found that people treated with this therapy showed considerable improvement in their symptoms with a significant proportion of patients showing evidence of healing in their intestines as determined by colonoscopy and histology.", "date": "2025-09-04", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "f76d1f6d-e934-4675-bee3-d1847ccd0496"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:13+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q002", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q002-5", "query": "AZD5148 clinical pipeline TcdB antibody 2026", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "AZD5148 clinical pipeline TcdB antibody 2026", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 28704.8, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trials Appendix - Q1 2026 Results Update", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/eq1/Q1-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "Clostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\nEndpoints\nStatus\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\nAdults with qualifying C.\ndifficile infection episode\n230\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\nSecondary endpoint: safety, PK\n•\nFPCD: Q4 2025\n•\nData anticipated: >2027\nPhase I\nNCT06469151\nHealthy volunteers\n84\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose escalation\n•\nCohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2b: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.,  Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 3: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4a: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4b: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v., Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 5: AZD5148 (dose 4, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: safety\n•\nSecondary endpoint: PK parameters\n•\nFPCD: Q2 2024\n•\nLPCD: Q4 2024\n•\nData readout: Q1 2026", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-09"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "H1 and Q2 2026 Results Clinical Trials Appendix", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/h1q2/H1-and-Q2-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "H1 2026 Results Update\n27 July 2026\n...\nAZD5148\nanti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of \nC.diff recurrence\n...\nClostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\nEndpoints\nStatus\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\nAdults with qualifying C.\ndifficile infection episode\n230\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\nSecondary endpoint: safety, PK\n•\nFPCD: Q4 2025\n•\nData anticipated: >2027\nPhase I\nNCT06469151\nHealthy volunteers\n84\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose escalation\n•\nCohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2b: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.,  Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 3: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4a: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4b: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v., Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 5: AZD5148 (dose 4, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: safety\n•\nSecondary endpoint: PK parameters\n•\nFPCD: Q2 2024\n•\nLPCD: Q4 2024\n•\nData readout: Q1 2026\n131", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/our-therapy-areas/pipeline.html", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD4144\n** *cardiorenal disease*\n#### AZD4144 - Phase II\nClose\n- **Mechanism:** NLRP3\n- **Area under investigation:** cardiorenal disease\n...\nAZD5148\n** *reduction of C. diff recurrence*\n#### AZD5148 - Phase II\nClose\n- **Mechanism:** anti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb\n- **Area under investigation:** reduction of C. diff recurrence\n- **Molecule size:** Large molecule", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=2487fb5c-a174-4489-a3aa-8931585c2cf7", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\n...\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].\n...\n* Interestingly, ScFv and VHH targeting TcdB of *C. difficile* have been developed.\nThe binding and structural data were encouraging, though no efficacy data were published [332].", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-04"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Ozmosi | AZD-5148 Drug Profile", "url": "https://pryzm.ozmosi.com/product/33039", "domain": "pryzm.ozmosi.com", "snippet": "Anti-toxin B neutralizing mAb AZD5148 provides protection in a Clostridioides difficile gnotobiotic piglet model.\n...\nMechanisms of Action: TcdB Inhibitor\nNovel Mechanism: Yes\nModality: Antibody\nRoute of Administration: Intravenous,Intramuscular\nFDA Designation: *\nApproval Status: Not Approved\nApproved Countries: None\nApproved Indications: None\n...\nCountries in Clinic: Japan, United States\n###### Active Clinical Trial Count: 3\n## Recent & Upcoming Milestones\n## Highest Development Phases\nPhase 1: Clostridium Infections|Healthy Volunteers\n|Trial|Phase|Trial Status|Disease|Primary Completion Date|Probability of Success|Latest Trial Update Date|Data Updated|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|D8820C00005|P1|Active, not recruiting|Clostridium Infections|2025-12-29|69%|2025-01-23|Primary Endpoints|Study Completion Date|Treatments|Trial Status|\n|jRCT2071240071|P1|Not yet recruiting|Healthy Volunteers|2026-01-12|\n|D8820C00002|P1|Active, not recruiting|Healthy Volunteers|2025-11-07|69%|2025-01-22|", "date": "2025-01-23", "last_updated": "2025-06-13"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Prevention of Recurrent C. Difficile Infection Study With AZD5148 Monoclonal Antibody (NCT07285213)", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gg/study/NCT07285213", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gg", "snippet": "RecruitingNCT07285213PRISMUpdated Aug 12, 2026\n# Prevention of Recurrent C. Difficile Infection Study With AZD5148 Monoclonal Antibody\nA Phase 2 interventional study of AZD5148 and Placebo in Clostridioides Difficile Infection, sponsored by AstraZeneca.\nRecruiting at 116 sites in 14 countries.\nOpen to participants aged 18 Years and older.\nPer ClinicalTrials.gov, last updated 2026-08-12.\nSponsored by **AstraZeneca** · Phase 2, Interventional, and Prevention\n...\nPhase 2\n- **Study type**\nInterventional\n- **Enrollment**\n230\n...\nThe purpose of this study is to evaluate the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for prevention of recurrence of Clostridioides difficile infection in Individuals 18 years of age and above.\n...\nApproximately 230 participants will be enrolled and randomized 1:1 to receive a single dose of either AZD5148 or placebo (normal saline).\nRoute of administration (intramuscular or intravenous push) will be according to the Investigator's choice.\nStratification will be based on geographical region.\n...\n## Conditions studied\n- Clostridioides Difficile Infection\n...\nParticipant must be ≥ 18 years of age at the time of signing the informed consent, capable of giving signed informed consent.\nParticipants with a qualifying C. difficile infection episode at the time of providing informed consent defined by:\n- History of 3 or more unformed stools (Bristol stool scale 6 or 7) in ≤ 24 hours for 2 consecutive calendar days, (Note: Diarrhea is not required to be present on the day of IMP administration) and\n- Positive local C. difficile toxin test (eg, immune assay or CCNA) on a stool sample collected during this episode, as part of routine clinical care (Note: Toxin testing, where not part of routine clinical care, may be conducted as a pre- screening activity; if this occurs, participant consent is required), and\n- Planned initiation or receipt of antibacterial drug therapy for C. difficile infection (fidaxomicin, vancomycin or metronidazole) for this episode,\n...\n## Study design\n- **Phase**\nPhase 2\n- **Primary purpose**\nPrevention\n...\nAZD5148: dose A\nParticipants will receive a single dose A of AZD5148 administered via either intramuscular injection or intravenous push.\n...\nParticipants will receive a single dose of placebo via intramuscular or intravenous push.\n...\n### Primary outcomes\n1. First occurrence of recurrence of C difficile infection\nRecurrence of C difficile infection (rCDI) occurring after initial clinical cure (ICC) of the qualifying C difficile infection (CDI).\nCDI is defined as a history of diarrhea (\\>=3 unformed stools, ie, type 6 or 7 stool on Bristol Stool Scale in \\<=24 hours for 2 consecutive calendar days) accompanied by a positive stool test for C difficile toxin.\nTime frame: Day 1 through day 91\n...\n## Key details\n- **Study ID**\nNCT07285213\n- **Lead sponsor**\nAstraZeneca\n...\n- **Start date**\nDec 10, 2025\n- **Primary completion**\nApr 20, 2027 (estimated)\n- **Completion**\nJan 18, 2028 (estimated)\n- **Last update**", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-18"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Clinical Trials Appendix - FY 2025 Results Update - AstraZeneca", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2025/Q4-FY/Full-year-Q4-2025-clinical-trial-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD5148\nanti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of C. diff recurrence\n...\n139\nClostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\nEndpoints\nStatus\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\n≥ 18 years, with a qualifying C. \ndifficile infection episode at \nthe time of providing\ninformed consent\n230\n•\nRandomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo (1:1)\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\nSecondary endpoint: safety, PK\n•\nFPCD: Q4 2025\n•\nData anticipated: >2027\nPhase I\nNCT06469151\nHealthy volunteers\n84\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose escalation\n•\nCohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo\n…", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Prevention of Recurrent C. difficile Infection Study with AZD5148 ...", "url": "https://www.astrazenecaclinicaltrials.com/study/D8820C00003/", "domain": "astrazenecaclinicaltrials.com", "snippet": "# Prevention of Recurrent C. difficile Infection Study with AZD5148 Monoclonal Antibody - PRISM\nStudy identifier:D8820C00003\nClinicalTrials.gov identifier:NCT07285213\nEudraCT identifier:N/A\nCTIS identifier:2025-521416-19-00\nRecruiting\n### Official Title\nA Phase IIb, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled Study to Evaluate the Efficacy and Safety of AZD5148 for Prevention of Recurrence of Clostridioides difficile Infection in Individuals 18 Years of Age and Above\n### Medical condition\nClostridioides difficile Infection\n### Phase\nPhase 2\n### Healthy volunteers\nNo\n### Study drug\nAZD5148\n...\n### Estimated Enrollment\n230\n...\n**Study Start Date:** 10 Dec 2025\n**Estimated Primary Completion Date:** 20 Apr 2027\n**Estimated Study Completion Date:** 18 Jan 2028\n### Study design\nAllocation: Randomized\nEndpoint Classification: -\nIntervention Model: Parallel Assignment\nMasking: -\nPrimary Purpose: Prevention\n### Verification:\nVerified 01 Sept 2026 by AstraZeneca\n### Sponsors\nAstraZeneca\n...\nSort by status\n...\n- Recruiting\n- Not yet recruiting\n- Recruiting complete\n- Completed\n...\nNo locations available\n...\n|Experimental: AZD5148: dose A Participants will receive a single dose A of AZD5148 administered via either intramuscular injection or intravenous push.|Drug: AZD5148 Participants will receive a single dose A of AZD5148 administered via either intramuscular injection or intravenous push.Other Name: N/A|\n|Placebo Comparator: Placebo Participants will receive a single dose of placebo via intramuscular or intravenous push.|Other: Placebo Participants will receive a single dose of placebo (0.9% (w/v) sodium chloride for injection) administered via either intramuscular injection or intravenous push.Other Name: N/A|\n...\nAstraZeneca Clinical Study Information Center\n1-877-240-9479\n[email protected]", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-15"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12614991/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "*Clostridioides difficile* toxin B is a virulence factor for *C. difficile* infections and a clinically validated target for prevention of *C. difficile* infection recurrence.\nAZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes.\n...\nWe recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16].\n...\nAZD5148 targets the GTD region of TcdB (Figure 1*A*).\n...\nTaken together, this study has revealed that AZD5148 is expected to neutralize >97% of recently circulating *C. difficile* strains in 2 cell lines.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-08-09"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad Review of the Field", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13337890/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\nTcdB binds at least three classes of receptors including CSPG4, Nectin-3, and PVRL3 [35–37].\n...\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].\n...\n- 40.AstraZeneca.\nA phase I double-blind, placebo-controlled study to evaluate the safety, tolerability and pharmacokinetics of AZD5148 in healthy adults.\n2025. https://www.clinicaltrials.gov.\nAccessed 10 Nov 2025.\n- 41.AstraZeneca.\nA phase IIb, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for prevention of recurrence of Clostridioides difficile infection in individuals 18 years of age and above.\n2025. https://www.clinicaltrials.gov.\nAccessed 10 Dec 2025.\n...\n- 59.Efficacy and safety of suvratoxumab for prevention of *Staphylococcus aureus* ventilator-associated pneumonia (SAATELLITE): a multicentre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group, phase 2 pilot trial.\n...\n- 62.Aridis Pharmaceuticals, Inc.", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-07-18"}], "raw": {"id": "f9fe3323-3e4e-40ce-9f13-2755c4b365b9"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:13+00:00"}
