ence\n(clinicaltrials.gov)\n- P2/3 | N=375 | Recruiting | Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc.\n| Not yet recruiting ➔ Recruiting\nEnrollment open • Infectious Disease\n...\nLMN-201 for Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence\n(clinicaltrials.gov)\n- P2/3 | N=375 | Not yet recruiting | Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc.\n| Trial completion date: Dec 2025 ➔ Apr 2026 | Trial primary completion date: Apr 2025 ➔ Dec 2025\n...\nMay 17, 2023\nLumen Bioscience Receives Fast Track Designation from U.S. FDA for LMN-201\n(PRNewswire)\n- \"Lumen Bioscience...announced that the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has granted Fast Track Designation for LMN-201, an investigational, orally delivered biologic drug to treat and prevent C. difficile infection (CDI).\"\n...\nLMN-201 for Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence\n(clinicaltrials.gov)\n- P2/3 | N=375 | Not yet recruiting | Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc.\n| Trial completion date: Dec 2023 ➔ Dec 2025 | Initiation date: Sep 2022 ➔ Apr 2023 | Trial primary completion date: Sep 2023 ➔ Apr 2025\n...\nExploratory Study to Assess Delivery of LMN-201 Components Via Enteric Capsules in the Gut of Individuals With Ostomies\n(clinicaltrials.gov)\n- P1 | N=12 | Completed | Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc.\n| Recruiting ➔ Completed | Trial completion date: Jul 2022 ➔ Feb 2022\n...\n- P1; N=12; Recruiting; Sponsor: Lumen Bioscience, Inc.; Not yet recruiting ➔ Recruiting", "date": "2024-09-19", "last_updated": "2025-09-18"}], "raw": {"id": "752494ad-3b44-4eff-a38d-bcc46f7e81f8"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:21+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q008", "objective": "Clostridioides Difficile Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as LMN-201, including Antibody Fragment Enzyme Biological Biologic Cocktail and Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB therapies", "anchors": ["LMN-201"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q008-5", "query": "LMN-201 bezlotoxumab withdrawal current status", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "LMN-201 bezlotoxumab withdrawal current status", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 449.8, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "LMN-201 Preliminary Cohort in Repreve Trial", "url": "https://www.lumen.bio/news/2025/04/02/lmn-201-achieves-100-initial-c-difficile-clinical-cure-in-preliminary-cohort-of-re-preve-trial", "domain": "lumen.bio", "snippet": "The RePreve Trial was designed to facilitate comparisons to the MODIFY trial, which targeted the same patient population with the same SoC antibiotics combined with the IV-infused monoclonal antibody, bezlotoxumab.\n...\nSustained cure rates through Week 4 for SoC alone (MODIFY I/II), SoC plus LMN-201 (RePreve sentinel cohort) and SoC plus bezlotoxumab (MODIFY I/II) were 59.5% (460/773; 95% CI 56-63%), 95.2% (20/21; 95% CI 77-100%) and 68.9% (538/781; 95% CI 66-72%), respectively (see enclosed figure).\n...\nIf borne out in future studies, the RePreve Trial will mark a major advance in the CDI field.\nBezlotoxumab did not improve initial clinical cure rates and only modestly improved CDI recurrence, but came with a risk of heart failure on its label; it was recently withdrawn from the market.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience's Oral Biologic LMN-201 Achieves 100% Clinical ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/news/lumen-bioscience-s-oral-biologic-lmn-201-achieves-100-clinical-cure-rate-for-c-difficile-infection-in-preliminary-trial", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "## Key Insights\n...\n- Only one patient (4.76%) experienced recurrence within 28 days after LMN-201View drug details treatment, compared to 26% with standard care alone and 14% with bezlotoxumabView drug details in previous MODIFY trials, suggesting strong potential for reducing the burden of recurrent infections.\n- The RePreve Trial has now advanced to its main cohort, a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study that will enroll approximately 350 patients, with extended dosing from 7 days to approximately 70 days to provide protection throughout the period of greatest recurrence risk.\n...\nThis compares favorably to the 26% recurrence rate (161 of 621 patients; 95% CI: 23%-30%) observed after standard-of-care alone in the MODIFY I/II trials, and the 14% recurrence rate (87 of 625 patients; 95% CI: 11%-17%) after standard-of-care plus bezlotoxumabView drug details treatment.\nThe sustained clinical cure rate through Week 4—defined as initial clinical cure plus prevention of CDI (search)Search disease recurrence—was 95.2% for LMN-201View drug details plus standard-of-care, compared to 59.5% for standard-of-care alone and 68.9% for standard-of-care plus bezlotoxumabView drug details in previous trials.\n...\nWith these promising results, Lumen has now begun Part B (main cohort), a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study that will enroll approximately 350 patients at research centers across the U.S.\n...\n\"We are pleased that LMN-201View drug details appears to be safe and well-tolerated in patients,\" added Finrow.\n...\nLMN-201View drug details offers several potential advantages over existing treatments.\nUnlike bezlotoxumabView drug details, which was recently withdrawn from the market and carried a risk of heart failure, LMN-201 appears to be safe and well-tolerated.", "date": "2025-04-03", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "LMN-201 Clinical Mixture of 3", "url": "https://db.antibodysociety.org/db0/937/", "domain": "db.antibodysociety.org", "snippet": "|INN|None|\n|Status|Clinical|\n...\n|Most advanced stage of development (global)|Phase 2/3|\n|--|--|\n|Status|Active|\n|Start of clinical phase (IND filing or first Phase 1)|August 15, 2021|\n|Start of Phase 2| |\n|Start of Phase 3|August 29, 2024|\n...\n|US or EU approved indications|None|\n...\n|Comments about company or candidate|NCT05330182 Phase 2/3 study of Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence started in Aug 2024.\nMay 17, 2023: Lumen Bioscience announced that the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has granted Fast Track Designation for LMN-201, an investigational, orally delivered biologic drug to treat and prevent C. difficile infection (CDI).\nThe FDA previously cleared a planned Phase 2/3 study of LMN-201, which will begin enrolling approximately 375 patients at sites across the United States later this year.|\n...\n|Anticipated events|None|\n|--|--|\n|Factor(s) contributing to discontinuation|None|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Addressing Personalized Needs in Clostridioides Difficile Infection", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT04725123", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "NCT03829475 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE2\n- LMN-201 for Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence\nNCT05330182 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2/PHASE3", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Pfizer, Inc., et al.; Withdrawal of Approval of 26 New Drug Applications", "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/11/19/2024-26913/pfizer-inc-et-al-withdrawal-of-approval-of-26-new-drug-applications", "domain": "federalregister.gov", "snippet": "Enter a search term or FR citation e.g. 88 FR 382 30 FR 7878 2024-13208 USDA 09/05/24 RULE 0503-AA39 SORN", "date": "2024-11-19", "last_updated": "2025-05-26"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Pfizer, Inc., et al.; Withdrawal of Approval of 23 New Drug Applications", "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/06/03/2024-12065/pfizer-inc-et-al-withdrawal-of-approval-of-23-new-drug-applications", "domain": "federalregister.gov", "snippet": "Enter a search term or FR citation e.g. 88 FR 382 30 FR 7878 2024-13208 USDA 09/05/24 RULE 0503-AA39 SORN", "date": "2024-06-03", "last_updated": "2025-05-27"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Drug news and alerts", "url": "https://www.humana.com/pharmacy/news", "domain": "humana.com", "snippet": "The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) announced on April 6, 2023 the final decision to withdraw approval of Makena and its generic.", "date": "2024-03-21", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Investigational Oral Biologic Achieves 100% C difficile ...", "url": "https://www.lumen.bio/news/2025/04/03/investigational-oral-biologic-achieves-100-c-difficile-clinical-cure-in-preliminary-cohort", "domain": "lumen.bio", "snippet": "Today, Lumen Bioscience reported that its investigational oral biologic, LMN-201, achieved a 100% clinical cure rate of C difficile infection (CDI) in the sentinel cohort (Part A) of its RePreve clinical trial.\n...\nOnly 1 of 21 patients (4.76%; 95% CI: 0.24%-23%) who completed 7 days of standard-of-care (SoC) plus LMN-201 experienced CDI recurrence within 28 days, compared to 161 of 621 patients (26%; 95% CI: 23%-30%) who experienced recurrence after SoC alone in MODIFY I/II, and 87 of 625 patients (14%; 95% CI: 11%-17%) after SoC plus bezlotoxumab treatment in MODIFY I/II within the same time period.\n...\nLMN-201 is delivered in capsules and is intended for use with and following antibiotics to improve clinical outcomes for C difficile infection.\n...\nWith the sentinel cohort now completed, Lumen has begun Part B (the main cohort).\nThis pivotal phase is a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study that will enroll approximately 350 patients at research centers across the U.S. Participants will be randomized 1:1 to receive either LMN-201 or a placebo—each alongside standard antibiotic therapy—to rigorously evaluate the drug’s efficacy in a larger population.\n...\n*The RePreve Trial main cohort is now recruiting.For investigators and potential participants interested in participating, they can email trials@lumen.bio or visit the official RePreve study webpage.*", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience | Home", "url": "https://www.lumen.bio/", "domain": "lumen.bio", "snippet": "### Core pipeline\n- C. difficile (LMN-201)\nOral biologic cocktail\nMOA Previously Demonstrated\nPreclinical\nPhase 1\n(safety)\nPhase 2\n(proof of concept)\nPhase 3\n(definitive efficacy)", "date": "2026-09-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "ACX-362E [Ibezapolstat] for Oral Treatment of Recurrent Clostridioides Difficile Infection", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT07513285", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "NCT05831189 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE3\n...\n- LMN-201 for Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence\nNCT05330182 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2/PHASE3\n- Efficacy, Safety, and Tolerability Study of Oral Full-Spectrum MicrobiotaTM (CP101) in Subjects With Recurrent C. Diff\n...\n- Phase I Trial of a Single Dose of CRS3123", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-16"}], "raw": {"id": "fe879c2f-6af2-47dc-a5a3-1f4ccfda8452"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:21+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q009", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as CHRM1 CHRM3 CHRM4 CHRM5 Small Molecule", "anchors": ["Dicyclomine"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q009-1", "query": "Dicyclomine inflammatory bowel disease muscarinic competitors", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Dicyclomine inflammatory bowel disease muscarinic competitors", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 497.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Antispasmodics for Chronic Abdominal Pain: Analysis of North ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8315189/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agents inhibit GI smooth muscle contraction, in part, by blocking calcium transport through calcium channels (31); furthermore, these agents decrease colonic motility (32).\n...\n##### Dicyclomine.\nIn 2 randomized, placebo-controlled studies, dicyclomine improved symptoms of IBS relative to\nplacebo (Table 2) (18,43–49).\nOne study reported no difference in adverse event (AE) rates with dicyclomine vs placebo (43), whereas the other reported that AEs occurred in a greater percentage of patients (69%) receiving dicyclomine 160 mg/d continuously for 2 weeks vs patients receiving placebo (16%; Table 2) (18).\n...\nHyoscine, also known as scopolamine, is an anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agent and smooth muscle relaxant (20).\nIn 3 studies, hyoscine taken for a duration of 4 weeks to 3 months was more efficacious than placebo at improving IBS symptoms (Table 2) (44–46).\n...\nDicyclomine, hyoscine, and hyoscyamine are anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agents available in the United States.\nAlthough placebo-controlled efficacy and safety data related to the use of these antispasmodics in patients with IBS seem favorable, the studies of dicyclomine (18,43) and hyoscine (44–47) identified in this review were published in 1990 or earlier and used different doses, treatment durations, and outcome assessments.\n...\nIn summary, data supporting the use of antispasmodics for the treatment of chronic abdominal pain in patients with DGBI, including IBS and FD, are limited.\nLimited sample size, short duration of therapy, heterogeneity in outcomes, and concerns over potential bias with study design make it difficult to recommend these agents for clinical use, especially when compared with the data sets available from large, randomized, controlled trials that characterize the current US FDA-approved IBS medications.", "date": "2021-05-17", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Pain management in patients with inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3437534/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Since one of the cardinal features of intestinal inflammation is abdominal pain, inducing remission using standard of care IBD treatments (e.g. aminosalicylates, antibiotics, corticosteroids, enteral therapy, immunomodulators, and biological response modifiers) can alleviate inflammation-related pain in most patients.\n...\nAntispasmodics have some utility in providing a bridge of pain relief as remission is induced and aiding with pain if standard medical therapies are unable to control inflammation.\nAs pain from inflammation or partial obstruction usually manifests with intestinal spasms, anticholinergic antispasmodic medications are frequently employed as analgesic agents.\n...\nOverall antispasmodics were found to be more efficacious than placebo for functional abdominal pain.\nOf the medications studied by their group, hyoscine (hyoscyamine) was found to be the most efficacious and is the only one available in US markets.\nPractically speaking, the antispasmodics used most frequently are hyoscyamine and dicyclomine.\nUnfortunately, though antispasmodics seem to be a reasonable choice for pain management associated with inflammation or partial obstruction, there is no definitive evidence supporting their usage in IBD [Makharia, 2011].\nFurthermore, one must be wary of potential side effects, including fulminant obstruction in an already partially obstructed gut or worsening dysmotility in a gut that is already prone to motility problems secondary to inflammation and its sequelae.\n...\nIn addition to standard IBD-specific treatments, medical therapy for pain arising from inflammation or strictures/adhesions could include antispasmodics [i.e. hyoscyamine (hyoscine), dicyclomine (dicycloverine)] as needed.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Antispasmodics and IBD", "url": "https://www.ibdrelief.com/learn/treatment/medication-for-ibd/antispasmodics-and-ibd", "domain": "ibdrelief.com", "snippet": "Antispasmodics suppress muscle spasms, helping to reduce stomach pain, cramping and bloating.\nThey are sometimes used in IBD to relieve symptoms.\n...\nAntispasmodic medicines are a type of symptomatic medicine, which suppress muscle spasms.\nThey are used to help ease symptoms such as functional abdominal pain, cramping and bloating you may have as a result of a wide range of conditions, including inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).\nThey can also help to reduce the movement (motility) of your gut.\n...\nPeople who have IBD, such as Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis, may be advised to take antispasmodic medicines to help to ease pain, discomfort and symptoms whilst other medicines take effect, or if they have symptoms that are not responding to other IBD medicines.\n...\nThere are two main types of antispasmodics, antimuscarinics (also called anticholinergics) and smooth muscle relaxants.\n...\nAs mentioned earlier, your body sends chemical messages to your gut, telling the muscles to contract.\nAntimuscarinics block the action of the chemical messenger (neurotransmitter) acetylcholine, reducing the strength of muscle contractions, spasms and cramps.\nThis also reduces gut motility.\nExamples include:\n- Dicyclomine (Bentyl)\n- Hyoscyamine\n- Hyoscine butylbromide (Buscopan)\n- Atropine\n- Propantheline\n...\nSmooth muscle relaxants work directly on the smooth muscle wall of the gut, helping it to relax and relieving pain associated with contractions, cramps and spasms.\nExamples include:\n- Mebeverine (Colofac, Duspamen and Duspatalin)\n- Alverine citrate (Spasmonal)\n...\nSmooth muscle relaxants tend to have fewer side effects so will often be suggested first.\nSome people seem to respond better to one type of antispasmodic than to another, so if one type does not work for you, it may be worth trying another.\n...\nIt is generally recommended that you only take antispasmodics when necessary, stopping if the symptoms settle down.\n...\nGenerally smooth muscle relaxant antispasmodics will have fewer, milder side effects than antimuscarinics.\n...\nThis will be listed in the patient information leaflets, and includes:\n- If you have, or might have a bowel obstruction\n- If you already have gut motility issues\n- If you have myasthenia gravis, a condition that causes muscle weakness", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacology", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00804", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "snippet": "- Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor M1Antagonist\n...\n**Dicyclomine**is an antimuscarinic agent used to treat IBS.\n...\nDicyclomine is a muscarinic M1, M3, and M2 receptor antagonist as well as a non-competitive inhibitor of histamine and bradykinin used to treat spasms of the intestines seen in functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nDicyclomine is indicated for the treatment of functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nDicyclomine is an anticholinergic drug used to relax the smooth muscles of the intestines.\n...\nDicyclomine achieves its action partially through direct antimuscarinic activity of the M1, M3, and M2 receptors; and partially through antagonism of bradykinin and histamine.\nDicyclomine non-competitively inhibits the action of bradykinin and histamine, resulting in direct action on the smooth muscle, and decreased strength of contractions seen in spasms of the ileum.\n...\nAlcohol may increase drowsiness caused by dicyclomine.", "date": "2025-04-30", "last_updated": "2025-08-29"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "www.droracle.ai · articles · 856897What is the most appropriate over‑the‑counter treatment for ...", "url": "https://www.droracle.ai/articles/856897/what-is-the-most-appropriate-overthecounter-treatment-for-simultaneous", "domain": "droracle.ai", "snippet": "**For simultaneous abdominal cramps and diarrhea, loperamide is the single most effective over-the-counter medication, addressing diarrhea control while providing modest pain relief, though antispasmodics like dicyclomine can be added if cramping persists.** 1\n...\nIf cramping remains bothersome despite loperamide, **add an antispasmodic such as dicyclomine or hyoscine butylbromide (if available in your region).**\n### Antispasmodic Evidence\n...\n- Dicyclomine and hyoscine butylbromide are antimuscarinics that reduce intestinal motility and spasm.\n1\n...\n**Stop loperamide immediately and seek medical attention if:**\n- Diarrhea worsens or is accompanied by fever, severe abdominal pain, or bloody stools—these suggest infection or inflammatory bowel disease requiring antibiotics or other treatment.\n...\n- You have known inflammatory bowel disease during an active flare, as loperamide can precipitate toxic megacolon.\n8, 9\n...\n- **Do not use loperamide chronically without investigating the underlying cause** of diarrhea—conditions like IBS, inflammatory bowel disease, or malabsorption require specific treatment.\n8\n- **Do not exceed 8 mg daily of OTC loperamide** without medical supervision, as higher doses require monitoring for cardiac effects.", "date": "2026-02-16", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Phase II-(VI) Antispasmodic & antiulcer drugs (cyclopentolate ...", "url": "https://api.upums.ac.in/pdfs/doc-1712402525891-163237152.pdf", "domain": "api.upums.ac.in", "snippet": "Antispasmodics can be classified in three major subclasses: \n1. Anticholinergic / antimuscarinic agents: Examples are \n• Dicyclomine Hydrochloride \n• Hyoscyamine Sulfate \n• Cimetropium Bromide \n• Otilanium Bromide\n• Octylonium Bromide \n• Prifinium Bromide \n• Darifenacin \n...\nIn the United States the antispasmodics dicyclomimne and hyoscyamine are available for inflammatory bowel disease (IBS).\ncymetropium bromide is a muscarinic compound with strong antispasmodic activity.\n...\nPropantheline bromide (PRO-BANTHINE) is a widely used synthetic nonselective muscarinic receptor antagonist.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-07"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] CAG_CPG_for_Management_of...", "url": "https://www.cag-acg.org/_Library/clinical_cpgs_position_papers/CAG_CPG_for_Management_of_IBS_JCAG_Jan2019.pdf", "domain": "cag-acg.org", "snippet": "20: \u0007We suggest offering IBS patients certain antispasmodics (such as dicyclomine, hyoscine, pinaverium) to improve IBS \nsymptoms.\n...\nOf the four antispasmodics available in Canada, hyoscine, pinaverium, and dicyclomine (dicylcoverine), were shown to be effective, while trimebutine was not.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-20"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Dicyclomine Alternatives Compared - Drugs.com", "url": "https://www.drugs.com/compare/dicyclomine", "domain": "drugs.com", "snippet": "|Dicyclomine|Bentyl (dicyclomine)|Hyoscyamine||\n|--|--|--|--|\n...\n|Prescription only Dicyclomine is used for the relief of colicky pain caused by intestinal muscle spasms in functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\nSide effects include dry mouth, a decreased ability to sweat...\nView more|Prescription only Prescribed for Irritable Bowel Syndrome.\nBentyl may also be used for purposes not listed in this medication guide.|Prescription only Hyoscyamine treats various GI problems including cramps, bladder spasms, IBS, diverticulitis, pancreatitis, and peptic ulcers.\nIt controls excessive secretions, reduces rhinitis/saliva production...|Bentyl|N/A|Anaspaz, Ed-Spaz, Hyosyne, Levbid, Levsin, Levsin SL, NuLev, Oscimin, Symax Duotab, Symax SL, Symax SR View more|", "date": "2000-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-10-01"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Gastrointestinal Agents - LiverTox - NCBI Bookshelf", "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548487/", "domain": "ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Inflammatory bowel disease encompasses several disorders, most commonly ulcerative colitis and Crohn colitis.\n...\nAgents discussed in LiverTox include the following, which are linked to the specific drug record:\n- Antimuscarinics/Antispasmodics [See Anticholinergic Agents]\n- Dicyclomine, Glycopyrrolate, Hyoscyamine, Methscopolamine", "date": "2019-04-25", "last_updated": "2026-07-23"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "PRIME PubMed", "url": "https://www.unboundmedicine.com/medline/research/dicyclomine", "domain": "unboundmedicine.com", "snippet": "2025 Mar 14 [Online ahead of print]JP\n- Introduction: Dicyclomine is an antimuscarinic agent approved for treatment of irritable bowel syndrome-associated abdominal pain.\n...\nNational Library of Medicine (US): Bethesda (MD)BOOK\n- Dicyclomine has not been well studied during breastfeeding.\nHowever, one possible case of apnea has been reported in a breastfed infant that is similar to reactions that have occurred in infants given the drug directly.[1] Dicyclomine should not be used during lactation.\n...\n[Journal Article]\n- CONCLUSIONS: Based on the study findings, fluoxetine and duloxetine had greater therapeutic effects on all symptoms of IBD than dicyclomine, with duloxetine, specifically, being more effective than fluoxetine.", "date": "2010-02-25", "last_updated": "2025-03-18"}], "raw": {"id": "87649e8a-60c4-4eb4-8c7e-3cd19c5666c8"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:22+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q007", "objective": "Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as FN1 IL-10RB IL10RA Interleukin-10 Antibody-Cytokine Conjugate Fusion Protein Biologic", "anchors": ["Dekavil"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q007-2", "query": "Dekavil ulcerative colitis antibody-cytokine fusion pipeline", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Dekavil ulcerative colitis antibody-cytokine fusion pipeline", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 8846.5, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Dekavil - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c932383d8dcb4af180c9a51971a82032", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Drug Type Fusion protein|\n|--|\n|Synonyms F8-IL10, PF 06687234, PF-06687234|\n|Target EDA-FN x IL-10R|\n|Action inhibitors, stimulants|\n|Mechanism EDA-FN inhibitors(extra domain A of fibronectin inhibitors), IL-10R stimulants(Interleukin 10 receptor stimulants)|\n|Therapeutic Areas Immune System DiseasesDigestive System DisordersSkin and Musculoskeletal Diseases|\n|Active Indication-|\n|Inactive Indication Colitis, UlcerativeCrohn DiseaseRheumatoid Arthritis|\n|Originator Organization Philogen SpA|\n...\n|Inactive Organization Pfizer Inc.Philogen SpA|\n|License Organization Pfizer Inc.Philogen SpA|\n|Drug Highest PhaseDiscontinuedPhase 2|\n...\nNCT05622175\n/ WithdrawnPhase 1\n...\nNCT03414788\n/ WithdrawnPhase 1\nPHASE 1B, OPEN LABEL STUDY TO CHARACTERIZE THE DISTRIBUTION OF A SINGLE INTRAVENOUS DOSE OF [124I]-IODOBENZOYL (IB)-PF-06687234 WITH CONCURRENT ADMINISTRATION OF NON-RADIOLABELED PF-06687234 AS ASSESSED WITH POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY AND COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY (PET-CT) IMAGING IN MODERATE TO SEVERE ULCERATIVE AND CROHN'S COLITIS SUBJECTS\nNCT03269695\n/ TerminatedPhase 2\nA PHASE 2A, RANDOMIZED, DOUBLE-BLIND, PLACEBO-CONTROLLED STUDY TO EVALUATE THE EFFICACY, SAFETY, TOLERABILITY AND PHARMACOKINETICS OF PF-06687234 AS ADD-ON THERAPY TO INFLIXIMAB IN ACTIVE ULCERATIVE COLITIS SUBJECTS WHO ARE NOT IN REMISSION (BUILD UC)\n...\nEfficacy and safety of the interleukin-10–fragment F8 fusion protein PF-06687234 as add-on therapy to infliximab in patients with active ulcerative colitis: a randomized, phase IIa clinical trial\nArticle\n...\nBackground::\nPF-06687234 is a novel, human, single-chain variable fragment cytokine fusion protein comprising the antibody fragment F8 and the immunoregulatory cytokine interleukin-10.\n...\nWe evaluated the efficacy and safety of PF-06687234 as an add-on therapy to infliximab in participants with active ulcerative colitis (UC).\nDesign::\nThis phase IIa, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group, multicenter study randomized (1:1) participants with active UC to subcutaneous PF-06687234 20 mg or placebo once weekly, and participants continued intravenous background infliximab every 6 or 8 weeks, for 12 weeks.\n...\nAfter 20 participants were randomized and treated, this study was stopped early for futility.\nThere were no statistically significant differences between groups for any efficacy endpoint (all\np\n> 0.05), although numerical trends towards efficacy were observed in some secondary endpoints.\nPF-06687234 was detected at a low concentration in only one colon tissue sample.\nThere were no differences in AEs between the groups.\nThe most frequently reported AE in the PF-06687234-treated group was injection site reaction.\n...\nPF-06687234 was well tolerated, but when combined with background infliximab, did not meet the primary efficacy endpoint in this cohort of participants with active UC.\nEfficacious PF-06687234 tissue concentrations may not have been achieved at the dose tested.\n...\nEfficacy, safety, and tolerability of PF-06687234 as add-on therapy to infliximab in active UC subjects not in remission.\nhttps://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03269695\n. NCT03269695.\n...\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|United States|Pfizer Inc.|20 Dec 2017|\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|Australia|Pfizer Inc.|20 Dec 2017|\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|Belgium|Pfizer Inc.|20 Dec 2017|\n...\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|Israel|Pfizer Inc.|20 Dec 2017|\n...\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|Saudi Arabia|Pfizer Inc.|20 Dec 2017|\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|Serbia|Pfizer Inc.|20 Dec 2017|\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|South Korea|Pfizer Inc.|20 Dec 2017|\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|Turkey|Pfizer Inc.|20 Dec 2017|\n...\n|NCT03269695 (BUILD UC, CTgov)|Phase 2|Colitis, Ulcerative|20|Infliximab+PF-06687234 (PF-06687234 20 mg + Infliximab)|pvodujgcel = vvdkmjzwve ghbrentcpd (abwmcfttoo, iavhspejwe - dxvdfohkrg) View more|-|28 Dec 2021|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-08"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Updates on Molecular ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5491079/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "^37^ However, no clear therapeutic effect of IL-10 has yet been demonstrated in human clinical trials.\nHowever, newer formulations of IL-10 targeted at sites of inflammation, such as F8-IL-10 (Dekavil) may show promise for the treatment of IBD.\nDekavil is an immunocytokine consisting of a targeting antibody fused to the anti-inflammatory cytokine IL-10.\nDekavil has entered Phase II clinical trials in combination with methotrexate for the therapy of Rheumatoid Arthritis and investigations in IBD indications are being considered.", "date": "2017-05-11", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "F8IL10, F8-IL10, PF-06687234 Clinical Immunoconjugate ...", "url": "https://db.antibodysociety.org/db0/622/", "domain": "db.antibodysociety.org", "snippet": "# F8IL10, F8-IL10, PF-06687234 Clinical Immunoconjugate, Immunocytokine\n...\n|INN|None|\n|Status|Clinical|\n|Drug code(s)|F8IL10, F8-IL10, PF-06687234|\n|Brand name|Dekavil|\n...\n|General Molecular Category|Immunoconjugate, Immunocytokine|\n|Format, general category|Fragment fusion|\n|Format details|scFv-IL-10|\n...\n|Conjugated/fused moiety|IL-10|\n|Discovery method/technology|Phage display-derived|\n...\n|Target(s)|Fibronectin EDA|\n|--|--|\n|Indications of clinical studies|Ulcerative colitis, Rheumatoid arthritis|\n|Primary therapeutic area|Immune-mediated / inflammatory disorders|\n...\n|Most advanced stage of development (global)|Phase 2|\n|--|--|\n|Status|Active|\n|Start of clinical phase (IND filing or first Phase 1)|July 01, 2011|\n|Start of Phase 2|October 15, 2014|\n|Start of Phase 3| |\n...\n|US or EU approved indications|None|\n...\n|Company|Philogen S.p.A.|\n|--|--|\n|Licensee/Partner|Pfizer|\n|Comments about company or candidate|Listed as Phase 2 asset as of Oct 2024 (https://www.philogen.com/philogen/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Philogen_webinar-October-Website.pdf) July 2023: Still listed in Philogen pipeline accessed online as Phase 2 for chronic inflammatory disorders NCT05622175 Phase 1 in RA due to start in March 2023.\nStill listed in Philogen pipeline accessed online on June 10 2021 as Phase 2 for RA; listed as terminated for UC in Pfizer pipeline dated March 2021.\n...\nNCT03269695 Phase 2 in UC started in Dec 2017 Terminated (The Sponsor changed R&D strategy and priority.) as of last update in March 2021.\nOct 2018: Pfizer terminates a phase I trial for Ulcerative colitis due to an interruption in supply of the radiolabelled material (IV) (NCT03414788).\n...\nVascular targeting antibody fused to the anti-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-10 (IL10).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-23"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Antibody-cytokine fusion proteins: a novel class of ... - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6994257/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Antibody-cytokine fusion proteins represent a novel class of biopharmaceuticals, with the potential to increase the therapeutic index of cytokine payloads and to promote leukocyte infiltration at the site of disease.\n...\nOne product based on IL10 (F8-IL10) is currently being investigated in Phase II clinical trials in patients with rheumatoid arthritis or ulterative colitis, while other products have shown promising signs of efficacy in animal models [14, 168].\n...\nThe fully human F8-IL10 fusion protein (Dekavil) was tested in a Phase Ib clinical trial against rheumatoid arthritis in combination with methotrexate (NCT02076659).\n15 out of 23 treated patients experienced therapeutic benefit, including 2 long-lasting remissions [177] and is now being investigated in multiple Phase II clinical trials (NCT02270632, EudraCT number 2013-005418-37).\nAdditionally, F8-IL10 is tested in a Phase II clinical trial as add-on therapy to infliximab in patients with ulcerative colitis (EudraCT number 2017-002108-28).", "date": "2019-04-13", "last_updated": "2026-06-24"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Dekavil (F8-IL10) / Pfizer, Philogen", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=38609b33-9fa9-4d28-ad56-2b75573a091a", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "Dekavil (F8-IL10)\n/ Pfizer, Philogen\n...\nFebruary 13, 2026\nEfficacy and safety of the interleukin-10-fragment F8 fusion protein PF-06687234 as add-on therapy to infliximab in patients with active ulcerative colitis: a randomized, phase IIa clinical trial.\n(PubMed, Therap Adv Gastroenterol)\n- P2a | \"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03269695.\nNCT03269695.\"\n...\nMarch 08, 2025\nEFFICACY AND SAFETY OF THE INTERLEUKIN-10-FRAGMENT F8 FUSION PROTEIN, PF06687234, AS ADD-ON THERAPY TO INFLIXIMAB IN PATIENTS WITH ACTIVE ULCERATIVE COLITIS: A RANDOMIZED, PHASE 2A CLINICAL TRIAL\n(DDW 2025)\n- P2a | \"PF06687234 was well tolerated, but when combined with background infliximab treatment, did not meet the efficacy endpoints in this small cohort of participants with active UC.\nThe inability to detect high levels of PF06687234 in mucosal biopsies, suggests that efficacious tissue concentrations may not have been achieved at the dose tested.\nThis therapeutic hypothesis should be investigated in larger studies and with higher doses than studied here.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-08"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Efficacy and safety of the interleukin-10–fragment F8 fusion ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12891412/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "# Efficacy and safety of the interleukin-10–fragment F8 fusion protein PF-06687234 as add-on therapy to infliximab in patients with active ulcerative colitis: a randomized, phase IIa clinical trial\n...\nPF-06687234 is a novel, human, single-chain variable fragment cytokine fusion protein comprising the antibody fragment F8 and the immunoregulatory cytokine interleukin-10.\n...\nWe evaluated the efficacy and safety of PF-06687234 as an add-on therapy to infliximab in participants with active ulcerative colitis (UC).\n...\nThis phase IIa, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group, multicenter study randomized (1:1) participants with active UC to subcutaneous PF-06687234 20 mg or placebo once weekly, and participants continued intravenous background infliximab every 6 or 8 weeks, for 12 weeks.\n...\nAfter 20 participants were randomized and treated, this study was stopped early for futility.\nThere were no statistically significant differences between groups for any efficacy endpoint (all *p* > 0.05), although numerical trends towards efficacy were observed in some secondary endpoints.\nPF-06687234 was detected at a low concentration in only one colon tissue sample.\nThere were no differences in AEs between the groups.\nThe most frequently reported AE in the PF-06687234-treated group was injection site reaction.\n...\nPF-06687234 was well tolerated, but when combined with background infliximab, did not meet the primary efficacy endpoint in this cohort of participants with active UC.\nEfficacious PF-06687234 tissue concentrations may not have been achieved at the dose tested.\n...\nEfficacy, safety, and tolerability of PF-06687234 as add-on therapy to infliximab in active UC subjects not in remission.\nhttps://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03269695.\nNCT03269695.\n...\nOne approach to such combination therapy is PF-06687234 (also known as Dekavil or IL10-F8), a novel, human, single-chain variable fragment fusion protein comprising the antibody fragment F8 and IL-10.\nF8 selectively binds to fibronectin extra-domain A, which is primarily expressed in blood vessels during inflammation.^\n20 ^ The inclusion of the F8 component in PF-06687234 is designed to enable the retention of IL-10 at sites of active inflammation in the tissues of patients with active UC.\n...\nThe conjugation of IL-10 and F8 is intended to increase the retention of the recombinant cytokine at sites of inflammation and could offer a promising solution to overcome these challenges.\n...\nTaken together, these findings suggest that PF-06687234 could be a potential treatment for UC, either as a standalone therapeutic or as part of a combination regimen that can overcome the dose-limiting toxicity of IL-10.\n...\nStudy B7581002 (NCT03269695) was a phase IIa, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group, multicenter study conducted at 27 sites across 11 countries in participants with active UC and prior partial response but nonremission to infliximab.\n...\nEligible participants were randomized (1:1) to SC PF-06687234 20 mg and intravenous (IV) infliximab (referred to as the “PF-06687234 plus infliximab” group) or placebo and infliximab (referred to as the “infliximab-only” group).\n...\nOur approach to deliver IL-10 to the sites of inflammation in the colonic mucosa employed PF-06687234, a protein comprising the antibody fragment F8 and the immunoregulatory cytokine IL-10.", "date": "2026-02-10", "last_updated": "2026-04-09"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Pfizer Pipeline", "url": "https://cdn.pfizer.com/pfizercom/product-pipeline/Pipeline_Update_29JAN2019.pdf", "domain": "cdn.pfizer.com", "snippet": "Pipeline represents progress of R&D \nprograms as of October 30, 2018 \n...\n• XELJANZ® (tofacitinib) for treatment of adult patients with moderately to \nseverely active Ulcerative Colitis (EU) \n...\nPhase 3\nDekavil\nIL-10\nRheumatoid Arthritis (Biologic)\nPhase 2\nDekavil\nIL-10\nInflammatory Bowel Disease (Biologic)\nPhase 2\nPF-06480605\nTNFSF15 Blocker\nUlcerative Colitis (Biologic)\nPhase 2\n...\nPF-06651600\nJAK3\nUlcerative Colitis\nPhase 2", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-11"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] WJG - Semantic Scholar", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/e659/43e0b91137413dba6c4280cef57b025291df.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "IL-10 helps to maintain tissue integrity and promotes intestinal tissue homeostasis through its anti-inflammatory, anti-apoptotic, and tissue regeneration properties.\nThe new immune cytokine, F8-IL-10 (Dekavil), a targeted antibody associated with IL-10, is currently being evaluated in phase 2 clinical trials in patients with rheumatoid arthritis, potentially paving the way for its use in IBD patients[12].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-09"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Treatment of a disease of the gastrointestinal tract with il-10 or an il-10 agonist", "url": "https://patents.google.com/patent/WO2018183931A1/en", "domain": "patents.google.com", "snippet": "- the present invention provides an ingestible device for use in a method of treating a disease of the gastrointestinal tract in a subject, wherein the method comprises orally administering to the subject the ingestible device loaded with a therapeutically effective amount of IL-10 or an IL-10 agonist, wherein the IL-10 or IL-10 agonist is released by the device at a location in the gastrointestinal tract of the subject that is proximate to one or more sites of disease.\n...\n- a method of treating a disease of the gastrointestinal tract in a subject comprises administering to the subject a pharmaceutical formulation comprising IL-10 or an IL-10 agonist wherein the pharmaceutical formulation is released in the subject's gastrointestinal tract proximate to one or more sites of disease.\n...\n- the IL-10 receptor agonist is a fusion protein.\nIn some embodiments, the IL-10 receptor agonist is a fusion protein.\n- the fusion protein comprises the amino acid sequence of an IL-10 protein (or a functional fragment thereof) and a fusion partner (e.g., an Fc region (e.g., human IgG Fc) or human serum albumin).\n...\n- the fusion partner can be an antibody or an antigen-binding antibody fragment (e.g., an scFv) that targets IL-10 receptor agonist to an inflamed tissue.\n...\n- an IL-10 receptor agonist that is a fusion protein can be, e.g., F8-IL-10, such as Dekavil (Philogen).\n- the fusion protein is a L19-IL-10 fusion protein, a HyHEL10-IL- 10 fusion protein, or a variant thereof.\n...\nIn some embodiments, an IL-10 receptor agonist that is a fusion protein can be, e.g., F8-IL-10, such as Dekavil (Philogen).\n...\nIn some embodiments, the IL-10 receptor agonist is an antibody (e.g., F8-IL10 (also known as DEKAVIL) or a variant thereof (see, e.g., Schwager et al., Arthritis Res. Ther.", "date": "2018-03-30", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Pfizer Pipeline", "url": "https://cdn.pfizer.com/pfizercom/product-pipeline/Pipeline_Update_04MAY2021.pdf?VersionId=.06Q_HtLdkBW8lme0Y3EXpraBpgGspyg", "domain": "cdn.pfizer.com", "snippet": "Pipeline represents progress of R&D \nprograms as of May 4, 2021\n\n17 programs advanced or are new\n\n2 programs discontinued since last \nupdate\n...\nIncluded are 68 NMEs, 31 additional \n...\nJAK3/TEC Inhibitor\n...\nPhase 3\nNew Molecular Entity\nDekavil\nIL-10\nRheumatoid Arthritis (Biologic)\nPhase 2\nNew Molecular Entity\nPF-06480605\nTNFSF15 Blocker\nUlcerative Colitis (Biologic)\nPhase 2\nNew Molecular Entity\nritlecitinib +/- PF-06650833\nJAK3/TEC Inhibitor\nIRAK4 Inhibitor\n...\nritlecitinib (PF-06651600)\nbrepocitinib (PF-06700841)\nJAK3/TEC Inhibitor\nTYK2/JAK1 Inhibitor\nUlcerative Colitis\nPhase 2\n...\nDevelopment\nSubmission Type\nDekavil\nIL-10\nUlcerative Colitis (Biologic)", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-06"}], "raw": {"id": "4a7c2a05-f261-48a9-9493-ac7f4d2f76e7"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:22+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q009", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as CHRM1 CHRM3 CHRM4 CHRM5 Small Molecule", "anchors": ["Dicyclomine"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q009-3", "query": "Dicyclomine inflammatory bowel disease clinical development", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Dicyclomine inflammatory bowel disease clinical development", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 4503.0, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "efficacy and safety of hyoscyamine, dicyclomine, and desipramine in ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/crohnscolitis360/article/8/2/otag063/8711826", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "We performed a retrospective cohort study of IPAA patients followed at a pouch clinic between 2014 and 2025.\nPatients treated with hyoscyamine extended release (ER), dicyclomine, or desipramine for high bowel frequency or abdominal pain were included.\n...\nIn patients with high bowel frequency, 235 treatment courses resulted in clinical improvement in 68.5% of patients receiving hyoscyamine ER, 65.0% of those receiving dicyclomine, and 63.5% of those receiving desipramine.\nFor abdominal pain (94 treatment courses), improvement for hyoscyamine ER, dicyclomine, and desipramine was observed in 61.5%, 68.3%, and 76.2% of courses, respectively.\nApproximately 60% of treatment courses persisted beyond one year.\nLong-term persistence (≥3 years) was highest with dicyclomine (48.7%), followed by desipramine (38.7%) and hyoscyamine ER (37.8%).\nAdverse events were infrequent and consistent with known safety profiles.\n...\nIn summary, antispasmodic agents and low-dose neuromodulators appear to be effective and generally well tolerated for the management of high bowel frequency and abdominal pain in patients with irritable pouch syndrome.", "date": "2026-04-22", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Dicyclomine 20mg", "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=a330a08e-0cb6-bc66-e053-2995a90ad9e9&type=display", "domain": "dailymed.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Dicyclomine hydrochloride tablets, USP are indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nThe recommended initial dose is 20 mg four times a day.\nAfter one week treatment with the initial dose, the dose may be increased to 40 mg four times a day unless side effects limit dosage escalation.\nIf efficacy is not achieved within 2 weeks or side effects require doses below 80 mg per day, the drug should be discontinued.\nDocumented safety data are not available for doses above 80 mg daily for periods longer than 2 weeks.\n...\nRarely development of Ogilvie’s syndrome (colonic pseudo-obstruction) has been reported.\n...\nCaution should be taken in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nLarge doses may suppress intestinal motility to the point of producing a paralytic ileus and the use of this drug may precipitate or aggravate the serious complication of toxic megacolon\n...\nThe data described below reflect exposure in controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of 160 mg daily (40 mg four times a day).\n...\nNine percent (9%) of patients were discontinued from dicyclomine hydrochloride because of one or more of these side effects (compared with 2% in the placebo group).\nIn 41% of the patients with side effects, side effects disappeared or were tolerated at the 160 mg daily dose without reduction.\n...\nDicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract.\n...\nIn controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients who received drug, 82% of patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of 160 mg daily (40 mg four times daily) demonstrated a favorable clinical response compared with 55% treated with placebo (p<0.05).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Dicyclomine: Package Insert", "url": "https://www.drugs.com/pro/dicyclomine.html", "domain": "drugs.com", "snippet": "Initial U.S. Approval: 1950\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent indicated for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome (1) (1)\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nThe recommended initial dose is 20 mg four times a day.\nAfter one week treatment with the initial dose, the dose may be increased to 40 mg four times a day unless side effects limit dosage escalation.\nIf efficacy is not achieved within 2 weeks or side effects require doses below 80 mg per day, the drug should be discontinued.\n...\nCaution should be taken in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nLarge doses may suppress intestinal motility to the point of producing a paralytic ileus and the use of this drug may precipitate or aggravate the serious complication of toxic megacolon [ see Adverse Reactions (6.3)].\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is contraindicated in patients with severe ulcerative colitis [ see Contraindications (4)].\n...\nDicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract.\n...\nIn controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients who received drug, 82% of patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of 160 mg daily (40 mg four times daily) demonstrated a favorable clinical response compared with 55% treated with placebo (p<0.05).", "date": "2026-07-19", "last_updated": "2026-07-19"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "https://nctr-crs.fda.gov/fdalabel/services/spl/ ...", "url": "https://nctr-crs.fda.gov/fdalabel/services/spl/set-ids/40e21575-320f-2ac6-e054-00144ff88e88/spl-doc?hl", "domain": "nctr-crs.fda.gov", "snippet": "Initial U.S. Approval: 1950\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent indicated for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome (\n...\nStarting dose: 20 mg four times a day.\nAfter a week treatment with the starting dose, the dose may be escalated to 40 mg four times a day, unless side effects limit dosage escalation\n...\nIf efficacy is not achieved within 2 weeks or side effects require doses below 80 mg per day, the drug should be discontinued.\nDocumented safety data are not available for doses above 80 mg daily for periods longer than 2 weeks.\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride needs to be used with caution in conditions characterized by tachyarrhythmia such as thyrotoxicosis, congestive heart failure and in cardiac surgery, where they may further accelerate the heart rate.\nInvestigate any tachycardia before administration of dicyclomine hydrochloride.\nCare is required in patients with coronary heart disease, as ischemia and infarction may be worsened, and in patients with hypertension\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride may produce drowsiness, dizziness or blurred vision.\n...\nToxic dilatation of intestine and intestinal perforation is possible when anticholinergic agents are administered in patients with Salmonella dysentery.\n5.7 Ulcerative Colitis\nCaution should be taken in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nLarge doses may suppress intestinal motility to the point of producing a paralytic ileus and the use of this drug may precipitate or aggravate the serious complication of toxic megacolon\n[see\n...\n. Dicyclomine hydrochloride is contraindicated in patients with severe ulcerative colitis\n...\nThe most serious adverse reactions reported with dicyclomine hydrochloride include cardiovascular and central nervous system symptoms\n...\nThe most common adverse reactions (> 5% of patients) are dizziness, dry mouth, vision blurred, nausea, somnolence, asthenia and nervousness (\n...\nIn controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients who received drug, 82% of patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of 160 mg daily (40 mg four times daily) demonstrated a favorable clinical response compared with 55% treated with placebo (p<0.05).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-23"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "The Impact of Antispasmodic Use on Abdominal Pain and ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12116234/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Patients with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) are often prescribed antispasmodics for chronic abdominal pain.\nLarge-scale data regarding efficacy and impact on clinical outcomes are lacking.\n...\nWe included 85,859 patients (median age 50; 53.8% female) with IBD; 5,661 used antispasmodics.\nOn follow up, those with antispasmodic use had higher rates of abdominal pain and opioid use (P < 0.001) regardless of baseline abdominal pain or opioid use.\nAfter matching, 5,629 patients remained per group.\nPatients who used antispasmodics had higher rates of abdominal pain at 1 month, regardless of baseline abdominal pain.\nOpioid-naïve patients who used antispasmodics had higher rates of opioid use at follow up (1.1% *vs*.\n0.2%; P < 0.001).\nLikelihood of corticosteroid use, clinic visits, ER visits and hospitalization were higher in those with antispasmodic use.\nNo differences in IBD-related complications, surgery or mortality were observed.\n...\nAntispasmodic use in patients with IBD was associated with increased abdominal pain and opioid use in opioid-naïve patients.\nAntispasmodic use was associated with increased likelihood of corticosteroid use, clinic and ER visits and hospitalization.\n...\nIn this large, population-based study, based on propensity-matched retrospective analyses of claims-based data from the TriNetX Diamond Network, IBD patients prescribed antispasmodics demonstrated increased likelihood of abdominal pain and opioid use after one month.\n...\nAntispasmodic medications, like dicyclomine, have been used to good effect in irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)^6, 7^.\n...\nAs such, these medications are recommended by the American Gastroenterological Association for patients with IBD and IBS or functional symptoms^11^.\nDespite these recommendations, no large scale and/or systematic studies have been undertaken to assess their efficacy in management of abdominal pain, or their impact on other clinical outcomes in IBD^12^.\nHere, we employed a large commercial and government insurance and electronic medical record database to assess the efficacy of antispasmodics on abdominal pain and rates of opioid use in IBD, stratified by baseline abdominal pain and opioid use.\n...\nWe identified patients receiving an antispasmodic prescription (dicyclomine or hyoscyamine) with at least 30-day supply based on RxNorm codes (Supplementary Table1).\n...\nAntispasmodic use was not associated with improvement in abdominal pain or opioid use in patients with IBD.\nIn fact, the converse was observed even after controlling for metrics of IBD severity and baseline characteristics including opioid use and abdominal pain.\n...\nConsidering the findings of this study as a whole, there was a general lack of evidence indicating that antispasmodics help control abdominal pain in the setting of IBD.\n...\nThese data represent the first large scale assessment of the efficacy of antispasmodics in patients with IBD.", "date": "2025-04-11", "last_updated": "2026-07-19"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacology", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00804", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "snippet": "## Overview\n...\n- A medication used to treat disorders affecting the large intestines.\n...\n- YES\n...\n- Clinical Trials\n- Phase 0\n- 0\n- Phase 1\n- 0\n- Phase 2\n- 0\n- Phase 3\n- 0\n- Phase 4\n- 0\n...\n**Dicyclomine**is an antimuscarinic agent used to treat IBS.\n...\nDicyclomine is a muscarinic M1, M3, and M2 receptor antagonist as well as a non-competitive inhibitor of histamine and bradykinin used to treat spasms of the intestines seen in functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome.\nThough it is commonly prescribed, its recommendation may have been based on a small amount of evidence and so its prescription is becoming less favourable.\nDicyclomine was granted FDA approval on 11 May 1950.\n...\n- Small Molecule\n...\n- 3DMOLSDF3D-SDFPDBSMILESInChI Similar Structures\n...\nDicyclomine is indicated for the treatment of functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nDicyclomine is an anticholinergic drug used to relax the smooth muscles of the intestines.\nIt's duration of action is not especially long as it is usually taken 4 times daily with individual doses of 20-40mg orally or 10-20mg by intramuscular injection.\nDicyclomine should not be administered intravenously.\n- Mechanism of action\nDicyclomine achieves its action partially through direct antimuscarinic activity of the M1, M3, and M2 receptors; and partially through antagonism of bradykinin and histamine.\nDicyclomine non-competitively inhibits the action of bradykinin and histamine, resulting in direct action on the smooth muscle, and decreased strength of contractions seen in spasms of the ileum.\n...\n- Volume of distribution\n...\nDicyclomine is 79.5% eliminated in the urine and 8.4% in the feces.\n...\nThe mean plasma elimination half life is approximately 1.8 hours.\n...\n- Adverse Effects\n...\nPatients experiencing an overdose may present with headache, nausea, vomiting, blurred vision, dilated pupils, dizziness, dry mouth, difficulty swallowing, CNS stimulation, as well as hot, dry skin.\n...\n- Not Available", "date": "2025-04-30", "last_updated": "2025-08-29"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Managing Pain in Inflammatory Bowel Disease - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3264972/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Even after careful verification of clinical and endoscopic remission, 20% of patients will continue to have pain.^9^ In the SONIC trial, over one third of patients entering the study with a diagnosis of moderate-to-severe Crohn's disease did not have evidence of active disease on endoscopy.^10^ One reason for this finding may be the high rates of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) in patients with IBD.\n...\nWhen pain is associated with active IBD, the primary treatment will often be escalation of IBD therapy.\nHowever, pain may persist despite adequate IBD therapy, or pain may arise from a non-IBD source.\n...\n|Antispasmodics|Hyoscyamine XR|0.125–0.25 mg every 4–6 hours or 0.375–0.75 mg BID|1.5 mg/day|Constipation, dizziness, dry mouth, sedation, urinary retention|\n|Antispasmodics|Dicyclomine|20 mg QID|160 mg/day|Constipation, dizziness, dry mouth, sedation, urinary retention|\n...\nControlled trials of NSAIDs in IBD have been lacking.\nA small study showed a 20–30% increased risk of flares in patients who were started on NSAIDs compared to patients who were started on acetaminophen.^46^ All NSAID-treated patients experienced a flare within 9 days of starting the medication, but most were able to re-attain remission simply by stopping the NSAID.^46^", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Pain management in patients with inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3437534/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Since one of the cardinal features of intestinal inflammation is abdominal pain, inducing remission using standard of care IBD treatments (e.g. aminosalicylates, antibiotics, corticosteroids, enteral therapy, immunomodulators, and biological response modifiers) can alleviate inflammation-related pain in most patients.\n...\nAntispasmodics have some utility in providing a bridge of pain relief as remission is induced and aiding with pain if standard medical therapies are unable to control inflammation.\n...\nOverall antispasmodics were found to be more efficacious than placebo for functional abdominal pain.\nOf the medications studied by their group, hyoscine (hyoscyamine) was found to be the most efficacious and is the only one available in US markets.\nPractically speaking, the antispasmodics used most frequently are hyoscyamine and dicyclomine.\nUnfortunately, though antispasmodics seem to be a reasonable choice for pain management associated with inflammation or partial obstruction, there is no definitive evidence supporting their usage in IBD [Makharia, 2011].\nFurthermore, one must be wary of potential side effects, including fulminant obstruction in an already partially obstructed gut or worsening dysmotility in a gut that is already prone to motility problems secondary to inflammation and its sequelae.\n...\nHowever, multiple studies have demonstrated that NSAIDs may increase the risk for IBD relapse [Kefelakes *et al*.\n2009; Cryer, 2005; Felder *et al*.\n2000 ], flares [Evans *et al*. 1997], and overall increase in disease activity [Bonner *et al*. 2000].\n...\nUnfortunately, COX-2 inhibitors have not been shown to be beneficial in terms of safety, toxicity, or even anti-inflammatory effect.\n...\nRegardless of the criticisms of COX-2 inhibitors, it is important to note there are no trials specifically investigating the utility of these medications for abdominal pain associated with IBD.\n...\nBased on the current literature and clinical consensus, we recommend a trial of low-dose TCA for functional abdominal pain in IBD.\n...\nIn addition to standard IBD-specific treatments, medical therapy for pain arising from inflammation or strictures/adhesions could include antispasmodics [i.e. hyoscyamine (hyoscine), dicyclomine (dicycloverine)] as needed.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Ther Adv Gastroenterol", "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt1jq8w664/qt1jq8w664_noSplash_fdbe6351b18cc7bb8acc8d1f7d921485.pdf?t=sq66vr", "domain": "escholarship.org", "snippet": "Hyoscyamine [Drugs.\ncom, 2015]\nDicyclomine [US \nFood and Drug \nAdministration, 2013a]\nCommonly prescribed \nfor IBS [Trinkley and \nNahata, 2014]\nOften used for \npostprandial IBS \nsymptoms [Chang et al. \n2014]\nBlock muscarinic acetylcholine \nreceptors, causing smooth \nmuscle relaxation [Trinkley and \nNahata 2014]\nHyoscyamine: up to 1.5 \nmg/day\nDicyclomine: 20–40 mg \nPO QID\n[Trinkley and Nahata 2014]\nImproves global IBS symptoms and abdominal \npain\n[Chang et al. 2014]\nA Cochrane review shows improvement in \nabdominal pain was not clinically meaningful \n[Ruepert et al. 2011]\n...\nCommon AEs: constipation, dry \n...\nWith regard to treatment, gut-acting agents, such as antispasmodics like dicyclomine, which blunt the gastrocolonic reflex responsible for the postprandial urge to defecate, are appro- priate as first-line pharmacologic treatment in this case [Chey et al. 2015b].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-02-25"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Drug Repurposing Opportunities for Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://meddocsonline.org/ebooks/inflammatory-bowel-disease/drug-repurposing-opportunities-for-inflammatory-bowel-disease.pdf", "domain": "meddocsonline.org", "snippet": "Con­ sequently, several trials were carried out to demonstrate that thioguanine in adequate dose of 0.2–0.3 mg/kg (not exceeding\n25 mg per day), does not cause any liver abnormalities and was\ntherapeutically repositioned as an immunosuppressive drug for the treatment of patients with IBD in the year 2001 [18].\n...\nINCB3284 and MLNL120 are the two experimental CCR2 antagonist drugs that are being currently investigated for the possible treatment of IBD.\n...\nAntispasmodic agents\nInhibits gastrointestinal propulsive \nmotility due to its antimuscarinic \nand musculotropic effects\nDicyclomine\nSevere constipation, bloating, or \nstomach pain, worsening of diarrhea or\nother irritable bowel symptoms", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-07"}], "raw": {"id": "75da8378-c23e-41a8-a85b-7b1c85848634"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:26+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q010", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication LY4395089, Eli Lilly & Company, Crohn''s Disease", "anchors": ["LY4395089"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q010-1", "query": "LY4395089 mechanism target Crohn’s disease", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "LY4395089 mechanism target Crohn’s disease", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 7659.4, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "LY-4395089 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/026286e1f04d4c549c14023c3c12d3ed", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Therapeutic Areas\nImmune System Diseases\nDigestive System Disorders\nActive Indication\nCrohn Disease\nCrohn's disease, active moderate\nCrohn's disease, active severe", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-16"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Study Details | NCT07483099 | ClinicalTrials.gov - ClinicalTrials.gov", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07483099", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "The main purpose of this study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD).\n...\n|--|--|--|\n|Percentage of Participants with Crohn's Disease who Achieve Endoscopic Response|The percentage of participants who achieve endoscopic response at week 12 of Study Period 1.|Baseline, Up to Week 12|\n...\n|Percentage of Participants who Achieve Clinical Remission| |Baseline, Up to Week 12|\n|Percent Change from Baseline in Fecal Calprotectin| |Baseline, Up to Week 12|", "date": "2026-04-21", "last_updated": "2026-04-21"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Multiple Drugs for Ulcerative Colitis or Crohn's Disease", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-2-crohn-disease-3-2026-c639c", "domain": "withpower.com", "snippet": "This trial tests new treatments for individuals with ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease, conditions that cause inflammation in the digestive tract.\nThe focus is on evaluating the effectiveness and safety of these treatments for those with moderate to severe symptoms.\n...\nFor LY4395089, less information is available because it is still under testing and not yet approved by the FDA.\nThis trial is in an early stage (Phase 2), meaning researchers are still assessing its safety and effectiveness.\nWhile some safety data exist, further testing is necessary to confirm its safety.\n...\nResearchers are excited about these treatments for ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease because they offer new ways to tackle these conditions.\nUnlike standard treatments like corticosteroids and immunosuppressants, Mirikizumab is a monoclonal antibody that targets the p19 subunit of IL-23, potentially offering a more targeted approach to inflammation.\nThe co-administration of LY4395089 with Mirikizumab introduces an oral component to the treatment, which could enhance convenience and effectiveness.\nThis combination approach could lead to more personalized and efficient treatment, potentially improving patient outcomes and quality of life.\n...\nResearch has shown that mirikizumab, one of the treatments in this trial, effectively treats both ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\nStudies indicate that about 63% of patients with ulcerative colitis experienced symptom improvement.\nFor Crohn's disease, mirikizumab helped over 80% of patients maintain relief from symptoms like the urgent need to use the bathroom.\n...\nEarly results suggest that the combination might be more effective, as LY4395089 outperformed a placebo in other studies.\n...\nThe study tests new drugs (LY4395089 and Mirikizumab given by IV or injection) in people with moderate to severe Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis.\nParticipants are randomly assigned to different treatment groups.\nThe study lasts at least 62 weeks.\n...\nLY4395089 is an investigational drug, meaning it is currently being studied in clinical trials and is not yet approved by the FDA for any ...", "date": "2024-07-03", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Mirikizumab | Drug Profile | Forefront", "url": "https://forefrontedge.com/drug/mirikizumab-2668384", "domain": "forefrontedge.com", "snippet": "Canonical name\nMirikizumab\n...\nPrimary mechanism\nInterleukin-23 inhibitor\nRegulatory jurisdictions\n...\n## Pharmacology & targets\n2 targets\nKnown molecular targets and mechanisms supported by curated pharmacology databases.\nInterleukin-12 subunit beta\nIL12B · P29460\nINHIBITORInterleukin-23 inhibitor\nInterleukin-23 subunit alpha\nIL23A · Q9NPF7\nINHIBITORInterleukin-23 inhibitor\n...\nOmvoh is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis who have had an inadequate response with, lost response to, or were intolerant to either conventional therapy or a biologic treatment.", "date": "2023-05-26", "last_updated": "2026-09-04"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "LY4395089 + Mirikizumab for Crohn's Disease - WithPower", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-2-crohn-disease-3-2026-c750c", "domain": "withpower.com", "snippet": "This trial tests a new combination treatment for individuals with moderate to severe Crohn's disease, a condition that causes inflammation in the digestive tract.\nResearchers aim to determine if combining LY4395089, an experimental drug, with mirikizumab is more effective than using mirikizumab alone.\n...\nLY4395089 is a novel oral treatment that, when combined with mirikizumab, could provide a more targeted approach to managing inflammation.\nMirikizumab, an IL-23 inhibitor, is administered intravenously and then subcutaneously, which might offer more sustained relief for patients who respond well.\nThis combination has the potential to enhance efficacy and improve patient outcomes by leveraging different mechanisms of action and routes of administration.\n...\nResearch has shown that mirikizumab effectively treats Crohn's disease.\nSpecifically, studies found that over 92% of patients who were symptom-free remained so after two years of treatment.\nAdditionally, about 60-67% of patients experienced significant symptom improvement.\n...\nLY4395089 reduces inflammation, and its use with mirikizumab may yield even better results by more effectively addressing gut inflammation.\nThis combination aims to provide strong, long-lasting control of Crohn's disease symptoms.^1^^2^^5^^6^^7^\n...\nThe study compares the safety and effectiveness of a new drug (LY4395089) given together with mirikizumab versus mirikizumab alone.\nTreatments are given by IV or under the skin over about 62 weeks.", "date": "2021-10-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "A clinical research study for people with moderately to ...", "url": "https://trials.lilly.com/en-US/trial/706080", "domain": "trials.lilly.com", "snippet": "A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease to test if 2 investigational medicines [LY4395089 and mirikizumab (LY3074828)] given together can help treat Crohn’s disease (CD01 study).", "date": "2026-05-04", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Lilly Crohn's and Ulcerative Colitis Clinical Trials | IBD Research", "url": "https://trials.lilly.com/en-US/research-areas/immunology/crohns-ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "trials.lilly.com", "snippet": "Experts believe that, in some people, normal bacteria in the digestive tract may cause the immune system to attack healthy normal cells.\nThis is called an autoimmune reaction.\n...\n### A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease to test if different investigati...\nENROLLING\nAGE\n18 - 80 years\nConditions\nModerately to severely active ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease\nInvestigational Medicines\nMirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089 or another investigational medicine\nInvestigational Medicines\nMirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089 or another investigational medicine\nPhase\nII", "date": "2026-09-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "A clinical research study for people with ... - Lilly Trials", "url": "https://trials.lilly.com/en-US/trial/706079", "domain": "trials.lilly.com", "snippet": "The purpose of the overall study is to test different investigational medicines (medicines being studied) to see if they can be used alone, or together with mirikizumab, to help treat people with ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease.", "date": "2026-05-04", "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study of LY4395089 and Mirikizumab (LY3074828) Given ...", "url": "https://research.patient.info/research/trials/crohn-disease-nct07483099", "domain": "research.patient.info", "snippet": "One is mirikizumab, a drug that is already being studied and used for inflammatory bowel diseases.\nThe other is a new drug called LY4395089, which works in a different way in the body.", "date": "2026-06-11", "last_updated": "2026-07-31"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohns' Disease Clinical Trial Drug Development Pipeline ...", "url": "https://www.barchart.com/story/news/4798717/crohns-disease-clinical-trial-drug-development-pipeline-expands-with-contributions-from-90-key-companies-delveinsight", "domain": "barchart.com", "snippet": "- ***On September 22, 2026- Eli Lilly and Company* ** initiated a phase 2 study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD).\nThis study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks.", "date": "2026-09-25", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}], "raw": {"id": "56c9e268-e146-459e-a8f9-aae7d10591e0"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:30+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q011", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as Small Molecule targeting ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7", "anchors": ["α4β7 Inhibitor", "α4β7"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q011-1", "query": "α4β7 Inhibitor etrolizumab mechanism IBD", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "α4β7 Inhibitor etrolizumab mechanism IBD", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 756.0, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for inflammatory ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12967-025-07385-3?error=cookies_not_supported&code=1e53ce24-191f-48ed-a22e-45f1dec47600", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "In IBD (CD, UC, pediatric CD), TNFR1/TNFR2 blockade dampens NF‑κB/MAPK activation in gut‑homing lymphocytes, restores epithelial barrier integrity, and limits mucosal infiltration.\n...\nTo reduce systemic toxicity, newer therapies such as vedolizumab exploit gut-selective targeting via α4β7–MAdCAM-1 blockade, limiting interference with CNS immune surveillance and respiratory tract lymphocyte trafficking [21,22,23].\nPharmacokinetic studies confirm minimal systemic immunosuppression and no observed PML cases in the pivotal GEMINI trials.Etrolizumab, by contrast, inhibits both α4β7–MAdCAM-1 and αEβ7–E-cadherin, aiming to block lymphocyte gut homing and retention in the intestinal epithelium.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting β7.\nIt works by blocking the interaction of α4β7 with MAdCAM-1 and the interaction of αEβ7 with E-cadherin, thereby reducing the transport of lymphocytes to the intestine and their retention in the epithelium.\nMouse models and genetic analyses have shown that dual blockade of α4β7 and αEβ7 can more effectively reduce the accumulation of cytotoxic intraepithelial lymphocytes and inflammatory CD8^+^ T cells in the intestinal mucosa of patients with CD [23].", "date": "2025-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-11"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "A randomised phase I study of etrolizumab (rhuMAb β7) in ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3711369/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Etrolizumab (rhuMAb β7, anti-β7, PRO145223) is a humanised monoclonal antibody targeting the β7 subunit of the heterodimeric integrins α4β7 and αEβ7, which are implicated in leucocyte migration and retention in ulcerative colitis (UC).\n...\n- Etrolizumab (antibodies to β7) does inhibit the migration of lymphocytes to the gut and antagonises the retention of lymphocytes in the mucosa by blocking the αEβ7–E-cadherin interaction.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanised IgG1 monoclonal antibody which targets the β7 subunit of the α4β7^29^ ^30^ and αEβ7^15^ integrins with high affinity, blocking binding to their ligands, MAdCAM-1 and E-cadherin, respectively.\n...\nEtrolizumab provides a dual approach mechanism of action for the treatment of IBD that involves inhibition of leucocyte trafficking to the gut (through blocking of α4β7–MAdCAM-1 interactions) as well as inhibition of retention of leucocytes in the intraepithelial lining of the gut (through blocking αEβ7–E-cadherin interactions), which may provide improved efficacy over currently available therapies.\n...\nThus, etrolizumab could affect leucocyte composition within the gut mucosa through multiple mechanisms.\nFirst, as discussed above, it could block α4β7-expressing leucocytes from entering the gut by inhibiting their extravasation through MAdCAM-1-expressing mucosal endothelial venules.", "date": "2012-06-20", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Etrolizumab", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12189", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "snippet": "**Etrolizumab**is a humanized IgG1κ anti-β7 integrin subunit monoclonal antibody under investigation for use in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized IgG1κ monoclonal antibody directed against the β7 subunit of gastrointestinal α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins that, due to its target specificity, appears as or more efficacious than vedolizumab and without the CNS effects of natalizumab.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized anti-β7 integrin subunit monoclonal antibody that impairs the targeting and retention of gut-specific lymphocytes contributing to an inflammatory microenvironment in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\n...\nInflammatory bowel disease (IBD), including ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease, is characterized by chronic inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract.\nThis inflammation is driven, at least in part, by excessive infiltration and retention of intestinal-homing lymphocytes.\nAn adhesion cascade drives the infiltration of immune cells.\nThe binding of immune cell glycoproteins to their corresponding epithelial cell selectins results in partial retention and the \"rolling\" of immune cells along the endothelium.\nA stable arrest is mediated by stronger interactions between immune cell integrins and cell adhesion molecules (CAMs) on the endothelial cell; once arrested, immune cells will extravasate and enter the target tissue.\nTissue retention is mediated by similar interactions between cell-surface molecules within the target tissue, which impedes immune cells' re-entry into the systemic circulation.\n...\nOf primary importance to intestinal homing is the mucosal addressin cellular adhesion molecule (MAdCAM-1), which interacts strongly with α4β7 integrins, and vascular cell adhesion molecule (VCAM-1), which binds with lower affinity.\n...\nα4β7 integrin is expressed at high levels on intestinal-homing lymphocytes but can also be found on natural killer cells, basophils, eosinophils, macrophages, activated monocytes, and mast cells.\n...\nThe infiltration and retention of immune cells as a mechanism of IBD is suggested by observations across multiple studies of an increase in the number of, and alteration in the secreted cytokine profile of, α4β7\n+and αEβ7 +immune cells in the gastrointestinal tract of IBD patients.\n...\nAlthough the mechanism has not been completely elucidated, it is clear that an increase in the number and retention of gut-homing immune cells capable of sustaining an inflammatory microenvironment is a crucial element of IBD pathophysiology.\nEtrolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody specific for the β7 integrin subunit; it has a K\ndof 116 ± 11 pmol/L against human α4β7 and 1800 ± 170 pmol/L against human αEβ7, as assessed by transfection in HEK293 cells.\nThis strong binding provides efficient inhibition of integrin/CAM interactions.\nEtrolizumab has an IC 50for the α4β7 interactions with MAdCAM-1, VCAM-1, and fibronectin of 0.075 ± 0.034, 0.089 ± 0.009, and 0.119 ± 0.056 nmol/L, respectively.\nEtrolizumab also inhibits the interaction of αEβ7 with E-cadherin, albeit less effectively (IC 50of 3.96 ± 1.78 nmol/L).\nWhile etrolizumab does not exhibit antibody-dependent cytotoxicity, it disrupts the integrin-CAM interactions involved in gut immune cell residence, in part by inducing the endocytic uptake of bound integrin receptors, and therefore reduces the inflammatory burden in IBD.\n...\nIntegrin ITGA4/ITGB7 (alpha-4/beta-7) (Peyer patches-specific homing receptor LPAM-1) is an adhesion molecule that mediates lymphocyte migration and homing to gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT) (Probable).", "date": "2012-02-12", "last_updated": "2025-08-15"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12967-025-07385-3?error=cookies_not_supported&code=0c8fefc4-9b58-46fd-9932-8178652bd368", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "As shown in Fig. 1, current and emerging targeted therapies act via blocking extracellular signals, interfering with intracellular signaling, and modulating leukocyte trafficking.\n...\nIn IBD (CD, UC, pediatric CD), TNFR1/TNFR2 blockade dampens NF‑κB/MAPK activation in gut‑homing lymphocytes, restores epithelial barrier integrity, and limits mucosal infiltration.\n...\nTo reduce systemic toxicity, newer therapies such as vedolizumab exploit gut-selective targeting via α4β7–MAdCAM-1 blockade, limiting interference with CNS immune surveillance and respiratory tract lymphocyte trafficking [21,22,23].\nPharmacokinetic studies confirm minimal systemic immunosuppression and no observed PML cases in the pivotal GEMINI trials.Etrolizumab, by contrast, inhibits both α4β7–MAdCAM-1 and αEβ7–E-cadherin, aiming to block lymphocyte gut homing and retention in the intestinal epithelium.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting β7.\nIt works by blocking the interaction of α4β7 with MAdCAM-1 and the interaction of αEβ7 with E-cadherin, thereby reducing the transport of lymphocytes to the intestine and their retention in the epithelium.\nMouse models and genetic analyses have shown that dual blockade of α4β7 and αEβ7 can more effectively reduce the accumulation of cytotoxic intraepithelial lymphocytes and inflammatory CD8^+^ T cells in the intestinal mucosa of patients with CD [23].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-21"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Review article: nonclinical and clinical pharmacology ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6001693/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Etrolizumab exerts its effect via a unique dual MOA that inhibits both leucocyte trafficking to the intestinal mucosa and retention within the intestinal epithelial layer.\nThe gut‐selectivity of etrolizumab results from its specific targeting of the β7 subunit of α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins.\nEtrolizumab does not bind to α4β1 integrin, which mediates lymphocyte trafficking to tissues including the central nervous system, a characteristic underlying its favourable safety with regard to progressive multifocal leucoencephalopathy.\n...\nBinding to β7 integrin enables etrolizumab to block both leucocyte trafficking to the gut via α4β7 interactions with its endothelial ligand mucosal vascular addressin cell adhesion molecule 1 (MAdCAM‐1),17, 18 expressed primarily on endothelium of venules of intestinal lamina\npropria and associated lymphoid tissues19, 20 as well as the retention of lymphocyte subsets within the intestinal mucosa through the interaction of αEβ7 with its ligand E‐cadherin, expressed on the gut epithelium21 (Figure 1, Table 1).22, 23, 24, 25\n...\nSince etrolizumab has a unique dual MOA associated with inhibition of both the α4β7 and αEβ7 pathways, the integrated pharmacological information summarised in this review aims to provide researchers and investigators with a clear view of its unique biology distinct from other anti‐integrin drugs in the class.\n...\nα4β7 integrin plays a crucial role in leucocyte homing to the intestinal mucosa and associated lymphoid tissues.\nα4β7 integrin is constitutively expressed at a modest level on naive T and B cells and at higher levels on intestinal‐homing effector/memory T and B cells.\n...\nIn addition, etrolizumab is highly specific for the β7 subunit of α4β7 and αEβ7 and does not bind to α4 or β1 integrin subunits.", "date": "2018-03-30", "last_updated": "2026-03-05"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Cellular Mechanisms of Etrolizumab Treatment in Inflammatory ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6367223/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Mechanistically, etrolizumab is known to block β7 integrin ligand binding and reduces intestinal trafficking of β7-expressing cells.\nEtrolizumab blocks β7 integrin ligand binding and reduces β7-positive lymphocyte migration and retention in the inflamed gut mucosa, but the exact mechanisms by which this inhibition occurs are not fully understood.\n...\n**Results:** As demonstrated by several different approaches, etrolizumab and etrolizumab-s treatment led to internalization of β7 integrin.\nThis resulted in impaired dynamic adhesion to MAdCAM-1.\n...\nThe translational potential of this mechanism has been impressively demonstrated by the successful clinical implementation of inhibiting the α4β7 integrin by the monoclonal antibody vedolizumab as a therapeutic strategy in IBD (Feagan et al., 2013; Sandborn et al., 2013).\n...\nThe anti-β7 antibody etrolizumab is currently being investigated in several phase III trials in IBD patients and blocks both α4β7-mediated gut homing as well as αEβ7-controlled retention (Vermeire et al., 2014; Zundler et al., 2017c).\n...\nWe show with complementary techniques including molecular microscopy with stimulated emission depletion (STED) imaging and ImageStream^®^ analyses that etrolizumab leads to internalization of α4β7 integrin, that this functionally impairs β7-dependent adhesion to MAdCAM-1, and that internalization of α4β7 integrin is higher with etrolizumab compared with vedolizumab.\n...\nMechanistically, it is believed that targeted inhibition of the interaction of integrins with respective ligands by monoclonal antibodies leads to impaired cell trafficking and subsequent reduction of pro-inflammatory cell infiltration in the gut (Habtezion et al., 2016; Zundler and Neurath, 2017).", "date": "2019-02-01", "last_updated": "2026-03-05"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "2.4. Integrin αeβ7 (cd103)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6973243/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Through interactions between α4β7:MAdCAM‐1 or α4β1:VCAM‐1, migration of effector T lymphocytes to the inflamed gut contributes to the local production of pro‐inflammatory mediators including IFN‐γ, IL‐6, IL‐9, and IL‐17.\n...\nVedolizumab targets the α4β7 subunit and inhibits its interactions with MAdCAM‐1.\nEtrolizumab targets the β7 integrin and inhibits the interaction of α4β7 and αEβ7 with MAdCAM‐1 or E‐cadherin, respectively.\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized antibody which binds to α4β7 and was shown to specifically inhibit the interaction between α4β*7* integrin and its ligands, MAdCAM‐1 and fibronectin (Table 1).106 Given that MAdCAM‐1 expression is largely restricted to the intestine, the inhibitory effects of vedolizumab are regarded as gut selective.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized immunoglobulin (Ig)G1 monoclonal antibody that selectively targets the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins with high affinity and blocks interactions with their respective ligands, MAdCAM‐1 and E‐cadherin (Table 1).", "date": "2019-06-19", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Anti-Integrins for the Treatment of Inflammatory Bowel ...", "url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.2147/CEG.S293272", "domain": "tandfonline.com", "snippet": "Etrolizumab (anti-β7) blocks leukocyte trafficking via α4β7 and cell adhesion via αEβ7 integrins.\n...\nVedolizumab (Takeda) is a humanized monoclonal IgG1 antibody that targets the α4β7 heterodimer.\nThe antibody selectively blocks α4β7:MAdCAM-1 interactions—preventing leukocyte adhesion to intestinal endothelium—but does not affect α4β1:VCAM-1 interactions.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a monoclonal antibody that blocks cellular adhesion by binding the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins.\nThe α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins function as trafficking molecules that localize leukocytes to inflammatory sites in the gut.^Citation56^ Etrolizumab binds α4β7 and prevents binding to MAdCAM-1, a receptor that is highly upregulated in the intestinal venules of patients with UC and CD.\nEtrolizumab also binds αEβ7 and prevents interaction with E-cadherin found on intestinal mucosal cells.\nThe αEβ7-expressing lymphocytes residing in the gut participate in the inflammatory responses observed in IBD.^Citation57^ Thus, the inhibition of αEβ7 results in decreased inflammation and increased retention of leukocytes within the intraepithelial space of the intestinal mucosa.", "date": "2021-08-24", "last_updated": "2026-09-04"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting T-cell integrins in autoimmune and inflammatory diseases", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/cei/article/215/1/15/7239982?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "MAdCAM-1 is expressed in the HEVs of the small intestine, Peyer’s patches, and large intestine, and interacts with l-selectin and α4β7 to mediate rolling and firm adhesion of T cells, respectively [14].\n...\nVCAM-1, which is expressed only when endothelial cells are stimulated by pro-inflammatory cytokines, also binds α4β7 (with lower affinity than MAdCAM-1 does) as well as α4β1.\n...\nThus, drugs that inhibit the action of α4β7, α4β1, and αEβ7 have been developed, trialled, and authorized for the treatment of IBD (Fig. 1) [27].\n...\nVedolizumab binding to α4β7 inhibits the interaction of α4β7 with MAdCAM-1 and fibronectin, but not VCAM-1, as VCAM-1 binds to α4β7 without clashing with vedolizumab [48].\nVedolizumab binding to α4β7 induces the internalization of α4β7, and removal of vedolizumab restores α4β7 expression and MAdCAM-1 binding to normal within 4 days, in vitro.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanised anti-β7 monoclonal antibody and, as such, binds to α4β7 and αEβ7 to inhibit their binding to MAdCAM-1 and VCAM-1, and E-cadherin, respectively.\nT cells expressing αEβ7 bind to E-cadherin that is expressed by epithelial cells on their basolateral surface, causing retention of these T cells in the lamina propria [30].\n...\nAnother mechanism of action of etrolizumab may be the inhibition of αEβ7 on colonic DCs.\n...\nIn IBD, targeting the α4β7-MAdCAM-1 interaction with vedolizumab has shown efficacy in clinical trials and possesses an advantage with regard to safety due to the selectivity of said interaction to the gut, as does targeting β7-chain on its own with etrolizumab.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Advances in the Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis—From Conventional Therapies to Targeted Biologics and Small Molecules", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/27/3/1534", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "Vedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that aims to limit lymphocyte migration toward the intestines by specifically binding to the α4β7 heterodimer found on the surface of intestinal lymphocytes.\n...\nVedolizumab’s mechanism of action is primarily based on disrupting the transport of T cells and leukocytes, which involves the adhesion, activation, and migration of these molecules across vascular walls.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a new-generation anti-adhesion molecule.\nIt is a humanized monoclonal antibody that selectively binds to the β7 subunit, which is part of the heterodimers of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins [53].\n...\nIntegrin α4β7 plays a significant role in leukocyte infiltration into the gastrointestinal tract by interacting with MAdCAM-1 in the vascular endothelium of the mucosa.\nAdditionally, it selectively binds to E-cadherin, which favorably influences T-cell adhesion to the intestinal epithelium.\n...\nEtrolizumab has a bimodal mechanism of action, corresponding to two therapeutic targets [53].\nThis antibody can inhibit leukocyte migration into the intestine by inhibiting α4β7 and MAdCAM-1 and retain leukocytes in the intestinal epithelium by blocking the interaction between E-cadherin and αEβ7.\nThis binding is thought to reduce lymphocyte adhesion and retention, potentially reducing inflammation in the intestine.", "date": "2026-02-04", "last_updated": "2026-08-12"}], "raw": {"id": "54f63d32-f7d4-443a-8ec0-53f84fab7bb0"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:30+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q010", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication LY4395089, Eli Lilly & Company, Crohn''s Disease", "anchors": ["LY4395089"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q010-2", "query": "LY4395089 competing agents Crohn’s disease same mechanism", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "LY4395089 competing agents Crohn’s disease same mechanism", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 4286.7, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "LY4395089 - Crohn Disease | Phase 2 | Eli Lilly and ...", "url": "https://www.biopharmawatch.com/company/LLY/drug/LY4395089", "domain": "biopharmawatch.com", "snippet": "Highest phase Phase 2\n...\nNCT07483099A Study of LY4395089 and Mirikizumab (LY3074828) Given Together and Mirikizumab (Alone) in Adults With Crohn's DiseaseCrohn Disease\n...\n|Itraconazole|DRUG|Administered orally|\n...\n## Competitive Landscape -Crohn's Disease 77 trials (matched to \"Crohn Disease\")\nTop 11 of 13 competitors\n|Company|Ticker|Trials|Lead Phase|Drugs|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Sanofi SA Sponsored ADR|SNY|6|PHASE3|Duvakitug, SAR441566, balinatunfib, SAR442970|\n|Eli Lilly and Company|LLY|8|PHASE3|Mirikizumab, Tirzepatide, MORF-057, LY4395089|\n|AbbVie, Inc.|ABBV|14|PHASE3|Risankizumab, Upadacitinib, Lutikizumab, Trosunilimab, ABBV-8736|\n|Takeda Pharmaceutical Co.\nLtd.\nSponsored ADR|TAK|16|PHASE3|Vedolizumab, Zasocitinib, Darvadstrocel|\n|Johnson & Johnson|JNJ|13|PHASE3|Ustekinumab, Guselkumab, Golimumab, JNJ-78934804, Icotrokinra|\n|Merck & Co., Inc.|MRK|2|PHASE3|Tulisokibart|\n|AstraZeneca PLC|AZN|2|PHASE2|AZD7798|\n|Disc Medicine, Inc.|IRON|1|PHASE2|DISC-0974|\n|Spyre Therapeutics, Inc|SYRE|1|PHASE2|SPY001, SPY002, SPY003|\n|Abivax SA Sponsored ADR|ABVX|1|PHASE2|Obefazimod|\n|Palisade Bio, Inc.|PALI|1|PHASE1|PALI-2108|\n...\nLY4395089 is an investigational small molecule being studied for the treatment of Crohn's disease.\nIt is currently in Phase 2 clinical development, where it is being evaluated in combination with mirikizumab and compared against mirikizumab alone in adults with Crohn's disease.\n...\nLY4395089 is being developed by Eli Lilly and Company, a biopharmaceutical company listed on the New York Stock Exchange under the ticker symbol LLY.\n...\nLY4395089 is in Phase 2 clinical development for Crohn's disease.\nA Phase 1 study in healthy participants has been completed.\nThe drug is investigational and has not been approved by regulatory authorities.\n...\nLY4395089 is being studied in a Phase 2 trial, NCT07483099, which is recruiting adults with Crohn's disease across multiple countries including the United States, Belgium, Brazil, Canada, China, Germany, Hungary, Israel, Italy, and Poland.\n...\nYes, LY4395089 is being tested in combination with mirikizumab in a Phase 2 trial for Crohn's disease.\nThe study compares the combination of LY4395089 and mirikizumab against mirikizumab alone in adult participants with the condition.", "date": "2026-09-22", "last_updated": "2026-09-22"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohns' Disease Pipeline Appears Robust With 90+ Key Pharma ...", "url": "https://www.barchart.com/story/news/2494549/crohns-disease-pipeline-appears-robust-with-90-key-pharma-companies-actively-working-in-the-domain-delveinsight", "domain": "barchart.com", "snippet": "Around 540 participants will be enrolled in the study at approximately 300 sites worldwide.\n- ***On June 04, 2026- Eli Lilly and Company* ** announced a phase 2 study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD).\nThis study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks.", "date": "2026-06-16", "last_updated": "2026-07-01"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Study Details | NCT07483099 | ClinicalTrials.gov - ClinicalTrials.gov", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07483099", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "## A Study of LY4395089 and Mirikizumab (LY3074828) Given Together and Mirikizumab (Alone) in Adults With Crohn's Disease\n...\nThe main purpose of this study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD).\nThis study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks.\nOfficial Title\nA Phase 2, Multicenter, Randomized, Open-Label, Active-Controlled Study to Investigate LY4395089/Mirikizumab Co-administration Compared With Mirikizumab in Adults With Moderately to Severely Active Crohn's Disease\n...\nIntervention / Treatment **\n- Drug: LY4395089\n- Drug: Mirikizumab - Intravenous (IV)\n- Drug: Mirikizumab - Subcutaneous (SC)\n...\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: LY4395089 and Mirikizumab Co-Administration Study Period 1: Co-administration of LY4395089 administered orally and mirikizumab administered IV.Study Period 2: Responders will stop LY4395089 and receive mirikizumab SC.|\n...\n|Percentage of Participants with Crohn's Disease who Achieve Endoscopic Response|The percentage of participants who achieve endoscopic response at week 12 of Study Period 1.|Baseline, Up to Week 12|\n...\n|Percentage of Participants who Achieve Clinical Remission| |Baseline, Up to Week 12|\n|Percent Change from Baseline in Fecal Calprotectin| |Baseline, Up to Week 12|", "date": "2026-04-21", "last_updated": "2026-04-21"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohns' Disease Clinical Trial Drug Development Pipeline ...", "url": "https://www.barchart.com/story/news/4798717/crohns-disease-clinical-trial-drug-development-pipeline-expands-with-contributions-from-90-key-companies-delveinsight", "domain": "barchart.com", "snippet": "- ***On September 22, 2026- Eli Lilly and Company* ** initiated a phase 2 study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD).\nThis study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks.", "date": "2026-09-25", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Ontamalimab (Ph 3) - Takeda Pharmaceutical Company Limited", "url": "https://www.biopharmawatch.com/company/TAK/drug/Ontamalimab", "domain": "biopharmawatch.com", "snippet": "Crohn's Disease | Monoclonal antibody | Gastrointestinal |Takeda Pharmaceutical Company Limited|** Last Updated: Jun 21, 2024\n## Target and mechanism\nMolecular targetMADCAM1\nTarget classInhibitor\nModalityMonoclonal antibody\n...\nIndicationAll indications (2)Crohn's DiseaseUlcerative Colitis\n...\nNCT03290781An Efficacy and Safety Study of Ontamalimab as Maintenance Therapy in Participants With Moderate to Severe Ulcerative ColitisUlcerative Colitis\n...\nNCT03283085A Safety Extension Study of Ontamalimab in Participants With Moderate to Severe Ulcerative Colitis or Crohn's Disease (AIDA)Crohn's Disease\nCOMPLETED** 557 ** Analytics\n...\n|Crohn's disease (CD): Ontamalimab 25 mg|EXPERIMENTAL|Participants received 25 mg of ontamalimab solution for injection SC, Q4W, for up to 3 years.|\n...\nTop 11 of 13 competitors\n|Company|Ticker|Trials|Lead Phase|Drugs|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Sanofi SA Sponsored ADR|SNY|6|PHASE3|Duvakitug, SAR441566, balinatunfib, SAR442970|\n|Eli Lilly and Company|LLY|8|PHASE3|Mirikizumab, Tirzepatide, MORF-057, LY4395089|\n|AbbVie, Inc.|ABBV|14|PHASE3|Risankizumab, Upadacitinib, Lutikizumab, Trosunilimab, ABBV-8736|\n...\nOntamalimab is an investigational monoclonal antibody being developed for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\nIt is designed as a maintenance therapy for these inflammatory bowel diseases.\nIt has not been approved by the FDA and remains in clinical development.\n...\nOntamalimab targets MADCAM1, also known as mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule 1.\nIt acts as an inhibitor of this target.\nBy binding to MADCAM1, ontamalimab is thought to interfere with the migration of inflammatory cells into the gastrointestinal tract, which may help reduce inflammation in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\n...\nOntamalimab is in Phase 3 clinical development.\nIt is an investigational drug and has not been approved by the FDA for any indication.\nThe Phase 3 program includes completed trials in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\n...\nOntamalimab has been studied in two completed Phase 3 trials.\nNCT03290781 evaluated ontamalimab as maintenance therapy in 366 participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis.\nNCT03283085, known as the AIDA study, was a safety extension study in 557 participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-21"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Find Lilly Clinical Trials", "url": "https://trials.lilly.com/en-US/find", "domain": "trials.lilly.com", "snippet": "### A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease to test if different investigati...\nENROLLING\nAGE\n18 - 80 years\nConditions\nModerately to severely active ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease\nInvestigational Medicines\nMirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089 or another investigational medicine\nInvestigational Medicines\n...\n### A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease to test if 2 investigational medicines [LY4395089 and ...\nENROLLING\nAGE\n18 - 80 years\nConditions\nModerately to severely active Crohn’s disease\nInvestigational Medicines\nMirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089\nInvestigational Medicines", "date": "2026-09-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for luminal and perianal Crohn's disease management - Luisa Bertin, Martina Crepaldi, Miriana Zanconato, Greta Lorenzon, Daria Maniero, Caterina de Barba, Erica Bonazzi, Sonia Facchin, Marco Scarpa, Cesare Ruffolo, Imerio Angriman, Andrea Buda, Fabiana Zingone, Brigida Barberio, Edoardo Vincenzo Savarino, 2024", "url": "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/17562848241303651", "domain": "journals.sagepub.com", "snippet": "We highlight promising therapies that target known mechanisms, including selective Janus kinase inhibitors, anti-adhesion molecules, tumor necrosis factor inhibitors, and IL-23 selective inhibitors.\n...\nSome of these drugs target well-known pathways, like JAK inhibitors and IL-23 blockers, while others focus on newer areas, such as specific receptors or molecules involved in inflammation.\n...\n^6,7^ To date, the therapeutic arsenal for inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) has included mesalamine, immunosuppressants such as methotrexate and thiopurine, cyclosporin, anti-tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNFα) agents, anti-integrin therapies, anti-IL-12 and IL-23 monoclonal antibodies, and a few small molecules.^8–11^\n...\n|TNF inhibitors|V565|TNFα|Oral|Phase II|Completed|NCT02976129|2019-03-08|✔|✔|✘|✘|✘|✘|\n...\nIL-23, belonging to the IL-12 cytokine family, consists of p40 and p19 subunits, with p19 being exclusive to IL-23.\nIt plays a vital role in regulating and enhancing T-helper 17 cells and activating various innate immune cells, which holds significance in developing chronic inflammatory disorders like CD.\n...\nBrazikumab (MEDI2070) is a human IgG2 monoclonal antibody that binds exclusively to the p19 subunit of IL-23.\n...\nGuselkumab’s efficacy, a monoclonal human IgG1 antibody specifically targeting p19 IL-23, was analyzed in a program consisting in three separate studies: a 48-week phase II dose-ranging study (GALAXI 1) and two 48-week phase III confirmatory studies (GALAXI 2 and GALAXI 3).\n...\nBiologic agents targeting TNFα have become a well-established therapy, after authorization by the European Medicines Agency (EMA) of infliximab, adalimumab, and certolizumab.^27–30^ Thereafter, additional compounds have been investigated.\n...\n^33,34^ Modulating this pathway holds the potential to impede lymphocyte migration into the gut, a critical factor in chronic inflammation, thereby reducing inflammation and minimizing tissue damage.^35^ Oral small molecules that can modulate S1P receptors have been developed, offering advantages over biologic agents such as ease of administration and rapid action.\n...\n^43^ Blocking the activation of this pathway halts the activity of several chemokines involved in mediating inflammation.\nSeveral pan-JAK inhibitors and selective JAK inhibitors, including the newer TYK2 inhibitors, are in various stages of development for IBD.\n...\nNumerous clinical trials are underway to evaluate the safety and efficacy of novel therapies, including molecules like S1PR modulators, selective JAK inhibitors, anti-TL1A antibodies, and PPARγ agonists.", "date": "2024-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohn Disease Clinical Trials (171 Recruiting) | ClinicalTrialsFinder", "url": "https://www.clinicaltrialsfinder.org/conditions/crohn-disease", "domain": "clinicaltrialsfinder.org", "snippet": "Infliximab (Remicade), approved in 1998 after groundbreaking clinical trials, was the first biologic therapy for Crohn disease and revolutionized treatment by demonstrating that targeting the inflammatory protein TNF-alpha could heal the intestinal lining, not just reduce symptoms.\nSince then, trials have delivered additional biologics including adalimumab, vedolizumab, and ustekinumab, as well as the newer oral JAK inhibitor upadacitinib.\n...\nTrials are testing drugs that target new inflammatory pathways, combination strategies pairing biologics with other agents, **diet-based interventions** with scientific rigor, and even **stem cell therapies** for patients with complex fistulizing disease.\n...\n### A Study of LY4395089 and Mirikizumab (LY3074828) Given Together and Mirikizumab (Alone) in Adults With Crohn's Disease\nCrohn Disease\nEli Lilly and Company60 enrolled73 locationsNCT07483099\nRecruiting\nPhase 3", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-21"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "LY-4395089 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/026286e1f04d4c549c14023c3c12d3ed", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Active Indication\nCrohn Disease\nCrohn's disease, active moderate\nCrohn's disease, active severe", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-16"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Ezy Medical Research | Miami Lakes, FL - ClinicalTrials.Veeva", "url": "https://ctv.veeva.com/site/ezy-medical-research-miami-lakes-fl", "domain": "ctv.veeva.com", "snippet": "### A Master Protocol (IIBD): A Study of Multiple Drugs in Adults With Ulcerative Colitis or Crohn's Disease\n...\nEnrolling\nColitis, Ulcerative\nCrohn Disease\nDrug: Mirikizumab - Subcutaneous (SC)\nDrug: LY4395089\n...\n### A Study of LY4395089 and Mirikizumab (LY3074828) Given Together and Mirikizumab (Alone) in Adults With Crohn's Disease\nThe main purpose of this study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikiz...\nEnrolling\nCrohn Disease\nDrug: Mirikizumab - Subcutaneous (SC)\nDrug: LY4395089", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-29"}], "raw": {"id": "a4793e39-68df-47e2-b49e-da656d0f95b2"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:30+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q009", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as CHRM1 CHRM3 CHRM4 CHRM5 Small Molecule", "anchors": ["Dicyclomine"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q009-2", "query": "Dicyclomine IBD CHRM pipeline", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Dicyclomine IBD CHRM pipeline", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 11398.4, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "DICYCLOMINE HYDROCHLORIDE", "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=2131a563-f739-4aee-a3ea-cfdc399799ab", "domain": "dailymed.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Dicyclomine hydrochloride is indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nCaution should be taken in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nLarge doses may suppress intestinal motility to the point of producing a paralytic ileus and the use of this drug may precipitate or aggravate the serious complication of toxic megacolon [see Adverse Reactions (6.3)].\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is contraindicated in patients with severe ulcerative colitis [see Contraindications (4)].\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent available in the following dosage forms:\n- Dicyclomine hydrochloride capsules for oral use contain 10 mg dicyclomine hydrochloride USP.\n...\n- Dicyclomine hydrochloride tablets for oral use contain 20 mg dicyclomine hydrochloride USP.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "dicyclomine hydrochloride tablet - DailyMed - NIH", "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=5a553bda-1e3d-4b80-913f-73dd54655033", "domain": "dailymed.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Dicyclomine hydrochloride tablets are an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent indicated for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome (1)\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride tablets are indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nThe recommended initial dose is 20 mg four times a day.\nAfter one week treatment with the initial dose, the dose may be increased to 40 mg four times a day unless side effects limit dosage escalation.\n...\nCaution should be taken in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nLarge doses may suppress intestinal motility to the point of producing a paralytic ileus and the use of this drug may precipitate or aggravate the serious complication of toxic megacolon [see Adverse Reactions (6.3)].\n...\nDicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract.\n...\n|2|0143-1227-10|", "date": "2021-11-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "highlights of prescribing information", "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=bb099db9-f635-4a64-8892-6236971dec45", "domain": "dailymed.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Dicyclomine hydrochloride is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent indicated for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome (1)\n...\nCaution should be taken in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nLarge doses may suppress intestinal motility to the point of producing a paralytic ileus and the use of this drug may precipitate or aggravate the serious complication of toxic megacolon [see Adverse Reactions (6.3)].\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is contraindicated in patients with severe ulcerative colitis [see Contraindications (4)].\n...\nDicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-21"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "highlights of prescribing information - DailyMed", "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=88942e82-b798-4122-9ea2-0932ef09357f&type=display", "domain": "dailymed.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Dicyclomine hydrochloride is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent indicated for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome (1)\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nThe recommended initial dose is 20 mg four times a day.\nAfter one week treatment with the initial dose, the dose may be increased to 40 mg four times a day unless side effects limit dosage escalation.\n...\nDicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract.\n...\nDicyclomine Hydrochloride Capsules, USP\n10 mg\nNDC 53808-1055-2", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "DICYCLOMINE - Inxight Drugs", "url": "https://drugs.ncats.io/drug/4KV4X8IF6V", "domain": "drugs.ncats.io", "snippet": "Dicyclomine is an anticholinergic tertiary amine used frequently by oral and parenteral route as an effective anti-spasmodic agent.\nDicyclomine hydrochloride salt is approved under brand name bentyl for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nIt was shown, that Dicyclomine is a selective M1 and M3 muscarinic receptors antagonist, but os shown pharmacological activity via the M1 receptor.\n...\n|Irritable bowel syndrome 61 Sources:&nbsphttps://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2007/007409s040,007961s026,008370s031lbl.pdf|Palliative| |Approved 8583|BENTYL\n#### Approved Use Dicyclomine hydrochloride oral solution is indicated for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\n|202.67 ng × h/mL OTHER GOV https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/anda/99/40230_Dicyclomine%20Hydrochloride_Bioeqr.pdf|20 mg single, oral dose: 20 mg route of administration: Oral experiment type: SINGLE co-administered:| |DICYCLOMINE plasma|Homo sapiens population: HEALTHY age: ADULT sex: MALE food status: FASTED|\n...\nAdults-Oral.\nThe only oral dose clearly shown to be effective is 160 mg per day (in 4 equally divided doses).\nSince this dose is associated with a significant incidence of side effects, it is prudent to begin with 80 mg per day (in 4 equally divided doses).\nDepending upon the patient’s response during the first week of therapy, the dose should be increased to 160 mg per day unless side effects limit dosage escalation.\nIf efficacy is not achieved within 2 weeks or side effects require doses below 80 mg per day, the drug should be discontinued.\nDocumented safety data are not available for doses above 80 mg daily for periods longer than 2 weeks.\nAdults-Intramuscular Injection.\nNOT FOR INTRAVENOUS USE.\nThe intramuscular dosage form is to be used temporarily when the patient cannot take oral medication.\nIntramuscular injection is about twice as bioavailable as oral dosage forms; consequently, the recommended intramuscular dose is 80 mg daily (in 4 equally divided doses).\nOral dicyclomine hydrochloride should be started as soon as possible and the intramuscular form should not be used for periods longer than 1 or 2 days.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-29"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Dicyclomine | Medication Guide", "url": "https://www.zmg.us/medications/dicyclomine", "domain": "zmg.us", "snippet": "Dicyclomine is FDA-approved for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nDicyclomine is contraindicated in infants younger than 6 months due to reports of serious respiratory symptoms, seizures, and death in this population.\n...\nOur article on IBS vs IBD: understanding the difference explains why establishing which of the two a patient has comes before any of this, since inflammatory bowel disease requires a different approach entirely and is one of the settings in which antispasmodics are avoided.", "date": "2025-11-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "dicyclomine hydrochloride", "url": "https://www.glowm.com/resources/glowm/cd/pages/drugs/d034.html", "domain": "glowm.com", "snippet": "** *Adults: * Initially, 20 mg P.O. q.i.d.; then increase to 40 mg P.O. q.i.d. during first week of therapy unless precluded by adverse reactions.\nOr, 20 mg I.M. q 4 to 6 hours.\n...\nPharmacodynamics **Antispasmodic action:** Dicyclomine exerts a nonspecific, direct spasmolytic action on smooth muscle.\nIt also has some local anesthetic properties that may contribute to spasmolysis in the GI and biliary tracts.\n**Antimuscarinic action: ** Drug blocks parasympathetic nerve impulses at the acetylcholine-receptor site, relieving smooth-muscle contraction, thereby slowing bowel action and decreasing production of stomach acid.\n...\nContraindications and precautions Contraindicated in patients with obstructive uropathy, obstructive disease of the GI tract, reflux esophagitis, severe ulcerative colitis, myasthenia gravis, hypersensitivity to anticholinergics, unstable CV status in acute hemorrhage, or glaucoma.\nAlso contraindicated in breast-feeding women and in children younger than age 6 months.\nUse cautiously in patients with autonomic neuropathy, hyperthyroidism, coronary artery disease, arrhythmias, heart failure, hypertension, hiatal hernia, hepatic or renal disease, prostatic hyperplasia, and ulcerative colitis.\n...\nAdverse reactions **CNS: ** *headache; dizziness;* insomnia; fever; light-headedness; drowsiness; nervousness, confusion, and excitement in elderly patients.\n**CV: ** *palpitations,* tachycardia.\n**EENT: ** blurred vision, increased intraocular pressure, mydriasis.\n**GI: ** nausea, vomiting, *constipation, dry mouth, * abdominal distention, heartburn, paralytic ileus.\n**GU: ** *urinary hesitancy, urine retention,* impotence.\n**Skin: ** urticaria, decreased sweating or possible anhidrosis, other dermal manifestations, local irritation.\n**Other: ** allergic reactions, dependence.\n...\nOverdose and treatment Signs and symptoms of overdose include curare-like CNS stimulation followed by depression, and such psychotic symptoms as disorientation, confusion, hallucinations, delusions, anxiety, agitation, and restlessness.\nPeripheral effects may include dilated, nonreactive pupils; hot, flushed, dry skin; tachycardia; hypertension; and increased respiration.\nTreatment is primarily symptomatic and supportive, as needed.\nMaintain patent airway.\nIf patient is alert, induce emesis (or use gastric lavage) and follow with a saline cathartic and activated charcoal to prevent further drug absorption.\nIn severe cases, physostigmine may be administered to block the antimuscarinic effects of dicyclomine.\nGive fluids, as needed, to treat shock; diazepam to control psychotic symptoms; and pilocarpine (instilled into the eyes) to relieve mydriasis.\n...\nSpecial considerations ALERT High environmental temperatures may induce heatstroke during drug use.\nIf symptoms occur, discontinue drug.\n Never give dicyclomine I.V. or S.C.\n*** Breast-feeding patients * **  Dicyclomine may appear in breast milk; it also may decrease milk production.\nAvoid use in breast-feeding women.\n*** Pediatric patients * **  Safety and effectiveness in children haven’t been established.\nAdminister cautiously to infants age 6 months and older; seizures have been reported.\nContraindicated in infants younger than age 6 months.\n*** Geriatric patients * **  Administer drug cautiously and in reduced doses.\n...\n Warn patient that high environmental temperatures may induce heatstroke during therapy; tell patient to avoid such temperatures.\n Advise patient to avoid driving and other hazardous activities if drowsiness or blurred vision occurs.\n Tell patient to report rash or other skin eruption.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-21"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Disclaimer:  The Rapid Response Service is an information service for those involved in planning and providing health care in", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK350056/pdf/Bookshelf_NBK350056.pdf", "domain": "ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Dicyclomine, also known as dicycloverine, is used to treat hypermotility in gastrointestinal (GI) conditions that involve smooth muscle spasm such as irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) which, according to US data, may affect 10-15% of the population1,2 and ulcerative colitis.3 Dicyclomine\n...\nRecent evidence on the use of dicyclomine for GI tract conditions involving smooth muscle spasm is lacking.\n...\nThere was no evidence identified on the clinical effectiveness and safety of dicyclomine for gastrointestinal tract conditions involving smooth muscle spasm.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-03-25"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacology", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00804", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "snippet": "## Overview\n...\n- A medication used to treat disorders affecting the large intestines.\n...\n- Clinical Trials\n- Phase 0\n- 0\n- Phase 1\n- 0\n- Phase 2\n- 0\n- Phase 3\n- 0\n- Phase 4\n- 0\n...\n**Dicyclomine**is an antimuscarinic agent used to treat IBS.\n...\nDicyclomine is a muscarinic M1, M3, and M2 receptor antagonist as well as a non-competitive inhibitor of histamine and bradykinin used to treat spasms of the intestines seen in functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nDicyclomine is indicated for the treatment of functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nDicyclomine is an anticholinergic drug used to relax the smooth muscles of the intestines.\n...\nDicyclomine achieves its action partially through direct antimuscarinic activity of the M1, M3, and M2 receptors; and partially through antagonism of bradykinin and histamine.\nDicyclomine non-competitively inhibits the action of bradykinin and histamine, resulting in direct action on the smooth muscle, and decreased strength of contractions seen in spasms of the ileum.", "date": "2025-04-30", "last_updated": "2025-08-29"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "NCATS Inxight Drugs — DICYCLOMINE HYDROCHLORIDE", "url": "https://drugs.ncats.io/substance/CQ903KQA31", "domain": "drugs.ncats.io", "snippet": "Dicyclomine is an anticholinergic tertiary amine used frequently by oral and parenteral route as an effective anti-spasmodic agent.\nDicyclomine hydrochloride salt is approved under brand name bentyl for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\n|Palliative|BENTYL ## Approved Use Dicyclomine hydrochloride oral solution is indicated for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n## Launch Date 1984|\n...\n|1.8 h|20 mg single, oral dose: 20 mg route of administration: Oral experiment type: SINGLE co-administered:|DICYCLOMINE plasma|Homo sapiens population: UNKNOWN age: UNKNOWN sex: UNKNOWN food status: UNKNOWN|", "date": "2011-07-14", "last_updated": "2025-08-24"}], "raw": {"id": "ce96e252-3963-41d9-b536-f26241a58050"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:33+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q008", "objective": "Clostridioides Difficile Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as LMN-201, including Antibody Fragment Enzyme Biological Biologic Cocktail and Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB therapies", "anchors": ["LMN-201"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q008-3", "query": "LMN-201 BL5-6.2 clinical development", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "LMN-201 BL5-6.2 clinical development", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 15360.8, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Investigational Oral Biologic Achieves 100% C difficile ...", "url": "https://www.lumen.bio/news/2025/04/03/investigational-oral-biologic-achieves-100-c-difficile-clinical-cure-in-preliminary-cohort", "domain": "lumen.bio", "snippet": "Today, Lumen Bioscience reported that its investigational oral biologic, LMN-201, achieved a 100% clinical cure rate of C difficile infection (CDI) in the sentinel cohort (Part A) of its RePreve clinical trial.\nAll patients (21/21; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 85%-100%) achieved initial clinical resolution, significantly outperforming (p << 0.001) the 80% initial clinical cure rate (625/781; 95% CI: 77%-83%) observed in the active arm of the large MODIFY I and II studies.1\n...\nOnly 1 of 21 patients (4.76%; 95% CI: 0.24%-23%) who completed 7 days of standard-of-care (SoC) plus LMN-201 experienced CDI recurrence within 28 days, compared to 161 of 621 patients (26%; 95% CI: 23%-30%) who experienced recurrence after SoC alone in MODIFY I/II, and 87 of 625 patients (14%; 95% CI: 11%-17%) after SoC plus bezlotoxumab treatment in MODIFY I/II within the same time period.\nOf the 21 subjects in the RePreve Trial sentinel cohort who completed seven days of LMN-201, 16 received vancomycin, 4 fidaxomicin, and 1 metronidazole.1\nLMN-201 is delivered in capsules and is intended for use with and following antibiotics to improve clinical outcomes for C difficile infection.\nIt is made with Lumen’s proprietary spirulina-based GMP manufacturing system.\n...\nThese sentinel cohort results were achieved with only seven days of dosing.\nIn the RePreve Trial’s main cohort (Part B), the LMN-201 dosing window will increase from seven days to approximately 70 days (until eight weeks after initial clinical cure).\nThis extended treatment duration aims to provide protection throughout the period of greatest risk for CDI recurrence: the weeks immediately after antibiotic use when the healthy commensal microbiome naturally re-engrafts.1\nWith the sentinel cohort now completed, Lumen has begun Part B (the main cohort).\nThis pivotal phase is a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study that will enroll approximately 350 patients at research centers across the U.S. Participants will be randomized 1:1 to receive either LMN-201 or a placebo—each alongside standard antibiotic therapy—to rigorously evaluate the drug’s efficacy in a larger population.\nThe primary endpoint is “sustained clinical cure” (initial clinical cure plus no recurrence at 12 weeks) relative to placebo.1\n*The RePreve Trial main cohort is now recruiting.For investigators and potential participants interested in participating, they can email trials@lumen.bio or visit the official RePreve study webpage.*", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Meds Pipeline Monitor 2026", "url": "https://www.canada.ca/en/patented-medicine-prices-review/services/npduis/analytical-studies/meds-pipeline-monitor-2026.html", "domain": "canada.ca", "snippet": "|**LMN-201** Lumen Bioscience Inc.- Increased safety and efficacy - Novel mechanism - Fast Track|*C. difficile* infection|- It is a biologic cocktail of therapeutic proteins that neutralizes the *C. difficile* bacterium.^Footnote 146^ - Administered orally.\n- *C. difficile* infection is an antimicrobial resistance “Urgent Threat” according to the U.S. CDC, with growing resistance to the main antibiotic used in CDI treatment.^Footnote 147^ **Clinical Trials**- Part A of RePreve trial is complete.\nTwenty-one patients with *C. difficile* infection were enrolled.\nAll participants in this open-label cohort received LMN-201 in addition to antibiotics, starting within 7 days of diagnosis.\nAll patients (21/21) achieved initial clinical resolution.^Footnote 148^ - Phase III arm of RePreve trial has begun.^Footnote 149^ - There is one drug with this indication in Phase II.\nIt does not have the same mechanism of action as LMN-201.|\n...\nA Phase 2, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled Study of LMN-201 for Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence.\nClinicalTrials.gov: NCT05330182 (Recruiting).\nhttps://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05330182 Return to footnote 149 referrer", "date": "2026-04-27", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/b8442753a9f122a5435bfcee84970177", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "A Phase 2, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled Study of LMN-201 for Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence\n...\nLumen Bioscience's LMN-201 Achieves 100% Initial C. difficile Clinical Cure in Preliminary Cohort of RePreve Trial\nClinical ResultPhase 3", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-15"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience's LMN-201 Achieves 100% Initial C. difficile Clinical Cure in Preliminary Cohort of RePreve Trial", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/lumen-biosciences-lmn-201-achieves-100-initial-c-difficile-clinical-cure-in-preliminary-cohort-of-repreve-trial-302419130.html", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "snippet": "SEATTLE, April 3, 2025 /PRNewswire/ -- In a landmark achievement for its pioneering spirulina-based drug development platform, Lumen announced today top-line results from the sentinel cohort of its RePreve Clinical Trial evaluating LMN-201 for Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI).\nLMN-201 is an oral biologic cocktail, delivered in capsules, intended for use with and following antibiotics to improve clinical outcomes for C. difficile infection.\nIt is made with Lumen's proprietary spirulina-based GMP manufacturing system.\n...\n100% Initial Clinical Cure: 21 patients completed 7 days of LMN-201 plus standard of care (SoC) antibiotics (metronidazole, vancomycin, or fidaxomicin).\nAll patients (21/21; 95% confidence interval (CI) 85%-100%) achieved initial clinical resolution, significantly outperforming (p<<0.001) the 80% initial clinical cure rate (625/781; 95% CI 77-83%) observed in the active arm of the large MODIFY I and II studies.\nThe RePreve Trial was designed to facilitate comparisons to the MODIFY trial, which targeted the same patient population with the same SoC antibiotics combined with the IV-infused monoclonal antibody, bezlotoxumab.\n...\nSustained cure rates through Week 4 for SoC alone (MODIFY I/II), SoC plus LMN-201 (RePreve sentinel cohort) and SoC plus bezlotoxumab (MODIFY I/II) were 59.5% (460/773; 95% CI 56-63%), 95.2% (20/21; 95% CI 77-100%) and 68.9% (538/781; 95% CI 66-72%), respectively (see enclosed figure).\nThe improvement in sustained cure through Week 4 between LMN-201 and SoC was 35.7% (p<<0.001) and between LMN-201 and bezlotoxumab was 26.3% (p<0.001).\nMarked CDI Recurrence Reduction: Only 1 of 21 patients (4.76%; 95% CI 0.24-23%) completing seven days of SoC plus LMN-201 experienced CDI recurrence within 28 days, versus 161 of 621 patients (26%; 95% CI 23-30%) recurrence observed after SoC alone in MODIFY I/II and 87 of 625 (14%; 95% CI 11-17%) after SoC plus bezlotoxumab treatment in MODIFY I/II in the same time period.\n...\nLMN-201 Appears to be Safe and Well-Tolerated: No dose-related severe or serious adverse events were reported.\nNotably, these sentinel cohort results were achieved with only 7 days of dosing.\nIn the RePreve Trial's main cohort (Part B), the LMN-201 dosing window will increase from 7 days to ~70 days (until 8 weeks after initial clinical cure).\nThis will offer protection throughout the period of greatest risk of CDI recurrence: the weeks immediately after antibiotics treatment when the healthy commensal microbiome naturally re-engrafts.\n...\nWe can now proceed to the placebo-controlled phase with a solid foundation of safety and an encouraging preliminary efficacy signal.\nWe look forward to confirming these results in the larger trial, which is recruiting now.\"\n...\nThe RePreve Trial has a two-part design.\nEnrollment criteria are the same in both cohorts, and, to facilitate comparison, they are nearly identical to those used in the MODIFY trial for bezlotoxumab.\nPart A (Sentinel Cohort) was designed to confirm LMN-201's safety in high-risk individuals with CDI, and to allow Lumen to optimize the trial infrastructure and patient recruiting.\nTwenty-one CDI patients on standard antibiotic therapy were enrolled.\nAll participants in this open-label cohort received LMN-201 in addition to antibiotics, starting within 7 days of diagnosis.\nWith the sentinel cohort successfully completed, Lumen has begun Part B (main cohort).\nThis pivotal portion is a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study that will enroll approximately 350 patients at research centers across the U.S. Participants are randomized 1:1 to receive either LMN-201 or placebo—in each case alongside standard antibiotic therapy—to rigorously evaluate the drug's efficacy in a larger population.\nThe primary endpoint is \"sustained clinical cure\" (initial clinical cure plus no recurrence at 12 weeks) relative to placebo.\n...\nLMN-201 is orally delivered (capsules; no enema or \"bowel cleanse\" required) and highly scalable, allowing for much broader potential use in routine CDI management.\nLumen previously released a preprint with LMN-201's pre-clinical data.\nA manuscript for peer-reviewed publication is in preparation, to include results of this sentinel cohort (NCT05330182) and an earlier trial assessing LMN-201's safety, tolerability, and gastrointestinal pharmacokinetics (NCT04893239).", "date": "2025-04-03", "last_updated": "2025-04-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience News and Press Releases | PR Newswire", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news/lumen-bioscience/", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "snippet": "### Apr 03, 2025, 07:00 ET Lumen Bioscience's LMN-201 Achieves 100% Initial C. difficile Clinical Cure in Preliminary Cohort of RePreve Trial\nIn a landmark achievement for its pioneering spirulina-based drug development platform, Lumen announced today top-line results from the sentinel...\n### Oct 17, 2023, 08:00 ET Lumen Bioscience awarded $16.2 million in DoD funding to advance LMN-201 through late-stage trials\n...\n### May 17, 2023, 08:00 ET Lumen Bioscience Receives Fast Track Designation from U.S. FDA for LMN-201\n...\n### Apr 18, 2022, 07:00 ET Lumen Bioscience Announces Clinical Advancement of LMN-201 for C. difficile Infection\nLumen Bioscience, a clinical-stage biotechnology company developing biologic drugs for highly prevalent diseases, today announced clinical...", "date": "2025-10-08", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "The latest on Lumen Biosience's experimental c. diff biologic", "url": "https://www.drugdiscoverytrends.com/how-an-engineered-algae-based-biologic-aims-to-break-the-annual-5b-cycle-of-c-diff-reinfection/", "domain": "drugdiscoverytrends.com", "snippet": "One potential contender to help break this cycle of recurrent infections is Lumen Bioscience’s LMN-201, an oral biologic designed to bind and neutralize C. diff toxins while degrading the pathogen’s cell wall.\nIn a Phase 2/3 randomized trial with an estimated 375 participants, LMN-201 is administered alongside standard-of-care antibiotics such as fidaxomicin or vancomycin, aiming to prevent recurrence by specifically targeting C. diff and sparing the broader microbiome.\n...\nEngineered within spirulina cells, LMN-201’s enzyme-antibody cocktail neutralizes C. diff toxins directly in the gut, potentially breaking the cycle of recurrence while sparing the broader microbiome.\n...\nAs LMN-201, which has Fast Track status, moves through clinical development, Lumen Bioscience is advancing a dual-mechanism approach by pairing it with antibiotics.\n...\nOne of the therapeutic proteins in LMN-201 is a phage-derived endolysin, which enzymatically degrades the peptidoglycan in the bacterial cell wall, leading to the destruction of* C. difficile* cells.\n...\nHe added that secondary endpoints in the trial may reveal whether LMN-201 can reduce the necessary dose or duration of antibiotic therapy.", "date": "2025-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-03"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "LMN-201 - MedPath", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/drug/b7926ed2b4ecf585/lmn-201", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "## Clinical Trials\n|Title|Posted|Study ID|Phase|Status|Sponsor|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|LMN-201 for Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence|2022/04/15|NCT05330182|Phase 2|Recruiting|Lumen Bioscience, Inc.|\n...\n|No FDA approvals found for this drug.|\n...\n|No EMA approvals found for this drug.|", "date": "2025-04-03", "last_updated": "2025-09-17"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience pursues oral biologic to prevent recurrent C. diff", "url": "https://www.drugdiscoverytrends.com/lumen-bioscience-oral-biologic-prevents-recurrent-c-diff/", "domain": "drugdiscoverytrends.com", "snippet": "Lumen Bioscience (Seattle) is pursuing a novel approach to prevent recurrent CDI by developing an oral biologic drug, LMN-201.\n...\n## Late-stage testing for LMN-201 to start in 2023\nThe biologic combines antibody and enzyme proteins designed to neutralize both C. difficile bacteria and toxins directly in the gastrointestinal tract.\nIn October, Lumen won $16.2 million in Department of Defense funding to advance LMN-201 through late-stage trials starting in late 2023.\nThe Phase 2/3 REPREVE trial will enroll roughly 375 patients across the U.S. to test LMN-201’s ability to both improve treatment outcomes and prevent recurrence in recently diagnosed CDI patients.\nLMN-201 combines multiple therapeutic proteins that work together to neutralize both C. difficile bacteria and the toxins it produces directly in the gastrointestinal tract.\n...\nLMN-201 won FDA Fast Track designation earlier in 2023.", "date": "2024-01-19", "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience Announces Clinical Advancement of LMN-201 ...", "url": "https://www.lumen.bio/news/2022/04/15/lumen-bioscience-announces-clinical-advancement-of-lmn-201-for-c-difficile-infection", "domain": "lumen.bio", "snippet": "FDA clearance of investigational new drug (IND) application.\nInitiation of multisite Phase 2 study in spring 2022.\nPhase 1 pharmacokinetic study completed\nSeattle, WA, April 18, 2022 – Lumen Bioscience, a clinical-stage biotechnology company developing biologic drugs for highly prevalent diseases, today announced clinical development progress for LMN-201, an investigational orally delivered cocktail to treat and prevent C. difficile infection (CDI).\nAchievements include FDA clearance of a planned Phase 2 study of LMN-201 and successful completion of a Phase 1 first-in-human study, which validated drug delivery of enteric capsules into the gut.\nLMN-201 combines four therapeutic proteins—manufactured and orally delivered in the edible microorganism spirulina—that work synergistically to neutralize both the C. difficile bacterium and the toxin that causes its virulence.\nLMN-201 is the world’s first complex biologic cocktail to enter human clinical trials and represents a significant advance in the field of polypharmacology.\nLumen recently published its pre-clinical data on LMN-201 on bioRxiv, pending peer review.\n...\nThe Phase 2 trial (NCT05330182) will begin enrolling approximately 375 patients at sites across the United States.\nPhysicians interested in participating are encouraged to contact Lumen’s clinical operations team at trials@lumen.bio.\nThe double-blind, placebo-controlled study will evaluate the efficacy of LMN-201 in participants recently diagnosed with CDI receiving standard-of-care antibiotics.\nThe primary goal of the Phase 1 study of LMN-201 recently completed in Australia was to confirm the dissolution kinetics of enteric capsules designed to release LMN-201’s therapeutic proteins at or before the terminal ileum, where C. difficile becomes established in most patients.\nThe trial met its primary endpoint, indicating the capsules can successfully deliver LMN-201 where needed.\nInitial analysis also confirmed the therapeutic proteins were bioactive upon release and demonstrated robust stability against gastrointestinal protease digestion over time, meeting the exploratory endpoints.\n...\nLMN-201 is a cocktail of two classes of therapeutic proteins: the world’s first complex biologic cocktail drug.\n...\nRather than treating CDI, which in most patients is easily cleared with generic antibiotics, therefore, LMN-201’s primary purpose is to be delivered alongside and for a short period after antibiotics to prevent future bouts with CDI, a common side effect of antibiotics.\nLumen recently published its pre-clinical data on LMN-201 on bioRxiv, pending peer review.", "date": "2022-04-15", "last_updated": "2025-10-10"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience Details 2022 Company and Facilities ...", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/lumen-bioscience-details-2022-company-and-facilities-growth-new-funding-and-clinical-pipeline-progress-301714234.html", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "snippet": "**Commercial Pipeline — ** An accelerating pipeline of clinical stage programs targeting prevalent, unmet needs backed by blockbuster commercial opportunities.\n- **LMN-201 for prevention of *C. difficile* infection** – LMN-201 is the world's first rationally engineered, complex biologic cocktail to enter human clinical trials.\nThe investigational product is orally delivered and designed to prevent *C. difficile* infection, a >$5 billion cash drain on the U.S. healthcare system.\n2022 progress includes:\n- - FDA cleared IND application supporting a Phase 2/3 multi-center, placebo-controlled trial to evaluate the efficacy of LMN-201 in preventing *C. difficile* recurrence (NCT05330182).\n- Completed Phase 1 pharmacokinetics trial (NCT04893239) demonstrating successful ileal delivery of LMN-201 and stability against human gastrointestinal protease digestion.\n- Published LMN-201 pre-clinical data manuscript (journal submission process underway).\n- Completed preliminary commercial planning and market analysis.", "date": "2023-01-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "0d1dabbb-688f-4543-a588-b74d06b5589c"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:34+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q011", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as Small Molecule targeting ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7", "anchors": ["α4β7 Inhibitor", "α4β7"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q011-3", "query": "α4β7 Inhibitor abrilumab current status IBD", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "α4β7 Inhibitor abrilumab current status IBD", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 3841.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Our Science", "url": "https://www.aviarapharma.com/ourscience", "domain": "aviarapharma.com", "snippet": "Vedolizumab and natalizumab, the leading biological drugs (antibodies) that are used to treat IBD, act principally on the a4- integrins; the former targets the integrin α4β7, whereas the latter targets both α4β1 and α4β7 integrins.\n...\nNevertheless, both α4β1 and α4β7 integrins are validated, high-value targets and continue to be investigated clinically with the risk of PML in mind.\nA newer α4β7-specific antibody, abrilumab (AMG181) has been shown to induce remission and mucosal healing in patients with moderate to severe UC without any incidence of PML.\nTwo orally delivered, selective antagonists of α4β7 are in clinical trials: MORF-057, a small molecule from Morphic Therapeutics has completed a phase I trial; however, it does not inhibit integrin α4β1 and may have the same non-responder, and secondary loss of response issues as vedolizumab.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-15"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Internal Medicine Vol.65", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/6495/b5ef74c5b2f46901ddb86ce97a93ccddb0b8.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "Currently, antibody preparations targeting α4β7 integrin (vedolizumab) and an oral α4 integrin inhibi- tor (carotegrast methyl) are available in Japan (Figure).\n...\nAbrilumab (AMG 181)\nAbrilumab is a fully humanized IgG2 mAb that targets α\n4β7 and functions through a mechanism of action similar to\nthat of vedolizumab (10).\nA phase II study evaluated in pa- tients with moderate-to-severe UC refractory to anti-TNF-α and immunomodulatory therapies.\nThe participants received subcutaneous administration of abrilumab at doses of 7, 21, or 70 mg on day 1, week 2, and week 4, followed by ran- domization every 4 weeks to receive either abrilumab (210\nmg) on day 1 or placebo.\nThe clinical remission rates at\nweek 8 were 98 (13.3%), 79 (12.7%), and 116 (4.3%) (p�\n0.05) for abrilumab 70 mg, 210 mg, and placebo, respec-\ntively.\nSerious adverse events were not observed.\nThe most commonly observed adverse events in both groups included injection site reactions, nasopharyngitis, headaches, and ar- thralgia (11).\nAnother phase II study assessed abrilumab in patients with CD, randomizing participants to receive pla- cebo or subcutaneous abrilumab at doses of 21 mg or 70 mg on day 1, week 2, and week 4, every 4 weeks for 24 weeks, or a single 210 mg dose on day 1.\nHowever, the study did not meet its primary endpoint and showed no significant dif- ference in clinical remission rates compared with the pla- cebo group (12).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-15"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov · articles · PMC13453429Integrin α4β7: Pathogenic Roles in Metabolic and Inflammatory...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13453429/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "^23^ Current research on integrin α4β7 primarily focuses on its association with IBD.\nIt is considered a validated therapeutic target for IBD treatment, with numerous reports on the use of α4β7 antagonists, such as vedolizumab.^24^ However, there is currently a lack of systematic summaries of its role in other inflammatory and metabolic diseases, as well as elucidations of the mechanisms by which α4β7 contributes to these diseases, including IBD.\n...\n^86^ Anti-α4β7 integrin therapy can alleviate colitis symptoms by reducing intestinal inflammation and improving the function of the intestinal epithelial barrier.\n...\nSeveral monoclonal and small-molecule antibodies targeting α4β7 have been developed.\nSome have been approved for clinical use, while others are still under investigation, primarily for the treatment of IBD, as well as for other diseases such as HIV and relapsing multiple sclerosis (RMS).\nBelow is a summary of agents that are currently approved or in clinical trials (Table 1).\n...\n|Vedolizumab|Intravenous|Monoclonal Antibody|α4β7 integrin|UC, CD|Approved|\n...\n|Vedolizumab|Intravenous|Monoclonal Antibody|α4β7 integrin|Chronic Pouchitis|Phase IV|\n|Abrilumab (AMG 181 or MEDI 7183)|Subcutaneous|Monoclonal Antibody|α4β7 integrin|CD|Phase IIb|\n|Abrilumab (AMG 181 or MEDI 7183)|Subcutaneous|Monoclonal Antibody|α4β7 integrin|UC|Phase IIb|\n...\nVedolizumab, a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody, primarily functions to selectively antagonize the α4β7 integrin by specifically blocking its interaction with MAdCAM-1.^167^ It was approved by the FDA in 2014 for the treatment of moderate-to-severe UC and CD in patients who are refractory or intolerant to TNF-α inhibitors,^167^^,^^168^ it is used for both induction and maintenance therapy.\n...\nAbrilumab (also known as AMG 181 or MEDI7183) is a fully human IgG2κ monoclonal antibody that selectively blocks α4β7-MAdCAM-1 interactions to inhibit lymphocyte gut homing without binding to α4β1 or αEβ7 integrins, thereby preserving systemic immune surveillance.\n^176^ Administered via subcutaneous injection, Abrilumab was evaluated in a pivotal Phase IIb UC trial (NCT01694485) involving 354 patients using regimens including 7 mg, 21 mg, 70 mg every 4 weeks, and a single 210 mg dose followed by placebo.\nAt Week 8, the 70 mg and 210 mg doses demonstrated significant efficacy, achieving clinical remission rates of 13.5% and 13.4%, respectively, compared to 4.4% for placebo.^177^ A separate Japanese Phase II study similarly showed numerically higher remission rates with these doses and confirmed a favorable safety profile with no PML cases.\n^178^ However, in CD, a Phase II study (NCT01696396) missed its primary endpoint of clinical remission at Week 8, with the 21 mg and 210 mg doses failing to show statistical superiority over placebo, and inconsistent results ultimately limited its further development.^179^ Although abrilumab demonstrated a favorable safety, immunogenicity, pharmacokinetic, and efficacy profile suitable for further UC testing, its development was discontinued in 2016.\n...\n^175^ While effective for moderately to severely active CD, earning FDA approval in 2008, concerns about PML restrict its use to second- or third-line therapy in patients with inadequate response to conventional or anti-TNF agents, and it should not be combined with other immunosuppressants.^180^ A Phase II trial in active UC demonstrated modest efficacy, including significant improvements in response and remission rates at Week 6, but safety concerns and competition from gut-selective agents like vedolizumab halted further UC-specific development.\n...\nThe development of α4β7-targeted therapies has yielded clinical success, most notably with vedolizumab in IBD, and continues to evolve with novel agents such as oral small molecules and peptide antagonists.\nHowever, challenges remain, including variability in therapeutic response, the need to preserve beneficial regulatory T cell populations, and the emergence of alternative homing pathways that may limit efficacy.", "date": "2026-08-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies for Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Advances in Therapy", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12325-018-0795-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=93e08bcb-943e-4265-9cfd-c0a51a461b0d", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "Other agents are currently being developed at various stages of clinical trials including anti-adhesion agents such as etrolizumab and abrilumab, JAK inhibitors such as tofacitinib, and anti-trafficking molecules.\n...\nAMG 181 (abrilumab) is an IgG2 human monoclonal antibody that targets the α4β7 heterodimer, hence blocking the interaction with the MAdCAM-1 receptor.\nThe first human study was conducted in 68 healthy subjects and in 4 subjects with active UC (3 active drug, 1 placebo), investigating its pharmacokinetic properties [11].\nThe UC patients treated with the drug achieved remission with mucosal healing at different time points.\nNo treatment-related serious adverse events were observed.\nAfter several other pharmacokinetic (PK) and pharmacodynamic (PD) studies were done [12], AMG 181 was studied in IBD patients in phase 2b clinical trials with preliminary data reported to date in the form of abstracts.\nIn CD, patients were recruited to receive subcutaneous placebo, 21 mg, 70 mg, and 210 mg every 4 weeks.\nDespite the absence of statistical significance, higher rates of remission and response were observed in the active treatment arms at week 12, particularly in patients with prior failure of TNF antagonists assigned to the 210 mg abrilumab group (24.8% and 37.4%, respectively, for the treatment group and 8.2% and 14.2% in the placebo group) [13].\nThe drug was similarly studied in 354 patients with moderate-to-severe UC [14].\nCombined remission rates (the primary endpoint for the study) were 1.6%, 2.9%, 13.5%, and 13.4% in the 7, 21, 70, and 210 mg abrilumab arm, respectively, versus 4.4% in the placebo arm.\n...\nInterestingly, efficacy did not appear to correlate with peripheral target coverage or changes in α4β7-high T cells.\nFor both studies, adverse events were balanced among groups through week 24, with no cases of PML or mortalities reported.", "date": "2018-10-29", "last_updated": "2026-09-02"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 4.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12649877/table/biomedicines-13-02659-t004/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|Abrilumab|Human IgG2 anti-α4β7 integrin dimer mAb|CD/UC|Phase II|Amgen|CD: NCT01696396 UC: NCT01694485|", "date": "2025-02-19", "last_updated": "2026-01-16"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Treatment Options in Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Janus Kinases, Stem Cells, and More", "url": "https://karger.com/dig/article/101/Suppl.%201/69/103670/Emerging-Treatment-Options-in-Inflammatory-Bowel", "domain": "karger.com", "snippet": "The α4β7 integrin inhibitor vedolizumab is an established treatment in IBD, and long-term data of pivotal studies are now available.\nAdditional molecules with therapeutic potential are α4β7-specific abrilumab, β7-specific etrolizumab, and the α4-specific small molecule AJM300.\n...\nVedolizumab, a monoclonal antibody directed against integrin-α4β7, is an effective and approved treatment for CD and UC [67, 68].\nLong-term effectiveness and safety data of the pivotal studies with up to 9 years of follow-up are now available, demonstrating a generally favorable safety profile, with nasopharyngitis as the most frequent side effect [69].\n...\nAbrilumab is another monoclonal antibody, inhibiting integrin-α4β7, which showed some efficacy for the treatment of UC (13.3% remission with 70 mg and 12.7% remission with 210 mg vs. 4.3% with placebo) [72].", "date": "2020-09-29", "last_updated": "2026-09-05"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "|α4β7 antagonist|Abrilumab|Human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7 integrin|NCT01694485, NCT01959165|Phase 2b|Global study completed; 13.3% remission (70mg) vs 4.3% placebo|\n...\nAbrilumab, a human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7 integrin, was evaluated in two Phase 2 studies for moderate-to-severe UC.\nThe global Phase 2b trial (NCT01694485) enrolled 354 patients aged 18–65 with Mayo Score 6–12 who had inadequate response or intolerance to conventional therapies, randomizing them to subcutaneous abrilumab at 7, 21, or 70 mg (day 1, weeks 2, 4, then every 4 weeks) or a single 210 mg dose versus placebo, with the primary endpoint of clinical remission at week 8 (Mayo Score ≤2, no subscore >1).\nResults showed significantly higher remission rates with abrilumab 70 mg (13.3%, OR 3.35, p=0.021) and 210 mg (12.7%, OR 3.33, p=0.030) versus placebo (4.3%), along with superior response rates (49.0% and 46.8% vs 25.9%) and mucosal healing rates (32.7% and 29.1% vs 21.6%), with good tolerability and no cases of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy or deaths (47).", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ueg2.12305", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Phase 3 studies are currently underway in UC, CD as well as in paediatrics (including LUCENT-3, VIVID-1 and 2).^44-46^\n...\nEtrolizumab (PRO145223) is a humanized monoclonal antibody that selectively binds the *ß* ~7~ subunit of the heterodimeric integrins α~4~β~7~ and α~E~β~7~.\n...\nn which IL-22 is linked with the Fc portion of IgG4 allowing improvement of pharmacokinetic characteristics).^139^ New oral JAK inhibitors are also being developed such as brepocitinib (JAK1 and TYK2 inhibitor),^140, 141^ deucravacitinib (TYK2 inhibitor),^142-144^ ritlecitinib and OST-122 (JAK-3 inhibitors)^140, 141, 145^ and peficitinib (pan-JAK inhibitor).^146^ Regarding anti-integrins, abrilumab SC (AMG181) is an anti-integrin α~4~β~7~ with proven effectiveness in a phase 2b study in UC^147-149^ and PN-9\n…", "date": "2022-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov › articles › PMC6537282New biologics and small molecules in inflammatory bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6537282/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Currently, there are three classes of biologicals available to treat IBD.\nThese are\nantagonists to tumor necrosis factor (TNF), anti-integrins and inhibitors of\ninterleukin (IL) 12/IL-23.\n...\n|Anti-integrin|Abrilumab|s.c.|Fully human mAb targeting α4β7|Phase II|Phase II|\n...\nintegrin ligand, α4ß7, results in the recruitment of lymphocytes into the gut or\ngut-associated lymphoid tissue.^\n30 ^ Natalizumab, an anti-α4 integrin antibody, and vedolizumab, an anti-α4ß7 integrin antibody, are efficacious in the treatment of IBD.\n...\n40 ^ An extensive phase III program is ongoing [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT02118584, NCT02100696, NCT02165215, NCT02394028, NCT02403323], including head-to-head trials with adalimumab [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT02171429 and NCT02163759] and infliximab [ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT02136069].\n...\nAbrilumab, formerly known as AMG-181 or MEDI 7183, is a monoclonal antibody\nagainst the α4β7 integrin.\nThe phase IIb RCT with patients with\nmoderate-to-severe Crohn’s disease failed to meet the primary endpoint (clinical\nremission, CDAI < 150, at week 8).^\n43 ^ In contrast, in the phase IIb RCT with 354 patients with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis, abrilumab had a favorable safety and efficacy profile.\nPatients randomized to the active-treatment groups of abrilumab 70 mg or 210 mg had significantly higher remission rates in comparison with the placebo group (13.5%, 13.4%, and 4.4%, respectively).^\n44 ^ Phase III programs in IBD populations are yet to be announced.\n...\n54 ^ A phase III program is ongoing in Crohn’s disease [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03104413, NCT03105128, and NCT03105102] and ulcerative colitis [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03398148 and NCT03398135].", "date": "2019-05-26", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Anti-integrin therapy for inflammatory bowel disease - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5937204/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Vedolizumab produces few systemic adverse effects because it acts on gut-trophic α4β7 integrin, and has been approved and is being used to treat IBD.\n...\nNewer anti-integrin drugs that act on different integrins-related targets, such as AJM300, abrilumab, etrolizumab, and PF-00547659 have also been developed and are in clinical trials.\n...\n|Abrilumab (AMG 181/MEDI 7183)|Fully human IgG~2~ mAb|α4β7-integrin|*s.c*.|Phase IIb|Induction in UC|\n...\nVedolizumab was approved by the FDA and the European Medicines Agency, for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis and CD adult patients which are not responding to one or more conventional treatment such as steroids, immunosuppressive agents, or TNF antagonists.\n...\nAbrilumab (AMG 181/MEDI 7183) is a fully human monoclonal IgG2 antibody against α4β7 integrin that has recently been used in several clinical trials.\nIn a phase IIb study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of abrilumab in 354 moderate-to-severe UC patients who showed an inappropriate response or LOR to anti-TNFs, immunomodulators, or corticosteroid therapy, patients were divided into a placebo group, groups receiving subcutaneous abrilumab 7, 21, or 70 mg at weeks 0, 2, and 4, followed by its administration once every four weeks, and a group receiving a single subcutaneous 210 mg dose of abrilumab[44].\nThe primary endpoint, which was remission rate at week 8, was 1.6%, 2.9%, 13.5%, and 13.4% in the abrilumab 7 mg, 21 mg, 70 mg, and 210 mg groups, respectively, and was 4.4% in the placebo group; the abrilumab 70 mg group (*P* = 0.021) and 210 mg group (*P* = 0.030) both showed a significantly higher remission rate than the placebo group.\n...\nNo PML or severe adverse events were observed in the abrilumab groups through week 24 and no patients developed neutralizing antibodies to abrilumab.\nThus, abrilumab showed advantageous pharmacokinetics, pharmacodynamics, very low immunogenicity, and an acceptable safety profile; further results are expected in the future.\n...\nAdverse events up to week 24 were the same in the abrilumab groups and the placebo group, and there were no cases of PML or death in any of the abrilumab groups.\n...\nVDZ has been approved and is currently used in clinical practice.\nNewer anti-integrin drugs that act on different targets associated with integrin, such as AJM300, abrilumab, etrolizumab, and PF-00547659 are also being developed and currently undergoing clinical trials.", "date": "2018-05-07", "last_updated": "2026-08-20"}], "raw": {"id": "94f71bbf-e531-4f55-b07f-5067d8fa960e"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:34+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q011", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as Small Molecule targeting ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7", "anchors": ["α4β7 Inhibitor", "α4β7"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q011-4", "query": "α4β7 Inhibitor Phase 2 Phase 3 Crohn's disease competitors", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "α4β7 Inhibitor Phase 2 Phase 3 Crohn's disease competitors", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1650.0, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Next generation therapeutics for inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5901649/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "This, along with the overall increase in clinical remission rates and treatment effect size by week 32, would suggest that treatment efficacy is time dependent, and extended treatment may be associated with improved healing.[9] Phase 3 trials in ulcerative colitis and Phase 2 trials in Crohn’s disease are currently underway and should help to address this question.\n...\nVedolizumab, a monoclonal antibody that targets the α4β7 integrin, was approved for use in UC and CD, and is now widely used in routine practice.[10, 11] (Tables 1 and 2) For UC, treatment outcomes in clinical practice have mirrored those seen in the RCT with rates of clinical remission after induction ranging from 24% - 36%.[\n...\nNonetheless, these data are quite promising and phase 3 trials in UC and CD are currently underway, including a head to head comparison against adalimumab.\n...\nTofacitinib, an oral small molecule that inhibits JAK 1 and 3 (and JAK 2 at higher doses), has undergone phase 2 and 3 studies in UC.", "date": "2014-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-16"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Anti-Integrins for the Treatment of Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8402953/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Natalizumab (anti-α4) is approved for moderate to severe Crohn’s disease (CD), but its use is limited due to potential risk of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy.\nVedolizumab (anti-α4β7) is approved for the treatment of ulcerative colitis (UC) and CD.\n...\nLarge phase 3 clinical trials evaluating efficacy of etrolizumab in the induction and maintenance of patients with IBD are underway.\nOther investigational anti-integrin therapies include abrilumab (anti-α4β7 IgG2), PN-943 (orally administered and gut-restricted α4β7 antagonist peptide), AJM300 (orally active small molecule inhibitor of α4), and ontamalimab (anti-MAdCAM-1 IgG).\n...\nVedolizumab (Takeda) is a humanized monoclonal IgG1 antibody that targets the α4β7 heterodimer.\nThe antibody selectively blocks α4β7:MAdCAM-1 interactions—preventing leukocyte adhesion to intestinal endothelium—but does not affect α4β1:VCAM-1 interactions.36 Vedolizumab is FDA-approved for induction and maintenance therapy in patients with CD and CD.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a monoclonal antibody that blocks cellular adhesion by binding the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins.\n...\nMost patients in clinical remission came from the etrolizumab 100 mg group (*n*=39), with 21% achieving remission, followed by 10% in the 300 mg LD cohort (*n*=39), compared with 0% in the placebo cohort (*n*=41).59 Currently, there are several phase 3 clinical trials evaluating etrolizumab in IBD (UC: HIBISCUS I and II, GARDENIA, LAUREL, HICKORY;\nCD: BERGAMOT)60 and two open-label extension trials (UC: COTTONWOOD; CD: JUNIPER).\n...\nAbrilumab is a human anti-α4β7 IgG2 mAb with favorable PK and PD properties amenable to subcutaneous administration.63–65 Phase 2b trials of abrilumab were completed for both CD and UC.\nThe phase 2b placebo-controlled RCT for CD included 249 patients with inadequate or lost response to previous therapies, who were dosed with 21 mg, 70 mg, or 210 mg on day 1, week 2, week 4, and every 4 weeks for 24 weeks, with a primary endpoint of remission at week 12 and CDAI at weeks 8 and 12.66 Although the primary endpoints were not met, treatment groups had higher rates of remission at week 12, safety measures were balanced between treatment and placebo groups, and α4β7 receptor occupancy was sustained.\n...\nTo date, phase 3 trials for abrilumab have not been registered.\n...\nPN-943 is an orally administered and gut-restricted α4β7 antagonist peptide taking advantage of a topical route of administration.69 PN-943 is more effective for induction of remission in UC than PTG-100, an earlier-generation orally administered and gut-restricted α4β7 antagonist cysteine knot peptide developed\nby the same company that preferentially binds activated α4β7 on T cells.70,71 A phase 2 trial of PTG-100 was terminated, and a phase 2 trial of PN-943 is underway to evaluate the effects of 150 mg, 450 mg, or placebo twice daily on 12- and 52-week remission in moderate and severe UC (NCT04504383).\n...\nCarotegrast methyl is an orally active small molecule inhibitor of α4, with demonstrated activity against α4β7 and α4β1.72 The molecule is dosed thrice daily and characterized by generally mild adverse effects.73 A phase 2a placebo-controlled RCT in 102 patients with moderately active\nUC who had inadequate or lost response to previous therapies demonstrated significantly higher rates of clinical remission and mucosal healing at 8 weeks in the treatment compared to placebo group, as well as no serious adverse events, including PML.74 A smaller trial in CD using significantly lower doses showed no significant difference in CDAI at week 4 for carotegrast methyl compared to placebo, although the results were published in abstract form only.75 A phase 3 trial in UC is ongoing (NCT03531892).\n...\nOntamalimab is a subcutaneously administered fully human IgG2κ anti-MAdCAM-1 mAb that blocks α4β7 binding to the MAdCAM-1 ligand,76 with no observed effects on cerebrospinal fluid lymphocytes.77 The OPERA phase 2 placebo-controlled RCT in patients with active moderate to severe CD enrolled 265 patients with inadequate or lost response to previous therapies.78 The primary endpoint was reduction in CDAI at week 8 or 12, which was not significantly different between treatment and placebo; however, the remission rate was higher amongst patients with a higher baseline CRP.\n...\nAntibody blockade of leukocyte trafficking to the gastrointestinal tract with anti-integrin therapy (anti-α4β7) is effective for patients with IBD and is associated with favorable safety profiles.", "date": "2021-08-24", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Internal Medicine Vol.65", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/6495/b5ef74c5b2f46901ddb86ce97a93ccddb0b8.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "Currently, antibody preparations targeting α4β7 integrin (vedolizumab) and an oral α4 integrin inhibi- tor (carotegrast methyl) are available in Japan (Figure).\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody\n(mAb) that targets the β7 integrin subunit, inhibiting both α\n4β7-MAdCAM-1\nand\nαEβ7-with\nE-cadherin\ninterac-\ntions (7).\n...\nIn a phase II study, the subcutane- ous administration of etrolizumab demonstrated efficacy in inducing remission in UC patients (8).\nPhase III trials, com- prising multiple randomized controlled trials, are currently underway in patients with moderately to severely active UC and CD.\nThese include HIBISCUS I and II (etrolizumab versus adalimumab or placebo as induction therapy for mod- erately to severely active UC), GARDENIA (etrolizumab versus infliximab for the treatment of moderately to severely active UC), LAUREL (etrolizumab as maintenance therapy in patients with moderately to severely active UC), HICK-\nORY (etrolizumab as induction and maintenance therapy for\nUC in patients previously treated with tumor necrosis factor inhibitors), and BERGAMOT (etrolizumab as induction and maintenance therapy in patients with moderately to severely active CD).\n...\nAbrilumab is a fully humanized IgG2 mAb that targets α\n4β7 and functions through a mechanism of action similar to\nthat of vedolizumab (10).\nA phase II study evaluated in pa- tients with moderate-to-severe UC refractory to anti-TNF-α and immunomodulatory therapies.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-15"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "IBD therapeutics: what is in the pipeline? - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9234727/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Etrolizumab is a gut selective monoclonal antibody (mAb) that selectively targets the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and α4E7 integrins.^9^ The HICKORY phase III study showed 105 mg 4 weekly etrolizumab-induced remission was significantly higher in patients with UC (18.5%) previously treated with anti-TNF agent at week 14 vs placebo (6.3%).\n...\nCarotegrast methyl (AJM300) is an oral small molecule inhibitor that targets the α4 subunit of α4β7 and α4β1.", "date": "2022-06-15", "last_updated": "2025-09-25"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9752289/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "^29^ Phase 2 and phase 3 studies are currently underway in UC and CD (including a phase 3 study comparing risankizumab to UST in moderate to severe CD patients, who have failed anti‐TNF).\n...\nGuselkumab (CNTO1959) is another IL‐23 p19 inhibitor, already approved in plaque psoriasis ^31^ and psoriatic arthritis, ^32^ which has been shown to be effective in phases 2 studies in both CD and UC.\n...\n^85^ The development of ontamalimab, an anti‐ MAdCAM‐1, has recently been stopped ^86^ but 2 other anti‐integrins (etrolizumab and AJM300) are currently being evaluated in phase 3 CTs in IBD.\n...\nEtrolizumab (PRO145223) is a humanized monoclonal antibody that selectively binds the *ß* ~7~ subunit of the heterodimeric integrins α~4~β~7~ and α~E~β~7~.\n...\nAJM300 (carotegrast methyl) is a new small molecule, α4‐integrin antagonist, being evaluated in UC.\n...\n^146^ Regarding anti‐integrins, abrilumab SC (AMG181) is an anti‐integrin α~4~β~7~ with proven effectiveness in a phase 2b study in UC ^147^ , ^148^ , ^149^ and PN‐943 is a new oral gastro‐intestinal‐restricted peptide antagonist of α4β7 integrin being evaluated in a phase 2 study.", "date": "2022-09-16", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Integrin Inhibitor Competitive Landscape Analysis 2026 | Eureka", "url": "https://eureka.patsnap.com/blog/life-science/integrin-inhibitor-competitive-landscape-2025-vedolizumab-oral-small-molecules-fibrosis-frontier/", "domain": "eureka.patsnap.com", "snippet": "In July 2024, Eli Lilly acquired Morphic Therapeutic for approximately $3.2 billion.\nThis deal validated oral α4β7 integrin inhibition as a next-generation IBD strategy.\nSpecifically, MORF-057 targets the same pathway as vedolizumab but offers oral convenience.\n...\n|Drug|Company|Target|Indication|2024 Revenue|Stage|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Vedolizumab (Entyvio)|Takeda|α4β7|UC, Crohn’s, Pouchitis|~$5.6–5.8B|Approved|\n|Natalizumab (Tysabri)|Biogen|α4|Relapsing MS, Crohn’s|~$1.5–1.6B|Approved (biosimilar)|\n...\n|MORF-057|Eli Lilly (Morphic)|α4β7 (oral)|UC, Crohn’s|N/A|Phase 2|\n|Bexotegrast (PLN-74809)|Pliant Therapeutics|αvβ6/αvβ1|IPF, PSC|N/A|Phase 2/3|\n|PTG-965|Protagonist Therapeutics|α4β7 (oral peptide)|UC, Crohn’s|N/A|Phase 2|\n...\nSpecifically, MORF-057’s Phase 2 data in UC and Lilly’s $3.2B acquisition signal that oral α4β7 inhibition is no longer a theoretical concept.\n...\nThe asset is an oral small molecule α4β7 integrin inhibitor in Phase 2 trials for ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease.\n...\nPliant is advancing bexotegrast toward Phase 3 based on INTEGRIS-IPF results.", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov › articles › PMC6537282New biologics and small molecules in inflammatory bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6537282/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In this context, several new drugs have recently been tested in phase II or III\ntrials in patients with IBD (Table 1).\n...\n|Anti-integrin|Abrilumab|s.c.|Fully human mAb targeting α4β7|Phase II|Phase II|\n| |AJM-300|Oral|Small molecule that blocks the α4 integrin subunit|–|Phase II|\n| |Etrolizumab|s.c.|Humanized mAb targeting the β7 subunit|–|Phase II|\n...\n| |PTG-100|Oral|Small molecule that targets the α4β7 integrin|–|Phase II|\n...\n| |Ustekinumab^ a ^|i.v. and s.c.|Fully human mAb targeting the p40 subunit of IL-12/IL-23|Phase III|Phase II|\n...\nPhase III trials in patients with Crohn’s disease [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03345836 and NCT03345849] and ulcerative colitis [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03653026, NCT02819635, and NCT03006068] are ongoing.\n...\nintegrin ligand, α4ß7, results in the recruitment of lymphocytes into the gut or\ngut-associated lymphoid tissue.^\n30 ^ Natalizumab, an anti-α4 integrin antibody, and vedolizumab, an anti-α4ß7 integrin antibody, are efficacious in the treatment of IBD.\n...\nPTG-100 is an oral small molecule that targets the α4β7 integrin, hereby being\ngut selective.\n...\n40 ^ An extensive phase III program is ongoing [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT02118584, NCT02100696, NCT02165215, NCT02394028, NCT02403323], including head-to-head trials with adalimumab [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT02171429 and NCT02163759] and infliximab [ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT02136069].\n...\nPhase III programs in Crohn’s disease [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03627091, NCT03566823, and NCT03559517] and ulcerative colitis [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03259308, NCT03259334, and NCT03290781] are ongoing.\nAbrilumab, formerly known as AMG-181 or MEDI 7183, is a monoclonal antibody\nagainst the α4β7 integrin.\nThe phase IIb RCT with patients with\n...\nThis trial includes a comparative arm with adalimumab [ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT03759288].", "date": "2019-05-26", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Role of biologics and biosimilars in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5961645/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Currently, monoclonal antibodies against tumor necrosis factor-alpha (infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab, and golimumab), integrins (vedolizumab and natalizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12 and IL-23 antagonists (ustekinumab) are approved for use in IBD.\n...\nSome of the biologics in the Phases II and III of their development along with their mechanism of action as registered in the global clinical trial registry (www.clinicaltrials. gov) for use in IBD are summarized in Table 2.\n...\n|Serial number|Agent name|Mechanism of action|Current status|\n|--|--|--|--|\n|1|Etrolizumab|α4β7 integrin antagonist|Phase III (UC and Crohn’s disease)|\n|2|Risankizumab|IL-23 inhibitor|Phase III (Crohn’s disease)|\n...\n|11|SHP 647|α4β7 integrin antagonist|Phase II (Crohn’s disease) and Phase III (UC)|\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized MAb, which was approved by the FDA in July 2014 after a priority review.\nIt is essentially an integrin α4β7 receptor antagonist, which blocks the binding of Mucosal Addressin Cell Adhesion Molecule-1 (MAdCAM-1) to the integrin receptor, thereby producing anti-inflammatory action by preventing T-cell adhesion and infiltration.\n^53^ Since integrin α4β7 receptors are found more in the gut epithelium, vedolizumab is indicated currently for moderate-to-severe CD and UC.^54^ Clinical trials in pediatric population are also ongoing for UC and CD since November 2017.^55^^,^^56^ The agent is available in the market as 300 mg of lyophilized cake in a single-dose 20 mL vial for reconstitution.", "date": "2018-05-16", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Novel Therapies and Approaches to Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD)", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2077-0383/11/15/4374", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "For Etrolizumab (anti-β7), there are several phase 3 clinical trials evaluating its efficacy (UC: Hibiscus I and II, Gardenia, Laurel, Hickory; CD: Bergamot) and two open-label extension trials (UC: Cottonwood; CD: Juniper).\n...\nAbrilumab (anti α4β7, AMG-181/MEDI-7183) was evaluated in phase 2 studies in UC, achieving a significant clinical remission rate at week 8 compared with a placebo [9], unfortunately in CD patients, the primary endpoint (clinical remission at week 12) was not met [10].\nTo date, phase 3 trials for abrilumab have not been registered.\nCarotegrast (anti α4, AJM300) is an oral drug tested in phase 2 trials for UC, demonstrating significantly higher rates of clinical remission and mucosal healing at 8 weeks compared with the placebo group [11], and there is an ongoing phase 3 trial in UC [12].\n...\nFinally, PN-943 and PTG-100, two oral anti α4β7 drugs evaluated in UC patients [16], seem promising and there are ongoing trials evaluating both PTG-100 [17] and PN-943 (IDEAL study) [18].", "date": "2022-07-28", "last_updated": "2024-12-23"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Exploring the Pipeline of Novel Therapies for Inflammatory Bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10045261/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|Anti-adhesion Molecules|Etrolizumab|α4β7 integrin|SC|Phase I|Phase I|\n|Anti-adhesion Molecules|PN-943|α4β7 integrin|Oral|Phase I| |\n|Anti-adhesion Molecules|Vedolizumab|α4β7 integrin|IV|Phase IV|Phase IV|\n...\nA phase III clinical trial and two OLE clinical trials are evaluating etrolizumab as a potential therapy for IBD [29].\n...\nAbrilumab is a monoclonal antibody that selectively targets α4β7 integrin, which is already a validated target in the treatment of IBD.\nSeveral clinical trials evaluated abrilumab for the treatment of moderate-to-severe CD and moderate-to-severe UC in patients with inadequate or loss of response to immunosuppressive agents, corticosteroids, or anti-TNF agents.", "date": "2023-03-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}], "raw": {"id": "ffe27282-7481-4a75-a93e-3c392401e883"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:34+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q012", "objective": "Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab pivotal, registrational, or Phase 3 trial in Unresectable or Metastatic Hepatocellular Carcinoma: planned or initiated status and expected start date", "anchors": ["Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775", "Lenvatinib", "Nofazinlimab", "NCT04194775"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q012-2", "query": "Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775 current trial status", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775 current trial status", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 3085.8, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in... | Clinical Trial", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/clinical-trial/f021ad7c4cfbc551/nct04194775-nofazinlimab-lenvatinib-advanced-hepatocellular-carcinoma", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "NCT04194775Active, not recruitingPhase 3\n# A Multi-Center, Double-Blind, Randomized, Phase III Study to Investigate the Efficacy and Safety of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Combination With Lenvatinib Compared to Placebo in Combination With Lenvatinib as First-Line Therapy in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)\nCStone Pharmaceuticals125 sites in 6 countries534 enrolledStarted: December 13, 2019Last updated: July 30, 2026\n...\n- **Phase**\nPhase 3\n- **Status**\nActive, not recruiting\n...\n534\n...\n- **Primary Endpoint**\n...\nThis is a multi-center, double-blind, randomized, phase III study to investigate the efficacy and safety of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in combination with lenvatinib and placebo in combination with lenvatinib in the treatment of subjects with no prior systemic treatment and with unresectable advanced hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC).\n...\nOverall Survival (OS)\nTime Frame: From enrollment to end of follow-up, a median of 42.5 months\n...\nResults posted to ClinicalTrials.gov on 2026-07-30.\n...\n534 started · 0 completed\n...\nJune 18, 2025 (last year)\n- **Study Completion**\nSeptember 30, 2026 (2 days ago)\n- **Last Verified**", "date": "2026-07-30", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT04194775", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "Drug: Nofazinlimab (CS1003)+Lenvatinib Drug: Nofazinlimab (CS1003) Placebo+Lenvatinib\n- CS1003-305\n...\nInclusion criteria\n- Age ≥18 years on the day of signing informed consent-(For Taiwan, the lower limit of age is 20 years).\n- Subjects with unresectable advanced HCC, that is not eligible for surgery and/or locoregional therapy (Stage B or C based on Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer [BCLC] staging system, and meets either one of the following criteria: 1) histologically or cytologically confirmed diagnosis of HCC, 2) clinically confirmed diagnosis of HCC according to American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases (AASLD) criteria.\nPatients without cirrhosis require histological confirmation of diagnosis.\n- With at least one measurable lesion can be assessed\n- Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status (ECOG PS) 0 or 1.\n- Life expectancy ≥ 3 months.\n- Child-Pugh A\n- No prior systemic treatment for advanced HCC\n- Subjects with hepatitis B virus (HBV) infection, are willing to continue receiving antiviral treatment while on study.\nSubjects have adequate organ and marrow function.\nFemale subjects with childbearing potential must have negative serum pregnancy test result at screening.\n...\nExclusion criteria\n- Fibrolamellar-HCC, sarcomatoid, cholangiocellular carcinoma or mixed cholangiocarcinoma and HCC.\n- A prior bleeding event due to esophageal within 6 months or other gastrointestinal bleeding events within 28 days prior to screening.\n- Malabsorption syndrome or inability to take oral medication due to other causes.\n- HBV and HCV co-infection.\n...\n- Surgery or locoregional therapy for palliative purpose within 4 weeks prior to study treatment.\n...\n- Known history of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection or acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS).\n...\n- History of bone marrow transplantation or organ transplantation.\n- History of anaphylaxis or hypersensitivity to any ingredient of the investigational product.\n- Any contraindication of lenvatinib.\n...\n- Pregnant or lactating female subjects.\n...\n|Overall survival (OS)|Expected to be 5.5 years after the first patient is enrollment.|\n...\n|Objective response rate (ORR) assessed by blinded independent central review committee(BICR)|Expected to be 5.5 years after the first patient is enrollment.|\n|Progression-free survival(PFS) assessed by blinded independent central review committee(BICR) based on Response Evaluation Criteria in Solid Tumors(RECIST) v1.1|Expected to be 5.5 years after the first patient is enrollment.|\n...\n|Percentage of Participants with Adverse Events|Expected to be 5.5 years after the first patient is enrollment.|\n|Peak and trough serum concentrations of CS1003|Expected to be 5.5 years after the first patient is enrollment.|\n|Number and percentage of subjects who develop anti-CS1003 antibody (ADA)|Expected to be 5.5 years after the first patient is enrollment.|\n|Time to deterioration (TTD), defined as the time from randomization to the first deterioration of European Organization for the Research and Treatment of Cancer (EORTC) Quality-of-Life Questionnaire Core 30 (QLQ-C30) scale|Expected to be 5.5 years after the first patient is enrollment.|", "date": "2019-12-10", "last_updated": "2025-08-12"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "NCT04194775: An ongoing trial by CStone Pharmaceuticals", "url": "https://fdaaa.trialstracker.net/trial/NCT04194775/", "domain": "fdaaa.trialstracker.net", "snippet": "## NCT04194775: An ongoing trial by CStone Pharmaceuticals\nThis trial is ongoing.\nIt must report results 1 year from now.\n...\n|Title|A Multi-Center, Double-Blind, Randomized, Phase III Study to Investigate the Efficacy and Safety of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Combination With Lenvatinib Compared to Placebo in Combination With Lenvatinib as First-Line Therapy in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)|\n|Results Status|Ongoing|\n|ACT or pACT?|This is what FDAAA officially calls an \"Applicable Clinical Trial\"|\n|Start date|Dec. 13, 2019|\n|Completion date|June 30, 2025|\n|Required reporting date|June 30, 2026, midnight|\n|Actual reporting date|None|\n|Date last checked at ClinicalTrials.gov|June 13, 2025|", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-06-13"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study to Evaluate MIV-818 in Patients With Liver Cancer Manifestations", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT03781934", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "No results posted yet for this study\n...\nThis is an open-label, multicentre dose escalation/expansion study to assess safety and tolerability of MIV 818 as either monotherapy or in combination with 1) lenvatinib, a tyrosine kinase inhibitor used as a standard of care for the treatment of HCC or 2) pembrolizumab, a PD-1 inhibitor.\n...\n## Timeline & Regulatory\n- **Start**\n2018-09-05\n- **Primary Completion**\n2025-01-07\n- **Completion**\n2025-06-16\n...\n- A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma\nNCT04194775 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE3\n- Lenvatinib in Patients With Previously Treated Advanced Biliary Tract Cancer\n...\n- HAIC Plus Lenvatinib and Toripalimab for Advanced HCC", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Lenvatinib for the Treatment of Recurrent Hepatocellular Carcinoma After Liver Transplant", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT05103904", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "No results posted yet for this study\n...\nThis phase II trial evaluates lenvatinib for the treatment of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) that has come back (recurrent) after a liver transplant.\n...\n## Timeline & Regulatory\n- **Start**\n2022-04-19\n- **Primary Completion**\n2025-05-09\n- **Completion**\n2025-05-09\n- **FDA Drug**\nYes\n...\nNCT03713593 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE3\n- A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma\nNCT04194775 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE3", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Current status of the cost burden of first-line systemic treatment for ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/jjco/article/54/10/1071/7687758", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Thus, there is currently no consensus on the use of atezolizumab plus bevacizumab, durvalumab and tremelimumab for the treatment of advanced HCC.\nAdditionally, many ongoing trials still use TKIs as controls since trials comparing TKIs and ICIs were initiated almost simultaneously, and several trials have not yet reported their results (Table 2).\n...\n| |NCT04194775|Nofazinlimab (anti-PD-1 antibody) plus lenvatinib|Lenvatinib|III|OS|First-line|\n...\n| |NCT03764293|SHR-1210 (anti-PD-1 antibody) plus apatinib (TKI)|Sorafenib|III|OS & PFS|First-line|", "date": "2024-10-03", "last_updated": "2026-08-27"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Nofazinlimab - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/bf31aad0266d41688ed754613150c716", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|NCT04194775 (CS1003-305, ESMO2025)|Phase 3|Hepatocellular Carcinoma First line|534|Nofazinlimab + Lenvatinib|crgoxwqgbt(rzfiobiepn) = wfpwygxeca czvdkshulz (pvclpucluq ) View more|Positive|17 Oct 2025|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|NCT04194775 (CS1003-305, ESMO2025)|Phase 3|Hepatocellular Carcinoma First line|534|Placebo + Lenvatinib|crgoxwqgbt(rzfiobiepn) = cupywigtzp czvdkshulz (pvclpucluq ) View more|Positive|17 Oct 2025|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With ...", "url": "https://www.guidelinecentral.com/clinical-trial/?id=NCT04194775&v=tabular", "domain": "guidelinecentral.com", "snippet": "# A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma\n- Recruitment Status\n- ACTIVE, NOT RECRUITING(See Contacts and Locations)Verified July 2025 by CStone Pharmaceuticals\n...\n- NCT04194775\n...\n- Last Update Posted\n- July 31, 2025\n- Last Verified\n- July 2025\nClinicalTrials.gov processed this data on July 2025.\nLink to the current ClinicalTrials.gov record .\n...\n|Study Start Date|December 13, 2019|\n|Actual Primary Completion Date|June 18, 2025|\n...\n|Official Title|A Multi-Center, Double-Blind, Randomized, Phase III Study to Investigate the Efficacy and Safety of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Combination With Lenvatinib Compared to Placebo in Combination With Lenvatinib as First-Line Therapy in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)|\n|Brief Summary|This is a multi-center, double-blind, randomized, phase III study to investigate the efficacy and safety of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in combination with lenvatinib and placebo in combination with lenvatinib in the treatment of subjects with no prior systemic treatment and with unresectable advanced hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC).\nIn this study, Nofazinlimab (CS1003) (or placebo) and lenvatinib are both considered as the study treatment while Nofazinlimab (CS1003) (or placebo) is the investigational product of and lenvatinib is selected as the basic treatment for HCC.|\n...\n|Recruitment Status|Active, not recruiting|\n|--|--|\n|Anticipated Enrollment|534|\n|Anticipated Study Completion Date|1w 1d from now|\n|Anticipated Primary Study Completion Date|June 18, 2025|", "date": "2019-12-13", "last_updated": "2025-10-22"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies for Hepatocellular Carcinoma - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12100529/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Several phase 3 trials encompass combination therapy and monotherapy for advanced HCC.\nThese options include several PD-1 inhibitors, including tislelizumab, camrelizumab, budigalimab, toripalimab, finotonlimab, nofazinlimab, penpulimab, and sintilimab.\n...\n|**Nofazinlimab (CS1003)**| |Anti-PD-1 antibody| | |\n|+ Lenvatinib|NCT04194775|VEGFR-1, VEGFR-2, and VEGFR-3 inhibitor|Multinational|Phase 3 Active, not recruiting|\n...\nIn a double-blind, multicenter phase 3 study, toripalimab plus lenvatinib (NCT04523493) is being compared with lenvatinib monotherapy and placebo.\nA similar study combining nofazinlimab and lenvatinib is underway (NCT04194775).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "The current landscape of therapies for hepatocellular carcinoma", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10588973/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Selected ongoing prospective phase III randomized controlled trials of systemic therapies in advanced HCC in the United States\n...\n|CheckMate 459: A Study of Nivolumab vs Sorafenib as First-Line Treatment in Pts with Advanced HCC (NCT02576509)|743|Nivolumab vs Sorafenib (1L)|OS|16.4 vs 14.7 mo (HR 0.85, 95% CI 0.72–1.02, *P* = 0.075)|(77)|\n...\n|A Study of Nofazinlimab in Combination with Lenvatinib vs Lenvatinib Alone as First-Line Therapy in Pts with Advanced HCC (NCT04194775)|534|Nofazinlimab + Lenvatinib vs Lenvatinib (1L)|OS, PFS-BICR|Not yet completed| |", "date": "2023-07-10", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}], "raw": {"id": "23393a2d-a94c-4bb0-856e-ea9ed3025721"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:38+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q011", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as Small Molecule targeting ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7", "anchors": ["α4β7 Inhibitor", "α4β7"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q011-2", "query": "α4β7 Inhibitor vedolizumab current development status 2026", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "α4β7 Inhibitor vedolizumab current development status 2026", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 7920.2, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Takeda Pipeline, September 2026: 127 Active Clinical Trials ...", "url": "https://datalookout.com/takeda-clinical-trials", "domain": "datalookout.com", "snippet": "125\nActive\n82\nCurrently recruiting\n...\nIn gastroenterology, Entyvio (vedolizumab) for IBD has one of the most active label expansion programs in the specialty.\n...\n- **Entyvio (vedolizumab)**: α4β7 integrin, IBD/Crohn's/UC\n...\n|A Study of Vedolizumab in Adults With Ulcerative Colitis (UC) or Crohn's…NCT06581328|Recruiting|Primary completion moved earlier: 2027-10-01 → 2027-05-01|2026-09-16|\n...\nBased on current ClinicalTrials.gov data, 82 Takeda-sponsored trials are actively recruiting patients globally.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline | Spyre Therapeutics", "url": "https://www.spyre.com/pipeline", "domain": "spyre.com", "snippet": "SPY001\nα4β7\nPart A topline reported Q2 2026\n...\nPart A topline reported Q2 2026\n...\nSPY001 is a novel, half-life extended monoclonal antibody targeting α4β7 for the potential treatment of IBD\nIn head-to-head preclinical studies, SPY001 demonstrated similar potency and selectivity as vedolizumab\nInterim Phase 1 results demonstrated SPY001 was well tolerated, exhibited a differentiated PK profile supporting potential quarterly or twice annual maintenance dosing, and rapid and sustained target engagement\nSPY001 is being evaluated in the Phase 2 SKYLINE trial in UC patients\n...\nSPY120 is a combination of SPY001 (anti-α4β7) and SPY002 (anti-TL1A) for the potential treatment of IBD\n...\nProduct candidates discussed are investigational and currently being studied in clinical trials.\nTheir safety and efficacy have not been established and they have not been approved for any use by FDA, the European Commission, or any other health authority.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Integrin α4β7 Competitive Landscape After Lilly's Morphic ...", "url": "https://sleuthinsights.com/analysis/integrin-a4b7-landscape", "domain": "sleuthinsights.com", "snippet": "Takeda’s Entyvio (vedolizumab) already blocks integrin α4β7, and the November 2025 chart from Sleuth and Vibe Bio counts 72 α4β7 assets across more than 50 companies, 51 of them active and 21 inactive or of unknown status.\nMorphic’s MORF-057 is an oral small-molecule α4β7 inhibitor for inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), one of the most competitive areas in immunology and inflammation (I&I).\n...\nOf the 51 active assets on the November 2025 chart, 25 are preclinical, 4 are in Phase 1, 9 in Phase 2, 8 in Phase 3 and 5 marketed.\nAll eight active Phase 3 entries are vedolizumab biosimilars.\nThe marketed group is vedolizumab, in intravenous and subcutaneous forms, plus three drugs aimed at the closely related α4 subunit (CD49d): natalizumab, a natalizumab biosimilar and carotegrast methyl.\nActive Phase 2 entries include MORF-057, AbbVie’s ABBV-382, Spyre’s SPY001 and Gilead’s GS-1427.\n...\nAfter the snapshot, the FDA accepted Alvotech’s AVT16 biosimilar application, with a decision expected in the first quarter of 2027, and Polpharma Biologics told Takeda it expects approval of its PB016 in May 2027.\nIn August 2026, Takeda sued Polpharma under the BPCIA over vedolizumab dosing patents, so US launch timing will depend on the litigation.\n...\nIn the 35-patient, open-label Phase 2a EMERALD-1 trial in ulcerative colitis, MORF-057 met its primary endpoint, a 6.4-point reduction in the Robarts Histopathology Index, with 25.7% endoscopic improvement at week 12 and no serious treatment-related adverse events.\n...\nIn March 2026, Lilly started EMERALD-3, a placebo-controlled ulcerative colitis trial of about 1,400 participants with a 52-week remission endpoint.\n...\nBiosimilar filings, litigation and MORF-057 trial status are current as of September 2026.\nThis page is a dated snapshot.*", "date": "2025-11-20", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "www.fresenius-kabi.com › us › news-and-eventsFresenius Announces FDA and EMA ... - Fresenius Kabi Global", "url": "https://www.fresenius-kabi.com/us/news-and-events/review-vedolizumab-biosimilar", "domain": "fresenius-kabi.com", "snippet": "July 31, 2026\n- **U.S. FDA and EMA acceptance for review of Biologics License Application (BLA) and Marketing Authorisation Application (MAA), respectively, of intravenous vedolizumab biosimilar candidate, an integrin receptor antagonist.**\n...\n**Bad Homburg, Germany** – July 31, 2026 – Fresenius Kabi, an operating company of Fresenius, announced today that the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the European Medicines Agency (EMA) have accepted for review the Biologics License Application (BLA) and Marketing Authorisation Application (MAA), respectively, for PB016, a proposed vedolizumab biosimilar candidate to the reference product Entyvio®* (vedolizumab) lyophilized vial for intravenous (IV) administration.\nThe applications were submitted by Polpharma Biologics S.A., with Fresenius Kabi holding exclusive commercialization rights globally, excluding the Middle East and North Africa, pending regulatory approvals.\n...\nPB016, in-licensed from Polpharma Biologics S.A.^1^, is a proposed biosimilar to IV vedolizumab, an integrin receptor antagonist indicated for the treatment of adults with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease in adults.\n...\nThe submissions for the intravenous presentation are supported by a comprehensive development program evaluating the proposed biosimilar’s similarity to the reference product, consistent with regulatory requirements for biosimilar approval.\n...\nVedolizumab (PB016) is a monoclonal antibody that selectively targets the α4β7 integrin, reducing gastrointestinal inflammation by inhibiting the migration of lymphocytes into the gut.^4^\n...\nVedolizumab is administered by intravenous infusion.\nPB016 is being developed by Polpharma Biologics S.A., and Fresenius Kabi has exclusive commercialization rights globally, excluding the Middle East and North Africa, pending regulatory approval.\nThe biosimilar development program for vedolizumab is designed to demonstrate high similarity to the reference product through comprehensive analytical, non‑clinical, and clinical studies, in line with regulatory requirements for biosimilar approval.\nPB016 has been studied in a Phase 1 trial with healthy volunteers and a Phase 3 trial with ulcerative colitis patients (NCT05771155).^5^", "date": "2026-07-31", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Integrin Inhibitor Competitive Landscape Analysis 2026 | Eureka", "url": "https://eureka.patsnap.com/blog/life-science/integrin-inhibitor-competitive-landscape-2025-vedolizumab-oral-small-molecules-fibrosis-frontier/", "domain": "eureka.patsnap.com", "snippet": "The integrin inhibitor market stands at a pivotal crossroads in 2025.\nEstablished biologics face mounting biosimilar pressure.\nMeanwhile, oral small molecules are attracting blockbuster-scale investment.\nFurthermore, fibrosis programs are advancing toward Phase 3 readouts that could reshape the αv integrin class entirely.\n...\nIn July 2024, Eli Lilly acquired Morphic Therapeutic for approximately $3.2 billion.\nThis deal validated oral α4β7 integrin inhibition as a next-generation IBD strategy.\nSpecifically, MORF-057 targets the same pathway as vedolizumab but offers oral convenience.\n...\n|Drug|Company|Target|Indication|2024 Revenue|Stage|\n...\n|Vedolizumab (Entyvio)|Takeda|α4β7|UC, Crohn’s, Pouchitis|~$5.6–5.8B|Approved|\n...\n|MORF-057|Eli Lilly (Morphic)|α4β7 (oral)|UC, Crohn’s|N/A|Phase 2|\n...\n|PTG-965|Protagonist Therapeutics|α4β7 (oral peptide)|UC, Crohn’s|N/A|Phase 2|\n...\nThe shift toward oral small molecules is the defining strategic trend of 2024–2026.\nSpecifically, MORF-057’s Phase 2 data in UC and Lilly’s $3.2B acquisition signal that oral α4β7 inhibition is no longer a theoretical concept.\n...\nThe asset is an oral small molecule α4β7 integrin inhibitor in Phase 2 trials for ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease.\n...\nPliant is advancing bexotegrast toward Phase 3 based on INTEGRIS-IPF results.", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Top 7 Breakthrough Drugs for Ulcerative Colitis Treatment", "url": "https://www.delveinsight.com/blog/emerging-drugs-for-ulcerative-colitis-treatment", "domain": "delveinsight.com", "snippet": "**Vedolizumab** is a humanized monoclonal antibody that targets and blocks the alpha 4 beta 7 integrin, preventing it from binding to the intestinal mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule 1 (MAdCAM-1).\nThis integrin is present in a specific group of circulating white blood cells involved in the inflammatory processes of ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease.\n...\nIt is currently approved as a colitis treatment for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis in adults who have not responded to, have lost response to, or cannot tolerate conventional therapy or a TNF-alpha blocker.\n...\nZotemtegrast (LY4268989) is an oral, small‑molecule inhibitor targeting the α4β7 integrin pathway designed to reduce gut‑homing of inflammatory lymphocytes in ulcerative colitis, a mechanism similar to vedolizumab but delivered as a gut‑selective oral agent.\nEarly clinical development is evaluating zotemtegrast both as monotherapy and in combination with biologics (for example, a phase II program testing co‑administration with mirikizumab and placebo‑controlled studies in moderate‑to‑severe UC), with trials currently underway to assess safety, induction of clinical remission, and maintenance outcomes.\n...\nZotemtegrast (LY4268989) is an oral, small‑molecule inhibitor targeting the α4β7 integrin pathway designed to reduce gut‑homing of inflammatory lymphocytes in ulcerative colitis, a mechanism similar to vedolizumab but delivered as a gut‑selective oral agent.", "date": "2026-06-25", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|α4β7 antagonist|Abrilumab|Human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7 integrin|NCT01694485, NCT01959165|Phase 2b|Global study completed; 13.3% remission (70mg) vs 4.3% placebo|\n...\n|Combination|OD-07656 + Vedolizumab|IRAK4 inhibitor + α4β7 integrin antagonist|NCT06850727|Phase 2a|Started June 2025; estimated completion Nov 2026|\n...\nVedolizumab, a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody that specifically targets the α4β7 integrin heterodimer without affecting α4β1, represents the prototypical gut-selective anti-trafficking agent approved by FDA, EMA, and PMDA based on the GEMINI trials demonstrating superior efficacy over placebo for both induction and maintenance in moderate to severe UC (GEMINI 1: 47.1% clinical response and 16.9% remission at week 6;\n...\nVedolizumab’s mechanism involves selective inhibition of α4β7-MAdCAM-1 binding to prevent gut-homing of lymphocytes while preserving systemic immunity as demonstrated by maintained humoral responses to parenteral vaccines but blocked responses to oral vaccines, with proposed effects on naïve T and B cell trafficking to intestinal inductive sites and attenuation of lymphoid aggregates contributing to therapeutic benefit beyond simple effector cell blockade.\n...\nA Phase 2a trial (NCT06850727) organized as a two-part, open-label and randomized study evaluating the safety and efficacy of OD-07656 followed by vedolizumab therapy in patients with moderately to severely active UC, with primary outcomes measuring changes in the 3-component mMS from baseline to 12 weeks and treatment-emergent AEs.\n...\nThe trial started in June 2025 with an estimated enrollment of 57 participants across locations in Australia, Moldova, and New Zealand, with primary completion estimated for January 2026 and full study completion by November 2026, though results are not yet available as the study is currently in the recruiting Phase.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Vedolizumab Health Professional Drug Record | NIH", "url": "https://clinicalinfo.hiv.gov/en/drugs/vedolizumab/health-professional", "domain": "clinicalinfo.hiv.gov", "snippet": "α~4~β~7~ integrin antagonist.\nVedolizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody (mAb) that is currently FDA-approved for the treatment of ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease.\nAs such, it functions by binding to the α~4~β~7~ integrin receptor, located on the surface of certain T lymphocytes.\n...\nBlockade of α~4~β~7~ integrin by vedolizumab has been explored as a possible strategy to help individuals with HIV control viral load levels, even in the absence of ART.^11,12^\n...\n### Study Identifier: NCT03577782\n**Sponsor**: Hospitales Universitarios Virgen del Rocío\n**Phase**: 2\n**Status**: This study has been completed.\n**Study Purpose**: The purpose of this open-label study was to evaluate the safety of vedolizumab and whether vedolizumab plus ART administration could help treatment-naive individuals control viral load levels during an analytical treatment interruption of ART.\n...\n**Selected Study Results**: Results presented at CROI 2022 and published in *JCI Insight* (2024) showed that although vedolizumab was safe and well-tolerated, it had no substantial impact on viral remission after participants underwent an analytical treatment interruption of ART.^15,16^\n...\n### Study Identifiers: HAVARTI; NCT03147859\n**Sponsor**: Ottawa Hospital Research Institute\n**Phase**: 2\n**Status**: This study has been completed.\n**Study Purpose**: The purpose of this open-label trial was to evaluate vedolizumab’s safety and ability to control viral load levels in individuals with chronic HIV undergoing an analytical treatment interruption of ART.\n...\n**Selected Study Results**: Results presented at CROI 2019 showed that vedolizumab given at two different doses and administered both before and after a treatment interruption of ART was generally safe.\nNo drug-related severe adverse events (AEs) were reported.\nNo participants had sustained viral suppression after treatment interruption of ART.\nNotably, the higher dose of vedolizumab appeared to have a significantly greater effect on reducing viral rebound compared with the lower dose of vedolizumab.^17^\nA Phase 1 study (NCT02788175) evaluating the efficacy of vedolizumab in controlling viral rebound in individuals undergoing an analytical treatment interruption of ART has also been completed and results are available from *Science Translational Medicine* (2019).^18^\n...\nIn this Phase 2 study, ten participants were enrolled to receive 7 monthly infusions of vedolizumab over 24 weeks, in addition to ART, followed by interruption of treatment.\nVedolizumab was reported as being well tolerated.\n...\nIn this Phase 2 trial, eight adults with HIV received intravenous infusions of vedolizumab (four participants each in the 300 and 150 mg dose groups).\nVedolizumab was administered both before and after ART treatment interruption.\nNo drug-related severe AEs were reported.\nInfluenza and severe Grade 3 transient hepatitis each occurred in one participant.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-05"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "|α4β7 antagonist|MORF-057|Oral small-molecule inhibitor of α4β7 integrin|NCT05291689 (EMERALD-1), NCT05611671 (EMERALD-2)|Phase 2a, Phase 2b|EMERALD-1 completed Nov 2023; EMERALD-2 primary completion Nov 2024|\n...\n|Combination|OD-07656 + Vedolizumab|IRAK4 inhibitor + α4β7 integrin antagonist|NCT06850727|Phase 2a|Started June 2025; estimated completion Nov 2026|\n...\nVedolizumab, a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody that specifically targets the α4β7 integrin heterodimer without affecting α4β1, represents the prototypical gut-selective anti-trafficking agent approved by FDA, EMA, and PMDA based on the GEMINI trials demonstrating superior efficacy over placebo for both induction and maintenance in moderate to severe UC (GEMINI 1: 47.1% clinical response and 16.9% remission at week 6;\n...\nVedolizumab’s mechanism involves selective inhibition of α4β7-MAdCAM-1 binding to prevent gut-homing of lymphocytes while preserving systemic immunity as demonstrated by maintained humoral responses to parenteral vaccines but blocked responses to oral vaccines, with proposed effects on naïve T and B cell trafficking to intestinal inductive sites and attenuation of lymphoid aggregates contributing to therapeutic benefit beyond simple effector cell blockade.\n...\nA Phase 2a trial (NCT06850727) organized as a two-part, open-label and randomized study evaluating the safety and efficacy of OD-07656 followed by vedolizumab therapy in patients with moderately to severely active UC, with primary outcomes measuring changes in the 3-component mMS from baseline to 12 weeks and treatment-emergent AEs.\n...\nThe trial started in June 2025 with an estimated enrollment of 57 participants across locations in Australia, Moldova, and New Zealand, with primary completion estimated for January 2026 and full study completion by November 2026, though results are not yet available as the study is currently in the recruiting Phase.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Polpharma Biologics Announces FDA and EMA Acceptance for ...", "url": "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260728851657/en/Polpharma-Biologics-Announces-FDA-and-EMA-Acceptance-for-Review-of-PB016-Vedolizumab-Biosimilar-Candidate", "domain": "businesswire.com", "snippet": "ROTKREUZ, Switzerland--(BUSINESS WIRE)--Polpharma Biologics International AG today announces that the U.S.\nFood and Drug Administration (FDA) and the European Medicines Agency (EMA) have accepted for review the Biologics License Application (BLA) and Marketing Authorisation Application (MAA), respectively, for PB016, a proposed vedolizumab biosimilar candidate to Takeda’s reference product Entyvio®* (vedolizumab) lyophilized vial for intravenous (IV) administration for the treatment of adults with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\n...\nPB016, a proposed biosimilar to vedolizumab - an integrin receptor antagonist - is indicated for the treatment of adults with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease, delivered in a lyophilized vial for intravenous administration.\n...\nFresenius Kabi holds exclusive commercialization rights to PB016 globally, excluding the Middle East and North Africa, pending regulatory approvals.\nThe biosimilar development program was designed to demonstrate high similarity to the reference product through comprehensive analytical, non-clinical, and clinical studies, conducted in line with regulatory requirements for biosimilar approval.\nPB016 has been studied in a Phase 1 trial with healthy volunteers and a Phase 3 trial with ulcerative colitis patients (NCT05771155).^1,2^\n...\nVedolizumab (PB016) is a monoclonal antibody that selectively targets the α4β7 integrin, reducing gastrointestinal inflammation by inhibiting the migration of lymphocytes into the gut.^1^\nIt is used in the treatment of moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease, chronic inflammatory diseases that can significantly impact patients' quality of life.\nVedolizumab is administered by intravenous infusion.", "date": "2026-07-31", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "7ac371b5-598a-4f68-b45f-ab507b820dc8"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:38+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q011", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as Small Molecule targeting ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7", "anchors": ["α4β7 Inhibitor", "α4β7"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q011-5", "query": "α4β7 Inhibitor AJM300 natalizumab class comparison", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "α4β7 Inhibitor AJM300 natalizumab class comparison", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 4546.2, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Past, Present and Future of Therapeutic Interventions Targeting Leukocyte Trafficking in Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/12/suppl_2/S633/5077920?guestAccessKey=", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Subsequently, the anti-α4 monoclonal antibody natalizumab proved efficacious for induction and maintenance of remission in Crohn’s disease, but was associated with progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy due to its ability to interfere with both α4β1 and α4β7 function.\nLater developments in this area took advantage of the fairly selective expression of MAdCAM-1 in the digestive organs, showing that vedolizumab, a more specific monoclonal antibody selectively blocking MAdCAM-1 binding to integrin α4β7, was efficacious for induction and maintenance of remission in ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, and it was not associated with neurological complications.\n...\n|AJM300|SM|Integrin α4|UC|Phase II|Safe [8 weeks]|^65^|", "date": "2018-08-22", "last_updated": "2026-03-07"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Integrin antagonists as potential therapeutic options for ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4926164/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Two agents, natalizumab and vedolizumab, are approved by the United States Food and Drug Administration for the treatment of Crohn’s disease, with vedolizumab the primary anti-integrin used due to a more favorable safety profile.\n...\nAnti-integrin agents, like tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-α inhibitors, are biologic agents designed to target molecules that contribute to the development of intestinal inflammation.\nUnlike TNF-α inhibitors, which are antibodies directed against the pro-inflammatory cytokine TNF-α, anti-integrin therapy modulates inflammation by binding to integrins that contribute to leukocyte trafficking, thereby preventing leukocyte migration into GI mucosa and the development of inflammation within these tissues.\n...\nAt present, natalizumab and vedolizumab are the only two anti-integrin agents approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for use in CD.\n...\nThe first anti-integrin agent approved for use in patients with CD was natalizumab, a monoclonal antibody directed against the α4-integrin chain.\nBy targeting α4, natalizumab blocks both the α4β1 and α4β7 integrins.\n...\nAlthough natalizumab is effective for the induction of clinical response and remission in patients with moderate to severely active CD, its use is associated with the development of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), a rare but often fatal neurologic disease.\n...\n21] More recently, the use of natalizumab has largely been supplanted by vedolizumab, which has similar efficacy to natalizumab,[23] but has not been linked to PML.\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal IgG1 antibody that blocks the α4β7 integrin heterodimer without binding to the α4β1 integrin.\nBy only binding α4β7, vedolizumab prevents leukocyte extravasation into GI mucosa as mucosal vascular addressin cell adhesion molecular 1 (MAdCAM-1), α4β7’s ligand, is expressed on the endothelial surface of venules and lymphoid tissue within the GI tract.[\n24] Unlike natalizumab, vedolizumab does not bind α4β1.\n...\nA subsequent meta-analysis comparing the efficacy of natalizumab and vedolizumab found that both agents have similar efficacy in inducing remission and response in anti-TNF-naïve and anti-TNF-exposed patients with similar safety profiles and an absence of PML in patients treated with vedolizumab.[23,30]\n...\nAJM300 is an orally administered humanized anti-α4 integrin antagonist, preventing α4β1 from binding VCAM-1 and α4β7 from binding MAdCAM-1.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-21"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Old and New Biologics and Small Molecules in Inflammatory ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12285414/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Other investigational anti-integrin therapies include abrilumab (anti-α4β7 IgG2), PN-943 (orally administered and gut-restricted α4β7 antagonist peptide), AJM300 (orally active small molecule inhibitor of α4), and ontamalimab (anti-MAdCAM-1 IgG).\n...\n|Natalizmab|α4β1, α4β7|Humanized IgG1 mAb|IV|ENAC 1/2^15^ ENCOR^16^|CD|\n...\n|Carotegrast methyl (AJM 300)|α4β1, α4β7|Small molecule|Oral|Phase III^28^|UC|\n...\nNatalizumab은 비선택적 α4 antegrin에 대한 IgG1 단클론항체로 α4β7, α4β1을 차단하는 약제이다.^29^ Gut-specific α4β7/MAdCAM-1 작용을 차단함으로써 염증이 있는 장점막에 림프구가 축적되는 것을 막게 된다.\n...\n^16^ 하지만 이 약제는 α4β1 림프구의 중추신경계로의 이동 역시 억제하여,^30^바이러스에 대한 면역력을 감소시켜 심각한 부작용인 progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML)를 유발할 수 있다.\n...\nVDZ는 α4β7 integrin에 대한 IgG1 인간화 단클론 항체로 장에만 특이적으로 발현되는 α4β7과 MAdCAM-1의 작용을 차단하여 항염증 효과를 나타내는 약제이다.\n먼저 개발된 natalizumab과 차별점은 장내 림프구의 이동만 선택적으로 차단한다는 점으로 중추신경계에는 영향이 없다.^33^\n...\nCarotegrast methyl은 α4를 억제함으로써 α4β7, α4β1을 차단하는 경구 소분자 약제이다.^65^ 2상 b 임상 연구에서 102명의 중등증 궤양성 대장염 환자를 대상으로 위약 대비 8주째 높은 임상 관해 및 점막 치유율을 보여줬을 뿐 아니라 PML과 같은 심각한 부작용의 발생은 없었다.", "date": "2024-08-25", "last_updated": "2026-01-07"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Anti-integrin therapy for inflammatory bowel disease - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5937204/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Anti-integrin therapy blocks the action of integrin on the surface of circulating immune cells and endothelial cell adhesion molecules, thereby inhibiting the interactions between leukocytes and intestinal blood vessels.\nNatalizumab, which acts on α4-integrin, was the first such drug to be approved for Crohn’s disease, but its use is limited due to the risk of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy.\nVedolizumab produces few systemic adverse effects because it acts on gut-trophic α4β7 integrin, and has been approved and is being used to treat IBD.\n...\nNewer anti-integrin drugs that act on different integrins-related targets, such as AJM300, abrilumab, etrolizumab, and PF-00547659 have also been developed and are in clinical trials.\n...\n|Natalizumab|Humanized IgG~4~ mAb|α4-integrin|*i.v*.|ENCORE|Induction and maintenance in CD|\n|AJM300|Small molecule|α4-integrin|Oral|Phase IIa|Induction in UC|\n...\nNatalizumab is a chimeric recombinant human IgG4 antibody that targets the α4 subunit in α4β7 and α4β1 integrins on leukocytes.\nα4β1 integrin interacts with VCAM-1.\n...\nThus, natalizumab has been approved by the United States.\nFDA only in moderate-to-severe CD patients who did not respond to or were intolerant of conventional treatment or TNF inhibitor therapy; it has not been approved for use in Europe.\n...\nDespite safety issues for natalizumab, the oral α4 integrin antagonist AJM300 was developed and evaluated for use in UC.\n...\nwhen AJM300 960 mg or placebo was administered 3 times per day, the primary end point, which was the rate of clinical response (defined as a decrease of at least 3 points, and at least 30% compared to baseline, in the complete Mayo score, as well as a decrease of at\nleast 1 point for the rectal bleeding or an absolute rectal bleeding subscore of 1 point or less) at week 8, was significantly higher in the AJM300 group, at 62.7%, than in the placebo group, at 25.5% (*P* = 0.0002)[20].\n...\nIn this clinical study, serious adverse events did not occur, and adverse events were mild and self-limiting.\nHowever, considering that AJM300 shares the mechanism of natalizumab, and the number of subjects in this trial was small, and the study period was short, there are concerns about its practicality as a therapeutic drug.\nNevertheless, the duration of effect for AJM300 is very short compared to that of natalizumab, and since it is an oral formulation, there is some expectation that it may cause fewer systemic adverse events.\n...\nThe main difference between natalizumab and VDZ is that natalizumab inhibits leukocyte trafficking in multiple organs, including the brain, whereas VDZ acts specifically only on gut-trophic α4β7 heterodimers, and therefore, inhibits lymphocyte trafficking selectively in the intestine.", "date": "2018-05-07", "last_updated": "2026-08-20"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral treatment with a novel small molecule alpha 4 integrin antagonist, AJM300, prevents the development of experimental colitis in mice☆", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/7/11/e533/430319?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "*Results:* HCA2969 selectively inhibited the *in vitro* binding of α4 integrin (α4β7/α4β1) to the cell adhesion molecules.\nOral treatment with AJM300 dose-dependently inhibited lymphocyte homing to Peyer's patches and increased the peripheral lymphocyte count in the same dose range.\nAJM300 dose-dependently prevented the development of experimental colitis in mice.\n...\nThe maximum efficacy of AJM300 (1% diet) was comparable to that achieved by the saturated α4 integrin blockade with antibody.\n...\n9 In addition, α4 integrin blockade has been validated as a therapeutic approach for IBD in clinical studies with anti-α4 integrin antibody, natalizumab 10, 11 or anti-α4β7 integrin antibody, vedolizumab.\n...\nIn addition, the efficacy of the 1% AJM300M1 was comparable with anti-α4 integrin monoclonal antibody (PS/2) treatment at a dose of 10 mg/kg administered twice weekly (Fig.\n...\nThe maximum efficacy of AJM300 was comparable to that of the saturated α4 integrin blockade achieved by antibody treatment.\nThis study demonstrated that HCA2969, the active metabolite of AJM300, was a specific, dual α4β1/α4β7 integrin antagonist.\n...\n37 Whereas, the blockade of α4 integrin (α4β1/α4β7) leads to the inhibition of the lymphocyte migration mediated by the binding to MAdCAM-1 as well as VCAM-1.\nIndeed, an α4 integrin blockade with antibody (natalizumab) has successfully demonstrated the clinical benefit for the patients with multiple sclerosis (MS) 38, 39 as well as IBD.\n10, 11 Therefore AJM300, which has the same pleiotropic target as natalizumab and not gut-specific, could be effective for these diseases.\n...\nIn conclusion, these results suggest that oral treatment with the small molecule α4 integrin antagonist, AJM300 prevented the development of colitis and that its efficacy was comparable to that of the antibody to α4 integrin.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Frontiers | Frontiers in Drug Research and Development for Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2017.00400/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "**Natalizumab** is a recombinant humanized anti-α4 subunit monoclonal antibody belonging to the class of IgG4κ, for i.v. use, approved for the treatment of patients with highly active relapsing remitting MS who did not get any benefit from at least one disease modifying treatment, and CD, as second line therapy in the USA.\nBy binding to the integrins α 4β 1 and α 4β 7 on lymphocyte plasma membrane, natalizumab inhibits lymphocyte homing to most organs and tissues.\n...\nAnother drug in clinical development that targets a component of the integrin family is\n**AJM300** (Table 1).\nSimilar to natalizumab, it binds to α 4 subunit, inhibiting the interactions of α 4β 1 with VCAM-1 and α 4β 7 with MAdCAM-1 and VCAM-1.\nHowever, differently from natalizumab, it is not a monoclonal antibody, but a small molecule, suitable for oral administration.", "date": "2017-06-23", "last_updated": "2025-04-23"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "AJM300 (carotegrast methyl), an oral antagonist of Î±4- ...", "url": "https://www.darmzentrum-bern.ch/fileadmin/darmzentrum/Education/Journal_Club/2022/JC-22-04-27_AJM300__carotegrast_methyl___an_oral_antagonist_of.pdf", "domain": "darmzentrum-bern.ch", "snippet": "Background AJM300 is an oral, small-molecule α4-integrin antagonist.\n...\nAJM300 (carotegrast methyl) is a small-molecule α4-integrin antagonist, developed as an induction treat­ ment for patients with moderate ulcerative colitis.10 The\nactive \nmetabolite \nof\nAJM300 \ninhibits \nleukocyte \nextravasation into inflammatory sites by blocking the interaction of α4β1 or α4β7 integrins and their receptors,\nVCAM-1 and MAd-CAM-1.10 AJM300 shares the same mechanisms of action as natalizumab,11 which has not been used in patients with ulcerative colitis, but is approved for the treatment of Crohn’s disease in the USA.\nNatalizumab is associated with a risk of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), a rare oppor­ tunistic brain infection caused by John Cunningham virus\n(JCV).\n...\nUnlike biologicals, AJM300 is not immunogenic, and intermittent or cyclic therapy can be considered in patients who respond to induction therapy with this drug.\n...\nIn conclusion, oral administration of the small-molecule α4-integrin antagonist AJM300 induced clinical response and endoscopic remission in patients with moderately active ulcerative colitis who had an inadequate response or intolerance to mesalazine.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-24"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "The Expanding Therapeutic Armamentarium for Inflammatory Bowel Disease: How to Choose the Right Drug[s] for Our Patients?", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/12/1/105/4093864?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "However, two new classes of biologic drugs, the integrin-antagonists for both UC and CD, and the interleukin [IL]-12/23-antagonist ustekinumab for CD, are now available.\n...\n4 Anti-adhesion molecules antagonise this interaction by targeting either a shared epitope of the α4β7 integrin heterodimer [vedolizumab, Takeda; AMG 181, Amgen] or either of its individual subunits: α4 in the case of natalizumab [Biogen] and AJM300 [Ajinomoto Co.\nInc.]; and β7 for etrolizumab [Genentech].\nNatalizumab and AJM300 also target α4β1, the ligand of vascular cell adhesion molecule [VCAM]-1, whereas etrolizumab also targets αEβ7, preventing the latter integrin from binding E-cadherin, which is expressed on epithelial cells.\n...\nFinally, AJM300 was evaluated at a dose regimen of 960 mg three times daily for 8 weeks, in a phase 2 study including 102 patients with moderately active UC.\n10 Rates of clinical remission [defined as a total Mayo Clinic score < 3 with no subscore > 1] and mucosal healing [defined as a Mayo Clinic endoscopic subscore ≤ 1] were 23.5% vs 3.9% [ *p* = 0.0099] and 58.8% vs 29.4% [ *p* = 0.0014] in the AJM300 group vs the placebo group, respectively.\nEndoscopic improvement was accompanied by significant histological improvement as evaluated by the Riley score.\n...\nThe major safety concern of nonselective anti-integrin agents such as natalizumab and AJM300 is their interference with T cell trafficking to the central nervous system as a consequence of blocking interactions between α4β1 positive lymphocytes and VCAM-1, which is expressed on brain endothelial cells.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "2.4. Integrin αeβ7 (cd103)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6973243/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Natalizumab targets the α4 integrin and thus inhibits the interaction of α4β1 or α4β7 with VCAM‐1 or MAdCAM‐1, respectively.\nVedolizumab targets the α4β7 subunit and inhibits its interactions with MAdCAM‐1.\nEtrolizumab targets the β7 integrin and inhibits the interaction of α4β7 and αEβ7 with MAdCAM‐1 or E‐cadherin, respectively.\n...\n|Natalizumab|α4|Anti‐integrin monoclonal antibody|In the market (limited use)|Biogen|102, 103, 104|\n|Vedolizumab|α4β7|Anti‐integrin monoclonal antibody|In the market|Takeda|109, 110|\n|Etrolizumab|α4β7; αEβ7|Anti‐integrin monoclonal antibody|Phase 3|Genentech|130, 131, 132, 133|\n|AJM300|α4|Small molecule antagonist|Phase 2a|Ajinomoto Pharmaceuticals|105|\n...\nThe use of small molecule α4 antagonists has continued to be of interest for the treatment of IBD.\nThe orally active small molecule α4 antagonist AJM300 is one such agent currently in development.105 In a double‐blind, placebo‐controlled, phase 2a study in patients with moderately active UC, AJM300 improved all measured indices of IBD compared with placebo, including increased rates of clinical remission and mucosal healing, with no reported serious adverse events.105\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized antibody which binds to α4β7 and was shown to specifically inhibit the interaction between α4β*7* integrin and its ligands, MAdCAM‐1 and fibronectin (Table 1).106 Given that MAdCAM‐1 expression is largely restricted to the intestine, the inhibitory effects of vedolizumab are regarded as gut selective.", "date": "2019-06-19", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Treatment Options in Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Janus Kinases, Stem Cells, and More", "url": "https://karger.com/dig/article/101/Suppl.%201/69/103670/Emerging-Treatment-Options-in-Inflammatory-Bowel", "domain": "karger.com", "snippet": "The α4β7 integrin inhibitor vedolizumab is an established treatment in IBD, and long-term data of pivotal studies are now available.\nAdditional molecules with therapeutic potential are α4β7-specific abrilumab, β7-specific etrolizumab, and the α4-specific small molecule AJM300.\n...\nNatalizumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting the integrin-α4 subunit, is an effective treatment of IBD [63].\nHowever, since the integrin-α4 subunit is also critical for α4β1-dependent central nervous system homing of lymphocytes, natalizumab treatment can be complicated by progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) due to JC virus reactivation [64].\nTherefore, natalizumab is only rarely, if ever, used for IBD treatment and only in patients with JC-negative serology or with a very strict surveillance framework [65, 66].\n...\nInhibition of the integrin-α4 subunit is also therapeutically useful since AJM300, a small-molecule integrin-α4 inhibitor, induced a response in 63% of patients versus 26% with placebo at week 8 in a randomized controlled study with 102 UC patients [76].", "date": "2020-09-29", "last_updated": "2026-09-05"}], "raw": {"id": "f40ca2b4-d019-4f98-84be-8bd981bae7e6"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:38+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q012", "objective": "Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab pivotal, registrational, or Phase 3 trial in Unresectable or Metastatic Hepatocellular Carcinoma: planned or initiated status and expected start date", "anchors": ["Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775", "Lenvatinib", "Nofazinlimab", "NCT04194775"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q012-1", "query": "Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775 first patient enrollment date", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "Lenvatinib + Nofazinlimab NCT04194775 first patient enrollment date", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 4973.9, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma", "url": "https://www.cancerindex.io/trial/NCT04194775", "domain": "cancerindex.io", "snippet": "NCT04194775CI-TRIAL-00119920active not recruitingPhase 3Results postedClinicalTrials.gov clinicaltrialsProvenance- **Source**\n...\n### Dates and enrollment\n- **Start**\nDec 13, 2019\n- **Primary completion**\nJun 18, 2025\n- **Completion**\nSep 30, 2026\n- **First posted**\nDec 11, 2019\n- **Last update posted**\nJul 30, 2026\n- **Results first posted**\nJul 30, 2026\n- **Enrollment**\n534 (actual)", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-19"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in... | Clinical Trial", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/clinical-trial/f021ad7c4cfbc551/nct04194775-nofazinlimab-lenvatinib-advanced-hepatocellular-carcinoma", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "CStone Pharmaceuticals125 sites in 6 countries534 enrolledStarted: December 13, 2019Last updated: July 30, 2026\n...\n534\n...\n- **Primary Endpoint**\n...\n534 started · 0 completed", "date": "2026-07-30", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Liver Epithelial Neoplasm", "url": "https://www.ca