c�ԅ;�#6�m�f5�k���?� ����p��_�D�t(����I�b��X� �̫w��\\?��lQ5J�y ��sͭ��Ȟ�:M�=��%,KB� ÇvW2�t*���@_�@O��$��y�7r d>r�$S��2G����uhM��ā�+FϩOJP@�\\��Ӗ-��4 �� ��`u(��Sp��& �hr��I���70�4�s������e&.UPR�x^�l9�a�8����P����hH)��+���…", "url": "https://www.korea.kr/common/download.do?fileId=198564453&tblKey=GMN", "domain": "korea.kr", "snippet": "RS6��B�H�����K,��7 �HQS�Ps��*�n5p��!��c", "date": "2026-10-02", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Upcoming Revolutionary Paths in Preclinical Modeling of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6987422/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "PDTXs are made by transplanting a piece of patient's tumor tissue derived from surgical resection or from tumor biopsies in immune-deficient mice.\n...\nImportantly, CRISPR/Cas9 technology permitted the creation of syngeneic and humanized mice (50).\n...\nSyngeneic/allografts transplant models are developed through the transplanting of cancer cells or solid tumors derived from the same genetic strain of mice (50).\nThis method prevents the rejection of the transplant by the host's immune system, hence avoiding the need for immune-compromised mice, and allows the possibility of investigating immune-therapies in these models.\nBut the major shortcoming of syngeneic mice is that the tumor cells are rodent and therefore doesn't fully mimic human PDAC (50).\nSyngeneic mice were successful platforms for identifying the currently approved immunotherapies such as checkpoint blockers including anti-CTLA4 (52), anti-PD-1 and anti-PDL-1 (53).\n...\nIn 2015, the first human and murine PDAC organoid model was established; it recapitulated successfully the physiological and morphological similarities to normal and tumor tissues from mice and human.", "date": "2020-01-22", "last_updated": "2025-05-30"}], "raw": {"id": "119e21ae-44fb-421d-b92e-08e0d374e442"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:38:10+00:00"}
