", "url": "https://www.creative-biolabs.com/blog/adc/hyaluronidase-conjugated-liposomes-may-effectively-target-pancreatic-cancer/", "domain": "creative-biolabs.com", "snippet": "The research team developed an efficient drug delivery system: quercetin was encapsulated into liposomes, and hyaluronidase (HAase) was conjugated to the surface of the liposomes.\nThis novel liposome was named HQL.\n...\nIn vitro cell experiments demonstrated that HQL had a significant proliferation-inhibiting effect on pancreatic cancer cells (PANC-1 and Miapaca-2), with an IC50 value lower than that of quercetin and other controls.\n...\nIn experiments with 3D-cultured PANC-1 cells and PDAC organoids, HQL also showed superior ability to inhibit cell proliferation and promote apoptosis compared to other control groups.\nIn in vivo experiments, the researchers established a pancreatic cancer mouse model.\nResults showed that the tumor volume and weight in the HQL treatment group were significantly lower than those in the control group and other treatment groups, reducing tumor volume by 73% and decreasing HA levels in tumor tissues by 95%.\nFluorescent labeling experiments revealed that HQL mainly accumulated in tumor tissues, with less distribution in organs such as the liver.\nThe drug concentration of HQL in the blood could still be maintained at an effective level (50 μg/mL) within 48 hours, while the concentration of orally administered quercetin dropped to below 0.2 μg/mL after 48 hours.\nFurthermore, the in vivo enzymatic activity of HQL peaked at 60 U/mL at 2 hours, then gradually decreased and remained at approximately 20 U/mL within 48 hours.\nSafety evaluation indicated that HQL had no obvious impact on mouse body weight or the morphology and function of major organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney), showing no potential toxic side effects.\nMechanistic studies found that HQL may exert its effects of inhibiting tumor cell growth and promoting apoptosis by downregulating the expression of cell cycle-related proteins (CCNB1, CDK1, and PLK1) as well as apoptosis-related factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, the HQL liposomal drug delivery system developed in this study can effectively degrade HA in the pancreatic cancer tumor microenvironment, enhance drug penetration and delivery efficiency, significantly inhibit pancreatic cancer growth, and has good safety.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "A potential therapeutic target for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022356525397867", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-03"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "HQL-79 | TargetMol", "url": "https://www.targetmol.com/compound/hql-79", "domain": "targetmol.com", "snippet": "For example, if the intended dosage is **10 mg/kg** for animals weighing **20 g** , with a dosing volume of **100 μL** per animal, **10** animals are to be administered, using a formulation of **10%** DMSO+ **40%** PEG300+ **5%** Tween 80+ **45%** Saline/PBS/**ddH~2~O** , the resulting working solution concentration would be **2 mg/mL**.\nStock Solution Preparation:\nDissolve **2 mg** of the compound in **100 μL** DMSO to obtain a stock solution at a concentration of **20 mg/mL** . If the required concentration exceeds the compound's known solubility, please contact us for technical support before proceeding.\nPreparation of the In Vivo Formulation:\n1) Add **100 μL** of the DMSO stock solution to **400 µL** PEG300 and mix thoroughly until the solution becomes clear.\n2) Add **50 µL** Tween 80 and mix well until fully clarified.\n3) Add **450 µL** Saline,PBS or **ddH~2~O** and mix thoroughly until a homogeneous solution is obtained.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Genetics and biology of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://genesdev.cshlp.org/content/20/10/1218.full", "domain": "genesdev.cshlp.org", "snippet": "PDAC commonly arises in the head of the pancreas with infiltration into surrounding tissues including lymphatics, spleen, and peritoneal cavity, and with metastasis to the liver and lungs.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "4-(Benzhydryloxy)-1-[3-(1H-tetraazol-5-YL)propyl]piperidine", "url": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/hql-79", "domain": "pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "- DTXSID50424898\n- DTXCID70375732", "date": "2025-07-12", "last_updated": "2025-07-14"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC) circulating ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8939813/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The portal venous circulation provides a conduit for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) tumor cells to the liver parenchyma sinusoids, a frequent site of metastasis.\nTurbulent flow in the portal circulation promotes retention of PDAC shed circulating tumor cells (CTC) and myeloid-derived immunosuppressor cells (MDSC).\n...\nInteractions between PDAC CTC and M-FB in the portal blood promotes the formation of immunoresistant clusters that enhance CTC proliferation, migration, and survival.\n...\nFACS-isolated portal PDAC CTC and M-FB co-cultured *ex vivo* had increased CTC proliferation, motility, and cluster formation compared to CTC cultured alone.\n...\nWhen CTC and M-FB were first FACS isolated and then co-cultured *ex vivo*, they supported CTC proliferation (**Fig 1E**) and promoted CTC migration (**Fig 1F**) compared to CTC cultured alone.\n...\nBy using a 100 micron nozzle in FACS isolation prior to culture, we found clusters containing portal CTC and M-FB pre-existing *in vivo* (**Fig 1G**).", "date": "2022-03-22", "last_updated": "2026-05-19"}], "raw": {"id": "93968035-09ec-4757-96ed-752473c65871"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:39:14+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q027", "objective": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["HQL-79"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q027-2", "query": "HQL-79 monotherapy PDAC xenograft", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HQL-79 monotherapy PDAC xenograft", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 496.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40127723/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Hence, we developed an efficient drug delivery system by encapsulation of Que. into liposomes and conjugation with hyaluronidase (HAase) at liposome surface, termed as HQL.\nIn the presence of HAase, HQL were predominantly accumulated at tumor with enhanced permeability and retention effect.\nTreatment of xenografted tumor mice with HQL gave rise to suppressed tumor growth, while no toxic effects were observed in mice.\nHQL demonstrated the strong ability to inhibit cell proliferation, promote cell apoptosis, and induce arrest at G2/M cell cycle in pancreatic cancer lines, three-dimensional cultured cell spheroids and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC)-derived organoids.\nMechanistically, HQL downregulated expression of cell cycle-associated protein (CCNB1, CDK1 and PLK1) and cell apoptosis-associated factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, HQL degraded HA in the tumor microenvironment to enhance nano-particle penetration and inhibited tumor cell growth, eliciting efficacy of anti-tumor therapy.\nThereof, HQL may provide a novel efficient drug delivery approach for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer.", "date": "2025-06-10", "last_updated": "2025-09-17"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Figure 7.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/figure/fig7/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "HPGDS inhibition overcomes immunotherapy resistance and is effective in human settings.\nTumor growth (), tumor weight (), and representative images () of ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ genetically engineered mice (GEM) treated with vehicle, HQL-79 15 mg/kg twice daily, αPD1, or the combination of HQL-79 and αPD1 ( = 6).\n...\n). and Tumor weight () and representative pictures () of KPC FC1245 PDAC-bearing mice treated with vehicle/IgG, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination with αPD1 ( = 11–16).\n...\nBlocking HPGDS induces vascular normalization and immune surveillance, overcoming ICB resistance ().", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-08"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "A potential therapeutic target for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022356525397867", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-03"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "HQL-79, MedChemExpress - Cell Culture Media, Supplements, and Reagents, Cell Culture Reagents, Antibiotics and Supplements", "url": "https://www.fishersci.fi/shop/products/hql-79-medchemexpress/16466887", "domain": "fishersci.fi", "snippet": "MedChemExpress HQL-79, a potent, selective and orally active human hematopoietic prostaglandin D synthase (H-PGDS) inhibitor, highly selectively inhibits the synthesis of PGD2, and acts as an anti-allergic agent, with a Kd of 0.8 μM and an IC50 of 6 μM.\nShows no obvious effect on COX-1, COX-2, m-PGES, or L-PGDS.", "date": "2025-05-31", "last_updated": "2025-05-31"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 3.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12562412/table/biomolecules-15-01477-t003/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "| |DM4 (ravtansine)|Tubulin inhibitor (cleavable linker, allows for bystander effect)|anetumab ravtansine (anti-Mesothelin)|Stable disease in subset of PDAC patients; limited responses but synergy with checkpoint inhibitors + gemcitabine (100% disease control in one cohort).|[78,79,80]|", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-10-30"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Anti-Tumour Treatment", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305737224001178", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-15"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety, pharmacokinetics, and antitumor activity of the anti ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0923753422000035", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-26"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "4-(Benzhydryloxy)-1-[3-(1H-tetraazol-5-YL)propyl]piperidine", "url": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/hql-79", "domain": "pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "#### 3.2.1 Drugs\nPharmaceuticals -> Listed in ZINC15", "date": "2025-07-12", "last_updated": "2025-07-14"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "HQL-79 | TargetMol", "url": "https://www.targetmol.com/compound/hql-79", "domain": "targetmol.com", "snippet": "HQL-79 is a selective and orally active human hematopoietic prostaglandin D synthase (H-PGDS) inhibitor.\nIt inhibits the synthesis of PGD2 and acts as an anti-allergic agent (Kd: 0.8 μM and IC~50~: 6 μM).\nIt also shows no obvious effect on COX-1, COX-2, m-PGES, or L-PGDS.\n...\n|Targets & IC50|HPGDS:(kd)0.8 μM, HPGDS:6 μM|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "untitled", "url": "https://gut.bmj.com/content/gutjnl/66/1/168.full.pdf", "domain": "gut.bmj.com", "snippet": "combination with gemcitabine\nInferior compared with gemcitabine monotherapy with decreased quality of life", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-03-13"}], "raw": {"id": "e3d5e6b3-72b2-4968-a4ea-e2245450d3d2"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:39:14+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q025", "objective": "Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanism of action as HEMAY007, targeting TNF-α Tumor Necrosis Factor Alpha", "anchors": ["HEMAY007"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q025-3", "query": "HEMAY007 TNF-alpha ulcerative colitis clinical development status", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HEMAY007 TNF-alpha ulcerative colitis clinical development status", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 16396.9, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 1.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/table/T1/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties|\n|TNF-α/OX40L inhibitor|SAR442970|Bispecific pentavalent Nanobody targeting TNF-α and OX40L|NCT06975722|Phase 2b|Started July 2025; estimated completion Dec 2026|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-14"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] 概覽", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2026/108622/a133802/sehk26060801670_c.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "Hemay007*(10)(11)\nPDE4B/\nPDE4\nMufemilast**\n...\n主管機關\n...\n聯合治療\n...\n里程碑╱目前狀況\n...\nII期\n...\n國家藥監局\n單藥治療\n我們計劃於2026年第三季度完成II期臨床試驗\n...\n我們正在中國進行I期及Ib期臨床試驗，\n並於2023年6月獲FDA批准在美國進行臨床試驗\n我們計劃於2026年第二季度啟動I期臨床試驗。\n我們計劃於2026年完成II期臨床試驗\n...\n(10) \nHemay007的兩項II期臨床試驗均基於同一項I期臨床試驗(Hemay007AU01)所得的結果而獲得批准。\n...\nHemay007是通過多種藥理功能亞結構組合化合物庫篩選而來，是一種TNF-α小\n分子調節劑。在自發性UC猴模型中，Hemay007減少了受試者結腸中的潰瘍數量，治\n療後對腸道進行病理檢查發現，結腸組織幾乎恢復到正常狀態。截至最後實際可行日\n期，我們已完成單藥治療UC的II期臨床試驗。我們正在進行類風濕關節炎(RA)的II期\n臨床試驗。就Hemay007用於UC的II期臨床試驗第12週而言，無論是透過中央實驗室\n評估還是主要研究者(PI)評估，所有組別在FAS及PPS人群中的臨床反應率均未顯示出\n統計學上的顯著差異（所有比較P>0.05）。第12週的臨床緩解率在治療組與安慰劑組之\n間亦相當，未觀察到統計學上的顯著差異。目前，我們並無就UC的下一步制定正式\n時間表。我們計劃優先進行針對Mufemilast的臨床試驗開發，因為我們的財務資源有\n...\n我們已於2026年2月獲得國家藥監局批准開展Hemay5087的I期臨床試驗，並正在", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-24"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline Includes Seven Small Molecule Drug Candidates", "url": "https://news.futunn.com/en/post/74292485/ipo-news-he-mei-pharmaceuticals-re-files-application-with-hkex", "domain": "news.futunn.com", "snippet": "The company has completed a Phase II trial for ankylosing spondylitis (AS), and additional trials for ulcerative colitis (UC), atopic dermatitis (AD), psoriatic arthritis (PsA), Crohn’s disease (CD), and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) are either ongoing or planned.\n...\nHemay007\nHemay007 was identified through screening a compound library combining multiple pharmacologically functional substructures and is a small-molecule modulator of TNF-α.\nIn a spontaneous UC monkey model, Hemay007 reduced the number of ulcers in the colon of subjects; histopathological examination of the gut post-treatment revealed that colonic tissue had nearly returned to a normal state.\nAs of the latest practicable date, the company has completed a Phase II clinical trial of Hemay007 as monotherapy for UC.\nA Phase II clinical trial for rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is currently underway.\nFor the Week 12 primary endpoint of the Phase II trial in UC, neither central laboratory assessment nor principal investigator (PI) evaluation demonstrated statistically significant differences in clinical response rates across all groups in either the full analysis set (FAS) or per-protocol set (PPS) populations (all comparisons: p > 0.05).\nSimilarly, no statistically significant difference was observed in clinical remission rates between the treatment and placebo groups at Week 12.\nCurrently, the company has not established a formal timeline for next steps in UC development.\nGiven limited financial resources, the company intends to prioritize clinical development of Mufemilast.\nConsequently, as of the latest practicable date, the issuance of the Phase II RA clinical trial report will be placed on hold.", "date": "2026-06-09", "last_updated": "2026-07-26"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Hemay007 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c6cef31360b74c8b9fd3d4c563740a9e", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Inactive Indication\n...\nDrug Highest Phase\nPhase 2\n...\nNCT05247216\nCompleted\nPhase 2\n...\nNCT03977480\nTerminated\nPhase 2\nA Multicenter, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo Parallel Controlled Clinical Study on the Effecacy and Safety of Different Dosing Regimens of Hemay007 in Patients With Active Ulcerative Colitis\nThis study adopts a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, low-medium-high dose group and placebo parallel controlled clinical study design.\nAfter screening, patients with active ulcerative colitis who meet the inclusion criteria and do not meet the exclusion criteria will be randomized by 1:1:1:1 to Hemay007 400 mg BID group, 800 mg QD group, 600 mg BID group or placebo group, with proposed 72 cases in each group.\nAfter 12 weeks of double-blind inductive treatment period, the patients will enter the Hemay007 open treatment period of 12 weeks when Hemay007 600 mg BID will be used as the medication regimen.\nAll randomized subjects who have received the investigational drug should be subjected to a 4-week observation after the end of treatment.\nStart Date\n13 Aug 2019\nSponsor / Collaborator\nTianjin Hemei Biological Technology Co.,Ltd.\nCTR20182150\nTerminated\nPhase 2\nHemay007不同剂量给药方案用于活动性溃疡性结肠炎患者有效性和安全性多中心、随机、双盲、安慰剂平行对照临床研究\n[Translation]\nA multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical study on the efficacy and safety of different dosing regimens of Hemay007 in patients with active ulcerative colitis\n...\nPrimary objective: To explore the efficacy and dose-effect relationship of Hemay007 at different dosing regimens for active ulcerative colitis compared with placebo.\nSecondary objective: To evaluate the safety of Hemay007 at different dosing regimens for active ulcerative colitis compared with placebo.\nTo observe the population pharmacokinetic characteristics of Hemay007 in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nStart Date\n13 Aug 2019\nSponsor / Collaborator\nXiajiang Hemei Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd.\n...\n10\n...\nDiscovery\nChina\nTianjin Hemay Pharmaceutical Technology Co., Ltd.\n13 Aug 2019", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-30"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "a pipeline of novel drugs for UC management", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/efc2/a31ee078832397a8583aedd937aa7a780012.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "The therapeutic pipeline for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis currently encompasses over 100 investigational agents in Phase II and III clinical development.\n...\ninhibitor\nSAR442970\nBispecific pentavalent Nanobody targeting\nTNF-a and OX40L\nNCT06975722\nPhase 2b\nStarted July 2025; estimated completion Dec 2026\n...\nThe following section reviews investigational TNF pathway inhibitors currently in Phase II and III clinical development for moderate to severe UC.\n2.3.1 Hemay007\nA Phase II study (NCT03977480) evaluated Hemay007, a small molecule that acts as a TNF-a regulator, using a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled design with four treatment arms comparing different dosing regimens (400 mg\nBID, 800 mg QD, 600 mg BID, and placebo) over 12 weeks followed by an open-label extension.\nThe primary outcome measured clinical response defined as MS reduction of ≥3 points and ≥30% from baseline with RBS improvement, while inclusion criteria required patients aged 18–70 years with active UC (MS ≥4,\nMES ≥2) who had treatment failure or intolerance to standard therapies including 5-ASA, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or anti-TNF agents.\nThe study was terminated early due to recruitment difficulties, completing in August 2022 with only 70 of the planned 288 patients enrolled, and no efficacy or safety results have been posted on ClinicalTrials.gov.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-21"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Hemay-007 | MedPath", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/drug/d8a792d6a04f1b1f/hemay-007", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "## Clinical Trials\n|Title|Posted|Study ID|Phase|Status|Sponsor|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Phase II Study of Hemay007 in Patients With Active Ulcerative Colitis|2019/06/06|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated|Tianjin Hemay Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd|\n|PhaseⅠFirst-in-Human Study of Hemay007 in Healthy Volunteers|2015/11/11|NCT02603185|Phase 1|UNKNOWN|Hemay Pharmaceutical PTY.\nLTD.|\n## FDA Drug Approvals\n|Approved Product|Manufacturer|NDC Code|Route|Strength|Effective Date|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|No FDA approvals found for this drug.|\n...\n|Approved Product|Authorization Holder|Status|Issued Date|\n...\n|No EMA approvals found for this drug.|\n...\n|No HSA approvals found for this drug.|", "date": "2019-06-06", "last_updated": "2025-10-18"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties|\n|TNF-α/OX40L inhibitor|SAR442970|Bispecific pentavalent Nanobody targeting TNF-α and OX40L|NCT06975722|Phase 2b|Started July 2025; estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\nThe study led to the current Phase 3 trial (NCT05181137)) which began in November 2021 and, based on its estimated completion date, is anticipated to be ongoing; however, no interim results have been publicly reported to date.\n...\nIt is being tested in a Phase 2, multicenter, placebo-controlled, double-blind RCT for moderately to severely active UC in 207 participants aged 18–75 who have inadequate response to conventional, biologic, or advanced therapies, with the primary outcome measuring clinical remission at week 12 using mMS, currently recruiting with estimated completion in August 2027 (NCT06254950).\n...\nA Phase II study (NCT03977480) evaluated Hemay007, a small molecule that acts as a TNF-α regulator, using a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled design with four treatment arms comparing different dosing regimens (400 mg BID, 800 mg QD, 600 mg BID, and placebo) over 12 weeks followed by an open-label extension.\nThe primary outcome measured clinical response defined as MS reduction of ≥3 points and ≥30% from baseline with RBS improvement, while inclusion criteria required patients aged 18–70 years with active UC (MS ≥4, MES ≥2) who had treatment failure or intolerance to standard therapies including 5-ASA, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or anti-TNF agents.\nThe study was terminated early due to recruitment difficulties, completing in August 2022 with only 70 of the planned 288 patients enrolled, and no efficacy or safety results have been posted on ClinicalTrials.gov.\n...\nThe study began July 2025 with primary completion estimated December 2026 and study completion October 2029, including 16-week induction, maintenance period, and optional long-term extension up to 104 weeks for eligible participants.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Xiajiang Hemei Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd.", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/defdc02b973c4a2db52adc812c16492b", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Hemay007\nTarget\nTNF-α\nMechanism\nTNF-α inhibitors\nActive Org.\nXiajiang Hemei Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd.\n[+1]\n...\nActive Indication\nRheumatoid Arthritis\nInactive Indication\nColitis, Ulcerative\n[+1]\nDrug Highest Phase\nPhase 2\nFirst Approval Ctry.\n/ Loc.\nFirst Approval Date\n...\nClinical Trials associated with Xiajiang Hemei Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd.\nCTR20181976\nSuspended\nPhase 1\n...\nHemay007\n( TNF-α )\nColitis, Ulcerative\nMore\nDiscontinued", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-30"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Database of Drug Development for Rare Diseases - DDrare", "url": "http://raredis.nibiohn.go.jp/ddrare_Mar2022/cgi-bin/disease_who_e.cgi?id=97&query=%22Hemay007%22", "domain": "raredis.nibiohn.go.jp", "snippet": "|1|NCT03977480 (ClinicalTrials.gov)|August 13, 2019|27/4/2019|Phase II Study of Hemay007 in Patients With Active Ulcerative Colitis|A Multicenter, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo Parallel Controlled Clinical Study on the Effecacy and Safety of Different Dosing Regimens of Hemay007 in Patients With Active Ulcerative Colitis|Ulcerative Colitis|Drug: Hemay007;Drug: Placebo|Tianjin Hemay Pharmaceutical Co.,Ltd|NULL|Recruiting|18 Years|70 Years|All|70|Phase 2|China|", "date": "2019-08-13", "last_updated": "2025-11-01"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Hemay Pharmaceutical Pty Ltd - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/89e96363c322c7183ab587d4cba40b4c", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "TNF-α\n...\nActive Indication\nRheumatoid Arthritis\nInactive Indication\nColitis, Ulcerative\n[+1]\nDrug Highest Phase\nPhase 2\nFirst Approval Ctry.\n/ Loc.\nFirst Approval Date\n2\nClinical Trials associated with Hemay Pharmaceutical Pty Ltd\nNCT05941247\nCompleted\nPhase 1\nA Phase I, Single-arm, Open-label, Ethnobridging Study to Evaluate the Safety, Tolerability, and Pharmacokinetics (PK) of Single and Multiple Oral Doses of Hemay005 Tablets in Healthy Caucasian Adult Volunteers\nThe purpose of the study is to assess the safety and tolerability of single and multiple oral doses of Hemay005 tablets in healthy Caucasian adult volunteers.\nStart Date\n17 Jul 2023\n...\nNCT02603185\nUnknown status\nPhase 1\nA First-in-human, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled, Single and Multiple Ascending Oral Dose Study to Assess the Safety, Tolerability and Pharmacokinetics of Hemay007\nThis phase I study designed in 3 parts is a randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential ascending-dose study of healthy volunteers.\nThe safety, tolerability and pharmacokinetics of ascending single and multiple dose of Hemay007 will be assessed in Part 1 and Part 3, respectively.\nFood effect following a single oral dose will be evaluated in Part 2.\nStart Date\n09 Jan 2017\nSponsor / Collaborator\nHemay Pharmaceutical Pty Ltd\n...\nPipeline Snapshot as of 18 Sep 2025\n...\nGlobal Highest Phase\nHemay007\n( TNF-α )\nInflammatory Bowel Diseases", "date": "2025-09-18", "last_updated": "2025-09-18"}], "raw": {"id": "08abc38b-aba8-4425-8a57-4bbf0d3626ae"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:39:14+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q027", "objective": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["HQL-79"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q027-3", "query": "HQL-79 tumour regression animal model", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HQL-79 tumour regression animal model", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 4187.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Activated T Cells Break Tumor Immunosuppression by Macrophage ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "When monitoring the growth of orthotopic YUMM1.7 melanomas, tumor volume, and weight were significantly lower (80% and 60%, respectively) in *Hpgds*^*ΔMø*^ mice compared with Ctrl littermates, with four of nine *Hpgds*^*ΔMø*^ mice displaying tumor rejection (Fig.\n...\nTo translate our genetic results in a clinically relevant setting, we used the HPGDS inhibitor HQL-79 (38) in YUMM 1.7 melanoma-bearing mice.\nSystemic administration of HQL-79 (15 mg/kg twice daily) recapitulated the antitumor phenotype observed in *Hpgds*^ΔMø^ mice (Fig.\n6A–C; Supplementary Fig. S9A and S9B), achieving 78% of tumor growth inhibition with one of six mice undergoing total tumor rejection (Supplementary Fig. S9B).\nThe impairment in tumor growth was accompanied by a shift in TAMs toward antitumoral markers (Fig.\n6D–F) and robust infiltration of CD8^+^ T cells (Supplementary Fig. S9C–S9E).\nLike macrophage-specific *Hpgds* deletion, HQL-79 strongly affected the tumor vasculature by decreasing vessel density and size and by improving pericyte coverage and perfusion (Supplementary Fig.\nS9F–S9L), together with reduced hypoxia (Supplementary Fig. S9M and S9N), cancer cell intravasation into the bloodstream (Fig. 6G), and lung colonization (Fig. 6H).\n...\nHQL-79 was also tested in orthotopic *NRAS*^*Q61 K*^; *Ink4a*^*-/-*^ melanomas, resulting in 80% tumor growth inhibition with two of six mice undergoing total tumor rejection (Fig. 6K–M; Supplementary Fig. S10A and S10B).\n...\nTogether, these data prove that pharmacologic inhibition of HPGDS could provide a promising therapeutic option against melanoma.\n...\nFinally, we took advantage of a spontaneous *Braf*^*V600E;*^*Pten*^*-/-*^ melanoma model (41), induced by the administration of 4-hydroxytamoxifen (4-HT) on the skin of these genetically engineered mice, and treated the mice with HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 plus αPD1.\nHere, neither HPGDS inhibition by HQL-79 nor αPD1 alone affected tumor progression; however, their combination was greatly effective (Fig. 7A–C; Supplementary Fig. S11A–S11D).", "date": "2025-04-07", "last_updated": "2025-09-23"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Figure 7.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/figure/fig7/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "HPGDS inhibition overcomes immunotherapy resistance and is effective in human settings.\nTumor growth (), tumor weight (), and representative images () of ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ genetically engineered mice (GEM) treated with vehicle, HQL-79 15 mg/kg twice daily, αPD1, or the combination of HQL-79 and αPD1 ( = 6).\n...\nHistologic quantification of CD8^+^ T cells at the tumor margin vs. core in ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ GEM treated with vehicle/IgG, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination with αPD1 ( = 6\n). and Tumor weight () and representative pictures () of KPC FC1245 PDAC-bearing mice treated with vehicle/IgG, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination with αPD1 ( = 11–16).\n...\nBlocking HPGDS induces vascular normalization and immune surveillance, overcoming ICB resistance ().", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-08"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Figure 6.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/figure/fig6/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Pharmacologic HPGDS inhibition phenocopies the effect of its genetic deletion in TAMs.\nTumor growth (), tumor weight (), and tumor representative pictures () of YUMM 1.7 CD90.1^+^ melanoma-bearing mice treated by oral gavage with HQL-79, 15 mg/kg BID or with vehicle control ( = 6).\n...\nFACS analysis of YUMM 1.7 CD90.1^+^ cancer cell intravasation into the bloodstream in melanoma-bearing mice treated by oral gavage with HQL-79 or with vehicle control ( = 6).\n...\nmelanoma-bearing mice treated with vehicle or HQL-79 ( = 5–6). and Tumor growth () and tumor weight () of YUMM 1.7 CD90.1^+^ melanoma-bearing Ctrl or ^*ΔMø-ERT2*^ mice treated with vehicle or HQL-79 ( = 4).\n...\nTumor growth (), tumor weight (), and tumor representative pictures () of ^*Q61 K*^; ^*−/−*^ melanoma tumor-bearing mice treated by oral gavage with vehicle or HQL-79 15 mg/kg BID ( = 6).", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-08"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/oncology/fulltext/S2372-7705(22)00151-6", "domain": "cell.com", "snippet": "You've requested a page on a website (www.cell.com) that is on the Cloudflare network.\n...\nPlease try again in a few minutes.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/oncology/fulltext/S2372-7705(22)00001-8", "domain": "cell.com", "snippet": "You've requested a page on a website (www.cell.com) that is on the Cloudflare network.\n...\nPlease try again in a few minutes.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Antitumor agent-79", "url": "https://www.benchchem.com/product/b12399063", "domain": "benchchem.com", "snippet": "**Tumor Regression**: In murine models of breast cancer, treatment with this compound resulted in significant tumor size reduction compared to control groups.\n**Survival Rates**: Studies indicated improved survival rates in treated animals, suggesting that the compound may enhance overall therapeutic outcomes.\n...\nMethodological guidance:\n...\n- Validate findings in\n*in vivo*transplanted tumor models (e.g., murine xenografts) with strict kinetic monitoring of tumor growth rates using exponential or S-shaped growth functions .", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-04-15"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Buy 1-(8-hydroxy-4-methyl-3,4-dihydro-2H-quinolin-1-yl)-2-methylsulfonylethanone", "url": "https://www.smolecule.com/products/s7484455", "domain": "smolecule.com", "snippet": "In Stock\n* This item is exclusively intended for research purposes and is not designed for human therapeutic applications or veterinary use.\n...\n1-(8-hydroxy-4-methyl-3,4-dihydro-2H-quinolin-1-yl)-2-methylsulfonylethanone, also known as HQL-79, is a small molecule that belongs to the family of quinoline-based inhibitors of the enzyme poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase-1 (PARP-1).\n...\nHQL-79 is a potent and selective PARP-1 inhibitor that has been extensively studied in vitro and in vivo for its pharmacological properties.\n...\nHQL-79 has been shown to exhibit potent PARP-1 inhibitory activity, with an IC50 value of 9 nM in a biochemical assay.\n...\nHQL-79 has been tested in various cellular and animal models of DNA damage and oxidative stress, and has been shown to enhance the cytotoxicity and sensitization of cancer cells to chemotherapy and radiation therapy, as well as to reduce the inflammation and tissue damage in models of arthritis, stroke, and neurodegeneration.\n...\nHQL-79 has been shown to have low toxicity and good safety profiles in in vitro and in vivo studies.\nIt does not induce mutagenicity or genotoxicity in bacterial or mammalian cells, and does not cause significant changes in the vital organs or hematological parameters of rodents or primates.", "date": "2024-01-05", "last_updated": "2025-06-11"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Structural and functional characterization of HQL-79, an orally ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16547010/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "HQL-79 selectively inhibited PGD2 production by H-PGDS-expressing human megakaryocytes and rat mastocytoma cells with an IC50 value of about 100 microm but only marginally affected the production of other prostanoids, suggesting the tight functional engagement between H-PGDS and cyclooxygenase.\nOrally administered HQL-79 (30 mg/kg body weight) inhibited antigen-induced production of PGD2, without affecting the production of PGE2 and PGF2alpha, and ameliorated airway inflammation in wild-type and human H-PGDS-overexpressing mice.", "date": "2006-06-02", "last_updated": "2025-09-09"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Investigating the Anticancer Promise of Synthesized Methylated N-Substituted-4-Hydroxy-2-Quinolone-3-Carboxamides: Biological and Computational Characterization", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42219823/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Clipboard, Search History, and several other advanced features are temporarily unavailable.", "date": "2026-06-13", "last_updated": "2026-06-13"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "FIGURE 4.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12259406/figure/jcpe14180-fig-0004/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Effect oral administration of haematopoietic prostaglandin D synthase (HPGDS) inhibitor HQL‐79 on KK‐A^y^ mice with or without ligatures.\n...\n(D) Quantified alveolar bone loss of each mouse administered HQL‐79 (30 mg/kg) or vehicle ( = 6).\n...\n(K) Prostaglandin D2 (PGD2) levels in the glomeruli of C57BL/6 and KK‐A^y^ mice administered vehicle or HQL‐79 in the presence or absence of ligature ( = 6).", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2026-02-18"}], "raw": {"id": "1002a8cd-e33b-48be-8ffa-e1be98250b47"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:39:18+00:00"}
