{"row_id": "q026", "objective": "BM-013 first regulatory approval or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia", "anchors": ["BM-013"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q026-3", "query": "BM-013 WASP AML clinical development forecast", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "BM-013 WASP AML clinical development forecast", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 2, "latency_ms": 898.5, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "CLINICAL TRIALS", "url": "https://www.amgentrials.com/study/?id=MT103-104", "domain": "amgentrials.com", "snippet": "## CLINICAL TRIALS\n...\n© 1996–2024 Amgen Inc.", "date": "1996-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-05-02"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting multiple signaling pathways: the new approach to acute myeloid leukemia therapy", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41392-020-00361-x", "domain": "nature.com", "snippet": "In October of 2019, bemcentinib received fast-track designation from the FDA for the treatment of elderly patients with R/R AML based on unpublished data supporting its efficacy in phase II trial (NCT03824080).", "date": "2020-12-18", "last_updated": "2020-12-18"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Bimini Biotech company information, funding & investors", "url": "https://app.dealroom.co/companies/bimini_biotech", "domain": "app.dealroom.co", "snippet": "BIMINI Biotech's business model revolves around the development and commercialization of its proprietary portfolio of products, including BM-011, a WASp activator, and BM-013, a WASp inhibitor.\nThese products have shown promising results in treating difficult-to-treat non-Hodgkin lymphomas and leukemias, respectively.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-18"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Acute myeloid leukemia (AML)", "url": "https://www.onkopedia.com/en/onkopedia/guidelines/acute-myeloid-leukemia-aml/@@guideline/html/index.html", "domain": "onkopedia.com", "snippet": "Patients with a higher risk of recurrence should receive allogeneic stem cell transplantation as post-remission therapy if possible.", "date": "2026-05-18", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "2023 Annual Report to Shareholders - Haemonetics Corporation", "url": "https://haemonetics.gcs-web.com/static-files/506876ab-5893-4753-84f4-d043a5b67534", "domain": "haemonetics.gcs-web.com", "snippet": "��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-22"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Inside the Battle Against Acute Myeloid Leukemia - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12939906/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Similarly, pharmacogenomics and epigenomics analysis revealed the efficacy of Menin inhibitors in *KMT2A*-rearranged AML [59].", "date": "2026-02-13", "last_updated": "2026-05-27"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Frontiers | From monoclonals to bispecific T cell engagers: the evolving antibody-based therapy landscape in acute myeloid leukemia", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2026.1803185/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Currently, a phase-I clinical trial is evaluating the combination of flotetuzumab, a CD3xCD123 BTCE, with MGA012, an anti-PD-1 inhibitor, in patients with R/R AML (222).", "date": "2026-06-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Acute Myeloid Leukemia: 2025 Update on Diagnosis, Risk-Stratification, and Management", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ajh.27625", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Alongside revumenib, a multitude of different menin inhibitors are currently in various stages of clinical trial development either as monotherapy or in combination with chemotherapy or HMA + venetoclax.\nThese include ziftomenib, bleximenib [236], BMF-219, DS1594 and DSP 5336.", "date": "2025-05-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "VU Research Portal", "url": "https://research.vu.nl/ws/files/138247870/D%20G%20J%20%20Cucchi%20-%20thesis.pdf", "domain": "research.vu.nl", "snippet": "Twenty-eight agents were steered towards phase III, after three phase II trials on average.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-03-06"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Decoding Leukemic Stem Cells in AML: From Identification to ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12939826/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|Menin inhibitors|Enzomenib, Bleximenib, DS-1954, BMF-219|Menin-KMT2A interaction|LSCs in KMT2A-r/NPM1-mutated AML|Phase I/II clinical trials|\n...\nBeyond approved agents, several additional menin inhibitors (e.g., enzomenib, bleximenib, DS-1954, BMF-219) are under clinical evaluation as monotherapy and in combinations (e.g., venetoclax-based regimens, FLT3 inhibitors, HMAs, and intensive chemotherapy).", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-08-25"}], "raw": {"id": "c7b96133-5f64-4b60-a717-e93da4c27fd2"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:39:44+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q027", "objective": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["HQL-79"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q027-4", "query": "HQL-79 tumour growth inhibition pancreatic cancer", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HQL-79 tumour growth inhibition pancreatic cancer", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 2, "latency_ms": 7994.9, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Phytochemicals as Multitarget Therapeutics in Pancreatic Cancer: Mechanisms, Clinical Evidence, and Translational Challenges", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ptr.70430", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Curcumin, a Jab1 inhibitor, reduces tumor growth.\n...\nQuercetin has shown potential to inhibit tumor cell growth, but its effectiveness is limited by low bioavailability and poor solubility.\nHQL's ability to degrade HA in the tumor microenvironment facilitated NP penetration and contributed to its antitumor efficacy (Sun et al. 2025).\nQuercetin-3-*O*-glucoside may enhance gemcitabine's chemotherapeutic effects and inhibit metastasis, determined by various growth factors in PC (Lee et al. 2016).\n...\nGenistein inhibits tumor growth in PC.", "date": "2026-08-16", "last_updated": "2026-09-26"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40127723/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "# Quercetin liposomes conjugated with hyaluronidase: An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer\n...\nPancreatic cancer characterized with intense hydraulic tissue in tumor extracellular matrix (ECM) resists most of chemotherapeutic drugs.\nIncreased levels of hyaluronic acid (HA) represent the primary component of the hydraulic tissue, rendering tumors protective from drug targeting.\nQuercetin (Que), a natural flavonoid, has the ability to inhibit tumor cell growth in a number of cancers; however, its poor water solubility and low bioavailability largely limit its application in cancer therapy.\nHence, we developed an efficient drug delivery system by encapsulation of Que. into liposomes and conjugation with hyaluronidase (HAase) at liposome surface, termed as HQL.\nIn the presence of HAase, HQL were predominantly accumulated at tumor with enhanced permeability and retention effect.\nTreatment of xenografted tumor mice with HQL gave rise to suppressed tumor growth, while no toxic effects were observed in mice.\nHQL demonstrated the strong ability to inhibit cell proliferation, promote cell apoptosis, and induce arrest at G2/M cell cycle in pancreatic cancer lines, three-dimensional cultured cell spheroids and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC)-derived organoids.\nMechanistically, HQL downregulated expression of cell cycle-associated protein (CCNB1, CDK1 and PLK1) and cell apoptosis-associated factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, HQL degraded HA in the tumor microenvironment to enhance nano-particle penetration and inhibited tumor cell growth, eliciting efficacy of anti-tumor therapy.\nThereof, HQL may provide a novel efficient drug delivery approach for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer.", "date": "2025-06-10", "last_updated": "2025-09-17"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "www.creative-biolabs.com › blog › adcHyaluronidase-Conjugated Liposomes May Effectively Target ...", "url": "https://www.creative-biolabs.com/blog/adc/hyaluronidase-conjugated-liposomes-may-effectively-target-pancreatic-cancer/", "domain": "creative-biolabs.com", "snippet": "Recently, a study published in *J Control Release* entitled “Quercetin liposomes conjugated with hyaluronidase: An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer” has brought new hope for pancreatic cancer treatment.\nThe research team developed an efficient drug delivery system: quercetin was encapsulated into liposomes, and hyaluronidase (HAase) was conjugated to the surface of the liposomes.\nThis novel liposome was named HQL.\n...\nIn vitro experiments showed that HQL could stably exist in 10% fetal bovine serum and plasma for 15 days.\nIts release rate was approximately 30% in an acidic environment (pH = 5.5) and about 20% in a neutral environment (pH = 7.4) within 4 hours, with sustained release for over 50 hours.\n...\nAdditionally, HAase in HQL remained highly active, laying the foundation for degrading HA in the tumor microenvironment.\nIn vitro cell experiments demonstrated that HQL had a significant proliferation-inhibiting effect on pancreatic cancer cells (PANC-1 and Miapaca-2), with an IC50 value lower than that of quercetin and other controls.\nIt had no obvious inhibitory effect on the proliferation of normal pancreatic ductal epithelial cells (HPNE), showing good tumor cell specificity.\nHQL had stronger penetration ability in HA-rich agarose gels.\nIn 2D and 3D cell culture models, it was more effectively taken up by pancreatic cancer cells, promoted cancer cell apoptosis, and induced cell cycle arrest at the G2/M phase.\nIn experiments with 3D-cultured PANC-1 cells and PDAC organoids, HQL also showed superior ability to inhibit cell proliferation and promote apoptosis compared to other control groups.\nIn in vivo experiments, the researchers established a pancreatic cancer mouse model.\nResults showed that the tumor volume and weight in the HQL treatment group were significantly lower than those in the control group and other treatment groups, reducing tumor volume by 73% and decreasing HA levels in tumor tissues by 95%.\nFluorescent labeling experiments revealed that HQL mainly accumulated in tumor tissues, with less distribution in organs such as the liver.\nThe drug concentration of HQL in the blood could still be maintained at an effective level (50 μg/mL) within 48 hours, while the concentration of orally administered quercetin dropped to below 0.2 μg/mL after 48 hours.\nFurthermore, the in vivo enzymatic activity of HQL peaked at 60 U/mL at 2 hours, then gradually decreased and remained at approximately 20 U/mL within 48 hours.\nSafety evaluation indicated that HQL had no obvious impact on mouse body weight or the morphology and function of major organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney), showing no potential toxic side effects.\nMechanistic studies found that HQL may exert its effects of inhibiting tumor cell growth and promoting apoptosis by downregulating the expression of cell cycle-related proteins (CCNB1, CDK1, and PLK1) as well as apoptosis-related factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, the HQL liposomal drug delivery system developed in this study can effectively degrade HA in the pancreatic cancer tumor microenvironment, enhance drug penetration and delivery efficiency, significantly inhibit pancreatic cancer growth, and has good safety.\nIt provides a new and promising drug delivery method for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer, which is expected to bring new hope to pancreatic cancer patients in the future and promote further development in the field of pancreatic cancer treatment.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Buy 1-(8-hydroxy-4-methyl-3,4-dihydro-2H-quinolin-1-yl)-2-methylsulfonylethanone", "url": "https://www.smolecule.com/products/s7484455", "domain": "smolecule.com", "snippet": "In Stock\n* This item is exclusively intended for research purposes and is not designed for human therapeutic applications or veterinary use.\n...\n1-(8-hydroxy-4-methyl-3,4-dihydro-2H-quinolin-1-yl)-2-methylsulfonylethanone, also known as HQL-79, is a small molecule that belongs to the family of quinoline-based inhibitors of the enzyme poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase-1 (PARP-1).\nPARP-1 is an important enzyme involved in the DNA-repair mechanisms of cells, and its inhibition has become a promising strategy for the treatment of cancer, inflammatory diseases, and other disorders involving DNA damage and oxidative stress.\nHQL-79 is a potent and selective PARP-1 inhibitor that has been extensively studied in vitro and in vivo for its pharmacological properties.\n...\nHQL-79 has been shown to exhibit potent PARP-1 inhibitory activity, with an IC50 value of 9 nM in a biochemical assay.\nIt also has selectivity over other PARP isoforms, such as PARP-2 and tankyrase-1, as well as other DNA-repair enzymes, such as DNA-dependent protein kinase (DNA-PK) and ataxia telangiectasia mutated (ATM).\nHQL-79 has been tested in various cellular and animal models of DNA damage and oxidative stress, and has been shown to enhance the cytotoxicity and sensitization of cancer cells to chemotherapy and radiation therapy, as well as to reduce the inflammation and tissue damage in models of arthritis, stroke, and neurodegeneration.\n...\nHQL-79 has been shown to have low toxicity and good safety profiles in in vitro and in vivo studies.\nIt does not induce mutagenicity or genotoxicity in bacterial or mammalian cells, and does not cause significant changes in the vital organs or hematological parameters of rodents or primates.\nHowever, some studies have reported potential side effects of PARP inhibitors, including myelosuppression, nausea, vomiting, asthenia, and cardiovascular events.\nTherefore, the safety and tolerability of HQL-79 and other PARP inhibitors need to be carefully monitored and evaluated in clinical trials.\n...\nThe research on PARP inhibitors, including HQL-79, is a rapidly evolving field.\nAs of 2021, there are several PARP inhibitors approved by regulatory agencies for the treatment of various cancers, including ovarian, breast, prostate, and pancreatic cancers.\nThese inhibitors include olaparib, rucaparib, niraparib, and talazoparib.\nMany ongoing clinical trials are investigating the use of PARP inhibitors in other cancer types, such as lung, bladder, and gastric cancers, as well as in combination with other therapies, such as immune checkpoint inhibitors, chemotherapy, and radiotherapy.\n...\nThe potential implications of HQL-79 and other PARP inhibitors in various fields of research and industry include:\n- Cancer therapy: PARP inhibitors have been shown to enhance the sensitivity of cancer cells to DNA damage and induce synthetic lethality in cancer cells with genetic defects in DNA repair pathways.\nTherefore, they have the potential to be used as monotherapy or combination therapy in cancer patients with different molecular and histological backgrounds.\n...\nIn conclusion, HQL-79 is a potent and selective PARP-1 inhibitor that has been extensively studied for its pharmacological properties and therapeutic potential in cancer, inflammation, and other diseases involving DNA damage and oxidative stress.", "date": "2024-01-05", "last_updated": "2025-06-11"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine as anti-cancer agents", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5718030/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "* A similar observation can be made when comparing CQ efficacy in pancreatic cancer mouse models and a lung cancer mouse model: CQ significantly slowed down tumour growth and increased survival in the first, but not in the latter [27].\n...\nNext, CQ and HCQ were tested via *in vitro* assays, in which they were found to suppress pancreatic cancer cell proliferation [82, 83].\n...\nFurthermore, a study in a pancreatic cancer patient-derived xenograft model showed that CQ specifically targets highly aggressive cancer stem cells through inhibition of their self-renewal process.", "date": "2017-11-23", "last_updated": "2026-08-15"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Structural and functional characterization of HQL-79, an orally ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16547010/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "HQL-79 selectively inhibited PGD2 production by H-PGDS-expressing human megakaryocytes and rat mastocytoma cells with an IC50 value of about 100 microm but only marginally affected the production of other prostanoids, suggesting the tight functional engagement between H-PGDS and cyclooxygenase.", "date": "2006-06-02", "last_updated": "2025-09-09"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Molecular Mechanism of Autophagy and Its Regulation by Cannabinoids in Cancer", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/13/6/1211", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "Evidence has revealed the efficacy of CQ and HCQ in hindering cancer cell proliferation through autophagy inhibition in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma and bladder cancers [109].\nHQL is not effective as a monotherapy but shows improvement when used in combination with gemcitabine with nab-paclitaxel.\nParallel inhibition of ERK synergistically enhances HQL inhibition.\nInhibiting ERK impairs metabolic processes such as glycolysis and mitochondrial function, which leads to a dependence on autophagy for the tumour to progress.\nPairing that with HQL inhibition of autophagy leads to an enhanced efficacy of treatment [110].\n...\nIn addition, inhibition of GPR55 receptor by CBD decreased cancer cell proliferation and augmented gemcitabine efficacy in pancreatic cancer through MAPK signalling downregulation.", "date": "2021-03-10", "last_updated": "2026-08-13"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Frontiers | Huaier suppresses pancreatic cancer progression via activating cell autophagy induced ferroptosis", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/oncology/articles/10.3389/fonc.2022.960858/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "**Results:** The growth inhibitory effect of Huaier on PCs was concentration- and time-dependent, but this effect was significantly attenuated by ferroptosis inhibitors.\n...\nThe results revealed that Huaier inhibited cell growth in a concentration-dependent manner in Panc-1 and MiaPaCa-2 cells (Figures 1B, C).\n...\nTo sun up, these results indicated that Huaier can suppress the proliferation of pancreatic cancer cell *in vitro* and pancreatic tumour growth *in vivo*.\n...\nWe found that Huaier markedly suppressed tumour growth, and a robust decrease in tumour weight was observed in the Huaier group compared with the control group, but this phenomenon was reversed by Fer-1 (Figure 3A).\n...\nIn brief, this evidence confirmed that Huaier inhibited tumour growth *in vivo* by inducing ferroptosis.", "date": "2022-09-30", "last_updated": "2025-05-20"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting the Stromal Pro-Tumoral Hyaluronan-CD44 Pathway in Pancreatic Cancer", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/22/8/3953", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "The independent inhibition of hyaluronan-producing cells, hyaluronan synthesis, and/or CD44 expression, has been found to decrease the tumor cell’s proliferation, motility, invasion, and metastatic abilities.", "date": "2021-04-12", "last_updated": "2026-08-11"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "4-(Benzhydryloxy)-1-[3-(1H-tetraazol-5-YL)propyl]piperidine", "url": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/hql-79", "domain": "pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "- DTXSID50424898\n- DTXCID70375732\n...\n377.5 g/mol", "date": "2025-07-12", "last_updated": "2025-07-14"}], "raw": {"id": "40c829d4-756a-4c3f-a962-13c13d1b23ee"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:39:55+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q029", "objective": "TP-317 in vivo monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["TP-317 KPC", "TP-317"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q029-1", "query": "TP-317 KPC pancreatic cancer tumour regression monotherapy", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "TP-317 KPC pancreatic cancer tumour regression monotherapy", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 17148.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://aacrjournals.org/cancerres/article-pdf/60/14/3978/3239751/ch140003978p.pdf", "domain": "aacrjournals.org", "snippet": "If you are using a CNAME origin record, make sure it is valid and resolvable.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-06-21"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Award | SBIR", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/awards/203006", "domain": "sbir.gov", "snippet": "To address this opportunity, Thetis is developing TP-317, a Resolvin E1 (RvE1) therapy for PC.\n...\nPreliminary research conducted in collaboration with Dr. Dipak\nPanigrahy’s Lab (Harvard Medical School) has shown that TP-317 can suppress tumor growth in multiple animal\nmodels as a single agent and has an additive effect with chemotherapy.\n...\nThe overall goal of this Fast Track grant is\nto complete key preclinical studies to support an investigational new drug (IND) application and advance TP-317\ninto clinical trials for the treatment of PC.\n...\nPhase I, Specific Aim 1: To investigate TP-317 in Murine Pancreatic Cancer Models.\nThe efficacy of TP-\n317 will be assessed in two established mouse models of PC, the KPC model and a patient derived xenograft\nmodel.\n...\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or\ncombination arm compared to placebo or active comparator and identification of biomarkers that correlate with\nresponse/non-response preclinically, are clinically significant in PC and are easily assayed in clinical trials.\nPhase II, Specific Aim 2.\n...\nPhase II, Specific Aim 3.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-09"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Award | SBIR", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/awards/219884", "domain": "sbir.gov", "snippet": "To address this opportunity, Thetis is developing TP-317, a Resolvin E1 (RvE1) therapy for PC.\n...\nPreliminary research conducted in collaboration with Dr. Dipak Panigrahy’s Lab (Harvard Medical School) has shown that TP-317 can suppress tumor growth in multiple animal models as a single agent and has an additive effect with chemotherapy.\n...\nThe overall goal of this Fast Track grant is to complete key preclinical studies to support an investigational new drug (IND) application and advance TP-317 into clinical trials for the treatment of PC.\n...\nThe efficacy of TP- 317 will be assessed in two established mouse models of PC, the KPC model and a patient derived xenograft model.\n...\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or combination arm compared to placebo or active comparator and identification of biomarkers that correlate with response/non-response preclinically, are clinically significant in PC and are easily assayed in clinical trials.\nPhase II, Specific Aim 2.\n...\nPhase II, Specific Aim 3.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-18"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Therapeutic pancreatic cancer biomarkers and ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1044579X25001105", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Firm | SBIR", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/portfolio/406944", "domain": "sbir.gov", "snippet": "To address this opportunity, Thetis is developing TP-317, a Resolvin E1 (RvE1) therapy for PC.\n...\nPreliminary research conducted in collaboration with Dr. Dipak Panigrahy’s Lab (Harvard Medical School) has shown that TP-317 can suppress tumor growth in multiple animal models as a single agent and has an additive effect with chemotherapy.\n...\nThe overall goal of this Fast Track grant is to complete key preclinical studies to support an investigational new drug (IND) application and advance TP-317 into clinical trials for the treatment of PC.\nThis goal will be met through the following aims: Phase I, Specific Aim 1: To investigate TP-317 in Murine Pancreatic Cancer Models.\nThe efficacy of TP- 317 will be assessed in two established mouse models of PC, the KPC model and a patient derived xenograft model.\nIn addition, we will evaluate the effect of TP-317 on multiple biomarkers (e.g., inflammatory cytokines, immune cell phenotyping via flow cytometry, and RNA sequencing).\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or combination arm compared to placebo or active comparator and identification of biomarkers that correlate with response/non-response preclinically, are clinically significant in PC and are easily assayed in clinical trials.\nPhase II, Specific Aim 2.\n...\nPhase II, Specific Aim 3.\n...\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-09"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Graphical Abstract", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/view-large/figure/573834660/oyag337f5.tif?itm_medium=graphical+abstract+image&itm_content=open+image&itm_source=http://academic.oup.com/oncolo/advance-article/doi/10.1093/oncolo/oyag337/8768668&itm_campaign=graphical+abstract", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Graphical Abstract | Oxford Academic \nGraphical Abstract\n...\nEach link will stop working after 30 days or 10 uses.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-08"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Am J Transl Res 2020;12(3):1031-1043", "url": "https://e-century.us/files/ajtr/12/3/ajtr0104826.pdf", "domain": "e-century.us", "snippet": "Abstract: Dinaciclib is a small molecule cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor with the potential to treat multiple can­ cers.\nTo better understand its cytotoxic action in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC), we evaluated dinaciclib therapeutic effects in the transgenic mouse model (LSL-KrasG12D/+; LSL-Trp53R172H/+; Pdx-1-Cre mice; KPC mice).\n...\nDinaciclib \ntherapy significantly delayed tumor progression (P < 0.001) and prolonged survival (P = 0.007) in KPC mice.\nIn vitro \n...\nDinaciclib treatment inhibited proliferation and induced apoptosis in KPC tumor as as­ sessed by Ki67 and cleaved caspase 3, respectively.\n...\nThese \nfinding suggest that dinaciclib as a single agent can inhibit tumor growth and improve the overall survival in KPC \nmice.\n...\nOverall, our results revealed that dinaciclib therapy significantly improved the OS of treated KPC mice and supported clinical practice in this context.\n...\nInterestingly, dinaciclib treatment significantly delayed tumor growth compared with control group (Figure\n2B, 2C).\nIn addition, we evaluated the OS of \nKPC mice in different groups.\nTreatment with dinaciclib prolonged the OS of KPC mice from\n31 to 57 days (P < 0.01 (Median)) (Figure 2D).\n...\nSecond, in this study, no complete tumor elimination was observed in\nKPC mice bearing established highly invasive tumors, underlining that dinaciclib combination therapies may be important for achieving dura­ ble tumor regression in PDAC.\n...\nIn conclusion, our study demonstrated that dinaciclib as a single agent can inhibit tumor growth and improve the OS in KPC mice.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-06-08"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "TAMing pancreatic cancer: combat with a double edged sword", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6441154/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|17.|Palbociclib + Gedatolisib|PI3K/mTOR inhibitor|NCT03065062|I|↓ M2 polarization|", "date": "2019-03-30", "last_updated": "2025-09-10"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "The KPC model (LSL-Kras>G12D/+>;LSL-Trp53>R172H/+>;Pdx-1 ...", "url": "https://pure.johnshopkins.edu/en/publications/genetically-engineered-mouse-models-of-pancreatic-cancer-the-kpc-/", "domain": "pure.johnshopkins.edu", "snippet": "For patients with unresectable disease, treatment options are limited and lack curative potential.\n...\n|Volume|2016|", "date": "2016-06-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacogenetics of treatments for pancreatic cancer", "url": "https://pure.amsterdamumc.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/157527340/Pharmacogenetics-of-treatments-for-pancreatic-cancer.pdf", "domain": "pure.amsterdamumc.nl", "snippet": "It has been used for decades as the first-line treatment for metastatic pancreatic cancer, as it showed a better response compared to 5-FU treatment [17].\nHowever, the median OS witnessed with gem- citabine was not very promising.\nBesides, gemcitabine has a low response rate.\n...\nThe FOLFIRINOX regimen has been first introduced as a standard treatment for metastatic PDAC after the phase III clinical trial PRODIGE 4/ACCORD 11, which showed a better OS and progression-free survival (PFS) as compared to gemcita- bine monotherapy [50].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-21"}], "raw": {"id": "aa278d58-5daf-41f7-b5cf-b8266cba8c46"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:40:01+00:00"}
