{"row_id": "q030", "objective": "SGR-4174 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["SGR-4174"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q030-2", "query": "SGR-4174 PDAC xenograft tumour shrinkage single agent", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "SGR-4174 PDAC xenograft tumour shrinkage single agent", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2273.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "SOS1: tracking the evolving path from promising to actionable ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12943-026-02632-7?error=cookies_not_supported&code=89f85997-69de-41bd-b40a-f6985ed2b656", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "In vivo studies showed that single-agent administration (100 mg/kg; q.d; 21 days) inhibited tumor growth in a PDAC KRAS^G12C^ mutated model (Mia PaCa-2) but not in the KRAS^G12C^ CRC (SW837) or the KRAS^G12D^ PDAC (AsPC-1) models.\n...\nOral administration of HW071021 as a single-agent significantly caused TGI (78%) in a murine xenograft model of KRAS^G12C^-driven oncogenicity (Mia PaCa-2 cells) [79].\n...\n*SGR-4174* appears as a novel, potent, and highly selective inhibitor of the SOS1::KRAS^G12C^ complex.\nThis compound was rationally designed using the Schrödinger, Inc computational platform (Schrödinger, Inc., https://www.schrodinger.com), and its features were presented at the 2025 AACR meeting [80].\nMechanistically, SGR-4174 binds to the catalytic domain of SOS1, disrupting SOS1::KRAS^G12C^ PPI (IC~50~ of 13 nM) [80].\nPreclinical in vitro studies demonstrated that SGR-4174 administration (IC~50~ values ranging from 42 to107 nM) resulted in a robust impairment of RAS-mediated signal transduction and reduced cell viability (3D cell cultures) across a wide spectrum of human cancer cell lines harboring KRAS^G12C/D/V^ or EGFR mutations (since we had no access to the panel of the specific cells employed in this study, this information is not included in Supplementary Table 2) [80].\nIn KRAS^G12C^-mutated tumor xenograft models, including NSCLC and PDAC, single-agent administration of SGR-4174 elicited dose-dependent TGI (please note that we were unable to find the precise dosing regimens employed in this study) [80].\nIn addition, concomitant administration of SGR-4174 and Sotorasib resulted in a deeper and more sustained inhibition of KRAS signal transmission, pronounced inhibition of cell proliferation in 3D cell culture models, and tumor regression in xenograft models when compared to the respective monotherapies [80].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-19"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "SOS1: tracking the evolving path from promising to actionable ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1186/s12943-026-02632-7.pdf?error=cookies_not_supported&code=aa72aed6-b31a-4006-88df-722541dec95c", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "tion (100 mg/kg; q.d; 21 days) inhibited tumor growth in a PDAC KRASG12C mutated model (Mia PaCa-2) but not in the KRASG12C CRC (SW837) or the KRASG12D PDAC\n(AsPC-1) models.\n...\nSGR-4174 appears as a novel, potent, and highly selec­\n…\n...\nMechanistically, SGR-\n4174 binds to the catalytic domain of SOS1, disrupting \nSOS1::KRASG12C PPI (IC50 of 13 nM) [80].\n...\ngraft models, including NSCLC and PDAC, single-agent administration of SGR-4174 elicited dose-dependent\nTGI (please note that we were unable to find the pre­ cise dosing regimens employed in this study) [80].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-03"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Schrödinger, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/ee6d0cc13d500f7740bc81b30e6d5978", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "In KRAS G12C tumor xenograft mouse models, SGR-4174 monotherapy demonstrated dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and a decrease in the tumor PD biomarker pERK.SGR-4174 was examined in combination with KRAS G12C inhibitor sotorasib and MEK inhibitor trametinib in vitro and in vivo.\nWhen combined with sotorasib, SGR-4174 demonstrated deeper and more sustained inhibition of the KRAS signaling pathway, stronger suppression of cell proliferation in 3D cell culture, and tumor regression in xenograft models compared to single agent activity.\n...\nCompared to other published SOS1 inhibitors (BI-3406, MRTX0902), SGR-4174 is more potent, and demonstrated similar anti-tumor activity and target engagement at a lower dose both in monotherapy and combination therapy studies in xenograft tumor models.\nSGR-4174 was well tolerated as monotherapy and in combination with sotorasib or trametinib.SGR-4174 demonstrated favorable PK properties across preclinical species and toxicity profile was well characterized.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "SGR-4174 / Schrodinger - Larvol Delta", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=1b3e9eec-7ffe-4dfd-8404-27376217e477", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## Preclinical characterization of SGR-4174, a potent and selective SOS1 inhibitor for the treatment of pan KRAS mutant cancers in combination with KRAS pathway inhibitors (AACR 2025) - \"Compared to other published SOS1 inhibitors (BI-3406, MRTX0902), SGR-4174 is more potent, and demonstrated similar anti-tumor activity and target engagement at a lower dose both in monotherapy and combination therapy studies in xenograft tumor models. SGR-4174 was well tolerated as monotherapy and in combination with sotorasib or trametinib.SGR-4174 demonstrated favorable PK properties across preclinical species and toxicity profile was well characterized. Our comprehensive preclinical efficacy and safety data support progressing SGR-4174 to clinical development to treat KRAS mutant cancers.\"\n...\n## Schrödinger Presents New Preclinical Data at AACR Annual Meeting (Businesswire) - \"SGR-4174 Data at AACR: The poster (Abstract #4376)...includes preclinical data demonstrating the superior binding, selectivity, and drug-like properties of SGR-4147 compared to MRTX0902 as assessed via comprehensive in vitro potency, ADME, and safety assays. The preclinical data for SGR-4174 also show robust suppression of the RAS signaling pathway and potent cell killing activity across multiple cancer types harboring diverse KRAS mutations as well as EGFR and SOS1 mutations. SGR-4174 monotherapy achieved dose-dependent target engagement and tumor growth inhibition and induced tumor shrinkage when used in combination with MEK or KRAS inhibitors in preclinical models of pancreatic and non-small cell lung cancer.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Company Announcements", "url": "https://markets.ft.com/data/announce/detail?dockey=600-202504280800BIZWIRE_USPRX____20250428_BW184420-1", "domain": "markets.ft.com", "snippet": "The data also demonstrated that SGR-4174 has strong tumor growth inhibition as a monotherapy as well as in combination with MEK or KRAS inhibitors, while maintaining a favorable safety profile.\n...\nThe poster (Abstract #4376), “Preclinical characterization of SGR-4174, a potent and selective SOS1 inhibitor for the treatment of pan KRAS mutant cancers in combination with KRAS pathway inhibitors,” includes preclinical data demonstrating the superior binding, selectivity, and drug-like properties of SGR-4147 compared to MRTX0902 as assessed via comprehensive\n*in vitro* potency, ADME, and safety assays.\nThe preclinical data for SGR-4174 also show robust suppression of the RAS signaling pathway and potent cell killing activity across multiple cancer types harboring diverse KRAS mutations as well as EGFR and SOS1 mutations.\nSGR-4174 monotherapy achieved dose-dependent target engagement and tumor growth inhibition and induced tumor shrinkage when used in combination with MEK or KRAS inhibitors in preclinical models of pancreatic and non-small cell lung cancer.", "date": "2025-04-28", "last_updated": "2025-04-28"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Schrödinger to Present Preclinical Data at AACR Annual ...", "url": "https://s203.q4cdn.com/609444515/files/doc_news/Schrdinger-to-Present-Preclinical-Data-at-AACR-Annual-Meeting-2025.pdf", "domain": "s203.q4cdn.com", "snippet": "The SGR-4174 presentation will include data characterizing SGR-4174 in preclinical oncology models.\n...\nPreclinical data suggest SGR-\n4174 has a di�erentiated pro�le and support its continued development as a potential therapy for patients with", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-17"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Delving into the Latest Updates on SGR-4174 with Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/b8e102fe163a4c6eabf652c6ce503310", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Phase\nPopulation\nAnalyzed Enrollment\n...\nResults\nEvaluation\nPublication Date\nNo Data", "date": "2025-04-05", "last_updated": "2025-04-19"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Preclinical assessment with clinical validation of Selinexor ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7073299/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Selinexor given at 15 mg/kg every other day x 3weeks caused statistically significant reduction in the growth of PDAC CSC xenograft (Figure 6A and Supplementary Figure 7A, p<0.01).\n...\nExposure to selinexor (15 mg/kg QoDx2) or KPT-8602 (20 mg/kg QDx5) resulted in statistically significant reduction in tumor growth of this pancreatic PDX [Figure 6C graph (*p=0.04 and **p=0.0098) and tumor photographs 6D].", "date": "2019-12-12", "last_updated": "2025-10-08"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "E> ��?�\"�T����Ԩ$h��N$lyf~����������50#���W||w��衴�*�a��ǔ N�<������D�W�oP��-�9촦�Mr{��b4�����q�*����#��lQ��