{"row_id": "q035", "objective": "BS-HH-002 first regulatory approval or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia", "anchors": ["BS-HH-002"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q035-5", "query": "BS-HH-002 first patient AML", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "BS-HH-002 first patient AML", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1615.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Guidelines - British Society for Haematology", "url": "https://b-s-h.org.uk/guidelines/guidelines", "domain": "b-s-h.org.uk", "snippet": "** 020 7713 0990\n...\n© British Society for Haematology 2025", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "ACYLATED DERIVATIVE OF HOMOHARRINGTONINE, PREPARATION METHOD THEREFOR, AND APPLICATION THEREOF", "url": "https://patents.justia.com/patent/20140303147", "domain": "patents.justia.com", "snippet": "After the reaction is completed, the reaction solution is concentrated to give a viscous crude product, to which diethyl ether is added for curing.\nEthyl acetate is then added to the cured crude product to dissolve it.\nThe mixture is rinsed with a saturated solution of potassium carbonate and then concentrated.\nThe resulted crude product is purified by a Silica Gel Column Chromatography (EA:PE=1:2) to give a yellow oil, which is then recrystallized with diethyl ether to give compound BS-HH-002 (654 mg, 61%) as a white solid; the homoharringtonine HHT raw material is recovered in the meantime (354 mg).\n...\nBS-HH-002 improves the anti-Kasumi-1 (acute myeloid leukemia M2 type, AML-M2) activity by more than 3.4-fold, improves the anti-K562/adr (drug-resistant, chronic myeloid leukemia, CML) activity by more than 3.2-fold, improves the anti-NB4 (acute promyelocytic leukemia, AML) activity by more than 3-fold, and improves the anti-H9 (acute lymphoblastic leukemia, ALL) activities by more than 5-fold.", "date": "2012-08-20", "last_updated": "2025-04-15"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Delving into the Latest Updates on BS-HH-002 with Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/e8d3b9c25ec941369c758ac88507fd6c", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Active Indication\nAcute Myeloid Leukemia\n...\nDrug Highest Phase\nPhase 1\n...\nNCT04743115\nUnknown status\nPhase 1\nA Phase I, Open-Label, Dose Escalation and Cohort Expansion Study of BS HH 002.SA in Patients With Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) and Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS)\nIndication:Relapsed or refractory AML in patients for whom no established treatment options are available (this indication will heretofore be referred to as the protocol AML indication), or adult patients with MDS who are classified as high risk or very high risk according to the Revised International Prognosis Scoring System (IPSS-R).\n...\nStudy Population:Adult patients with the protocol AML indication or high-risk MDS.\nStart Date\n01 Feb 2021", "date": "2025-05-22", "last_updated": "2025-07-05"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Therapeutic effects of an innovative BS-HH-002 drug on pancreatic ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34847421/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "It is particularly efficient in treatment for acute myeloid leukemia and myelodysplastic syndromes.", "date": "2022-01-05", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical trial results - Bristol Myers Squibb", "url": "https://www.bms.com/research-and-development/clinical-trials/clinical-trial-results.html", "domain": "bms.com", "snippet": "Access summaries of Bristol Myers Squibb clinical trial results and learn more about our commitment to transparency in clinical research.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "BSB-1001 for Blood Cancers - Clinical Trials", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/bsb-1001-for-blood-cancers-8a66b", "domain": "withpower.com", "snippet": "This trial is no longer recruiting.My employer runs this trial\n...\nNo longer recruiting at 7 trial locations\n...\nThis trial tests a new cellular therapy called BSB-1001 for certain blood cancers, such as AML, ALL, and MDS.\n...\nParticipants must have a matched donor for the treatment.\nAs a Phase 1 trial, participants will be among the first to receive this new treatment, aiding researchers in understanding its effects in people.\n...\nThis trial is for patients with blood cancers like AML, ALL, or MDS who are getting a stem cell transplant from a donor that matches their tissue type.\nParticipants should not have had previous treatments that would interfere with the study.\n...\nI have a high-risk blood cancer such as AML, ALL, or MDS.\nSuitable for one of the approved conditioning regimens as defined in the protocol\n...\nThe study tests BSB-1001, an experimental cellular therapy added to standard care (SOC), in two parts: first, finding the right dose and then seeing how well it works at preventing cancer relapse after stem cell transplants.\n...\nAML, ALL and MDS HLA-A\\*02:01 and HA-1-positive (H/H or H/R) patients with an identified HLA-matched, HA-1-negative (R/R) donor will be dosed in dose escalation cohorts\n...\nOnce the maximum tolerated dose (MTD) or promising dose is reached additional AML HLA-A\\*02:01 and HA-1-positive (H/H or H/R) patients with an identified HLA-matched, HA-1-negative (R/R) donor will be enrolled in the expansion cohort.\n...\nBS-HH-002, a modified version of homoharringtonine, shows strong antitumor activity against pancreatic cancer by completely degrading the anti-apoptosis protein MCL-1, leading to inhibited cell growth and increased cell death.", "date": "2024-07-03", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "A Phase I, Open-Label, Dose Escalation and Cohort Expansion ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/clinical-trial/bb2e7167ad820824/nct04743115-study-bs-hh-002-sa-aml-mds", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "# A Phase I, Open-Label, Dose Escalation and Cohort Expansion Study of BS HH 002.SA in Patients With Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) and Myelodysplastic Syndrome (MDS)\nShanghai Bensen Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd.0 sites66 target enrollmentStarted: February 2021Last updated: February 8, 2021\n...\n- **Status**\nNot yet recruiting\n...\n- **Enrollment**\n66\n- **Primary Endpoint**\n...\nIndication:Relapsed or refractory AML in patients for whom no established treatment options are available (this indication will heretofore be referred to as the protocol AML indication), or adult patients with MDS who are classified as high risk or very high risk according to the Revised International Prognosis Scoring System (IPSS-R).\n...\nStudy Population:Adult patients with the protocol AML indication or high-risk MDS.\n...\nFemale and male patients ≥18 years of age\n...\nPatients with one of the following conditions: - Primary or secondary AML, pathologically confirmed according to World Health Organization (WHO) or IWG classification, who have relapsed or refractory disease and for whom no established treatment options are available, or who are not a candidate for current therapies\n- High risk or very high risk MDS, as evaluated according to the IPSS R, who are resistant or intolerant to standard treatment and not candidates for transplantation\n...\nIn the first 3-patient cohort, the dose of BS HH 002.SA will be 0.2 mg/m2/day.\nEnrollment to the next higher dose cohort will be initiated only if none of the 3 patients exhibits a DLT in the first 28-day cycle.\nDose escalation will proceed according to a modified Fibonacci scheme, eg, increments of 100%, 100%, 66%, and 33% and the dose of BS-HH-002.SA will be 0.4, 0.8, 1.3, 1.7 mg/m2/day.\nThe dose will be administered subcutaneously once daily on Days 1 through 6 and on Days 10 through 15 of a 28 day cycle.\n...\nNCT04743115", "date": "2021-02-08", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Therapeutic effects of an innovative BS-HH-002 drug on pancreatic cancer cells via induction of complete MCL-1 degradation", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1936523321002795", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "Therapeutic effects of an innovative BS-HH-002 drug on pancreatic cancer cells via induction of complete MCL-1 degradation", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Acylated derivative of homoharringtonine, preparation method therefor, and application thereof", "url": "https://patents.google.com/patent/WO2013023622A1/en", "domain": "patents.google.com", "snippet": "- Table 1 shows that the homoharringtonine alkali acylated derivative of the present invention can induce cell death of human chronic myeloid leukemia, acute myeloid leukemia and acute lymphocytic leukemia and inhibit the growth of these leukemia cells, compared with homoharringtonine itself,", "date": "2012-08-20", "last_updated": "2025-04-29"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting the translational machinery to overcome ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8790655/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "BS-HH-002 is a derivative of the parent compound HHT (Synribo / Omacetaxine), which was approved in 2012 by the FDA for the treatment of chronic myeloid leukemia [7].", "date": "2022-01-22", "last_updated": "2025-09-06"}], "raw": {"id": "effbecfe-f763-4fcb-be48-410bb42307a4"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:40:21+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q036", "objective": "VAR-101 in vivo monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic Ductal Carcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["VAR-101"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q036-3", "query": "VAR-101 aPKCi PDAC in vivo efficacy monotherapy", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "VAR-101 aPKCi PDAC in vivo efficacy monotherapy", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 479.2, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "register", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/my-account/register?siteId=5148&returnUrl=/carcin/advance-article-abstract/doi/10.1093/carcin/bgaf073/8301325?redirectedFrom=fulltext", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "This PDF is available to Subscribers OnlyView Article Abstract & Purchase Options\nFor full access to this pdf, sign in to an existing account, or purchase an annual subscription.", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-10-27"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Page not found", "url": "https://medschool.cuanschutz.edu/colorado-cancer-center/clinical-trials/214C19E7-3028-64D9-A5BF-FF0A001CE385", "domain": "medschool.cuanschutz.edu", "snippet": "303-724-4357 (4-HELP from a campus phone)", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-18"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "A potential therapeutic target for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022356525397867", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-03"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Study Details |", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05743244", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "**Note:** The ClinicalTrials.gov results database became available in September 2008.", "date": "2012-02-16", "last_updated": "2025-08-11"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] VP-VSJ-110-2101 - ClinicalTrials.gov", "url": "https://cdn.clinicaltrials.gov/large-docs/45/NCT04622345/Prot_SAP_000.pdf", "domain": "cdn.clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-05"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "NCT07195487 | A Clinical Study to Evaluate the Safety, ...", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07195487", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "**Note:** The ClinicalTrials.gov results database became available in September 2008.", "date": "2018-05-08", "last_updated": "2026-01-11"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "SPK Acquisition Corp. Form 425 Filed 2022-02-14", "url": "http://pdf.secdatabase.com/140/0001575705-22-000133.pdf", "domain": "pdf.secdatabase.com", "snippet": "The active pharmaceutical ingredient in VAR-101/102, an aPKCi inhibitor, has demonstrated dose dependent anti-tumor activity in murine and human BCC cell lines, as well as other cancer models.\nVarian Bio intends to develop VAR-101 in a topical formulation for\nBCC which has the potential to offer optimal clinical utility in BCC as a surgical neoadjuvant or adjuvant therapy.\n...\nVarian Bio, together with its collaborators, is initially developing two formulations of its lead aPKCi inhibitor that has been selected for potency, selectivity, biomarker activity and low toxicity in murine and large animal models.\n...\nVAR-101 is a dermatologic gel formulation of for the potential topical treatment Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC).\n...\nThe active aPKCi inhibitor (VAR-101) has demonstrated dose-dependent in vitro BCC cell killing with concordant biomarker Gli-1 mRNA knockdown (dose dependent reduction).\n...\nVAR-102 is an oral formulation of the aPKCi inhibitor for the potential treatment of multiple solid tumors, including non-small cell lung cancer, pancreatic, colorectal and other tumor types.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-08-03"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "2.1. Step 1: Dose Escalation...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2451865424000760", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-02"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Exploratory, multicenter, open-label study to evaluate the ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2451865422001363", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-08"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trial A phase 2b, randomized, double-blind ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0085253824002369", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-06"}], "raw": {"id": "0fe56b6d-61e5-4e6a-be4a-f2a75f6e63ae"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:40:21+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q025", "objective": "Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanism of action as HEMAY007, targeting TNF-α Tumor Necrosis Factor Alpha", "anchors": ["HEMAY007"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q025-4", "query": "HEMAY007 ulcerative colitis anti-TNF biosimilar landscape", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HEMAY007 ulcerative colitis anti-TNF biosimilar landscape", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 3, "latency_ms": 531.9, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 1.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/table/T1/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties|\n|TNF-α/OX40L inhibitor|SAR442970|Bispecific pentavalent Nanobody targeting TNF-α and OX40L|NCT06975722|Phase 2b|Started July 2025; estimated completion Dec 2026|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-14"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Accelerating Earlier Access to Anti-TNF- Agents with Biosimilar Medicines in the Management of Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/b3ee/47fc77d4c6e6c4ace86137d14d514f59a81f.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "However, the high cost of such therapies may restrict access and prevent timely treatment of IBD.\nBiosimilar anti-TNF-α medicines may represent a valuable opportunity for cost savings and optimized patient outcomes by improving access to advanced therapies and allowing earlier anti-TNF-α treatment initiation.\n...\nThe anti-tumor necrosis factor alpha (anti-TNF-α) agents infliximab and adalimumab are effective biologic treatments used to treat people with moderate-to-severe IBD [4,5], as well as populations defined as at ‘high-risk’ for disease complications [6].\n...\nBiosimilar biologics, which are biologics that are designed to have equivalent efficacy and comparable safety to another approved biologic ‘reference’ medicine (Table 1), pro- vide access to effective therapies without inflating costs.\nBiosimilar medicines provide outcomes consistent with the reference medicine while being typically associated with a lower purchase cost [14,15], thus, offering potential financial savings and increased, affordable access to high-efficacy treatment options [16–18].\n...\nAs of July 2024, there are 17 biosimilar medicines for IBD approved in the United States (US) and the European\nUnion (EU), and 15 in Canada [18–20].\nAnti-TNF-α biosimilars currently dominate the IBD biosimilar landscape, but as other high-efficacy treatments begin to reach patent expiry, the\nIBD biosimilar landscape is expanding with the approval of ustekinumab biosimilars and\n…\ncommonly used to treat IBD include anti-TNF-α agents (e.g., infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab, and certolizumab), adhesion molecule antagonists (e.g., vedolizumab), interleukin antagonists (e.g., ustekinumab), and JAK inhibitors (e.g., tofacitinib) [27].\n...\nA biosimilar is a biologic medicine that is designed to match an approved biologic medicine (the ‘reference’ medicine) in structure and function.\nBiosimilar medicines are reviewed and approved based on a rigorous ‘totality of evidence’ approach, to confirm that there are no clinically meaningful differences in safety, efficacy, and immunogenicity from the reference medicine [16,26,28].\nBiosimilar medicines used to treat IBD include biosimilars of infliximab, adalimumab, and ustekinumab biologics [18–20].\n...\nWith regard\nto UC, the evidence supporting the early use of biologic anti-TNF-α agents is less clear, potentially due to a lack of a defined ‘window of opportunity’ for early UC [30,31].\n...\nRecommends the use of anti-TNF-α\ntherapies over no treatment for\nmoderate-to-severe UC;\nconditionally recommends early\nanti-TNF-α use after failure of\n5-aminosalicylate therapy\n...\nRecommends anti-TNF-α therapy\nfor the induction of remission of\nmoderately-to-severely active UC", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-07-16"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "OVERVIEW", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2025/107914/a127807/sehk25120100842.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "Hemay007.\nOur key product, Hemay007 is screened from a compound library directed by multiple pharmacological functional substructures and is a TNF-�small molecule regulator.\nIn the spontaneous UC monkey model, Hemay007 reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the diseased monkeys, and pathological\n...\nHemay007\nHemay007 is screened from a compound library using multiple pharmacological approaches and is a TNF-�small molecule modulator.\nIn the spontaneous UC monkey model,\nHemay007 reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the diseased monkeys.\nPathological\n...\nMechanism of Action\nHemay007 inhibited the TLR4 signaling pathway, a key driver of inflammation.\n...\nBy inhibiting this pathway, Hemay007 blocks the release of pro-inflammatory factors, particularly TNF-�, thereby reducing the inflammatory response triggered by immune stimulation.\n...\nDesign.\nThe clinical trial was a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo- controlled, parallel-group study designed to evaluate the efficacy, safety, and dose-response relationship of various dosing regimens of Hemay007 in patients with UC.\nA total of 70 patients with UC were enrolled and randomized into four parallel groups: 16 patients received\nHemay007 400 mg twice daily (BID), 18 patients received 800 mg once daily (QD), 17 patients received 600 mg BID, and 19 patients received a placebo.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-04"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "TNF-Alpha Proteins, Antibodies & ELISA Kits", "url": "https://www.acrobiosystems.com/category/target-protein/tnf-alpha", "domain": "acrobiosystems.com", "snippet": "|Recombinant anti-TNFα monoclonal antibody (Wuhan Institute of Biological Products)|Phase 3 Clinical|Wuhan Institute Of Biological Products Co Ltd|Plaque psoriasis|Details| |\n...\n|Hemay007|Hemay-007|Phase 2 Clinical|Tianjin Hemay Bio-Tech Co Ltd|Arthritis, Rheu\n…", "date": "2024-09-23", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "BUSINESS", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2025/107411/a123461/sehk25052902266.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "Hemay007.\nOur key product, Hemay007 is screened from a compound library directed by multiple pharmacological functional substructures and is a TNF-�small molecule regulator.\nIn the spontaneous UC monkey model, Hemay007 significantly reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the diseased monkeys, and pathological examinations of the intestines after treatment revealed that the colon tissue almost returned to a normal state.\nAs of the Latest Practicable Date, we have completed the phase II clinical trial for monotherapy for UC.\n...\nWe plan to prioritize the clinical trial of our Core Products and currently do not have an expected timetable for the next steps of Hemay007.\n...\nHemay007\nHemay007 is screened from a compound library using multiple pharmacological approaches and is a TNF-�small molecule modulator.\nIn the spontaneous UC monkey model,\nHemay007 significantly reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the diseased monkeys.\nPathological examinations of the intestines after treatment revealed that the colon tissue had almost returned to a normal state.\nAs of the Latest Practicable Date, we have completed phase\nII clinical trials for monotherapy for UC in China.\nWe plan to prioritize the clinical trial of our\nCore Products and currently do not have an expected timetable for the next steps of Hemay007.\nMechanism of Action\nHemay007 inhibited the TLR4 signaling pathway, a key driver of inflammation.\nUpon stimulation by LPS (lipopolysaccharide), TLR4 activates a cascade of intracellular signaling events, beginning with the recruitment of MYD88 (myeloid differentiation factor 88), followed by the activation of IRAK (interleukin-1 receptor-associated kinase) and TRAF6 (TNF receptor-associated factor 6).\n...\nBy inhibiting this pathway, Hemay007 effectively blocks the release of pro-inflammatory factors, particularly\nTNF-�, thereby reducing the inflammatory response triggered by immune stimulation.\n...\nThe clinical trial was a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo- controlled, parallel-group study designed to evaluate the efficacy, safety, and dose-response relationship of various dosing regimens of Hemay007 in patients with ulcerative colitis (UC).\n...\nHemay007: We own two pending PRC patent applications, seven issued U.S. patents, two pending U.S. patent applications directed to Hemay007 as of the Latest Practicable Date.\nThe expected expiration for the issued U.S. patents will range from September 2025 to November\n2037, excluding any additional term for patent term extensions.\nWe intend to pursue marketing", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-13"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Guide to Navigating the Landscape of Biosimilars for ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11348546/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Biosimilars, which are biologic medications that are highly similar to the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA)-approved reference product with no clinically meaningful differences in safety, purity, or potency, offer the same therapeutic benefits at a reduced cost.\n...\nThis article provides an overview of the biosimilar landscape in IBD, including FDA-approved biosimilars available, and a clinical guide to navigate switching to biosimilars in various clinical scenarios based on current evidence.\n...\nBiosimilars are developed from the same type of living source and administered by the same route, frequency, and dosage.\nThese medications also produce efficacy and safety effects comparable to the reference product, essentially providing the same treatment benefits but at a lower cost.^7^ Biosimilars undergo a robust FDA review process; as long as biosimilarity to the already FDA-approved reference product is demonstrated through structural and functional studies, extensive large-scale studies evaluating biosimilar clinical effectiveness in each individual disease state are not warranted.\n...\n^7^ Interchangeable biosimilars can be substituted and dispensed for the reference product at the pharmacy level without the approval of the prescribing provider, depending on state pharmacy laws.\nThe interchangeability designation does not indicate superiority over other biosimilars and is a status specific to the United States; in Europe, all biosimilars are automatically considered interchangeable.\n...\nA list of all currently available infliximab and adalimumab biosimilars (some of which are interchangeable, as well as some products that are unbranded formulations of infliximab and adalimumab) indicated for IBD is provided in the Table.\nThe FDA approved 2 biosimilars for ustekinumab: ustekinumab-auub (Wezlana, Amgen), which is interchangeable, in 2023; and ustekinumab-aekn (Selarsdi, Teva Pharmaceuticals) in 2024.\n...\n^15^ Biosimilars for vedolizumab are not anticipated to enter the US marketplace until 2032.^16^\n...\n|Biosimilar|Manufacturer|FDA approval|Indications|\n...\n|**Infliximab biosimilars**|**Infliximab biosimilars**|**Infliximab biosimilars**|**Infliximab biosimilars**|\n|Avsola (infliximab-axxq)|Amgen|2019|CD (adult/pediatric), UC (adult/pediatric), RA, AS, PsA, Ps|\n|Inflectra (infliximab-dyyb)|Pfizer|2016|CD (adult/pediatric), UC (adult/pediatric), RA, AS, PsA, Ps|\n...\n|Abrilada (adalimumab-afzb)^b^|Pfizer|2019|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Amjevita (adalimumab-atto)^c^^-^^d^|Amgen|2016|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Cyltezo (adalimumab-adbm)^b^^-^^d^|Boehringer Ingelheim|2017|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Hadlima (adalimumab-bwwd)^d^|Organon/ Samsung Bioepis|2019|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Hulio (adalimumab-fkjp)^c^|Mylan/Fujifilm Kyowa Kirin Biologics|2020|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Hyrimoz (adalimumab-adaz)^b^^-^^d^|Sandoz|2018|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Idacio (adalimumab-aacf )|Fresenius Kabi|2022|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Simlandi (adalimumab-ryvk)^b^|Alvotech/Teva|2024|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Yuflyma (adalimumab-aaty)|Celltrion|2023|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Yusimry (adalimumab-aqvh)|Coherus BioSciences|2021|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n...\nAlthough most infliximab and adalimumab biosimilars received FDA approval for use in IBD through the process of extrapolation, many published studies and realworld evidence from Europe demonstrate that biosimilars result in similar patient outcomes as expected with the reference product, such as sustaining remission without increased adverse effects or immunogenicity risks.\n...\nCurrently in the United States, multiple biosimilars are available for infliximab and adalimumab, with more biosimilars in the pipeline for ustekinumab and vedolizumab.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "a pipeline of novel drugs for UC management", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/efc2/a31ee078832397a8583aedd937aa7a780012.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "TNF modulator\nHemay007\nSmall molecule TNF-a regulator\nNCT03977480\nPhase 2\nTerminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment\ndifficulties\n...\nAnti-TNF biologics revolutionized IBD treatment beginning in the late 1990s and early 2000s, establishing the paradigm of targeted biological therapy for moderate to severe disease.\nThree anti-TNF monoclonal antibodies are currently approved for UC: infliximab\n(chimeric mouse-human IgG1), adalimumab (fully human IgG1),\nand golimumab (fully human IgG1).\n...\nNext-generation TNF pathway inhibitors employ several innovative strategies to address these limitations: selective TNFR1 antagonists preserving TNFR2 signaling (balinatunfib/SAR441566), bispecific antibodies targeting TNF-a plus additional mediators for synergistic blockade (SAR442970 targeting TNF-a and OX40L), small molecule TNF modulators avoiding immunogenicity (hemay007),\nCD40 antagonists indirectly modulating TNF-related pathways\n...\nThe following section reviews investigational TNF pathway inhibitors currently in Phase II and III clinical development for moderate to severe UC.\n2.3.1 Hemay007\nA Phase II study (NCT03977480) evaluated Hemay007, a small molecule that acts as a TNF-a regulator, using a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled design with four treatment arms comparing different dosing regimens (400 mg\nBID, 800 mg QD, 600 mg BID, and placebo) over 12 weeks followed by an open-label extension.\nThe primary outcome measured clinical response defined as MS reduction of ≥3 points and ≥30% from baseline with RBS improvement, while inclusion criteria required patients aged 18–70 years with active UC (MS ≥4,\nMES ≥2) who had treatment failure or intolerance to standard therapies including 5-ASA, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or anti-TNF agents.\nThe study was terminated early due to recruitment difficulties, completing in August 2022 with only 70 of the planned 288 patients enrolled, and no efficacy or safety results have been posted on ClinicalTrials.gov.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-21"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Biosimilars in Crohn's Disease and Ulcerative Colitis", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ibdjournal/article/22/4/994/4561865", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "On August 24, 1998, infliximab, an antibody to tumor necrosis factor alpha (anti-TNF) became the first biologic approved for inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).\nAt that time, the approval was limited to induction therapy for moderate to severe luminal and fistulizing Crohn's disease; no Federal Drug Agency (FDA) approval for maintenance therapy was given.\nThe anti-TNFs have changed the course of Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis for many patients over the past 17 years.\nThe benefit derived from these biologic drugs, the induction and maintenance of response and remission, and improvement of quality of life, are well known.\nWith the advent of biosimilars, a new type of biologic has arrived.\nBiosimilars are biological medicines that are developed after the patent on a reference biologic has expired.^1^ Biosimilars to infliximab have been approved by the European Medicines Agency (EMA) and used in European countries.\nThey are now being considered for approval by the FDA.^2^\nOther biologics in Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis have been developed and approved in the past 17 years and these include 3 other anti-TNFs, adalimumab, certolizumab pegol, and golimumab and 2 anti-integrins, natalizumab and vedolizumab.\nBecause of patent expiration, biosimilars to infliximab and adalimumab have been developed.\nPatient access to biologics has been excellent for some patients but limited for others.\n...\nAnti-TNF agents and anti-integrins have been made available to patients of financial need through “patient assistance programs,” which cover patients with modest or no income, although these programs also have limitations.\n...\nA biosimilar is a biological product i.e., approved based on it being highly similar to an FDA-approved biological product and has no clinically meaningful differences in terms of safety and effectiveness from the reference product.^5^ According to U.S. law, H.R. 3590, the Affordable Care Act, a biological product is biosimilar to a reference product if^4^:", "date": "2016-04-01", "last_updated": "2026-03-15"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Seminars in Arthritis and Rheumatism", "url": "https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/186815/1/1-s2.0-S0049017222000749-main.pdf", "domain": "eprints.whiterose.ac.uk", "snippet": "To date, there are several anti-inflammatory biosimilars approved for use in patients with rheumatoid arthritis (RA), axial spondyloar­ thritis (axSpA), juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA), Crohn’s disease (CD), ulcerative colitis (UC), psoriasis (PsO), and psoriatic arthritis (PsA)\n(Table 1).\nThese biosimilars have been approved for all indications for which the RPs are approved.\n...\ndications but not Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis.\n...\nTable 4 \nSelected publications of clinical studies evaluating biosimilars for the therapy of Crohn’s disease/ulcerative colitis until 2021 [37,38,56-74].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-14"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ueg2.12305", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "AJM300 (carotegrast methyl) is a new small molecule, α4-integrin antagonist, being evaluated in UC.^88^ In the phase III study (NCT03531892), AJM300-treated patients (960 mg three times daily orally) had a significantly higher rate of clinical response (45%; *p* = 0.00028) than PBO gro\n...\n- 157 Phase II study of Hemay007 in patients with active ulcerative colitis - full text view. ClinicalTrials.gov. https://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT03977480. Accessed 4 August 2022.", "date": "2022-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}], "raw": {"id": "78ff5422-f196-41a3-8a94-a556531ae5a6"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:40:21+00:00"}
