ttps://amloceanographic.com/blogs/aml-oceanographic-acquired-by-union-park-capital-to-launch-marine-technology-platform", "domain": "amloceanographic.com", "snippet": "On February 1, 2026 the company entered its next phase of growth.", "date": "2026-02-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "35d8d2ba-ba8c-4d8f-8706-fcfb391518f5"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:41:59+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q058", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo efficacy in combination with another agent in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma, including the combination partner", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q058-2", "query": "HEC234055 combination partner PDAC xenograft", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HEC234055 combination partner PDAC xenograft", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 435.3, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Three innovative drugs showcased at the 2026 AACR Annual ...", "url": "https://www.moomoo.com/news/post/68755735/dongyang-sunshine-pharmaceutical-06887-three-innovative-drugs-showcased-at-the", "domain": "moomoo.com", "snippet": "Synergistic enhancement in combination therapy: Co-administration with PD-1 antibodies further significantly enhanced anti-tumor efficacy, showing broad prospects for combination therapy.\n...\nHEC234055 - An oral high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor targeting undruggable targets.\n...\nThe investigational product HEC234055 is an orally administered, high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the group, designed for broad-spectrum coverage of various KRAS mutations and wild-type amplifications.\n...\nSignificant in vivo efficacy: In various xenograft tumor models with KRAS mutations (covering pancreatic cancer, lung cancer, and colorectal cancer), oral administration produced dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induced tumor regression.\n...\nBroad combination therapy potential: Co-administration with chemotherapy drugs (5-FU), anti-VEGF antibodies, VEGFR inhibitors, and KRAS G12C inhibitors all exhibited significant synergistic effects.", "date": "2026-04-23", "last_updated": "2026-04-24"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "A potential therapeutic target for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022356525397867", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-03"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Inhibition of a pancreatic cancer model by cooperative ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30952434/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Cetuximab, a clinically approved anti-EGFR monoclonal antibody (mAb) weakly inhibited PDAC xenografts, similar to trastuzumab, a mAb against HER2, a co-receptor of EGFR.\nBecause the combination of cetuximab and trastuzumab only moderately enhanced the anti-tumor effects, we combined each with a home-made mAb to the same receptor and identified two cooperative pairs.\nThe pair of trastuzumab and a murine anti-HER2 mAb better than the anti-EGFR pair inhibited PDAC xenografts, although HER2's abundance in our model is 15-fold lower than the level of EGFR.\nIn vitro studies attribute cooperation to forced receptor endocytosis/degradation and inhibition of both DNA synthesis and cell migration.\nTaken together, our results identify cooperative pairs of anti-PDAC antibodies and highlight potential mechanisms of anti-tumor effects.", "date": "2019-05-21", "last_updated": "2025-12-22"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Combinatorial screening of pancreatic adenocarcinoma reveals ...", "url": "https://scispace.com/pdf/combinatorial-screening-of-pancreatic-adenocarcinoma-reveals-4agzt1hdnh.pdf", "domain": "scispace.com", "snippet": "Dual bromodomain/neddylation blockade inhibited in vivo growth of PDAC cell line xenografts.\n...\nOf note, the bro- modomain inhibitor JQ1 and neddylation inhibitor MLN4924 combine superadditively to halt growth and inhibit viability and colony formation in a broad range of PDAC cell lines, and control human PDAC grown as mouse xenografts.\n...\nImportantly, combination of JQ1 and MLN4924 suppressed growth of Mia-\n...\nWe show for the first time that the combination of the JQ1 bromodomain inhibitor and MLN4924 neddylation inhibitor is highly effective for PDAC in both in vitro and in vivo studies.\n...\nHence, it is noteworthy that we identified a series of effective combination partners both for JQ1 and for MLN4924.\nJQ1 combined well with gemcitabine, BI-2536, MLN-8237, peme- trexed, and PF-431396.\nMLN4924 combination partners includ- ed PF-431396, SN-38, MLN-8237, oligomycin, and pemetrexed.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-11"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Treatment of pancreatic cancer with a combination ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0923753419478381", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-19"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Combinatorial Screening of Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma Reveals Sensitivity to Drug Combinations Including Bromodomain Inhibitor plus Neddylation Inhibitor", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5457712/", "domain": "ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Combination of the bromodomain inhibitor JQ1 and the neddylation inhibitor MLN4294 altered production of reactive oxygen species in PDAC cells, ultimately leading to defects in the DNA damage response.\nDual bromodomain/neddylation blockade inhibited *in vivo* growth of PDAC cell line xenografts.\nOverall, this work revealed novel combinatorial regimens, including JQ1 plus MLN4294, which show promise for the treatment of *RAS*-driven PDAC.\n...\nAgents that contribute most often to effective combinations include SN-38, the active metabolite of irinotecan (topoisomerase I inhibitor), PF-431396 (FAK, PYK2 inhibitor), MLN4924 (neddylation activating enzyme inhibitor), JQ1 (BET family inhibitor), Foretinib (MET, VEGFR2 inhibitor), and GSK1120212 (MEK inhibitor).\nOf note, the bromodomain inhibitor JQ1 and neddylation inhibitor MLN4924 combine superadditively to halt growth and inhibit viability and colony formation in a broad range of PDAC cell lines, and control human PDAC grown as mouse xenografts.\nEffectiveness of this combination occurs in part through the induction of reactive oxygen species (ROS) that promote increased DNA damage, leading to apoptosis.\n...\nWhen the size of the xenograft (n = 3-5 per group) reached approximately 80-120 mm\n3, the mice were randomized and intraperitoneally treated with JQ1 (50 mg/kg, daily) and/or MLN4924 (25 mg/kg, every other day) for 14 days and showed significant synergistic effect.\n...\nImportantly, combination of JQ1 and MLN4924 suppressed growth of MiaPaCa-2 mouse xenografts under conditions where the single agents were ineffectual (Fig. 6F, left).\nThis reinforces the potential for substantial improvement over single-agent responses with this combination regimen.\n...\nPairwise combination of these seven agents reduced clonogenic colony formation and were often super-additive.", "date": "2017-06-11", "last_updated": "2024-10-11"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "A pan-cancer screen identifies drug combination benefit in ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666379124004087", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-03"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 2.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8000738/table/jcm-10-01151-t002/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Bioconjugation strategy has been categorized into three methods: lysine-based random, site-specific via sortase-mediated reaction, and direct iodination.", "date": "2021-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-10-29"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Pre-clinical Models of Metastasis in Pancreatic Cancer.", "url": "https://scispace.com/pdf/pre-clinical-models-of-metastasis-in-pancreatic-cancer-4ef2byhp8q.pdf", "domain": "scispace.com", "snippet": "One of the solutions to address the many shortcomings of 2D cell lines is to establish cell line derived xenografts (CDX).\nPDAC cell lines are implanted into mouse models to research and test the efficacy of anti-cancer therapies in vivo and metastasis formation.\n...\nIn contrast to allograft models, human\nPDAC CDX are established by transplanting PDAC cell lines into immunocompromised mice.\nResuspended cells in Matrigel for injection to establish an orthotopic mouse model of PDAC is a common method.\n...\nPDAC PDXs develop between one and 4 months after\ntransplantation, with an engraftment rate between 20 and\n80% (Garcia et al., 2020).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-18"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Molecular alterations and targeted therapy in pancreatic ...", "url": "https://d-nb.info/122186016X/34", "domain": "d-nb.info", "snippet": "When applied together with AZD6244, BKM120, a PI3K inhibitor, leads to robust apoptosis in PDAC-derived organotypic models or murine models, resulting in a longer median survival (131.5 vs 71 days) [59] and indi- cating that the combined inhibition of MEK and PI3K may have clinical value.\n...\nUlixertinib, an ERK inhibitor, exerts an inhibitory\neffect on solid tumour xenograft models [60] and ap- pears to prevent tumour growth to a greater extent when combined with MEK inhibitors [61].\n...\nThe application of abemaciclib and YAP1 or HuR inhibitors also exerts a synergistic inhibitory effect on PDAC cell lines [114].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-02-21"}], "raw": {"id": "ab27f476-bdfb-4d66-b0a6-97b11a360230"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:00+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q058", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo efficacy in combination with another agent in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma, including the combination partner", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q058-3", "query": "HEC234055 AACR 2026 combination efficacy", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HEC234055 AACR 2026 combination efficacy", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 3631.2, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "C262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4", "url": "https://www.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301900_c.pdf", "domain": "hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "本集團欣然宣佈，於2026 年4 月17 日至22 日在美國加利福尼亞州聖地亞哥舉行的\n2026年美國癌症研究協會(AACR)年會上，本集團以三份壁報形式集中展示了腫瘤\n...\n二、HEC234055 — 攻克「不可成藥」靶點的口服高活性Pan-RAS分子膠抑制劑（摘\n要號#2634）\n...\n亞型仍存在巨大未滿足的臨床需求。在研產品HEC234055 為本集團自主研發\n的口服高活性Pan-RAS 分子膠抑制劑，旨在廣譜覆蓋KRAS 多種突變及野生型\n...\n廣譜覆蓋與高活性：可同時作用於KRAS 的活性（三磷酸鳥苷（「GTP 」）結\n合）與非活性（GDP 結合）狀態，並覆蓋G12D 、G12C 、G12V 、G13D 、\n...\n亞納摩爾級細胞活性：在多種KRAS 突變驅動的癌細胞系中，其抗增殖活\n性達到亞納摩爾級別，半抑制濃度（「IC50」）低至0.49 納摩爾（「n M」），顯\n示出優於當前對標分子的強效抑制能力。\n...\n顯著的體內療效：在多種KRAS 突變的異種移植瘤模型中（涵蓋胰腺癌、\n肺癌及結直腸癌），口服給藥可產生劑量依賴性的腫瘤生長抑制乃至誘導\n腫瘤消退。\n...\n廣泛的聯合用藥潛力：與化療藥物(5 -FU) 、抗VEGF 抗體、VEGFR 抑制劑\n及KRAS G12C抑制劑聯合給藥，均表現出顯著的協同增效作用。", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-01"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Sunshine Lake Pharma Co., Ltd. Showcases Three Innovative ...", "url": "https://www.marketscreener.com/news/sunshine-lake-pharma-co-ltd-showcases-three-innovative-oncology-drug-candidates-at-2026-aacr-annu-ce7f58d8dd8ffe27", "domain": "marketscreener.com", "snippet": "At the 2026 Annual Meeting of the American Association for Cancer Research (AACR), held from 17 to 22 April 2026 in San Diego, California, USA, the Group presented the latest preclinical research results of three oncology pipeline candidates through three poster presentations.\n...\nHEC234055 is an orally available high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the Group, designed to target a broad spectrum of multiple KRAS mutations as well as wild-type amplifications.\nThe preclinical data highlights announced this time are as follows: Broad-spectrum coverage and high activity: It can act on both active (guanosine triphosphate (GTP)-bound) and inactive (GDP-bound) states of KRAS, and covers various mutation types such as G12D, G12C, G12V, G13D, Q61H, and wild-type amplification.\nSub-nanomolar cell activity: In a variety of KRAS mutation-driven cancer cell lines, its anti-proliferative activity reaches the sub-nanomolar level, with half-maximal inhibitory concentration (IC50) as low as 0.49 nanomolar (nM), showing potent inhibitory capabilities superior to current benchmark molecules.\nSignificant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models (including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression.\n...\nBroad potential for combination therapy: Co-administration with chemotherapy drugs (5-FU), anti-VEGF antibodies, VEGFR inhibitors, and KRASG12C inhibitors all exhibit significant synergistic effects.\n...\nThe preclinical research data disclosed in this announcement are derived from laboratory and animal model studies, and the relevant drug candidates have not yet undergone clinical trials in humans.", "date": "2026-04-23", "last_updated": "2026-05-14"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Three innovative drugs showcased at the 2026 AACR Annual ...", "url": "https://www.moomoo.com/news/post/68755735/dongyang-sunshine-pharmaceutical-06887-three-innovative-drugs-showcased-at-the", "domain": "moomoo.com", "snippet": "Dongyangguang Pharmaceutical (06887) announced that at the 2026 Annual Meeting of the American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) held in San Diego, California, USA, from April 17 to 22, 2026, the group presented the latest preclinical research findings on three pipeline candidates in the field of oncology treatment through three poster presentations.\n...\nHEC234055 - An oral high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor targeting undruggable targets.\n...\nThe investigational product HEC234055 is an orally administered, high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the group, designed for broad-spectrum coverage of various KRAS mutations and wild-type amplifications.\n...\nSub-nanomolar cellular activity: In various cancer cell lines driven by KRAS mutations, its anti-proliferative activity reaches sub-nanomolar levels, with a half-maximal inhibitory concentration (IC50) as low as 0.49 nanomoles (nM), demonstrating stronger inhibitory capabilities than current benchmark molecules.\nSignificant in vivo efficacy: In various xenograft tumor models with KRAS mutations (covering pancreatic cancer, lung cancer, and colorectal cancer), oral administration produced dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induced tumor regression.\n...\nBroad combination therapy potential: Co-administration with chemotherapy drugs (5-FU), anti-VEGF antibodies, VEGFR inhibitors, and KRAS G12C inhibitors all exhibited significant synergistic effects.", "date": "2026-04-23", "last_updated": "2026-04-24"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "AACR Annual Meeting 2026 – Post-event summary", "url": "https://www.insights.bio/bioinsights/journal/article/3821/aacr-annual-meeting-2026-postevent-summary-clinical-data-readouts-and-emerging-themes", "domain": "insights.bio", "snippet": "Within the ovarian cancer subgroup, 80% of patients demonstrated tumor shrinkage, with an ORR of 37.5% overall, increasing to 60% in the CCNE1-amplified subgroup.\n...\nAfter a median follow-up of 11.7 months, the confirmed ORR was 47.1%, DCR was 94.1%, and mPFS reached 14 months, representing a clinically meaningful outcome in a population for whom no targeted therapeutic options currently exist.", "date": "2026-05-14", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Advances in antibody-drug conjugates in cancer - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13332605/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Trispecific constructs targeting PD-L1, B7-H3, and VEGF were designed to simultaneously deliver cytotoxic payloads while blocking immune checkpoints and inhibiting angiogenesis [3].\nThese agents demonstrated enhanced tumor binding, improved internalization, and superior antitumor efficacy compared to monospecific counterparts, while maintaining a favorable safety profile by sparing non-target immune cells.\nSuch designs effectively embed combination therapy within a single molecular entity, maximizing therapeutic synergy.", "date": "2026-07-04", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "AACR 2026", "url": "https://medical.emdserono.com/en_US/medinfo/therapeutic-areas/oncology/congress-presentations/aacr-2026.html", "domain": "medical.emdserono.com", "snippet": "2026 American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) Annual Meeting – April 17-22, 2026 / San Diego, CA", "date": "2026-05-11", "last_updated": "2026-09-21"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "AACR 2026: Emerging Antibody-Drug Conjugates for the ...", "url": "https://www.adcreview.com/conference-coverage/aacr/aacr-2026-emerging-antibody-drug-conjugates-for-the-treatment-of-solid-tumors-part-1/", "domain": "adcreview.com", "snippet": "An active phase 1/2 clinical trial (NCT07354711 and CTR20254555) is underway to evaluate the safety, efficacy, and pharmacokinetics of 3H-10000 in patients with unresectable or metastatic advanced solid tumors.[24]", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] AACR DRUG DISCOVERY AND DEVELOPMENT (AACR D3)", "url": "https://www.aacr.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/D32026_AbstractTitlesOnly.pdf", "domain": "aacr.org", "snippet": "B068 A sphingolipid-derived paclitaxel nanovesicle enhances efficacy of combination therapies in triple-negative breast cancer and pancreatic cancer.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-13"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "American Association for Cancer Research (AACR 2026)", "url": "https://www.jnjmedicalconnect.com/therapeutic-areas/oncology/congress/aacr-2026", "domain": "jnjmedicalconnect.com", "snippet": "Information about pipeline products or investigational uses of products does not imply FDA approval for these products or uses, nor does it establish the safety or efficacy of these products or uses.", "date": "2026-04-17", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "AACR 2026: Why Cancer Breakthroughs No Longer Stand Alone", "url": "https://www.cancerresearch.org/blog/aacr-2026", "domain": "cancerresearch.org", "snippet": "Analyses of more than 24,000 global immunotherapy trials show increasing diversity in therapeutic approaches and combination strategies, along with shifts in how biomarkers guide development.", "date": "2026-04-30", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "b7ddfc63-ba02-47d6-924c-ba21ae0e8702"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:03+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q056", "objective": "MNPS Asset planned or expected data readouts, regulatory submissions, regulatory decisions, and expected timing in Head and Neck Cancer", "anchors": ["MNPS"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q056-1", "query": "MNPS Asset first-line recurrent metastatic head and neck cancer data readout", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "MNPS Asset first-line recurrent metastatic head and neck cancer data readout", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 18810.9, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Internal Server Error", "url": "https://cdas.cancer.gov/approved-projects/5003/", "domain": "cdas.cancer.gov", "snippet": "The server encountered an internal error or misconfiguration and was unable to complete your request.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-10-20"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma...", "url": "https://datalookout.com/head-neck-cancer-clinical-trials", "domain": "datalookout.com", "snippet": "The checkpoint immunotherapy era transformed recurrent/metastatic HNSCC: pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is FDA-approved as first-line monotherapy for high CPS scores and in combination with chemotherapy for all-comers; nivolumab (Opdivo) is approved in platinum-refractory disease.\n...\n|NCT06589804|Cetuximab + pembrolizumab vs pembrolizumab in 2L+ relapsed/metastatic HNSCC|National Cancer Institute (NCI)|Recruiting|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-22"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "First-Line Therapy in Recurrent or Metastatic Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma: A Retrospective, Multicenter, Real-World Study", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/hed.28211", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "In Europe, pembrolizumab with or without chemotherapy is the recommended first-line treatment for recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC), with a combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1.\nIn France, the TPExtreme (TPEx) regimen is preferred for CPS < 1.\n...\nWe reviewed all R/M HNSCC cases treated with first-line regimens between January 2021 and December 2023 in Nancy and Lille.\nData on patient history, treatments, survival, response, and safety were collected.\n...\nThe analysis included 290 patients; 19% received pembrolizumab alone, 28% received pembrolizumab with chemotherapy, 19% received TPEx, and 20% received adapted regimens.\nPFS and OS differed significantly between groups.\nMedian PFS and OS were respectively 2.9 and 9.4 months for pembrolizumab, 6 and 15.5 months for pembrolizumab-chemotherapy, 5.9 and 13.7 months for TPEx, and 4.3 and 10 months for adapted regimens.\nPembrolizumab alone is the safest regimen.\n...\nThis retrospective study reflects real-world first-line therapy for R/M HNSCC in two French cancer institutes.\nPembrolizumab combined with chemotherapy is the preferred treatment option.\n...\nHistorically, the first-line treatment for recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC) has been chemotherapy alone.\n...\nThe European Society for Medical Oncology (ESMO) recommends [9] the use of pembrolizumab for patients with a CPS ≥ 1, potentially associated with chemotherapy, and the EXTREME or TPEx regimens for patients with a CPS < 1.\n...\nThis retrospective, multicentric study included patients treated for R/M HNSCC from January 1st 2021 to December 31th 2023, in Nancy (ICL) and Lille (COL).\n...\nBetween January 1, 2021 and December 31, 2023, 290 patients treated for R/M HNSCC were included: 157 (54%) in ICL and 133 (46%) in COL.\n...\nPFS differed significantly between groups, with a median PFS of 2.9 months [95% CI 2.1–3.7] for pembrolizumab, 6 [4.7–7.4] for pembrolizumab-chemotherapy, 5.9 [4.3–7.4] for TPEx, and 4.3 [3.2–5.4] for adapted regimens (Figure 1).\n...\nPembrolizumab, alone or in combination with chemotherapy, is favored for patients with a CPS ≥ 1, while the TPEx regimen remains common for patients with a CPS < 1.", "date": "2025-11-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Driving Progress in Recurrent and Metastatic Head and Neck Cancer", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13101959/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The KEYNOTE-048 study^8^ established the use of pembrolizumab in the first-line setting, either as monotherapy (limited to patients with PD-L1 ≥ 1 expression) or in combination with platinum and 5-fluorouracil (5-FU).\nAs a result, immunotherapy was established as the core element of standard-of-care in the first-line treatment of platinum-sensitive R/M HNSCC.\nThe updated results from this study with a median follow-up of 45 months have shown an improved median OS in the pembrolizumab group compared to chemotherapy in the CPS ≥ 20 (14.9 vs 10.8 months; HR 0.61; 95% CI 0.46–0.81) and CPS ≥ 1 populations (12.3 vs 10.4 months; HR 0.74; 95% CI 0.61–0.89)^14^.\nAlso the combination of pembrolizumab-chemotherapy group compared to chemotherapy alone demonstrated a benefit in median OS in the PD-L1 CPS ≥ 20 (14.7 vs 11.1 months; HR 0.62; 95% CI 0.46–0.84), CPS ≥ 1 (13.6 vs 10.6 months;\n...\nThe phase 4 study KEYNOTE-B10^15^ was a non-randomized, single arm study with ORR reaching 49% in the overall population.\nDespite the inherent differences between real-world populations and clinical trial cohorts, pembrolizumab-based regimens have shown comparable OS results in the real-world population to previously demonstrated in the KEYNOTE-048 trial^16,17^.\n...\nDespite biological^10^ and technical limitations, PD-L1 remains a dominant factor to guiding first-line treatment decision.\n...\nRetrospective analyses have demonstrated no statistically significant difference in survival outcomes between patients who discontinued versus continued immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy at one- or two-year timepoints^55^.\n...\nPreliminary results demonstrated an ORR of around 32% in the first-line setting for patients with PD-L1 CPS ≥1^58^.\n...\nImmune checkpoint blockade with different agents such as nivolumab, pembrolizumab or toripalimab paired with EGFR inhibitors have demonstrated overall response rates (ORR) ranging from 30% to 55% in immune-naïve populations^86,87,88,89^.\n...\nPetosentamab (MCLA-158), a bispecific antibody that targets the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) and the leucine-rich repeat containing G-protein-coupled receptor 5 (LGR5), currently holds two breakthrough therapy designations by the FDA for patients with R/M HNSCC who have experienced disease progression following treatment with platinum-based chemotherapy and a PD-L1/PD-1 inhibitor^91^ and also in the first-line setting in combination with pembrolizumab for patients with PD-L1 (CPS≥1)^92^.\nThe combination of petosentamab with pembrolizumab continues to be investigated in the front-line setting (NCT03526835).\nPreliminary results have shown ORR of 60% (50% for p16+ and 66% for p16-HNSCC).\n...\nThe phase II/III FORTIFI-HN01 study (NCT06788990) is currently evaluating the combination of pembrolizumab with ficerafusp alfa (BCA101) vs placebo in the first-line setting for PD-L1-positive R/M HNSCC^95^.\n...\nPrevious data suggest that second-line treatment should be considered after IO progression in patients with HNSCC, even in cases with poor PS^98^.", "date": "2026-07-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "First-line therapy for recurrent metastatic head and neck ...", "url": "https://tcr.amegroups.org/article/view/103355/html", "domain": "tcr.amegroups.org", "snippet": "Current first line treatment of locoregionally advanced HNSCC includes primary surgery followed by risk-adapted radiation with or without chemotherapy or with concurrent definitive chemoradiotherapy (CRT) (2).\n...\nKEYNOTE-048 (KN-048) established chemoimmunotherapy and single agent pembrolizumab as possible treatment regimens for newly diagnosed platinum sensitive R/M HNSCC.\n...\nResults showed that, compared to E, patients on the P arm had significantly increased OS in PD-L1 positive patients.\nHowever, P was not superior to E in the total population.\nCompared to E, the PC arm showed significantly increased OS in the all biomarker subgroups, with a safety profile comparable to E.\nThese results led to the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approval of PC as a first line treatment in the platinum sensitive R/M HNSCC population, regardless of PD-L1 status, and P in the PD-L1 positive [combined positive score (CPS) ≥1] population.\n...\nIn KEYNOTE-B10 (KN-B10), Dzienis\n...\n*discusses the results of a phase IV clinical trial exploring carboplatin, paclitaxel, and pembrolizumab (CPP) as first line therapy for R/M HNSCC (6).\n...\nThis study enrolled 101 patients (81 PD-L1 CPS ≥1, 20 PD-L1 CPS <1).\nThe confirmed ORR was 50%, with 8% of patients achieving a complete response (CR).\nThe median PFS was 5.6 months, with an estimated 12-month PFS rate of 12%.\nThe median OS was 13.1 months, with a 12-month OS rate of 52%.\n...\ntrAEs were reported in 95% of patients, with the most common being neutropenia, anemia, fatigue, and alopecia.\n...\nResults of KN-B10 appear promising and suggest that combination carboplatin/paclitaxel/pembrolizumab therapy provides antitumor activity similar to platinum/5-FU/pembrolizumab.", "date": "2025-07-30", "last_updated": "2025-08-30"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Current Therapy for Metastatic Head and Neck Cancer: Evidence, Opportunities, and Challenges", "url": "https://ascopubs.org/doi/10.1200/EDBK_350442", "domain": "ascopubs.org", "snippet": "1,2However, as systemic therapy has been more widely studied, the reproducible impact of multidrug chemotherapy on response and survival for the majority of fit patients has become clear.\n...\nNew drug development in metastatic HNSCC now takes place in the context of a clear role for first-line PD-1 inhibitor therapy when the cancer expresses PD-L1.\n...\n5The novel combination of chemotherapy with pembrolizumab improved overall survival (OS) compared with chemotherapy plus cetuximab in the total population (13 months vs. 10.7 months; HR, 0.77; p = .0034).\n...\n11Among patients with PD-L1 CPS greater than 20, survival was superior at 14.9 months for pembrolizumab monotherapy compared with 12.3 months for chemotherapy plus cetuximab.\nSimilarly, pembrolizumab was superior when PD-L1 CPS was 1 or greater, with OS of 12.3 compared with 10.3 months.\nAmong all patients treated with pembrolizumab, the inclusion of chemotherapy led to a higher objective response, and among HPV+ cancers, the relative benefit of pembrolizumab plus chemotherapy was strikingly greater than the benefit of pembrolizumab monotherapy when compared with chemotherapy and cetuximab, perhaps explained by the intrinsic chemosensitivity of HPV+ HNSCC.", "date": "2022-04-30", "last_updated": "2025-02-14"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Real world treatment patterns for recurrent and metastatic ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/oncology/articles/10.3389/fonc.2025.1577509/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "While KEYNOTE-048 established anti-PD1 with or without chemotherapy as first-line treatment for recurrent and metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) with combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1, treatment choice remains ambiguous given additional toxicity of combination treatment.\n...\nPatients treated first-line with anti-PD1 monotherapy, anti-PD1+chemotherapy, or cetuximab+chemotherapy in the Flatiron Health database were included.\n...\nOf 2577 patients included, Anti-PD1 monotherapy (n=1410) improved survival over cetuximab+chemotherapy (n=577, median survival 14.6 vs. 12.6 months, p=0.015), while anti-PD1+chemotherapy (n=590) showed a nonsignificant trend towards improvement (median survival 14.3 vs.\n12.6 months, p=0.053).\nIn HPV-associated disease, survival was equal between regimens.\nAddition of chemotherapy improved survival over anti-PD1 monotherapy in non-HPV associated tumors with CPS 1-9 (median survival 18.0 vs. 10.3 months, p=0.029) and in oral cavity primaries (median survival 10.3 vs. 7.6 months, p=0.003).\n...\nIn this cohort, 60.4% of patients had non-HPV associated disease, 32.6% had HPV-associated disease, and 7.0% had unknown HPV status or primary tumor site.\n1410 patients (54.7%) were treated with anti-PD1 monotherapy, 577 (22.4%) with combination anti-PD1 plus chemotherapy, and 590 (22.9%) with cetuximab plus chemotherapy in the front-line setting for recurrent or metastatic disease.\n...\nOut of 2577 patients, 1128 patients (43.8%) were reported to have received second line treatment (**Supplementary Tables 3, 4**).\nOf the 365 patients who received second line treatment after front line treatment with Cetuximab + chemotherapy, 191 (52.3%) of patients received an immunotherapy containing regimen.\n...\nThe median follow-up interval from initiation of front-line treatment was 9.7 months (range 0.03 – 131 months).\nIn the overall cohort, patients treated with anti-PD1 monotherapy had significantly improved survival compared to patients treated with cetuximab plus chemotherapy (median survival 14.6 months versus 12.6 months, p = 0.015 on log rank test, **Figure 1B**).\n...\nIn contrast, patients with HPV associated tumors did not have significantly improved survival after treatment with either anti-PD1 monotherapy (median survival 21.1 months, p = 0.972) or combination anti-PD1 plus chemotherapy (median survival 17.5 months, p = 0.381) compared to patients treated with cetuximab plus chemotherapy (median survival 20.2 months, **Figure 2B**).", "date": "2025-05-02", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Real-world treatment patterns and outcomes among ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10241070/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Pembrolizumab, a PD-1 immune checkpoint inhibitor, is approved as first-line (1L) treatment for recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC) as monotherapy or in combination with platinum and 5-fluorouracil chemotherapy.\nLimited data exist on the use of these regimens in real-world settings.\n...\nA 4-year follow-up study found continued survival benefits with 1L pembrolizumab-containing therapies compared to the EXTREME regimen (13).\nConsistent with the FDA approval, the National Comprehensive Cancer Network’s 2022 Clinical Practice Guidelines for Head and Neck Cancers recommend pembrolizumab monotherapy for tumors that express PD-L1 with CPS ≥1 (category 1) and in combination with platinum and 5-FU chemotherapy (category 1) as the preferred 1L treatment option for R/M HNSCC (14).\n...\nA total of 1,952 adults ≥18 years of age had a diagnosis of R/M HNSCC and initiated 1L therapy between July 1, 2019 and December 31, 2021, of whom 1,779 met all study criteria ( **Figure 1** ).\nAmong this group, 749 individuals received pembrolizumab-containing therapies, of whom 431 (57.5%) received pembrolizumab monotherapy, 215 (28.7%) received pembrolizumab in combination with platinum and 5-FU (‘pembrolizumab plus chemotherapy’; 84.2% of this group received carboplatin and 15.8% received cisplatin), and 103 (13.8%) received a different pembrolizumab combination therapy.\n...\nDistant metastatic disease (HNSCC with distant recurrence OR Stage IVc at initial diagnosis) was reported for 56.8% of the study population, HNSCC with locoregional recurrence for 23.1%, HNSCC not cured at initial diagnosis for 16.4%, and HPV-positive Stage IV oropharyngeal tumor at initial diagnosis for 3.7%.\nThe ECOG PS on the index date was 0–1 for 66.1% of the study population, 2–4 for 20.9%, and unknown or not documented for 13.0%.\nEvidence of PD-L1 testing of any kind was identified for 71.7% of the study population, and a CPS test result was available for 352 individuals (54.5%).\n...\nThe median (range) follow-up periods were 7.9 (0.0–35.1) months for individuals who received 1L pembrolizumab monotherapy and 8.5 (0.1–35.1) months for those who received 1L pembrolizumab plus chemotherapy.\n...\nThe median (95% CI) rwToT for the monotherapy group was 4.2 (3.5–4.6) months, while the median (95% CI) rwTTNT was 6.5 (5.4–7.4) months **Figures 2A, C, E** );\n...\nIn conclusion, this study provides information on the use of pembrolizumab-containing therapies for HNSCC in real-world US settings.\nOS outcomes were comparable between the pembrolizumab monotherapy and pembrolizumab plus chemotherapy groups.\nThe survival outcomes of both groups were also similar to the corresponding data observed in a registration clinical trial, despite the real-world study population being more heterogeneous.", "date": "2023-05-22", "last_updated": "2026-07-13"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "���K�� �%?��ב�o�iD@��͞ ��G�� �*<�'�[J�s��. ^�E���ٝ8P������Ԃ�o- ���e�A �>k�3ۓ\"a��bS�p&1��U�t�L\\}����B�{~�R�)?���>U��E��۾k� �K�ͷ,� �q�3R���--�炊H�! ��� @�u�+�ao�Ǥ���Ι6LSq�֚���[,��;�((��N���j���k5W�V�\\m�gf��W�d��K�-����߿��k y�T�N�%���4�q�غbN�� � �e��!C$��t�l�π!BwAo_���@M����%�b9�����HF �w�^���]�,8! ���C��L��#-Ի߳�Pm�W�-�i��i�� 2 \"�?#�Uc���3�k�K\\ �5A6��-�ʯ_'4� bq��zx���N7�+���'- L3(��zE�0��k2J�@b00��`l� �tj�UA�8�;J���d7V��R����W�90�.�EŃ\"��D(���J��ØLP;4ѻ�! ��F��$! �h0FƔ����f/�'����&UW+�e2!^ ��L���g�.u�B�H��G�Mzۼ�F ���(O�� WBU� G ��SM��bt��1�e�\\b�k$I���-4�*�@ib�B � �Y�[pf�?�v-�q��˥WMڕkX�L*��鳞gӽ�&��������=���@V+ߴio SW�����b��8! � -B�̠W Kp��d��� 6�ATQ�|��ɑh!$ꩠ�r�s�۴3����r�PZډ2�[X�����> ƈ1��F�p��Z�9��9�qح�К�S���Ԉ�Ӏ4X�\"��š��T��vf���\\�3����zˊ�YYw �B�' �@M4�w;ࠠ�Zj�� kUXNj�@���lA j�! �5&B��N��� f�hD�{GV��Y����� b�̱��Ι���F%��Q4�����Sn\"���Q�L_/R�����,N�l�(c��r�l^{�6��%����' t�m�� �,J��ЌlM�]a U��A�������jd �A;lS�!v�A�~X���e��! ������Z D@�P\"@�ز`L��KNګ[8��Z�2i�d�r�=��|�� �(�V�e