ng first-line treatment, OS benefit with nivolumab (*n* = 50) versus IC (*n* = 26) was maintained (median: 7.7 months versus 3.3 months; hazard ratio: 0.56; 95% confidence interval, 0.34-0.94) at 2 years.\n...\nIn summary, this long-term 2-year analysis of CheckMate 141 supports the use of nivolumab as a first-line treatment for patients with platinum-refractory R/M SCCHN.\n...\nAt 2-year follow-up, among patients who received first-line treatment for R/M SCCHN, nivolumab prolonged OS versus IC (median, 7.7 months versus 3.3 months; hazard ratio [HR], 0.56; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.34-0.94) (Figure 1).", "date": "2022-03-04", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}], "raw": {"id": "c05e068e-7233-45c6-b9b0-8d65e9ab3acc"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:08+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q058", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo efficacy in combination with another agent in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma, including the combination partner", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q058-1", "query": "HEC234055 combination pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma in vivo", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HEC234055 combination pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma in vivo", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 14475.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] E262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4 - HKEXnews", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301899.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "The drug candidate under development HEC234055 is an orally available high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the Group, designed to target a broad spectrum of multiple KRAS mutations as well as wild-type amplifications.\n...\nSignificant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models\n(including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce\ndose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression.\n...\nanti-VEGF\nantibodies,\n...\ninhibitors,\nand\nKRAS\nG12C\ninhibitors all exhibit significant synergistic effects.\n...\nThe preclinical research data disclosed in this announcement are derived from laboratory and animal model studies, and the relevant drug candidates have not yet undergone clinical trials\nin\nhumans.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-23"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Unbiased in vivo preclinical evaluation of anticancer drugs identifies effective therapy for the treatment of pancreatic adenocarcinoma - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33199632/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "To identify effective PDAC therapies, we leveraged a syngeneic orthotopic PDAC transplant mouse model to perform a large-scale, in vivo screen of 16 single-agent and 41 two-drug targeted therapy combinations in mice.\nAmong 57 drug conditions screened, combined inhibition of heat shock protein (Hsp)-90 and MEK was found to produce robust suppression of tumor growth, leading to an 80% increase in the survival of PDAC-bearing mice with no significant toxicity.\nMechanistically, we observed that single-agent MEK inhibition led to compensatory activation of resistance pathways, including components of the PI3K/AKT/mTOR signaling axis, which was overcome with the addition of HSP90 inhibition.\nThe combination of HSP90(i) + MEK(i) was also active in vitro in established human PDAC cell lines and in vivo in patient-derived organoid PDAC transplant models.\nThese findings encourage the clinical development of HSP90(i) + MEK(i) combination therapy and highlight the power of clinically relevant in vivo model systems for identifying cancer therapies.", "date": "2020-12-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-20"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma: Treatment hurdles, tumor ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7031151/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Τhe pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma microenvironment consists of a significant hurdle for the efficient application of chemotherapy drugs or immunotherapeutic compounds.", "date": "2020-02-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-27"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Contact us", "url": "https://www.malacards.org/card/pancreatic_ductal_adenocarcinoma", "domain": "malacards.org", "snippet": "|9|A Randomized Phase 2b/Phase 3 Study of the TGF-β2 Targeting Antisense Oligonucleotide OT-101 in Combination With mFOLFIRINOX Compared With mFOLFIRINOX Alone in Patients With Advanced and Unresectable or Metastatic Pancreatic Cancer|Recruiting|NCT06079346|Phase 2, Phase 3|OT-101; mFOLFIRINOX|", "date": "2025-02-24", "last_updated": "2025-07-01"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "A rapid in vivo screen for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma therapeutics", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4610235/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "We show that warfarin or an Axl kinase inhibitor (BGB324), in combination with gemcitabine and nab-paclitaxel, significantly reduce tumor initiation and growth.", "date": "2015-10-01", "last_updated": "2025-06-10"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Preclinical Models of Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma and Their Utility in Immunotherapy Studies", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/13/3/440", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "We also discuss patient-derived xenograft mouse models, human PDAC organoids, and ex vivo slice cultures of human PDAC tumors that can complement murine models for a more comprehensive approach to predict response and resistance to immunotherapy regimens.\n...\nAs a result, the combination of pan-ERBB inhibitors and MEK inhibitors demonstrates higher anti-tumor activity than either agent against PDAC organoids in vitro as well as in orthotopically implanted organoids [131].\n...\nIt should be noted that while the organoid cultures are useful for in vitro testing of anti-tumor responses, they cannot be used to test the safety of immunotherapy drugs.", "date": "2021-01-25", "last_updated": "2026-08-13"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Concerted cell and in vivo screen for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDA) chemotherapeutics", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7693321/", "domain": "ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In vivo 30 *,*the combination of GEM + TSA + JQ1 significantly reduced initiation and growth of spontaneous tumors.", "date": "2024-03-14", "last_updated": "2024-09-28"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma: Current and Evolving ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5535831/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Its importance has been confirmed by in vitro studies, in which knockdown of miR-155 resulted in significant decrease in expression of EGFR and KRAS, proteins essential for PDAC development, as well as reduced cell proliferation and colony formation [246].", "date": "2017-06-22", "last_updated": "2026-08-04"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "In vivo Mouse Models of Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://pancreapedia.org/node/9843", "domain": "pancreapedia.org", "snippet": "Patient-derived PDAC as primary or passaged cancer cells (PDX mice) or those allowed to spontaneously develop into 3D structures (PDOX mice) can be introduced into immunocompromised mice and then employed in a variety of studies to evaluate therapies, assess metabolic changes, and determine genomic/transcriptomic profiles.", "date": "2021-09-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov · pmc · articlesReciprocal regulation of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma ...", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7945295/", "domain": "ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "We found that HNF4α is a major activator of the Classical gene expression program and restrains PDAC growth in multiple models of the disease.\n...\nTo test the effect of HNF4α restoration *in vivo*, we first injected two murine (HC800 and HC569) and one human (PDX220) cell lines subcutaneously into mice fed control or dox-containing chow to induce HNF4α.\nHA-tagged HNF4α was robustly expressed in tumors of mice on dox chow (Figure S2E and S2H).\nExogenous HNF4α significantly inhibited tumor growth of all three PDAC cell lines as assessed by both volume and mass (Figure 2B), and this was accompanied by a significantly lower proliferation rate than controls (Figure 2C).\n...\nWe found that these cells grew readily in the peritoneum, forming numerous macroscopic nodules on the serosal surface of intraperitoneal organs.\nIn a formal survival study, dox-mediated induction of HNF4α reduced tumor burden at 20 days post injection (Figure 2D) and significantly increased overall survival (Figure 2E).\n...\nTaken together, these results show that restoring HNF4α can induce major changes in differentiation state and inhibit PDAC growth both *in vitro* and *in vivo*.\n...\nBoth HC800 SIX4-negative clones formed tumors that were significantly smaller (as assessed by volume and mass) and less proliferative than their control counterparts (Figure 7B).\nHC569 *Six4*-knockout clone (H#6) also displayed significantly impaired growth *in vivo* (Figure 7C).\n...\nThus, tumors arising from DKO cells displayed smaller sizes, lower weights and a significant reduction in proliferation compared control tumors (Figure 8 B–C).\n...\nThe mechanisms underlying PDAC subtype specification have not been fully characterized.\nHere we show that HNF4α, SIX4 and SIX1 are key lineage specifiers that coordinately regulate growth and molecular subtype of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC).\nHNF4α restrains PDAC progression, activates expression of genes associated with the Classical subtype, and directly represses expression of SIX4 and SIX1.", "date": "2020-08-21", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}], "raw": {"id": "5c60c818-b7b9-419d-8d8b-73a17e690d7b"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:12+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q053", "objective": "ADT-1004 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["ADT-1004"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q053-5", "query": "ADT-1004 MIA-AMG-R xenograft final tumour volume monotherapy", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "ADT-1004 MIA-AMG-R xenograft final tumour volume monotherapy", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 2, "latency_ms": 463.7, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "ADT-1004: a first-in-class, oral pan-RAS inhibitor with robust ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11905721/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "MIA PaCa-2 cells that developed resistance to sotorasib and adagrasib (MIA-AMG-R) cells were only slightly less sensitive to ADT-007 with an IC~50~ value of 16 nM, but completely lost sensitivity to sotorasib and adagrasib (Fig.\n...\nNext, experiments utilizing NSG mice xenografted subcutaneously with parental MIA PaCa-2 and MIA-AMG-R cells were conducted.\nMice implanted with parental MIA PaCa-2 cells showed comparable decreased tumor volume (Fig. 7E) and final tumor weights (Fig. 7F and G), following treatment with ADT-1004, sotorasib or adagrasib, each at a once-daily dose of 40 mg/kg (5x/week).\n...\nIn a side-by-side comparison, oral administration of ADT-1004 led to a dramatic decrease in tumor volume (Fig. 7I) and weight (Fig. 7J and K) compared with vehicle against tumors derived from MIA-AMG-R cells.\nBy contrast, neither sotorasib nor adagrasib were effective.", "date": "2025-03-13", "last_updated": "2026-02-17"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "ADT-1004 / Auburn University, ADT Pharma - LARVOL DELTA", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=6ff25253-89b9-4c9d-9da5-c4334486e2e5", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "(AACR 2024)\n...\n- \"ADT-1004 represents a 1st-in-class pan-RAS inhibitor with potential advantages over mutant-specific KRAS inhibitors for greater efficacy and ability to avert mechanisms of resistance.\nThese data support future clinical trials of ADT-1004 as a monotherapy for the treatment of patients with PDA regardless of the underlying mutation.\"", "date": "2025-12-31", "last_updated": "2026-02-17"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Efficacy advantages of a novel pan-RAS inhibitor, ADT-1004 ...", "url": "https://discovery.researcher.life/article/abstract-c027-efficacy-advantages-of-a-novel-pan-ras-inhibitor-adt-1004-over-mutant-specific-kras-inhibitors-for-suppressing-pancreatic-tumor-growth-in-murine-models/6d9618bf7c8b370e8e70b20f0bc3806f", "domain": "discovery.researcher.life", "snippet": "ADT-1004 also inhibited growth of KRASG12C mutant MIA PaCa-2 tumors to a comparable extent as sotorasib and adagrasib.\nSotorasib and adagrasib were ineffective against xenografted tumors of resistant cells derived from MIA PaCa-2 cells, while ADT-1004 strongly inhibited tumor growth.", "date": "2024-09-15", "last_updated": "2025-12-17"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Ivan Babic", "url": "https://www.scholarbro.com/a/ivan-babic-2292985625", "domain": "scholarbro.com", "snippet": "An orally bioavailable prodrug of ADT-1004, ADT-1004, demonstrated favorable tolerability and suppressed tumor growth in orthotopic and patient-derived xenograft models of pancreatic cancer, accompanied by reductions in activated RAS and p-ERK levels (Mol Cancer, 2025).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-16"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Abstract LB322: Antitumor activity of 1st-in-class pan-RAS inhibitor ADT-1004 in mouse models of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://aacrjournals.org/cancerres/article/83/8_Supplement/LB322/725683/Abstract-LB322-Antitumor-activity-of-1st-in-class", "domain": "aacrjournals.org", "snippet": "Once daily oral administration of ADT-1004 significantly inhibited the growth of orthotopically implanted PDA tumors with a corresponding reduction in RAS/MAPK signaling.\n...\nThese data support future clinical trials of ADT-1004 as a monotherapy for the treatment of patients with PDA regardless of the underlying mutation.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-04"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Developing and Characterizing the Anti-Tumor Efficacy of a ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0006497124099531", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-10"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Broad-Spectrum RAS Inhibition in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma: Mechanistic Advances and Therapeutic Promise", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8247/18/12/1788", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "Its prodrug, ADT-1004, exhibited improved pharmacokinetic stability and potent tumor regression in PDAC xenografts and syngeneic models.\n...\nOral administration of ADT-1004 demonstrated significant antitumor activity across multiple PDAC models, comprising orthotopically implanted murine cell lines and patient-derived xenografts (PDXs) possessing various KRAS mutations.\nThe therapy successfully suppressed RAS activation and subsequent MAPK signaling, as seen by reduced RAS-GTP and pERK levels.", "date": "2025-11-24", "last_updated": "2026-08-12"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "ADT-1004: a first-in-class, oral pan-RAS inhibitor with ...", "url": "https://consensus.app/papers/details/b731970c374f5dfd87dedeb853d681e0/", "domain": "consensus.app", "snippet": "ADT-1004 shows strong antitumor activity in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma models with unique selectivity for KRAS mutant cells, supporting clinical trials for KRAS mutant PDAC.\n- PopulationOlder adults (50-71 years)\n- Sample size24", "date": "2025-03-13", "last_updated": "2025-11-24"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Androgen Receptor Pathway Inhibitor Monotherapy in ...", "url": "https://www.research.unipd.it/retrieve/4642338e-0d77-4002-bbf7-04dfd1fb797a/Tama%20%C2%B4s%20Fazekas%20et%20al%20Eur%20Uro%20Focus%202025.pdf", "domain": "research.unipd.it", "snippet": "Similarly, Barrett et al [24] found durable reductions in tumor volume at 3 mo (–52%), 6 mo (–30%), and 18 mo (–\n20%).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-24"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "A potent and selective pan-RAS inhibitor, ADT-1004, targeting complex KRAS mutations for pancreatic cancer.", "url": "https://ascopubs.org/doi/pdf/10.1200/JCO.2024.42.16_suppl.e15085", "domain": "ascopubs.org", "snippet": "#### Article CitationA potent and selective pan-RAS inhibitor, ADT-1004, targeting complex KRAS mutations for pancreatic cancer..", "date": "2024-06-01", "last_updated": "2025-03-09"}], "raw": {"id": "15e4b279-1d20-4e38-8a31-f55e2a88145b"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:17+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q059", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo animal models and results: cell-line xenograft (CDX), patient-derived xenograft (PDX), syngeneic, orthotopic, genetically engineered models", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q059-4", "query": "HEC234055 orthotopic pancreatic model", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HEC234055 orthotopic pancreatic model", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 425.8, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "LP – Orthotopic Tumor Models", "url": "https://td2inc.com/orthotopic-tumor-models", "domain": "td2inc.com", "snippet": "TD2 has developed a comprehensive library of orthotopic models in mouse and human cell lines (Figure 1) at various implantation sites.\n...\n|Intrapancreatic|Pano2|\n...\n**Pan02 Syngeneic Orthotopic Pancreatic Tumor Model**", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "An Orthotopic Resectional Mouse Model of Pancreatic Cancer - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33044459/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "To address this deficiency, we describe a mouse model involving orthotopic implantation of PC followed by distal pancreatectomy and splenectomy.\nThe model has been demonstrated to be safe and suitably flexible for the study of various therapeutic approaches in adjuvant and neo adjuvant settings.\nIn this model, a pancreatic tumor is first generated by implanting a mixture of human pancreatic cancer cells (luciferase-tagged AsPC-1) and human cancer associated pancreatic stellate cells into the distal pancreas of Balb/c athymic nude mice.\nAfter three weeks, the cancer is resected by re-laparotomy, distal pancreatectomy and splenectomy.\nIn this model, bioluminescence imaging can be used to follow the progress of cancer development and effects of resection/treatments.\nFollowing resection, adjuvant therapy can be given.\nAlternatively, neoadjuvant treatment can be given prior to resection.\n...\nAll mice underwent successful distal pancreatectomy/splenectomy with no issues of hemostasis.\nA macroscopic proximal pancreatic margin greater than 5 mm was achieved in 43 (96%) mice.\nThe technical success rate of pancreatic resection was 100%, with 0% early mortality and morbidity.\nNone of the animals died during the week after resection.\nIn summary, we describe a robust and reproducible technique for a surgical resection model of pancreatic cancer in mice which mimics the clinical scenario.\nThe model may be useful for the testing of both adjuvant and neoadjuvant treatments.", "date": "2020-09-24", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Orthotopic Models", "url": "https://dda.creative-bioarray.com/orthotopic-models.html", "domain": "dda.creative-bioarray.com", "snippet": "Orthotopic tumor models (also known as* in situ* models) are created by injecting tumor cells/tissue fragments into the organ of origin.\nUnlike subcutaneous xenografts, orthotopically implanted tumors grow within the organ microenvironment, reconstituting more realistic stromal, vascular and immune interactions.\n...\n|Spleen/Intra-splenic|Pancreatic/Colorectal|PANC-1, MIA PaCa-2|\n...\n|Pancreas|Pancreatic Cancer|BxPC-3, PANC-1|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] E262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4 - HKEXnews", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301899.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "HEC234055 — Orally Available High-Activity Pan-RAS Molecular Glue Inhibitor\nTargeting the ‘‘Undruggable’’ (Abstract #2634)\n...\nThe drug candidate under development HEC234055 is an orally available high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the Group, designed to target a broad spectrum of multiple KRAS mutations as well as wild-type amplifications.\n...\nBroad-spectrum coverage and high activity: It can act on both active (guanosine triphosphate (‘‘GTP’’)-bound) and inactive (GDP-bound) states of KRAS, and covers various mutation types such as G12D, G12C, G12V, G13D, Q61H, and wild-type amplification.\n...\nSignificant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models\n(including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce\ndose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression.\n...\nThe preclinical research data disclosed in this announcement are derived from laboratory and animal model studies, and the relevant drug candidates have not yet undergone clinical trials\nin\nhumans.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-23"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Orthotopic models of human pancreatic cancer - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10415855/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Orthotopic transplantation of solid tumor fragments of human tumors in nude mice reproduces their pattern of local growth and distal dissemination.\nWhile lymphatic, hepatic or peritoneal dissemination can be reproduced, perineural invasion is absent.\nEarly passages (less than 3) of xenografts show a high degree of stability regarding K-ras, p53 and p16 gene status.\nOn the other hand, advanced passages of tumors acquire additional alterations in the p15 and Smad4 genes.\nMutations in K-ras, p53, p15 and Smad4 genes can be acquired, in this model system, in the more advanced stages of pancreatic tumor dissemination.\nFinally, it is also possible to standardize local growth of these tumors as well as its dissemination pattern giving us a preclinical tool to evaluate the anticancer activity of new drugs.", "date": "1999-06-30", "last_updated": "2025-07-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinically relevant orthotopic pancreatic cancer models for ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38191243/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** Using flow cytometry and immunohistochemistry, we characterized the immunological features and trafficking patterns of adoptively transferred T cells in orthotopic PDAC (C57BL/6) models using two mouse cell lines, KPC-Luc and MT-5, isolated from C57BL/6 KPC-GEMM (Kras^LSL-G12D/+^p53^-/-^ and Kras^LSL-G12D/+^p53^LSL-R172H/+^, respectively).\n...\n** The MT-5 orthotopic model best recapitulates the cellular and stromal features of the TME in the PDAC GEMM.\nIn contrast, far more host immune cells infiltrate the KPC-Luc tumors, which have less stroma, although CD4^+^ and CD8^+^ T cells were similarly detected in the MT-5 tumors compared with KPC-GEMM in mice.\n...\n** Our data establish for the first time a reproducible and realistic clinical system useful for modeling stroma-rich and stroma-devoid PDAC tumors.\nThese models shall serve an indepth study of how to overcome barriers that limit antitumor activity of adoptively transferred T cells.", "date": "2024-01-08", "last_updated": "2026-07-19"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Frontiers | Orthotopic and Heterotopic Murine Models of Pancreatic Cancer Exhibit Different Immunological Microenvironments and Different Responses to Immunotherapy", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2022.863346/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Herein, we describe surgical techniques for a novel orthotopic implantation of syngeneic pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) tissue slices.\nThis method has a high success modeling rate and stable growth kinetics, which makes it useful for testing novel therapeutics.\n...\nIn summary, this study describes construction of a novel orthotopic PDAC model through implantation of tissue slices and discusses resistance to immunotherapy from the perspective of a PDAC microenvironment.\n...\nTo obtain the tumor graft, Panc02 cells were subcutaneously inoculated in 6-week-old nude mice (2 × 10^7^ cells in each mouse).\nTumors were allowed to grow for 2-3 weeks until they were approximately 8~10 mm in diameter.\nNext, tumor samples were obtained and immediately transferred into ice-cold UW (University of Wisconsin) preservation solution.\nEach tumor was then wrapped in 5% low-melt agar and quickly sectioned using a tissue slicer at a thickness of 500μm.\n...\nAfter disinfection and sterilization, ophthalmic scissors were used to cut a 1 cm port above the spleen.\nAtraumatic forceps were then used to gently squeeze out the pancreas tail with spleen.\nThe capsule of the pancreas was opened with blunt dissection.\nNext, the open edge of the capsule was gently lifted and a tumor slice was carefully inserted into the subcapsular space.\nThe graft was fitted to the pancreas with 2μL of Vetbond (3M, 1469SB), followed by reinforcement using 5-0 absorbable sutures.\nFinally, the spleen and pancreas were incorporated into the abdominal cavity and the abdomen was closed.\n...\nThe pancreas was exposed following the same steps described above.\nBefore injection, the injection areas were warmed using 37°C saline.\nNext, 3×10^6^ Panc02 cells resuspended in 30μL Matrigel (Yeasen, 40134ES08) were immediately administered into the pancreas capsule.\nBefore needle drawing, a heating lamp was used to heat the injection site to promote solidification of the Matrigel.\nFinally, the spleen and pancreas were incorporated into the abdominal cavity and the abdomen was closed.\n...\nBriefly, the pre-formed tumor was uniformly sliced and used as a “seed”, which was then sutured with an absorbable thread to form a tumor in the tail of the mice pancreas (Figures 1A, B).\nHerein, we established and evaluated PDAC models using the TST and OI methods, respectively.\n...\nResults showed that the TST method formed larger orthotopic tumor sizes than the OI method but had a smaller coefficient of variation (9.9% vs. 53.57%), with the TST method exhibiting more stable growth kinetics (Figure 1C).\n...\nIn conclusion, the TST method is a reliable method for developing pancreatic cancer orthotopic mice models.", "date": "2022-07-07", "last_updated": "2026-05-18"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinically relevant orthotopic pancreatic cancer models ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10806555/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The MT-5 orthotopic model best recapitulates the cellular and stromal features of the TME in the PDAC GEMM.\n...\n## WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN ON THIS TOPIC\n...\n- To combat this barrier, we present a reproducible alternative: orthotopic implantation of genetically defined syngeneic murine cell lines into the pancreas of C57BL/6 mice.\n...\nOver time, these GEMMs have given rise to useful syngeneic cell lines that can be orthotopically implanted into the pancreas of C57BL/6 mice to generate organotropic tumors.\n...\nFor orthotopic experiments, 2×10^5^ murine PDAC cells were orthotopically implanted into the tail of the pancreas of 6-week to 8-week-old female 16 C57BL/6 mice (n=8 per cell line; Strain 000664, The Jackson Laboratory).\n...\nOrthotopic implantation of tumor cells into the pancreas of immune-competent mice represents a practical approach for testing immunotherapy in vivo.\n...\nOrthotopic KPC-Luc was a more aggressive model, with a median survival of mice at 23 days, while MT-5-bearing mice survived a median time of 36 days (figure 1B).", "date": "2024-01-08", "last_updated": "2026-03-22"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Inoculation of Pan02 cells produces tumor nodules in mouse ...", "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0300723", "domain": "journals.plos.org", "snippet": "Collectively, these data indicate that a 5x10^6^ Pan02-luc IP inoculation in syngeneic mice produces orthotopic pancreatic tumor consistently by day 7.\n...\nIn summary, we have developed and characterized a novel and high efficiency orthotopic pancreatic tumor model based on a one-step Pan02 pancreatic ductal carcinoma inoculation into the peritoneum.", "date": "2024-03-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "pone.0077065 1..10", "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/file?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0077065&type=printable", "domain": "journals.plos.org", "snippet": "Here, we describe the establishment and clinical, pathological, molecular and genetic validation of a murine, orthotopic xenograft model of PDAC.\nMethods: Human PDACs were resected and orthotopically implanted and propagated in immunocompromised mice.\n...\nResults: Fifteen human PDACs were propagated orthotopically in mice.\nXenograft-bearing mice developed peritoneal and liver metastases.\nTime to tumor growth and metastatic efficiency in mice each correlated with patient survival.\n...\nPropagated tumors closely exhibited the genetic and molecular features known to characterize pancreatic cancer (e.g. high rate of KRAS, P53, SMAD4 mutation and EGFR activation).\nThe correlation coefficient of gene expression between patient tumors and xenografts propagated through multiple generations was 93 to 99%.\n...\nConclusions: The orthotopic xenograft model derived from fresh human PDACs closely recapitulates the clinical, pathologic, genetic and molecular aspects of human disease.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-11-27"}], "raw": {"id": "691bc901-167d-4a83-8863-24111b34e838"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:17+00:00"}
