"title": "MMRRC Repository", "url": "https://www.mmrrc.org/", "domain": "mmrrc.org", "snippet": "These mice, especially the 5XFAD strain, are valuable for modeling Alzheimer's disease, allowing researchers to closely examine how AD pathology develops.\n...\nIn groundbreaking research, scientists have elucidated the effects of neuroinflammation on brain metabolism in Alzheimer's disease using the APPswe/PS1dE9 mouse model (MMRRC Strain # 034829).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "All Catalog Mouse Models - Ingenious targeting laboratory", "url": "https://www.genetargeting.com/all-catalog-mouse-models/", "domain": "genetargeting.com", "snippet": "Search our extensive catalog of over 14,774 genetically engineered mouse models including knockout, knockin, humanized, Cre driver, and reporter strains for biomedical research and drug development.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Mouse Model Generation & Catalog | 14,774+ Lines", "url": "https://www.genetargeting.com/", "domain": "genetargeting.com", "snippet": "Ingenious targeting laboratory generates mouse models including conventional knockouts, conditional knockouts (floxed alleles), knockins (point mutations, cDNA, gene replacement, reporters), Rosa26 and other targeted transgenics, and humanized models.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Cyagen Mouse Model Library | Humanized & Disease Models", "url": "https://www.cyagen.com/mouseatlas", "domain": "cyagen.com", "snippet": "Cyagen’s Premier Mouse Model Library Access a comprehensive mouse model catalog of 20,000+ ready-to-use strains.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Genetically Engineered Mouse Models", "url": "https://www.gempharmatech.com/animal-models/genetically-engineered-models", "domain": "gempharmatech.com", "snippet": "Browse our comprehensive catalog of over 30,000 mouse models covering various gene families, signaling pathways, and disease models.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Genetically Engineered Rodent Models and Humanized Mice", "url": "https://www.taconic.com/", "domain": "taconic.com", "snippet": "Taconic Biosciences provides genetically engineered mouse and rat models, humanized immune system mice, custom model generation, colony management, and *in vivo* research services to help accelerate drug discovery from model selection through study-ready cohorts.", "date": "2026-10-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "f728284a-f08f-485d-9867-2eed9a79780b"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:18+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q060", "objective": "EOM613 sponsor development stage indication public source", "anchors": ["EOM613 Crohn's disease", "EOM613"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q060-1", "query": "EOM613 Crohn's disease trial current status", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "EOM613 Crohn's disease trial current status", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 445.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Reports Promising Preclinical", "url": "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/01/07/3214524/0/en/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-reports-promising-preclinical-results-and-provides-an-update-on-eom613-and-eom147.html", "domain": "globenewswire.com", "snippet": "In 2025, the company completed IND-enabling toxicological and dosing preclinical animal studies for EOM613 with favorable results.\nNo adverse drug-related toxic effects were seen with daily subcutaneous injections of EOM613 for 28 consecutive days, including at 13.5 times the highest anticipated dosing levels in humans, indicating a wide margin of tolerability.\n...\nBased on these data, combined with previously reported promising results of our Phase I/IIa 2023 human clinical trial in hospitalized COVID-19 patients with cytokine-driven respiratory inflammation in Brazil, we are planning an initial Phase II exploratory clinical trial in patients with Crohn’s disease.\nThis trial is designed to include 15-20 patients and will be conducted at clinical sites in New Brunswick and Lakewood, NJ, under Principal Investigator Arkady Broder, MD, Director of Gastroenterology at the St. Peter’s Healthcare System.\nThe trial will enroll subjects with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease who have failed or could not tolerate at least one conventional therapy.\nEOM anticipates initiating the trial in the first quarter 2026 to test for signs of clinical remission and endoscopic remission with EOM613 treatment and to measure biomarkers such as C-reactive protein, calprotectin and pro-inflammatory serum cytokines.\n...\nIn light of the Brazilian clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn’s disease.", "date": "2026-01-07", "last_updated": "2026-05-19"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Reports FDA Clearance of ...", "url": "https://finance.yahoo.com/sectors/healthcare/articles/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-reports-fda-123000259.html", "domain": "finance.yahoo.com", "snippet": "# EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Reports FDA Clearance of an IND for a Clinical Trial in Cachectic Cancer Patients\n...\nMONTVALE, N.J., May 27, 2026 (GLOBE NEWSWIRE) -- **EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings, Inc.\n**(OTC: IMUC) (\"EOM\", the \"Company\") an innovative clinical-stage biotechnology company focused on developing transformative treatments for chronic inflammatory conditions and retinal diseases, is pleased to announce that the FDA has cleared its Investigational New Drug (IND) application to conduct a three-month Phase 2a clinical study of EOM613 in cachectic cancer patients.\n...\nEOM filed an IND application for the treatment of cachectic cancer patients at the FDA's Office of Cardiology, Hematology, Endocrinology and Nephrology Diseases (OCHEND) to conduct an open-label Phase 2 trial with Stage 4 cancer patients.\n...\nThe open-label clinical trial is designed to explore the effect of daily subcutaneous administration of EOM613 over a period of three months on weight, lean body mass, appetite, performance status and standardized measures of patient quality of life.\nIn addition, the safety and tolerability of EOM613 will be assessed.\n...\nAbout 20 patients, aged 18-80, are expected to be enrolled into the study.\nPatients at baseline will have a Karnofsky Performance Score of 40-80% and a life expectancy of at least four months.\nThe trial is anticipated to be initiated early in the third quarter of 2026.\n...\nThe Company is also planning an additional exploratory, open-label EOM613 clinical trial in Crohn's disease.\nThis trial is intended to be conducted at clinical sites in New Brunswick and Lakewood, NJ, under Principal Investigator Arkady Broder, MD, Director of Gastroenterology at the St.\nPeter's Healthcare System.\nThe trial is planned to enroll 15 to 20 subjects with moderate to severe Crohn's disease who have failed or could not tolerate at least one conventional therapy.\nEOM anticipates initiating the trial in the fourth quarter 2026 to test for signs of both clinical remission and endoscopic remission with EOM613 monotherapy treatment and to measure biomarkers such as C-reactive protein, calprotectin and pro-inflammatory serum cytokines.", "date": "2026-05-27", "last_updated": "2026-09-26"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "AVR-118 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/9eaf1a39a29c416eb6067ec010000bf4", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|COVID-19|Phase 1|Brazil|EOM Pharmaceuticals, Inc.|09 Aug 2021|\n...\n|NCT05212532 (RESCUE , PRNewswire) Manual|Phase 1/2|COVID-19|23|EOM613 (ICU cohort)|qsourgmemu(oghsbqiwtx) = aqfarldwqq gqoztpdyyo (saafxiyrvx )|Positive|06 Sep 2023|\n...\n|NCT05212532 (RESCUE , PRNewswire) Manual|Phase 1/2|COVID-19|23|EOM613 (non-ICU cohort)|-|Positive|06 Sep 2023|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Methods of treating inflammatory bowel diseases", "url": "https://patents.google.com/patent/WO2025049974A1/en", "domain": "patents.google.com", "snippet": "Of those patients, only approximately 20% to 30% go into remission, and of those in remission, at least half remain in remission over time based on clinical trial data (AC Moss, Gastroenterol. Hepatol. (NY), 2022: 18 (6) 360-363).\n...\n- the therapy was safe and well-tolerated using daily injections\nof up to 2 ml administered subcutaneously (sc) twice daily.\n- EOM613 was not previously investigated for use in treating IBD, such as Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis.\n...\nIn previous clinical use for treating AIDS, cancer cachexia, and COVID-19, the therapy was safe and well-tolerated using daily injections of up to 2 ml administered subcutaneously (sc) twice da\n...\nHowever, EOM613 was not previously investigated for use in treating IBD, such as Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis.\n...\nYet, the data disclosed herein demonstrates that EOM613 is an effective treatment for IBD.\n...\nAn appropriate dose can be determined, for example, in clinical trials.", "date": "2024-08-30", "last_updated": "2025-07-20"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trials | EOM Pharmaceuticals, Inc.", "url": "https://eompharma.com/clinical-trials/", "domain": "eompharma.com", "snippet": "|**Macular edema due to retinal vein Occlusion^1^** (Wroblewski and Hu, 2016)|- Phase 1, proof-of-concept study - Single-center, open-label randomized trial - Patients treated with topical squalamine (OHR102) and intravetral ranibizumab for 10 weeks, followed by randomization to continue or discontinue squalamine|20 treatment-naïve patients|At week 38, best-corrected visual acuity improved mean best-corrected visual acuity significantly more in patients who continued squalamine vs stopped|**Squalamine and ranbizumab were well tolerated**|", "date": "2020-12-02", "last_updated": "2026-09-05"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceuticals", "url": "http://biobase.whitefordresearch.com/companies/eom-pharmaceuticals", "domain": "biobase.whitefordresearch.com", "snippet": "The named pipeline consists of EOM613 and EOM147, both of which the company says have prior Phase 2 clinical experience and are intended to use a 505(b)(2) development pathway where possible.[5] EOM613 has been studied historically in cancer cachexia and in a Phase 1/2a COVID-19 respiratory-inflammation trial, and the company announced in 2023 that it was preparing a Crohn's disease clinical program based on cytokine findings from the COVID-19 study.[\n...\nEOM remains a development-stage therapeutics company, and its public filing states that neither EOM613 nor EOM147 is approved in any jurisdiction.[5] The company says it intends to pursue FDA 505(b)(2) filings for both assets, relying partly on prior clinical and public-domain data, but it also acknowledges that the FDA may require additional preclinical or clinical work.[5] Current value creation therefore depends on financing, execution of planned studies, and regulatory acceptance of the proposed development pathway.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-15"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Provides an Update on its Lead Drug Candidate EOM613", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-provides-an-update-on-its-lead-drug-candidate-eom613-301978052.html", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "snippet": "In light of these clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn's disease.\n...\n\"EOM is now planning and designing a future clinical program for Crohn's disease patients who have failed currently available therapies or are unable to tolerate them,\" stated Shalom Z. Hirschman, MD, Chief Medical Officer of EOM.\n...\nEOM is planning to undertake IND-enabling animal toxicology and preclinical studies relevant to Crohn's disease.\nEOM is also assembling a team of key opinion leader gastroenterologists, who are experts in clinical research relating to novel treatment approaches to IBD, to advise on the design and execution of the planned clinical trial.\nOn September 1, 2023, EOM filed a provisional patent application for EOM613 for use in inflammatory bowel disease with the United States Patent and Trademark Office (USPTO).\n...\n\"EOM's priority is to initiate a clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease as soon as possible,\" added Irach B. Taraporewala, PhD, Chief Executive Officer of EOM.", "date": "2023-11-07", "last_updated": "2025-07-05"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "ImmunoCellular Therapeutics Ltd. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/5ce8654ab76cb12db589696a5cb380f9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "NCT05212532\n/ Unknown statusPhase 1\nA Proof-of-Concept Study Evaluating Safety, Tolerability, and Preliminary Efficacy of EOM613 in COVID-19 Infected Patients With Severe Symptoms\n...\nIn light of these clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn's disease.\n...\n\"EOM is now planning and designing a future clinical program for Crohn's disease patients who have failed currently available therapies or are unable to tolerate them,\" stated Shalom Z. Hirschman, MD, Chief Medical Officer of EOM.\n...\nEOM is planning to undertake IND-enabling animal toxicology and preclinical studies relevant to Crohn's disease.\n...\n\"EOM's priority is to initiate a clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease as soon as possible,\" added Irach B.\n...\nEOM613, administered by subcutaneous (SC) injection was well-tolerated by hospitalized COVID-19 patients.\n...\nPost-baseline clinically significant beneficial changes were seen with EOM613 treatment for two of the blood serum cytokines involved in the cytokine storm in hospitalized COVID-19 patients, namely, soluble interleukin-2 receptor (sIL-2R) and interleukin-10 (IL-10).\n...\n|AVR-118|Crohn Disease More|Preclinical|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "ImmunoCellular Therapeutics, Ltd. (Form: S-1, Received: 06/24 ...", "url": "https://www.otcmarkets.com/filing/html?id=15911222&guid=Bhj-k6XpGGX4V3h", "domain": "otcmarkets.com", "snippet": "This drug product is currently being evaluated\nin a multicenter, open-label Phase ½a clinical trial in Brazil to treat hospitalized COVID-19 patients who have the deleterious\n“cytokine storm”- related hyperimmune response to viral infection.\nOur Brazilian clinical trial is currently generating important\ninformation on cytokines and the effect of EOM613 treatment on the levels of these cytokines and the relevance to clinical effects.\n...\nThis product has now been advanced for use in our Phase 1/2a Brazilian clinical trial in COVID-19 patients.\nThe current\nopen-label trial for COVID-19 in Brazil for EOM613 meets the regulatory Phase 1/2a clinical trial requirements of the Comissao Nacional\nde Etica em Pesquisa and the Brazilian regulatory body ANVISA.\n...\nIn\nAugust 2021, we enrolled our first patient in a 40-patient Phase 1/2a clinical trial for seriously ill COVID-19 patients in Brazil.\nThe clinical trial is currently in progress at five clinical sites across Brazil, and is approved by the Comissao Nacional de Etica em\nPesquisa national body in Brazil that reviews and approves clinical trials.\nThe clinical trial is still enrolling patients and is currently\nin progress; clinical data on the study results will be released when available.\n...\nThe drug product safety and efficacy are being evaluated over a 28-day study period in these patients.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-08"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "FDA Clears EOM Pharmaceuticals' IND For EOM613 In Treating Cancer Cachexia; Phase 2 Planned For Q3", "url": "https://www.rttnews.com/3655758/fda-clears-eom-pharmaceuticals-ind-for-eom613-in-treating-cancer-cachexia-phase-2-planned-for-q3.aspx", "domain": "rttnews.com", "snippet": "This clearance paves the way for the commencement of a Phase 2a clinical study.\n...\nThe initiation of the trial is projected for the third quarter of 2026.\nIn addition to this, EOM613 is also being developed for a planned open-label trial involving15- patients for treatment ofhn's disease.", "date": "2026-05-27", "last_updated": "2026-05-27"}], "raw": {"id": "4b9cc40a-179c-43fe-bf49-430e2915e4c4"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:18+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q059", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo animal models and results: cell-line xenograft (CDX), patient-derived xenograft (PDX), syngeneic, orthotopic, genetically engineered models", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q059-2", "query": "HEC234055 PDX model", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HEC234055 PDX model", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 18996.8, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "PDX Model Database Registration", "url": "https://www.crownbio.com/databases/pdx-models", "domain": "crownbio.com", "snippet": "Our online, searchable patient-derived xenograft (PDX) database enables scientists around the world to quickly and easily search for models that meet their specific research needs.", "date": "2025-09-26", "last_updated": "2026-07-28"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "{{ngMeta['og:title']}}", "url": "https://bio.tools/pdxnet_portal", "domain": "bio.tools", "snippet": "This is a development version of the registry.\nThere is no guarantee that the data entered here will be preserved in the future.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-10-21"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "PDX Models - PDXNet", "url": "https://www.pdxnetwork.org/models", "domain": "pdxnetwork.org", "snippet": "Models available for research are listed in CancerModels.org.\n...\nThe PDXNet Portal can be referenced for an up-to-date list of PDXNet-generated models.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-30"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Cancer Research: PDX Model Studies & Articles | Certis", "url": "https://www.certisoncology.com/resources/supporting-research/", "domain": "certisoncology.com", "snippet": "ONCOLOGY INTELLIGENCE^®^ is a registered trademark of Certis Oncology Solutions, Inc.", "date": "2026-02-05", "last_updated": "2026-07-31"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Sunshine Lake Pharma Co., Ltd. Showcases Three Innovative ...", "url": "https://www.marketscreener.com/news/sunshine-lake-pharma-co-ltd-showcases-three-innovative-oncology-drug-candidates-at-2026-aacr-annu-ce7f58d8dd8ffe27", "domain": "marketscreener.com", "snippet": "HEC234055 is an orally available high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the Group, designed to target a broad spectrum of multiple KRAS mutations as well as wild-type amplifications.\nThe preclinical data highlights announced this time are as follows: Broad-spectrum coverage and high activity: It can act on both active (guanosine triphosphate (GTP)-bound) and inactive (GDP-bound) states of KRAS, and covers various mutation types such as G12D, G12C, G12V, G13D, Q61H, and wild-type amplification.\n...\nSignificant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models (including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression.", "date": "2026-04-23", "last_updated": "2026-05-14"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] C262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4", "url": "https://stockn.xueqiu.com/06887/20260423391014.pdf", "domain": "stockn.xueqiu.com", "snippet": "二、HEC234055 — 攻克「不可成藥」靶點的口服高活性Pan-RAS分子膠抑制劑（摘\n要號#2634）\nRAS基因突變驅動約四分一的人類癌症，其中KRAS G12D、G12V等常見突變\n亞型仍存在巨大未滿足的臨床需求。在研產品HEC234055 為本集團自主研發\n的口服高活性Pan-RAS 分子膠抑制劑，旨在廣譜覆蓋KRAS 多種突變及野生型\n擴增。此次公佈的臨床前數據亮點如下：\n...\n廣譜覆蓋與高活性：可同時作用於KRAS 的活性（三磷酸鳥苷（「GTP 」）結\n合）與非活性（GDP 結合）狀態，並覆蓋G12D 、G12C 、G12V 、G13D 、\nQ61H等多種突變類型及野生型擴增。\n...\n亞納摩爾級細胞活性：在多種KRAS 突變驅動的癌細胞系中，其抗增殖活\n性達到亞納摩爾級別，半抑制濃度（「IC50」）低至0.49 納摩爾（「n M」），顯\n示出優於當前對標分子的強效抑制能力。\n...\n顯著的體內療效：在多種KRAS 突變的異種移植瘤模型中（涵蓋胰腺癌、\n肺癌及結直腸癌），口服給藥可產生劑量依賴性的腫瘤生長抑制乃至誘導\n腫瘤消退。", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-12"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Models", "url": "https://huggingface.co/models?p=13&sort=created&search=exl3", "domain": "huggingface.co", "snippet": "Inference Providers\nAdd your hardware\nHardware\n# Models\n1,815\n• 10B• Updated • 16 • 1\n...\n• 12B• Updated • 23", "date": "2026-08-11", "last_updated": "2026-08-19"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Establishment of Patient-derived Xenografts (PDX) Models ...", "url": "https://www.mdanderson.org/patients-family/diagnosis-treatment/clinical-trials/clinical-trials-index/clinical-trials-detail.IDPA13-0592.html", "domain": "mdanderson.org", "snippet": "Help us celebrate and fuel the groundbreaking innovations and transformative advancements that define MD Anderson.", "date": "2015-11-24", "last_updated": "2026-01-24"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "PDX models | CancerTools.org", "url": "https://cancertools.org/tools-category/pdx-models/", "domain": "cancertools.org", "snippet": "CancerTools provides large, diverse collections of Patient–Derived Xenograft (PDX) models for breast cancer and lung cancer that are:\n- Highly valuable in recapitulating patient tumour characteristics, including intratumour heterogeneity, genomic features, metastatic patterns, and drug responses\n- Highly translatable preclinical models that can be used to more accurately test therapy efficacy and predict patient drug responses than traditional models", "date": "2024-11-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Patient-Derived Xenograft (PDX) Models", "url": "https://www.criver.com/products-services/discovery-services/pharmacology-studies/oncology-immuno-oncology-studies/oncology-study-models/patient-derived-xenografts-pdx-models", "domain": "criver.com", "snippet": "Patient-derived xenografts (PDX) are models of cancer where the tissue or cells from a patient’s tumor are implanted into an immunodeficient or humanized mouse.", "date": "2023-04-10", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "868a1640-7be0-4727-b11e-cb441c0a2162"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:27+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q060", "objective": "EOM613 sponsor development stage indication public source", "anchors": ["EOM613 Crohn's disease", "EOM613"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q060-3", "query": "EOM613 Crohn's disease clinical trial registry", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "EOM613 Crohn's disease clinical trial registry", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 6154.2, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Reports Promising Preclinical", "url": "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/01/07/3214524/0/en/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-reports-promising-preclinical-results-and-provides-an-update-on-eom613-and-eom147.html", "domain": "globenewswire.com", "snippet": "Based on these data, combined with previously reported promising results of our Phase I/IIa 2023 human clinical trial in hospitalized COVID-19 patients with cytokine-driven respiratory inflammation in Brazil, we are planning an initial Phase II exploratory clinical trial in patients with Crohn’s disease.\nThis trial is designed to include 15-20 patients and will be conducted at clinical sites in New Brunswick and Lakewood, NJ, under Principal Investigator Arkady Broder, MD, Director of Gastroenterology at the St. Peter’s Healthcare System.\nThe trial will enroll subjects with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease who have failed or could not tolerate at least one conventional therapy.\nEOM anticipates initiating the trial in the first quarter 2026 to test for signs of clinical remission and endoscopic remission with EOM613 treatment and to measure biomarkers such as C-reactive protein, calprotectin and pro-inflammatory serum cytokines.\n...\nIn light of the Brazilian clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn’s disease.", "date": "2026-01-07", "last_updated": "2026-05-19"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Provides an Update on its Lead Drug Candidate EOM613", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-provides-an-update-on-its-lead-drug-candidate-eom613-301978052.html", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "snippet": "In light of these clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn's disease.\n...\n\"EOM is now planning and designing a future clinical program for Crohn's disease patients who have failed currently available therapies or are unable to tolerate them,\" stated Shalom Z. Hirschman, MD, Chief Medical Officer of EOM.\n...\nEOM is planning to undertake IND-enabling animal toxicology and preclinical studies relevant to Crohn's disease.\nEOM is also assembling a team of key opinion leader gastroenterologists, who are experts in clinical research relating to novel treatment approaches to IBD, to advise on the design and execution of the planned clinical trial.\n...\n\"EOM's priority is to initiate a clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease as soon as possible,\" added Irach B. Taraporewala, PhD, Chief Executive Officer of EOM.", "date": "2023-11-07", "last_updated": "2025-07-05"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "ImmunoCellular Therapeutics Ltd. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/5ce8654ab76cb12db589696a5cb380f9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "NCT05212532\n/ Unknown statusPhase 1\nA Proof-of-Concept Study Evaluating Safety, Tolerability, and Preliminary Efficacy of EOM613 in COVID-19 Infected Patients With Severe Symptoms\n...\nIn light of these clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn's disease.\n...\n\"EOM is now planning and designing a future clinical program for Crohn's disease patients who have failed currently available therapies or are unable to tolerate them,\" stated Shalom Z. Hirschman, MD, Chief Medical Officer of EOM.\n...\nEOM is planning to undertake IND-enabling animal toxicology and preclinical studies relevant to Crohn's disease.\n...\nThis exploratory Phase 1/2a clinical trial (NCT05212532), designated RESCUE, was a proof-of-concept, open-label study evaluating the safety, tolerability, and preliminary efficacy measures, such as the effects on serum cytokines, when EOM613 was added to the standard-of-care therapy.\n...\n|AVR-118|Crohn Disease More|Preclinical|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trials in the European Union - EMA", "url": "https://euclinicaltrials.eu/", "domain": "euclinicaltrials.eu", "snippet": "This website provides information on individual clinical trials in the European Union and European Economic Area since its launch on 31 January 2022.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-10"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "ICTRP Search Portal - World Health Organization (WHO)", "url": "https://www.who.int/tools/clinical-trials-registry-platform/the-ictrp-search-portal", "domain": "who.int", "snippet": "The ICTRP Search Portal aims to provide a single point of access to information about ongoing and completed clinical trials.\nIt provides a searchable database containing the trial registration data sets made available by data providers around the world meeting criteria for content and quality control.\nTrial registration data sets are available on the ICTRP Search Portal in English only.\n...\nIf available, these may be searched by going directly to the web sites\nof the individual registries.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Find Studies | ClinicalTrials.gov", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/find-studies", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "A patient registry is a type of observational study.\n**Note:** The ClinicalTrials.gov results database became available in September 2008.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-18"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Reports FDA Clearance of ...", "url": "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/05/27/3301943/0/en/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-reports-fda-clearance-of-an-ind-for-a-clinical-trial-in-cachectic-cancer-patients.html", "domain": "globenewswire.com", "snippet": "The Company is also planning an additional exploratory, open-label EOM613 clinical trial in Crohn’s disease.\nThis trial is intended to be conducted at clinical sites in New Brunswick and Lakewood, NJ, under Principal Investigator Arkady Broder, MD, Director of Gastroenterology at the St. Peter’s Healthcare System.\nThe trial is planned to enroll 15 to 20 subjects with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease who have failed or could not tolerate at least one conventional therapy.\nEOM anticipates initiating the trial in the fourth quarter 2026 to test for signs of both clinical remission and endoscopic remission with EOM613 monotherapy treatment and to measure biomarkers such as C-reactive protein, calprotectin and pro-inflammatory serum cytokines.", "date": "2026-05-27", "last_updated": "2026-05-28"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "FDA Clears IND for EOM613 Trial in Cancer Cachexia Patients", "url": "https://www.clinicaltrialvanguard.com/news/fda-clears-ind-for-eom613-trial-in-cancer-cachexia-patients/", "domain": "clinicaltrialvanguard.com", "snippet": "Cancer cachexia has killed patients before their underlying malignancy could, yet across decades of failed trials no drug has earned FDA approval for the condition — and EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings is now cleared to run only a 20-patient open-label Phase 2a study to test whether that changes.\n...\nThe company’s simultaneous planning for a Crohn’s diseaseCrohn’s disease trial in Q4 2026 signals an intent to validate EOM613’s cytokine-modulation mechanism across indications rather than commit resources to a single confirmatory cachexia program.", "date": "2026-05-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical plans 15-20-patient Phase II Crohn's disease ...", "url": "https://www.sahmcapital.com/news/content/eom-pharmaceutical-plans-15-20-patient-phase-ii-crohns-disease-trial-of-eom613-in-q1-2026-2026-03-24", "domain": "sahmcapital.com", "snippet": "EOM Pharmaceutical plans 15-20-patient Phase II Crohn’s disease trial of EOM613 in Q1 2026\n...\n- EOM reported that it completed IND-enabling toxicology and dosing animal studies for EOM613 with no adverse drug-related toxic effects observed after 28 days of daily subcutaneous dosing.\n- Prior Phase I/IIa human trial results in hospitalized COVID-19 patients were previously presented, and the company said it plans an initial Phase II exploratory trial of EOM613 in Crohn’s disease.\n- The planned Crohn’s study is expected to enroll 15–20 patients and is intended to start in Q1 2026 at sites in New Brunswick and Lakewood, New Jersey.", "date": "2026-03-24", "last_updated": "2026-04-05"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "EU Clinical Trials Register - Update", "url": "https://www.clinicaltrialsregister.eu/", "domain": "clinicaltrialsregister.eu", "snippet": "All ongoing clinical trials in the EU/EEA are displayed through the\nClinical Trials Information System.", "date": "2021-01-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "8466227e-f12a-4364-9e07-86782e8110b5"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:33+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q059", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo animal models and results: cell-line xenograft (CDX), patient-derived xenograft (PDX), syngeneic, orthotopic, genetically engineered models", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q059-1", "query": "HEC234055 HNSCC xenograft", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "HEC234055 HNSCC xenograft", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 29834.4, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] E262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4 - HKEXnews", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301899.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "HEC234055 — Orally Available High-Activity Pan-RAS Molecular Glue Inhibitor\nTargeting the ‘‘Undruggable’’ (Abstract #2634)\n...\nThe drug candidate under development HEC234055 is an orally available high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the Group, designed to target a broad spectrum of multiple KRAS mutations as well as wild-type amplifications.\n...\nBroad-spectrum coverage and high activity: It can act on both active (guanosine triphosphate (‘‘GTP’’)-bound) and inactive (GDP-bound) states of KRAS, and covers various mutation types such as G12D, G12C, G12V, G13D, Q61H, and wild-type amplification.\n...\nSignificant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models\n(including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce\ndose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression.\n...\nMDA-MB-231\n...\nbreast\ncancer),\n...\nsingle-agent\n...\nThe preclinical research data disclosed in this announcement are derived from laboratory and animal model studies, and the relevant drug candidates have not yet undergone clinical trials\nin\nhumans.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-23"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Xenograft Cell Line Reference Database", "url": "https://www.taconic.com/resources/databases/xenograft-cell-line-reference-database", "domain": "taconic.com", "snippet": "Taconic Biosciences has been a source of immunodeficient mice and rats for use in xenograft studies for more than 40 years.", "date": "2025-10-21", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Human Xenograft Models_GemPharmatech", "url": "https://www.gempharmatech.com/product/models_type100138_3733944.html", "domain": "gempharmatech.com", "snippet": "Let us know which event you're attending and we'd be happy to connect with you.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-21"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Head and neck squamous cell carcinoma cell lines: Established models and rationale for selection", "url": "https://scispace.com/pdf/head-and-neck-squamous-cell-carcinoma-cell-lines-established-1i5aezshpe.pdf", "domain": "scispace.com", "snippet": "HNSCC cell lines that have grown as xenografts in mice.\n...\nTable A5.\nHNSCC xenograft models.\nNude mice", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "xenograft.orgXenograft Model Database", "url": "https://xenograft.org/", "domain": "xenograft.org", "snippet": "Each model page includes biological background, growth kinetics, available molecular data, and study-ready configurations to streamline the design of your in vivo efficacy or pharmacology program.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "A controlled trial of HNSCC patient-derived xenografts reveals broad efficacy of PI3Kα inhibition in controlling tumor growth - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30468243/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "# A controlled trial of HNSCC patient-derived xenografts reveals broad efficacy of PI3Kα inhibition in controlling tumor growth\n...\nHere, using both an HNSCC specific, 28-cell line panel and a PDX clinical trial of 80 xenografts derived from 20 unique HNSCC tumors, we systematically examine patterns of response to PI3K inhibition in HNSCC.\nWe find EGFR, AKT1 and CSMD1 copy number aberrations, but not PIK3CA mutations, to be associated with responsiveness to PI3K-targeted drugs.\nFurther, we reveal PI3Kα inhibition to be almost globally tumoristatic in HNSCC xenografts regardless of PIK3CA mutational status, emphasizing its potential as a stabilizing neoadjuvant therapy for HNSCC patients.\n...\n** ClinicalTrials.gov NCT03138070.", "date": "2019-10-15", "last_updated": "2025-07-14"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "New patient derived head and neck cancer xenograft (PDX ...", "url": "https://www.epo-berlin.com/assets/documents/2023_PDX_poster_AACR_072023_final.pdf", "domain": "epo-berlin.com", "snippet": "Out of 176 transplanted patient HNSCC, we established and characterized 85 new xenografts (Figures 1-4).\n...\nOur PDX were established from fresh surgery tissue of primary and recurrent tumors or lymph nodes by direct subcutaneous implantation into immune-deficient mice.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-02-04"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Diagnostics of HNSCC Patients: An Analysis of Cell Lines ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9139588/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "b. We identified nine studies that used different pdx HPV-positive HNSCC models.\nThe majority of the analyzed studies were performed in the USA, and all authors looked for the successful establishment of pdx models from HNSCC, including preserving the genomic profile (e.g., HPV status, p16, PI3K).\nThe established pdx were treated with specific drugs: PI3K inhibitors alone or in combination with cetuximab, pan-HER inhibitors, and Spleen tyrosine kinase (SYK) inhibitors.", "date": "2022-04-25", "last_updated": "2025-09-13"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Tumor grafts derived from patients with head and neck squamous ...", "url": "https://d-nb.info/1096538458/34", "domain": "d-nb.info", "snippet": "We developed and characterized the patient-derived xenograft (PDX) model because it is likely to recapitulate human tumor biology.\nMethods: We transplanted 30 primary tumors directly into mice.\nThe histology and stromal components were analyzed by immunohistochemistry.\nGene expression analysis was conducted on patient tumors and on PDXs and cell lines derived from one PDX and from independent, human tumors.\nResults: Five of 30 (17%) transplanted tumors could be serially passaged.\nEngraftment was more frequent among\nHNSCC with poor differentiation and nodal disease.\nThe tumors maintained the histologic characteristics of the\n...\nEstablished HNSCC cell lines\n(Tu167 and Osc19) were maintained as previously de-\n...\nGeneration of patient-derived HNSCC xenografts\nWe received 30 residual tumor samples from surgically resected specimens.\nThirteen of the tissue samples were small (<0.5 cm3) and were implanted in their entirety, whereas 17 larger tumors (>0.5 cm3) were divided to allow RNA/DNA collection (Figure 1).\nThe tumors were predominantly oral squamous cancer, because these are most likely to undergo primary surgical resection.\nFive of the implanted tumors engrafted, for an overall en- graftment rate of 17%.\n...\nDiscussion\nIn this study we transplanted 30 human HNSCC tumors directly into mice and serially transplanted those that engrafted.\nThe overall engraftment rate was 17% with poorly differentiated and node-positive tumors more likely to engraft.\nThere were significant expression differences between the human tumors that subsequently grew in mice and those that did not.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-27"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Setup and characterization of a human head and neck squamous cell carcinoma xenograft model in nude rats - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17141073/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "A human head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) xenograft model in nude rats was established via subcutaneous inoculation of a human-origin HNSCC cell line, SCC-4.\nThe tumor was evaluated for growth characteristics, pathologic features by hematoxylin-eosin (HE) staining, and immunohistochemistry of epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR).\n2-[18F] fluoro-2-deoxy-D-glucose (18F-FDG) positron emission tomography (PET) imaging characteristics were studied too.\n...\nA new HNSCC animal model was successfully established.\nTumor sizes reached about 1 cm3 on day 15 after tumor cell inoculation.\nHE staining pathology has confirmed that this tumor is a typical SCC.\nEGFR immunohistochemistry demonstrated this tumor model to be strongly EGFR positive.\n18F-FDG PET study has shown that 18F-FDG accumulated in tumors.\n...\nThis study has demonstrated that this tumor model is an appropriate HNSCC tumor model for animal studies on HNSCC.", "date": "2006-12-19", "last_updated": "2025-04-19"}], "raw": {"id": "5e2c9e36-5277-4691-8a0f-312c08e6f9d0"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:33+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q061", "objective": "FG-3149 sponsor development stage indication public source", "anchors": ["FG-3149"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q061-2", "query": "FG-3149 Crohn's disease", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "FG-3149 Crohn's disease", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2352.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Mechanical stress-induced connective tissue growth factor ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11427090/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Treatment of CD rats with anti-CTGF antibody FG-3149 reduced fibrosis and collagen content in both *sites P* and *I* and exhibited consistent trends toward normalizing expression of collagen mRNAs.\nIn conclusion, our studies suggest that mechanical stress, by upregulating profibrotic mediators, i.e., CTGF, may play a critical role in fibrosis in CD.\n**NEW & NOTEWORTHY** We found that CTGF expression increased significantly not only in the inflammation site but in the distended segment proximal to inflammation in a rodent model of CD-like colitis.\nRelease of mechanical distention prevented CTGF expression in CD rats, whereas direct stretch induced CTGF expression.\nTreatment with anti-CTGF antibody reduced fibrosis and collagen contents in CD rats.\n...\nTo test the effect of neutralizing anti-CTGF antibody in the model, TNBS-instilled rats were administered with either IgG control or anti-CTGF antibody FG-3149 (supplied by FibroGen, Inc., San Fransisco, CA) at 40 mg/kg i.p. in 1 mL every 3 days (19–21), starting on the day TNBS was instilled.\n...\nFG-3149 treatment significantly (*P* < 0.05) mitigated fibrosis index in *site I* compared with IgG-treated group (Fig.\n...\nFG-3149 treatment exhibited consistent trends to normalize the expression of all collagen 1a1, collagen 1a2, and collagen 3a mRNAs but with no statistical significance between FG-3149 and IgG group (*P* values were in the range of 0.056–0.085) (Fig.", "date": "2024-07-02", "last_updated": "2025-11-28"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "New weapons for the management of perianal Crohn's disease", "url": "https://oamonitor.ireland.openaire.eu/national/search/publication?pid=10.1016/j.bpg.2025.102030", "domain": "oamonitor.ireland.openaire.eu", "snippet": "Researcher Monitors\n...\nWe’re experiencing a temporary issue.\nPlease\nrefresh the page", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-30"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Serum biomarkers of fibrostenotic Crohn's disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/1751-2980.12913", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Opens in a new window\nOpens an external website\nOpens an external website in a new window", "date": "2020-06-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-08"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Efficacy and safety of filgotinib as induction and maintenance therapy for Crohn's disease (DIVERSITY): a phase 3, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled trial", "url": "https://www.thelancet.com/journals/langas/article/PIIS2468-1253(24)00272-3/fulltext", "domain": "thelancet.com", "snippet": "*vs*15% for placebo) and a difference of 15% in the endoscopic response rate (25% for filgotinib 200 mg\n*vs*10% for placebo) at week 10.\n...\n|Induction study A|Induction study B|\n|--|--|\n|Placebo (n=237)|Filgotinib 100 mg (n=245)|Filgotinib 200 mg (n=222)|Placebo (n=229)|Filgotinib 100 mg (n=228)|Filgotinib 200 mg (n=202)|\n...\n|Crohn's disease duration, years ‡|9·3 (8·4)|9·9 (10·0)|9·2 (8·4)|13·0 (9·5)|13·3 (9·7)|11·5 (8·0)|\n...\nFor the key secondary induction endpoints, nominally significant differences were reported between filgotinib 200 mg and placebo for CDAI clinical remission in induction studies A and B, and for the combined endpoint of PRO2 clinical remission and an endoscopic response in induction study A (figure 2C, D).\n...\nSimilarly, a greater proportion of patients treated with filgotinib 200 mg had an endoscopic response than did placebo-treated patients (filgotinib 200 mg–filgotinib 200 mg\n...\nFor the key secondary maintenance endpoints, nominally significantly greater proportions of patients in the filgotinib 200 mg group had combined PRO2 clinical remission and an endoscopic response, and sustained PRO2 clinical remission than in the placebo group (figure 3B).\n...\nNo statistically significant difference in the endoscopic response rate was reported in either induction study for filgotinib 200 mg compared with placebo.\n...\nTreatment differences between the filgotinib 100 mg and placebo groups were not nominally significant for any of the endpoints in biologic-naive or biologic-experienced patients in the induction and maintenance studies.\n...\nThe most frequently reported serious TEAE (≥2% of patients in any filgotinib or associated placebo group) was an exacerbation, flare, or worsening of Crohn's disease (appendix p 39).", "date": "2025-02-01", "last_updated": "2025-08-09"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Intestinal Fibrosis in Crohn's Disease - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12194019/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "However, there are currently no approved anti-fibrotic therapies for CD.\n...\nPamrevlumab (FG-3019) is a human monoclonal antibody targeting CTGF and has been approved for the treatment of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis [115].\nDespite advancements in our understanding of CTGF’s role in fibrogenesis and the development of targeted therapies like pamrevlumab, there is still a significant lack of clinical evidence assessing their effectiveness in treating intestinal fibrosis associated with Crohn’s disease.\n...\nIn contrast to inflammation, there are no effective medical treatments for fibrosis, highlighting the urgency of targeted anti-fibrotic therapies.", "date": "2025-06-08", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "����������", "url": "https://cdn.clinicaltrials.gov/large-docs/73/NCT02697773/Prot_000.pdf", "domain": "cdn.clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "090177e1904dc815\\Approved\\Approved On: 04-Feb-2019 16:23 (GMT)", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-01-26"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "NI\\ #+ )レ", "url": "https://cdn.clinicaltrials.gov/large-docs/94/NCT02614794/Prot_001.pdf", "domain": "cdn.clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "���������\n�������������������������������\n���������\n��������������������������������", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-07"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for luminal and perianal Crohn's disease management - Luisa Bertin, Martina Crepaldi, Miriana Zanconato, Greta Lorenzon, Daria Maniero, Caterina de Barba, Erica Bonazzi, Sonia Facchin, Marco Scarpa, Cesare Ruffolo, Imerio Angriman, Andrea Buda, Fabiana Zingone, Brigida Barberio, Edoardo Vincenzo Savarino, 2024", "url": "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/17562848241303651", "domain": "journals.sagepub.com", "snippet": "|Combination therapy|Guselkumab and Golimumab|P19 IL23/TNF|IV, SC|Phase II|Active|NCT05242471|2029-03-27|✔|✘|✔|✘|✘|✘|\n...\nThe SPECIFI-CD trial (NCT06637631) is a phase II, multinational, multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study evaluating SAR441566 in adults with moderate-to-severe CD.^31^ The trial aims to assess the efficacy and safety of different doses of SAR441566 compared to a placebo.\nIt involves a 12-week induction phase and a 40-week maintenance period, followed by up to 92 weeks of open-label treatment.\nPrimary outcomes include endoscopic response and clinical remission.\nThe study will enroll 260 participants, with results expected by May 2029.\n...\nRVT-3101 is a fully human IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A, administered SC once a month.\nThe TAHOE study (NCT05910528) is a phase II multicenter double-blind two-arm evaluating two doses of RVT-3101 once in patients with moderate to severely active CD.\n...\nA 12-week double-blind, placebo-controlled phase II trial evaluating the efficacy and safety of E6011 (quetmolimab), a humanized IgG2 monoclonal antibody against human FKN, in participants with active CD, is completed (NCT03733314).\n...\nMORF-057 is a small molecule specifically designed to target the α4β7 receptor.^90^ The GARNET study is randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase II trial (NCT06226883) and is currently recruiting participants to investigate the safety and effectiveness of MORF-057 in adults diagnosed with moderately to severely active CD.\n...\nMBF-118 is a small molecule designed as a partial agonist of PPARγ.\nA phase IIa (NCT05940558) study with the aim of assessing the safety, tolerability, and exploratory efficacy of a daily oral regimen of 100 or 200 mg MBF-118 over 28 days in patients with CD exhibiting stenosis was recently completed.\n...\nAGMB-129 is an oral small molecule GI-restricted inhibitor of ALK5 (TGFβR1) designed for treating fibrostenosing CD.\nThe STENOVA clinical trial (NCT05843578) is actively recruiting participants.\nThis is a phase IIa study designed to be randomized, placebo-controlled, double-blind, that aims to assess the safety, tolerability, the PK, and PD of the investigational drug AGMB-129 in patients with fibrostenotic CD.\n...\nNumerous clinical trials are underway to evaluate the safety and efficacy of novel therapies, including molecules like S1PR modulators, selective JAK inhibitors, anti-TL1A antibodies, and PPARγ agonists.", "date": "2024-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohn's disease Drugs Pipeline 2026", "url": "https://www.delveinsight.com/report-store/crohns-disease-cd-pipeline-insight", "domain": "delveinsight.com", "snippet": "## AGMB-129: AgomAb Therapeutics\nAGMB-129 is an investigational oral, gastrointestinal-restricted small-molecule inhibitor of activin receptor-like kinase 5 (ALK5/TGF-?\nreceptor I) being developed by Agomab Therapeutics for fibrostenosing Crohn's disease, a form characterized by intestinal fibrosis and strictures.\nIn the Phase II STENOVA-2 trial, AGMB-129 is being evaluated against placebo in patients with symptomatic fibrostenotic Crohn's disease to assess its safety, efficacy, and ability to reduce fibrotic bowel narrowing while patients continue standard therapy.", "date": "2026-08-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Abdominal Extension of Fournier Gangrene From Undiagnosed Crohn’s Disease: A Case Report", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/milmed/article/188/1-2/e426/6179147?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Although complications of necrotizing soft tissue infections (NSTIs) such as Fournier Gangrene (FG) are rare, they are associated with significant morbidity and mortality.\nLaboratory and radiological studies may aid in the evaluation of NSTI; however, focus should remain on physical examination and prompt surgical consultation, as these infections can spread rapidly with significant increases in mortality with delayed management.\n...\nFollow-up evaluations revealed previously undiagnosed Crohn’s disease with fistula-in-ano as the inciting factor.\n...\nSuspicion for necrotizing soft tissue infections (NSTIs), including Fournier gangrene (FG), which can spread and destroy subcutaneous and fascial tissue with significant increases in mortality, should be a leading concern.\nWe describe a unique case in which an active duty male soldier diagnosed with persistent perineal and scrotal cellulitis despite outpatient antibiotics presented to the emergency department (ED) with severe FG extending into his abdominal cavity.\nSubsequent evaluations discovered fistula-in-ano with cellulitis of the left buttock secondary to undiagnosed Crohn’s disease as the primary cause.\nWe provide a brief review of literature on the incidence of NSTI secondary to Crohn’s disease and the priority for clinical evaluation and surgical consultation over laboratory testing in these cases.\n...\nFollow-up imaging identified a definitive fistula-in-ano approximately 1 month later (Fig. 3), with subsequent identification of underlying Crohn’s disease ultimately confirmed through endoscopy with biopsy.\n...\n10 Although a review of the literature does not demonstrate significant correlation between FG and Crohn’s disease, the initial presentation of the latter via anorectal diseases such as fistula-in-ano or perirectal abscess can precipitate FG.", "date": "2023-01-04", "last_updated": "2024-09-25"}], "raw": {"id": "b9285125-1ac9-45df-ad30-c37f01a2e3a3"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:36+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q061", "objective": "FG-3149 sponsor development stage indication public source", "anchors": ["FG-3149"], "mode": "queries", "case_id": "q061-3", "query": "FG-3149 FibroGen clinical development", "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": "FG-3149 FibroGen clinical development", "search_type": "fast", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2114.0, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "FibroGen Reports First Quarter 2024 Financial Results", "url": "https://investor.kyntrabio.com/node/13811/pdf", "domain": "investor.kyntrabio.com", "snippet": "Additional data from a total of 56 biomarker unselected and heavily pre-treated patients in a Phase 1 monotherapy study of\nFG-3246 in mCRPC reported.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-15"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "FibroGen Inc. (NASDAQ:FGEN) : Ptab Cases :: Law360", "url": "https://www.law360.co.uk/companies/fibrogen-inc/ptab_cases", "domain": "law360.co.uk", "snippet": "## PTAB Cases (74)\n...\n2. Filed: September 12, 2025 | IPR2025-01531 Apotex Inc.", "date": "2026-10-03", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Kyntra Bio - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyntra_Bio", "domain": "en.wikipedia.org", "snippet": "Following the sale of its Chinese subsidiary, FibroGen is now left primarily with FG-3246, an asset it acquired from Fortis Therapeutics, a defunct biotech company, at no upfront cost with a large milestone due only if the drug advances into Phase 3.\n...\n|Roxadustat|Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)|ANDES, OLYMPUS, ALPS, ROCKIES, SIERRAS, HIMALAYAS, PYRENEES|FDA rejected August 2021|Company changed prespecified statistical criteria for cardiovascular safety analysis and FDA raised concerns about data integrity|\n|Roxadustat|Anemia in Myelodysplastic Syndromes (MDS)|MATTERHORN|Failed May 2023|Did not meet primary endpoint|\n|Pamrevlumab|Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF)|ZEPHYRUS-1, ZEPHYRUS-2|Failed June 2023|ZEPHYRUS-1 missed primary endpoint; ZEPHYRUS-2 terminated after interim review|\n...\nAll of its US Phase 3 trials for both roxadustat and pamrevlumab including indications for anemia, Duchenne muscular dystrophy, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, and pancreatic cancer ultimately failed or were rejected.\nIn 2025, the only significant remaining asset is FG-3246.\n...\nFibroGen is also developing FG-3180, a PET imaging agent, to help track the cancer and predict responses to FG-3246.\nA Phase 2 trial of FG-3246 began in September 2025, with full completion expected in March 2028.", "date": "2025-09-10", "last_updated": "2026-06-27"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Our Partners - FibroGen", "url": "https://www.fibrogen.com/our-partners", "domain": "fibrogen.com", "snippet": "FG-3246 is being developed for the treatment of metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer (mCRPC).", "date": "2016-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-10-12"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "FibroGen, Inc. (NASDAQ:FGEN) Q1 2024 Earnings Conference Call ...", "url": "https://reportify-1252068037.cos.ap-beijing.myqcloud.com/media/production/s_4689968_a2b4c0ac65184dcf6818814db38a7a6b.pdf", "domain": "reportify-1252068037.cos.ap-beijing.myqcloud.com", "snippet": "In April, we released compelling data from our FG-3246 Phase 1 monotherapy trial which Deyaa will go into later on the call.\n...\nLastly, in April, we announced compelling top line results from our Phase 1 monotherapy study of FG-3246 in patients with metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer.\n...\nFG-3246 is a first-in-class ADC for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer, colorectal cancer, and other tumor types.\nFG-3246 binds to a cell receptor target that internalizes upon antibody binding and is present in approximately 50% to 70% of prostate tumors, but that demonstrates a very limited expression in most normal tissue, making it an ideal ADC target candidate.\nFG-3246 is comprised of an anti\nCD46 antibody, YS5 linked to an antimitotic agent, MMAE, which is clinically validated and FDA-approved\nADC payload.\n...\nFG-3246 has demonstrated monotherapy clinical efficacy in metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer.\n...\nThe completed Phase 1 trial includes a total of 56 metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer patients who are\n...\nFG-3246 demonstrated an acceptable safety profile with adverse events consistent with those observed in other anti-body drug conjugate therapies with an\nMMAE payload.\n...\nLastly, we are planning a meeting with the FDA in the third quarter to discuss the details of the FG-3246 development program, contingent upon reaching agreement with the FDA on the trial design, we anticipate the initiation of a Phase 2/3 dose optimization and registration-enabling study in metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer in the fourth quarter of 2024.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "FibroGen : Corporate Presentation - September 2025", "url": "https://www.marketscreener.com/news/fibrogen-corporate-presentation-september-2025-ce7d58d9db80f422", "domain": "marketscreener.com", "snippet": "FG-3180, a PET imaging agent, in clinical development as potential novel patient selection biomarker", "date": "2025-09-18", "last_updated": "2025-09-29"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "FibroGen | Drug Developments | Pipeline Prospector", "url": "https://www.pharmacompass.com/pipeline-prospector-drugs-in-development/fibrogen", "domain": "pharmacompass.com", "snippet": "The collaboration aims to focus on the development of FG-3165, a galectin-9 (Gal9) targeted antibody, combined with Libtayo for treating patients related to neoplasms.", "date": "2025-02-20", "last_updated": "2025-08-28"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "FibroGen Reports Third Quarter 2024 Financial Results - Kyntra Bio", "url": "https://investor.kyntrabio.com/news-releases/news-release-details/fibrogen-reports-third-quarter-2024-financial-results", "domain": "investor.kyntrabio.com", "snippet": "FG-3246 (also known as FOR46), a first-in-class antibody-drug conjugate (ADC) targeting CD46 is in development for the treatment of metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer.\nThis program also includes the development of an associated CD46-targeted PET imaging agent, FG-3180.", "date": "2024-11-12", "last_updated": "2026-09-27"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "FibroGen (FGEN) Drug Pipeline – Trials, Approvals & Status Updates", "url": "https://www.tipranks.com/stocks/ch:fgen/drug-pipeline", "domain": "tipranks.com", "snippet": "Fg-3246\nMetastatic Castration-Resistant Prostate Cancer\nPhase II\nNot Yet Recruiting\nA Study of FG-3246 in Participants With Metastatic Castration-Resistant Prostate Cancer (mCRPC)\n...\nWhat drugs does FibroGen (FGEN) have in its pipeline\nFGEN is currently developing the following drugs: Fg-3246, Roxadustat, Sepo®, Corticosteroids, Pamrevlumab.\nThese drug candidates are in various stages of clinical development as the company works toward FDA approval.", "date": "2025-10-10", "last_updated": "2025-10-10"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "FibroGen wields axe as pancreatic cancer drug is canned", "url": "https://pharmaphorum.com/news/fibrogen-wields-axe-pancreatic-cancer-drug-canned", "domain": "pharmaphorum.com", "snippet": "It also recently got FDA approval to start trials of FG-3165, an anti-galectin-9 antibody with potential as a treatment for solid tumours, and an anti-CCR8 antibody codenamed FG-3175 in preclinical development.", "date": "2026-09-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}], "raw": {"id": "efca79fe-aa61-410d-99d6-b0c92713a4bf"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:42:38+00:00"}
